export const JUNIOR_ADVENTURES = {
  addy: {
    id: "addy",
    title: "The Ledger of Lost Sums",
    tagline: "Tallyford's Great Ledger has stopped adding up, and somebody exact had better look into it.",
    items: [
      { id: "addy_abacus", name: "The Pocket Abacus",   stat: 0, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won with a sharp Arithmetic check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "addy_ledger", name: "The Balanced Ledger", stat: 2, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "addy_lens",   name: "The Counting Lens",   stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { brass_frame: "Brass Frame", clear_quartz: "Clear Quartz", tally_ribbon: "Tally Ribbon" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      /* ---------- ACT ONE: out of Little Reckoning ---------- */
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🐜", text: "Addy counted everything. Cobblestones on the way to market (four hundred and twelve), clouds shaped like clouds (all of them), the number of times the mayor said 'basically' in one speech (a personal record of thirty-one). Counting was not a hobby. Hobbies can be put down. Counting was more like breathing, if breathing occasionally corrected other people's arithmetic.\n\nSo when word came down the valley that Tallyford's Great Ledger had stopped adding up — that sums were wandering off its pages in the night and coming back different, or not coming back at all — nobody in Little Reckoning so much as looked at anyone else. Everyone simply looked at Addy.\n\n'I'll need a spare pencil,' said Addy, who had already packed one. 'And company. Bring the sort of friends who don't mind weather, walking, or being right at inconvenient moments.'", next: "glint_check" },
      glint_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u2728", stat: 0, dc: 10,
        text: "The lane out of Little Reckoning ran beside a ditch, and the ditch was full of morning light and dubious water. Something in it glinted. Then something else did. Then a dozen things at once, because ditches are like that.\n\nIn Addy's experience roughly one glint in nine was worth stooping for, which meant the trick was not the stooping. The trick was the counting. Count the glints, take the ninths, and stoop precisely once, on the right one, before the water rearranged its opinion.\n\nSomebody sharp with Arithmetic had better do the counting.",
        success: "find", fail: "glint_miss" },
      glint_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf0a", text: "The counting went muddy. Eighteen glints, or nineteen? The ninths refused to sit still, the stoop landed on a bottle top, and whatever had really been shining slid away down the culvert with the smug murmur of moving water.\n\n'It'll wash up somewhere,' said Addy, straightening with dignity. Which was true, and also no help at all, and Addy knew the difference between those two things better than anyone in the county.\n\n(The Pocket Abacus stays lost \u2014 this time. Another run through this adventure may find it.)", next: "gate_fight" },
      find: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\uddee", grantItem: "addy_abacus", text: "The count came out clean \u2014 nineteen glints, two worth a look, one worth the stoop \u2014 and the stoop was exact, and so was the prize: a pocket abacus, beads worn smooth by a thousand careful thumbs, the frame still true.\n\nSomebody exact had carried this once, and dropped it, and never come back for it, which was a small sad story Addy chose not to finish. The beads slid with the satisfying certainty of a thing that has never once been wrong.\n\nThe Pocket Abacus is yours. (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)", next: "gate_fight" },
      gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It was sitting on the waymark stone at the parish boundary: a Smudge, small and grey and pleased with itself, gently blurring the carved mileage to Tallyford from EIGHT into 'eight-ish, probably, who's asking'.\n\nAddy put down the pack. Some things cannot be walked past. A wrong number on a signpost is how lost people happen, and lost people are how everything else happens.\n\n'Move along,' said Addy, in the tone one column of figures uses to address another.\n\nThe Smudge did not move along. The Smudge got bigger.",
        enemy: { name: "The Signpost Smudge", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The abacus beads sang, the answer landed square, and a corner of the Smudge came away in a puff of corrected grey.",
          strikeText: "The Smudge billowed forward and the whole morning went briefly approximate. Somewhere in the fog one of the party sat down hard on a hedge that was only roughly there.",
          defeatText: "The Smudge came apart with a small embarrassed pop, and the signpost said EIGHT again — firmly, and, Addy liked to think, gratefully." },
        victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
      gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "The party dusted themselves down while the signpost recovered its convictions.\n\n'Two of them in a week,' muttered Addy, making a note in the margin of the day. First the Counting Stone at home, now a boundary marker on the Tallyford road. Small grey scouts, sitting on the exact things, seeing what gave. Somebody, somewhere, was checking the fences of the world — and the road to Tallyford ran straight through the gap in them.", next: "crossroads" },
      gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "There is no shame in an orderly withdrawal, Addy informed the party, provided it is properly minuted. It was minuted.\n\nThey went round by the sheep field instead, which took the best part of an hour, involved a great deal of polite negotiation with sheep, and put a smear of grey on the morning that would not quite polish out. Behind them the Smudge settled back onto the signpost, smug as fog on a window, and the mileage to Tallyford stayed eight-ish all day.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road to Tallyford split three ways at a signpost that had clearly been arguing with itself for years.\n\nThe left arm pointed to the MARKET OF ROUGHLY, where everything costs 'about' something and exact change is considered showing off. The middle arm pointed to the BRIDGE OF HALVES, famous for being only ever half finished, and then half of that. The right arm pointed into the FEN OF FEWER, of which the sign said only: SOME DISTANCE. VARIOUS DANGERS. FEWER TRAVELLERS COME OUT THAN GO IN, BUT NOT MANY FEWER.\n\nSomewhere down each road, if the tinkers' gossip ran true, a lens-maker's Brass Frame was waiting to be earned. Frames, like respect, are never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Market of Roughly", next: "market", preview: "Haggle through a market where nobody counts properly. A hard bargaining puzzle guards the Brass Frame." },
        { label: "Cross the Bridge of Halves", next: "bridge", preview: "Pick your way across a bridge built by someone who kept halving the plans. A dice challenge guards the Brass Frame." },
        { label: "Wade the Fen of Fewer", next: "fen", preview: "The marsh where numbers go missing. A Bog Roughling guards the Brass Frame — this one is a fight." },
      ] },
      /* ---- route one: the market ---- */
      market: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Market of Roughly was chaos with price tags. 'THREE-ISH groats!' bellowed a stallholder. 'Roughly a dozen!' cried another, holding nine onions and daring anyone to count them. Addy moved through it all totting up exact totals under its breath, and the market grew strangely quiet around the little ant, the way crowds do near someone who knows precisely what they are owed.\n\nAt a junk stall, wedged between a left boot and a jar of assorted lids, sat a Brass Frame — sturdy, hinged, exactly the right size for holding a lens. The stallholder, a weasel with an honest face and dishonest scales, watched Addy notice it, and smiled the smile of someone who has just heard money clear its throat.", next: "market_puzzle" },
      market_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'Brass Frame, is it?' said the weasel. 'Everything on this stall is a deal. Jars of pickled sevens: nine groats apiece, and for every four you buy I throw in a fifth free, on account of my generous nature.' The weasel leaned closer, smelling of vinegar and opportunity. 'Tell you what, exact one. Work out your best price, fair and square, and the Frame rides along for nothing. Get it wrong, and we'll call it market rates.'", question: "Jars cost 9 groats each. For every 4 jars you pay for, a 5th jar is free. Addy needs exactly 23 jars. What is the least Addy can pay?", options: ["162 groats", "171 groats", "180 groats", "189 groats", "207 groats"], answer: 1,
        hint: "Each deal means paying for 4 jars and walking away with 5. How many whole deals fit inside 23 jars, and how many jars are left to buy at full price?",
        solution: "Four whole deals give 4 × 5 = 20 jars for the price of 16. The remaining 3 jars cost full price, so Addy pays for 16 + 3 = 19 jars: 19 × 9 = 171 groats. (Taking a fifth deal gives 25 jars for 20 × 9 = 180 — more jars, but more money, and Addy is not made of groats.)",
        good: "market_good", mid: "market_mid" },
      market_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "brass_frame", text: "The weasel's face performed several expressions in quick succession, settling at last on respect, which fitted it worst.\n\n'Nineteen jars' money for twenty-three jars,' it said, counting Addy's groats twice and finding them exact both times. 'You'd ruin me in a week, you would. Take the Frame. Take it before I like you.'\n\nComponent found: Brass Frame.", next: "converge" },
      market_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", setMid: true, text: "The weasel worked the true sum out on the back of a fish crate, kindly and at considerable length, the way people are kind when it is also profitable. Market rates, alas, are market rates: the jars took every groat Addy had brought, and the Brass Frame stayed on the stall between the boot and the lids.\n\n'Come back richer,' the weasel suggested, wrapping the jars, 'or cleverer. Cleverer's cheaper.'", next: "converge" },
      /* ---- route two: the bridge ---- */
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Bridge of Halves went half way, then half of what was left, then half of that, in planks that ended up smaller than optimism. The locals swore blind you never quite reached the far bank. Addy, who knew a thing or two about series that sneak up on their limits, was privately confident, but privately confident and dry are two different things, and the river below was loud, cold and interested.\n\nThe trick, said a duck who had settled in to watch for entertainment, was rhythm. The planks got smaller but they came quicker. Jump on the beat and the bridge carried you; jump off it and the river did.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 3, text: "Roll 3 or more on a six-sided die to hold the rhythm across the shrinking planks. The duck has agreed to adjudicate.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 3 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 3, 4, 5 and 6 all succeed — that is 4 outcomes out of 6, and 4/6 = 2/3. Good odds, but a third of everything still ends up in the river. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your feet: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "brass_frame", text: "Addy crossed the whole diminishing series without once looking down, each hop landing half a beat after the last, and stepped onto the far bank to scattered applause from the duck, who had been rather hoping for a splash.\n\nWedged between the final two planks — each the size of a sensible thought — was a Brass Frame, presumably dropped by someone less rhythmic. Sturdy, hinged, exactly the right size for holding a lens.\n\nComponent found: Brass Frame.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", setMid: true, text: "The fourth plank was exactly half as forgiving as the third. Addy hit the river, which was cold in a way that felt personal, and was fished out a bumpy quarter-mile downstream by the duck, who charged two biscuits for the service and considered it a bargain on both sides.\n\nWhatever the bridge had been holding between its last two planks, the bridge kept. Some crossings you only get one clean chance at.", next: "converge" },
      /* ---- route three: the fen ---- */
      fen: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Fen of Fewer smelled of arithmetic gone off. Reeds grew in clumps that refused to be counted the same way twice, the mud made a noise like subtraction, and the path was the kind of path that is mostly a rumour with stones in it.\n\nHalfway across, on a hummock of almost-solid ground, something big was going through a stolen mail sack, eating the numbers off the letters. It was a Roughling: fog packed hard into the shape of a bailiff, with fists like weather. And around its neck, on a string, hung a Brass Frame — worn as a trophy by something with no idea what it was for, the way a magpie wears a wedding ring.", next: "fen_fight" },
      fen_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Roughling looked up from its correspondence. It did not seem alarmed. Things the shape of bailiffs seldom do.\n\n'TRESPASSING,' it said, in a voice like a ledger falling downstairs. 'FINE'S EVERYTHING YOU'VE GOT. ROUND FIGURES.'\n\n'We don't do round figures,' said Addy, and took off the pack, which is ant for rolling up sleeves.",
        enemy: { name: "The Bog Roughling", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer went home like a well-aimed invoice, and a fistful of the Roughling blew away across the reeds.",
          strikeText: "A fist like a rain cloud came round in a slow, enormous arc, and one of the party learned what the mud tasted of. (Subtraction, mostly.)",
          defeatText: "The Roughling came apart into ordinary weather, drifting off across the fen to bother somebody else's washing. Where it had stood, the mail sack sat in the mud, full of letters slowly remembering their numbers." },
        victory: "fen_win", retreat: "fen_retreat" },
      fen_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "brass_frame", text: "The Brass Frame dropped from the dissolving trophy-string into the mud with a plop that managed to sound grateful. Addy fished it out, wiped it thoughtfully on the nearest companion, and held it up to what light the fen was prepared to offer: sturdy, hinged, exactly the right size for holding a lens.\n\nThe party posted the mail sack at the next crooked postbox, because some victories are large and some are twenty-nine letters finally going where they were sent.\n\nComponent found: Brass Frame.", next: "converge" },
      fen_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The fen decided the matter, as fens do, by being on the Roughling's side. The party squelched back to firmer ground, bruised, down several biscuits of morale, and took the long way round the marsh with as much dignity as full boots allow.\n\nBehind them the Roughling settled back to its stolen correspondence, trophy swinging, eating the sevens first because sevens are the tastiest. Addy did not look back. Looking back, in Addy's experience, only encourages things.", next: "converge" },
      /* ---------- ACT TWO: the road to Tallyford ---------- */
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again on the far side, as roads do when they feel they've made their point, at a milestone that read TALLYFORD: 5. The five was crisp, unsmudged and confident, and everybody chose to find that encouraging.\n\nAhead, the land folded down toward the Quartz Stream, which lay across the valley bright and straight as a ruled line — the last clean-drawn thing, the tinkers said, between here and whatever was going wrong in Tallyford.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 6, target: 4, text: "The Quartz Stream ran fast and cold, with stepping stones spaced by someone with either very long legs or a sense of humour. There was nothing for it but to jump. Roll 4 or more on a six-sided die to land cleanly.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 4 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/3", "1/2", "2/3"], answer: 2, why: "The rolls 4, 5 and 6 succeed. That is 3 outcomes out of 6, and 3/6 = 1/2. A coin toss with wet consequences. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your nerve: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "quartz_good", fail: "quartz_miss" },
      quartz_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "clear_quartz", text: "Addy landed on the far stone with the tidy precision of a full stop, to a smattering of applause from the party and one pointed silence from the stream, which had been hoping.\n\nAnd there in the shallows, worn clear by a hundred years of exact water, sat a piece of Clear Quartz — so pure you could read small print through it, and so obviously waiting to be a lens that it was practically clearing its throat.\n\nComponent found: Clear Quartz.", next: "tinker_check" },
      quartz_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", setMid: true, text: "Addy jumped short. The stream received the little ant with enthusiasm and carried it a bumpy quarter-mile past several interested fish, one of whom appeared to be taking notes.\n\nBy the time Addy crawled out — soaked, dignified, recounting its own legs from principle — the current had swept every glint of quartz far beyond reach. Some things you only get one clean chance at. The adventure went on, a little more muddled than before.", next: "tinker_check" },
      tinker_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u2699\ufe0f", stat: 2, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found the party at the Tinkers' Camp, where a kettle sang and a tinker sat glaring at a clock that ran widdershins, on the grounds that time should be mended whichever way it was going.\n\n'Cogs are in,' said the tinker, 'but they're in wrong, and I'm blowed if I can see the order of them. Thirty-one teeth here, meshing that, driving this \u2014 it's not a screwdriver job, it's a THINKING job.'\n\nThe tinker looked up hopefully. Tinkers' favours, as everyone knows, are worth having. Somebody with good Logic should work out the gear order.",
        success: "tinker_fixed", fail: "tinker_stumped" },
      tinker_fixed: { type: "story", emoji: "\u2699\ufe0f", bonusStars: 2, text: "It fell into place the way good reasoning does: if the thirty-one-tooth wheel drives the escapement, and the escapement can't reverse, then the widdershins had to be coming in at the crown \u2014 and once you said it out loud the whole clock practically reassembled itself out of embarrassment.\n\nIt struck the hour. The correct hour. Going the correct way.\n\nThe tinker beamed and pressed a small oilcloth parcel of travelling biscuits on the party, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "tinker_choice" },
      tinker_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udd70\ufe0f", text: "The cogs defeated everyone. Each arrangement was wrong in a new and interesting way, until the clock struck fourteen going sideways and the tinker gently took it back.\n\n'Never mind,' said the tinker, kindly, in the way people are kind when they've stopped expecting anything. 'It's kept wrong time for nine years. It can keep it a while longer.'\n\nThe kettle sang. Nobody quite met anyone's eye.", next: "tinker_choice" },
      tinker_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "Dusk found the party at the Tinkers' Camp, where a kettle sang and a tinker sat fixing a clock that ran widdershins, on the grounds that time should be mended whichever way it was going.\n\n'Tallyford's an hour on,' said the tinker, not looking up. 'Push on through the night and carry my post, and I'll owe you a favour — tinkers' favours being worth having. Or sleep here by the kettle and go safe at dawn. Night road's chancy, mind. Chancier than usual, lately. Things on it that round you down.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll — but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the kettle is excellent." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night road twisted like a sum done in a hurry. With the tinker's lantern swinging ahead, you roll with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to keep your footing in the dark.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher — what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fail. Each fails half the time, so both fail 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4 of the time — which means you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Addy brought the party into Tallyford before the moon was properly done rising, posted the tinker's letters into the crooked box by the gate, and pocketed the promised favour: three bright stars, still warm from wherever tinkers keep them, and a hot supper that put a whole heart's worth of courage back into everybody.\n\nTaking a risk is fine, Addy noted in the margin of its thoughts, provided you have counted the odds first and the odds have agreed to be counted.\n\nBonus: +3 ⭐ at the end of the adventure, and one heart restored.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", setMid: true, text: "The night road won. Addy spent a damp hour in a ditch shaped exactly like an ant-sized mistake, the letters got soggy, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of postal condition.\n\nThe party limped the last mile in a silence that had opinions in it. The odds were good, Addy reflected, wringing out one boot at a time, but good odds still lose sometimes. That is what makes them odds, and not promises.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Addy slept under the tinker's cart and woke to a morning so crisp you could have filed it under M. The kettle had opinions about breakfast, all of them correct, and the widdershins clock struck seven going backwards, which everyone agreed still counted.\n\nThe walk to Tallyford took exactly the hour promised, which Addy found deeply reassuring. Sometimes the best move is the boring one. Expected value, as Addy told the party over the last of the tea, is allowed to be sensible.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      ambush_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The Tollhouse of Exactly stood where the road narrowed, as it had for two hundred years, taking its famous toll of one right answer per traveller. But this morning the queue was being run by the wrong management.\n\nTwo Roughling footpads had set up in front of it with a plank across two barrels and a hand-painted sign reading TOLE: EVRYTHING. Behind her bolted shutters the real keeper could be seen glaring like a kettle coming to the boil.\n\n'That's not even a word,' said Addy, which as declarations of war go was at least accurate.",
        enemy: { name: "Roughling Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and one of the footpads got noticeably thinner, in the way of fog that has been told firmly what o'clock it is.",
          strikeText: "A grey fist swept the plank like a bad bargain, and somebody in the party paid a toll they hadn't agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad looked at the space where the first had been, thought about the plank, the barrels and its career prospects, and dissolved on its own initiative. The sign fell over. Nobody picked it up." },
        victory: "ambush_win", retreat: "ambush_retreat" },
      ambush_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The keeper unbolted her shutters, then her door, then her expression, in that order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, sweeping the fallen sign into the ditch with one professional foot. 'First time in two hundred years, so enjoy it. But nobody passes the Tollhouse of Exactly without answering — rules are rules, especially on days when everything else is coming loose.' She produced a slate that had clearly seen off generations of travellers, and slid it across the counter.", next: "tollhouse_puzzle" },
      ambush_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The party fell back down the road with the footpads' laughter rolling after them like weather. There followed an educational half-hour in the nettles of the side path, which the nettles enjoyed a great deal more than anyone else.\n\nThey came at the Tollhouse in the end by the keeper's side gate, scratched and simmering. She let them into the porch with a look that took a full inventory of their condition and declined to comment on it. 'Bad morning,' she said. 'It's going round. But nobody passes without answering. Rules are rules — today of all days.' Out came the slate.", next: "tollhouse_puzzle" },
      tollhouse_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🍎", text: "The slate was old, the chalk was older, and the question was written in the keeper's careful, unforgiving hand. 'Take your time,' she said, in the voice of someone counting it.", question: "A stallholder sells apples in bags of 3 for 7 groats, and pears in bags of 5 for 12 groats. Addy needs exactly 30 pieces of fruit and pays exactly 71 groats. How many bags of apples does Addy buy?", options: ["2", "3", "4", "5", "6"], answer: 3,
        hint: "The fruit must total 30 first. If there are a apple bags and p pear bags, then 3a + 5p = 30. Only a few whole-number pairs work — test each one against the money.",
        solution: "Let a be apple bags and p be pear bags. Fruit: 3a + 5p = 30, so the whole-number options are (a, p) = (10, 0), (5, 3) and (0, 6). Check the cost 7a + 12p for each: 70, then 35 + 36 = 71 ✓, then 72. So Addy buys 5 bags of apples.",
        good: "toll_good", mid: "toll_mid" },
      toll_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the slate twice, sniffed in a way that in Tollhouse dialect meant deep admiration, and stamped Addy's paw with the ancient seal of the house, which read PRECISELY CORRECT in very small letters.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, almost smiling, which startled a passing pigeon. 'And hurry. The Ledger could use someone like you, and from what the pigeons say, it could use them yesterday.'", next: "tallyford" },
      toll_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The keeper shook her head, but she was not unkind about it. She turned the slate around and worked the answer through, step by careful step, and made Addy repeat it back twice, because in her experience twice was where knowing started.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the party through the side gate — the long way round, through the nettles, who could not believe their luck twice in one day.", next: "tallyford" },
      /* ---------- ACT THREE: Tallyford ---------- */
      tallyford: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Tallyford at last: a town built by people who measured twice and cut once, where the streets met at right angles out of respect and even the weathervane pointed accurately. It should have gleamed.\n\nIt did not gleam. A greyness lay over the rooftops, thin as doubt, and it was thickest around the Hall of Sums at the centre of town — pouring out of its windows in slow ribbons, like the building was thinking bad thoughts. Shopkeepers stood in doorways recounting their tills and getting different answers each time. Somewhere a church clock struck eleven-ish.\n\nAddy walked faster.", next: "page_check" },
      page_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udcc4", stat: 4, dc: 12,
        text: "The Hall of Sums' great doors opened on a draught with opinions. It went straight up the stair to the gallery, where the Great Ledger \u2014 already loose in its bindings from whatever had been gnawing its sums \u2014 lifted a fistful of middle pages into the air like a dare.\n\nPage 141 was up there somewhere, turning over and over among its neighbours, all of them planning to come down in whatever order caused the most trouble.\n\nSomebody quick had better catch them before they land wrong.",
        success: "page_quick", fail: "page_slow" },
      page_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "A blur, a leap, a snap of paper \u2014 and the pages came down stacked, squared and in order, 138 through 145 without a number out of place, caught before the draught had finished being pleased with itself.\n\nUp at the lectern the Clerk of Pages made a small approving noise, which by Clerk standards was a ticker-tape parade.", next: "clerk_puzzle" },
      page_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf42", setMid: true, text: "The pages came down the way autumn leaves do: everywhere, and wrong. It took the better part of an hour to re-sort them, checking every number twice, and by the end the light was going and the greyness over the Hall had thickened a shade \u2014 as if the Muddle had been given exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "clerk_puzzle" },
      clerk_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📖", text: "The Great Ledger of Tallyford stood on a lectern the size of a garden shed, in the gallery above the main hall, guarded by the Clerk of Pages — a long grey heron of a person with strong opinions about damp thumbs.\n\n'Prove you know the book,' said the Clerk, in a voice like an index, 'and you may touch it. The trouble is downstairs, but the truth is up here, and the truth is only for those who can count it.'", question: "Numbering the Great Ledger's pages — 1, 2, 3 and so on — used exactly 552 digits in total. How many pages does the Ledger have?", options: ["210", "216", "220", "224", "236"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Count digits in stages: pages 1–9 use one digit each, pages 10–99 use two digits each. Work out what is left over for the three-digit pages.",
        solution: "Pages 1–9 use 9 digits. Pages 10–99 are 90 pages × 2 digits = 180 digits, making 189 digits up to page 99. That leaves 552 − 189 = 363 digits for three-digit pages: 363 ÷ 3 = 121 pages, running from page 100 up to page 220. So the Ledger has 220 pages.",
        good: "ribbon_good", mid: "ribbon_mid" },
      ribbon_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎀", grantComponent: "tally_ribbon", text: "The Clerk of Pages blinked slowly, which for the Clerk was wild applause.\n\nFrom between pages 119 and 120 — 'the exact middle, if you know how to count, which apparently somebody finally does' — the Clerk drew out the Ledger's bookmark: a Tally Ribbon, silk gone soft with age, stitched with tiny fives struck through in the old style. 'It has marked the true middle of this book for nine generations,' said the Clerk, laying it across Addy's paws like a knighthood. 'It will not mind marking something new.'\n\nComponent found: Tally Ribbon.", next: "captain_fight" },
      ribbon_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", setMid: true, text: "The Clerk sighed the sigh of someone who has watched a great many people guess, and chalked the full working on the lectern for Addy to copy down — every stage, in a hand so neat it was practically a lecture.\n\n''Wrong once is how you get right forever,'' the Clerk said, in the tone of someone quoting a Tollhouse keeper, and there was no unkindness in it. But the Ledger's Tally Ribbon stayed exactly where it was, between the pages, keeping the true middle for someone who could find it unaided.", next: "captain_fight" },
      captain_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Down in the main hall, something was leaning over the day-books. The Roughling Captain was bigger than its footpads and greyer than its Smudges, and it was going through the town's accounts a page at a time with a wet grey thumb, smearing every total into 'thereabouts'. Where it had already been, the columns drooped like unwatered plants.\n\nIt looked up at the party on the gallery stairs with eyes like un-inked full stops.\n\n'CLOSED,' it said, 'FOR STOCKTAKING.'\n\n'You're rounding down a whole town,' said Addy, taking off the pack.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Captain", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer landed like an audit. The Captain lost an edge it had been fond of, and the nearest column of figures stood visibly straighter.",
          strikeText: "The Captain's fist came down and the whole hall went approximate. The flagstones were suddenly only mostly underfoot, and somebody found the floor the hard way.",
          defeatText: "The Captain went the way of all fog when the sun means it: edges first, opinions last. Its final word was 'THEREABO—', which pleased Addy, because even its ending didn't finish." },
        victory: "captain_win", retreat: "captain_retreat" },
      captain_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Page by page, the day-books' numbers crept back to their places like children who had been hiding behind the furniture the whole time and were pretending they hadn't. The grey ribbons stopped pouring from the windows. Outside, the church clock cleared its throat and struck eleven — plain eleven, all the way through.\n\nThe Clerk of Pages appeared at the gallery rail, surveyed the reclaimed hall, and inclined its head one entire degree, which witnesses agreed was unprecedented.", next: "forge" },
      captain_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Captain's fist came down again and the hall went so approximate that the doors were the only certain thing left, so the party used them.\n\nThey regrouped in the doorway of a bakery across the square, bruised, furious, and — after a short vote — fortified with buns. Then they took the back stairs to the Ledger gallery while the Captain glowered over the main floor below, smearing on. Not every battle is won by winning it, Addy noted grimly. Some are won by arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Ledger hall glowed the workshop of Smelda, forger of instruments, who could solder a decimal point back into place from twenty paces and had once repaired a broken promise, though she didn't like to talk about it.\n\n'A Counting Lens, is it?' she said, peering at Addy over half-moon spectacles that were themselves rather good lenses. 'Haven't made one of those since the Measurers' time. Brass Frame, Clear Quartz, Tally Ribbon — show me all three, and I'll make you a thing worth squinting through.'",
        forged: "The forge roared like an approving crowd. Smelda set the quartz in the brass, ground it true against a whetstone older than the town, and hung it on the ribbon so it could be worn about the neck of a very exact ant.\n\nThrough the Counting Lens, quantities simply gave up and admitted what they were. Addy looked at a basket of apples through it and the basket confessed to fourteen.\n\nThe Counting Lens is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Smelda counted the components on the bench, then counted them again, which was polite of her because it did not take long.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap in turn. 'A lens wants all three or it's just a wish with a frame round it. Come back when you've the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the maths.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "addy_lens", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the Great Ledger itself.\n\nAddy climbed the lectern, licked the pencil (a professional habit), and found the trouble on page 141: a single sum that had been told, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, that it was 'close enough' — and had been quietly falling apart ever since, the way anything does if you tell it that often enough. The rot had spread from that one entry down every column that touched it, out of the book, and into the town.\n\nAddy crossed out the roughly. Addy wrote in the exactly, in fencepost letters, and underlined it twice.\n\nThe whole book seemed to sit up straighter. Somewhere several pages away, a column of figures began, very quietly, to purr.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Tallyford threw a party with precisely the right amount of cake, which the town agreed was the highest honour it knew how to pay. The mayor gave a speech (eleven 'basically's — Addy counted, the record stood), the Clerk of Pages was seen to almost dance, and the keeper of the Tollhouse waived the toll for a second historic time.\n\nAt the end of it the town presented Addy with the Balanced Ledger: a pocket copy of the great book, every sum in perfect order, small enough to carry and true enough to matter. Addy walked home under a clear sky, counting the stars, and for once — the little ant was nearly sure of it — the stars felt counted back.",
        midText: "Tallyford was grateful, in a slightly muddled way — the party ran out of cake at a rate nobody had quite predicted, and the mayor's speech lost count of itself twice. Still, the town's sums held, the clock struck things it meant, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Clerk of Pages pressed the Balanced Ledger into Addy's paws: a pocket copy of the great book, small enough to carry and true enough to matter. 'You got here,' said the Clerk. 'The route was scenic. Routes often are.' Addy walked home already planning the next, tidier attempt — because a thing worth doing exactly is worth doing again.",
        grantItem: "addy_ledger" },
    },
  },
  cubble: {
    id: "cubble",
    title: "The Last True Corner",
    tagline: "The town of Trueangle is going soft at the edges, and somebody square-headed had better hold the line.",
    items: [
      { id: "cubble_compass", name: "The Cold Compass",   stat: 1, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Geometry. Won with a sharp Geometry check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "cubble_proof",   name: "The Frozen Proof",   stat: 3, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Science. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "cubble_heart",   name: "The Setsquare Heart",stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { true_edge: "The True Edge", clear_ice: "A Sliver of Clear Ice", plumb_line: "A Plumb Line" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      /* ---------- ACT ONE: out of the Cold Corner ---------- */
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🧊", text: "Cubble had six faces, twelve edges and eight corners, and was quietly proud of every one of them. In a world where most things sagged, drooped or rounded off at the slightest provocation, Cubble stayed exactly, gloriously square. It was not vanity. It was, Cubble felt, a public service.\n\nSo when the news came that Trueangle — the squarest town in the valley, built by people who mitred their corners out of principle — had begun to go soft, its right angles slumping into vague curves overnight, Cubble did not need to be asked twice. Cubble did not, in fact, need to be asked at all.\n\n'Fetch me my good ruler,' said Cubble. 'And two who don't mind the cold. Corners aren't going to hold themselves.'", next: "frost_check" },
      frost_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u2744\ufe0f", stat: 1, dc: 10,
        text: "The frost had made a lattice across the mill pond overnight, and the lattice was perfect \u2014 or nearly. Somewhere in that field of ice-diamonds one pane had frozen crooked, a true right angle amongst the leaning ones, and beneath it something gleamed that was not water.\n\nThe trick was to read the angles: every honest diamond met its neighbour at the same tilt, and the one that didn't was the one to trust. Skate to the wrong pane and the pond would swallow it.\n\nSomebody with a good eye for Geometry had better pick the true corner.",
        success: "found_compass", fail: "frost_miss" },
      frost_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf0a", text: "The angles blurred together the longer anyone stared, tilting one into another until the whole pond looked like one enormous shrug. The chosen pane cracked, wet and wrong, and whatever had gleamed beneath it slid off into the dark under the ice.\n\n'It'll settle somewhere true,' said Cubble, climbing out and squaring its shoulders on principle. Which was almost certainly a lie, but a well-angled one.\n\n(The Cold Compass stays lost \u2014 this time. Another run through this adventure may find it.)", next: "post_fight" },
      found_compass: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\udded", grantItem: "cubble_compass", text: "The right pane held Cubble's weight like a handshake, and there it was, frozen a hand's-breadth down: a compass, brass gone silver with cold, its two legs locked at a perfect angle no thaw had ever managed to bend.\n\nSomebody exact had drawn true circles with this once, and lost it to the ice, which is the sort of thing that happens to careful people exactly once. Cubble worked it free. The legs opened with a satisfying, uncompromising click.\n\nThe Cold Compass is yours. (+1 Geometry, kept forever.)", next: "post_fight" },
      post_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It had settled on the parish boundary post like fog with ambitions: a Smudge, small and grey, patiently rubbing the sharp painted arrow into a soft grey smear that pointed, generously, in roughly the direction of everywhere.\n\nCubble set down the ruler. A signpost that means 'thereabouts' is how travellers end up in ditches, and Cubble took ditches personally.\n\n'That was a right angle this morning,' said Cubble. 'Kindly put it back.' The Smudge, in reply, got bigger and vaguer.",
        enemy: { name: "The Corner Smudge", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed flat and square, and a whole edge of the Smudge snapped back into a crisp grey line before crumbling away.",
          strikeText: "The Smudge billowed and the world went briefly curved. For one horrible moment nothing in sight had a corner, and somebody sat down on a bench that turned out to be more of a suggestion.",
          defeatText: "The Smudge lost its last soft edge and simply stopped, the way fog does when the sun means business. The signpost's arrow stood sharp again, pointing at exactly one place, which is all a signpost has ever wanted." },
        victory: "post_win", retreat: "post_retreat" },
      post_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Cubble dusted itself down, all eight corners accounted for. 'Two in a week,' it noted grimly. First the Counting Stone gone vague at home, now a boundary post losing its edges here.\n\nSomething was going round the valley taking the sharpness off things, one true corner at a time \u2014 and Trueangle, squarest of all, was sitting right at the end of the road it was travelling.", next: "crossroads" },
      post_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "A tactical withdrawal, Cubble informed the party, is still a straight line if you walk it deliberately. They walked it deliberately, round by the frozen water-meadow, which cost the better part of an hour and left a grey film on the morning that would not buff out.\n\nBehind them the Smudge settled back onto the post, softening the arrow again with the patience of weather. Cubble did not look back. Round corners, in its experience, only encourage more round corners.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road forked three ways beneath a signpost that had clearly given up trying to point at anything in particular.\n\nThe left arm read THE GLAZIER'S ROW, where they cut ice into true panes and argue about it. The middle arm read THE STEPPING FALLS, a stair of frozen ledges you cross by rhythm or not at all. The right arm read THE SLUMP, of which the sign said only: SOFT GROUND. THINGS THAT SAG. MIND THE CORNERS, WHAT'S LEFT OF THEM.\n\nSomewhere down each road, the glaziers swore, a True Edge \u2014 a length of ice frozen dead straight \u2014 was waiting to be earned. True Edges, like promises, are never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Glazier's Row", next: "row", preview: "Haggle a straight answer out of an ice-cutter. A hard geometry puzzle guards the True Edge." },
        { label: "Cross the Stepping Falls", next: "falls", preview: "Cross a stair of frozen ledges by rhythm. A dice challenge guards the True Edge." },
        { label: "Wade the Slump", next: "slump", preview: "The soft ground where corners go to die. A Slumpling guards the True Edge \u2014 this one is a fight." },
      ] },
      /* ---- route one: the glazier's row ---- */
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Glazier's Row was all noise and glitter, ice-cutters bawling their wares. 'STRAIGHT AS YOU LIKE!' cried one, holding a pane that curved like a smile. 'TRUE TO THE MILLIMETRE!' cried another, whose millimetre was clearly self-taught.\n\nAt the end stall, packed in sawdust between a warped mirror and a jar of assorted angles, lay a True Edge \u2014 a straightedge of ice so honest it hummed. The glazier, a marten with a level in one paw and mischief in the other, watched Cubble notice it and smiled the smile of someone hearing money clear its throat.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The Edge? That's the good stuff,' said the marten. 'Tell you what, square one. I'm cutting a frame for a window: a rectangle exactly three times as long as it is wide, and I need the perimeter to come to sixty-four inches of good ice, no waste. Work out the area of glass I'll cut, and the Edge is yours. Get it wrong and you're paying market rates, which round up.'", question: "A rectangle is 3 times as long as it is wide. Its perimeter is 64 inches. What is its area, in square inches?", options: ["192", "128", "256", "144", "96"], answer: 0,
        hint: "Let the width be w. Then the length is 3w, and the perimeter is 2(w + 3w) = 8w. Solve for w first.",
        solution: "The perimeter is 2(length + width) = 2(3w + w) = 8w. So 8w = 64, giving w = 8. The length is 3 × 8 = 24. Area = length × width = 24 × 8 = 192 square inches.",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "true_edge", text: "The marten checked the working twice on the frosted counter and found it true both times. Its face tried on several expressions and settled, badly, on respect.\n\n'Cut to the millimetre and not a shaving over,' it muttered. 'You'd put honest cutters out of business, you would. Take the Edge. Take it before I start liking you.'\n\nComponent found: The True Edge.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The marten worked the true sum out on the back of a delivery slip, kindly and at length, the way people are kind when it also happens to be profitable. Market rates, alas, are market rates: the frame took every coin Cubble carried, and the True Edge stayed packed in its sawdust.\n\n'Come back squarer,' the marten suggested, wrapping the glass. 'Squarer's cheaper than sorry.'", next: "converge" },
      /* ---- route two: the stepping falls ---- */
      falls: { type: "story", emoji: "🧗", text: "The Stepping Falls froze in ledges, each one a hand lower than the last, and the trick \u2014 said a heron who had settled in to watch, hopefully \u2014 was rhythm. The ledges came steady if you trusted them, and slick as a lie if you hesitated.\n\nCubble, whose corners gave it excellent purchase but whose temperament preferred solid ground, eyed the drop. Hesitate and the pool at the bottom, black and unimpressed, would do the rest.", next: "falls_dice" },
      falls_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 3, text: "Roll 3 or more on a six-sided die to keep the rhythm down the frozen ledges. The heron has agreed to adjudicate, though its heart isn't in it.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 3 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 3, 4, 5 and 6 all clear the ledge \u2014 that is 4 outcomes out of 6, and 4/6 = 2/3. Solid odds, but a third of the time it's the black pool. Reading the odds first steadies your footing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "falls_good", fail: "falls_miss" },
      falls_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "true_edge", text: "Cubble took the ledges like a metronome, corner-first, never once looking down, and stepped onto the bottom flat to a disappointed silence from the heron, who had bet on a splash.\n\nWedged upright between the last two ledges, frozen dead straight, was a True Edge \u2014 a straightedge of ice so honest it hummed. Somebody less rhythmic had dropped it here and never dared the climb back.\n\nComponent found: The True Edge.", next: "converge" },
      falls_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The fourth ledge was exactly as forgiving as the third had pretended not to be. Cubble hit the black pool with a sound like a dropped principle and was fished out a shivering way downstream by the heron, who charged three minnows for the service and thought it a bargain.\n\nWhatever the Falls had held between its last two ledges, the Falls kept. Some crossings give you one clean chance and no more.", next: "converge" },
      /* ---- route three: the slump ---- */
      slump: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Slump was where corners came to give up. The ground gave underfoot like an unfinished thought, fences leaned into curves, and even the milestones had gone soft, reading TRUEANGLE: ROUGHLY THIS FAR in melting numerals.\n\nHalf across, on a hummock of nearly-solid mud, something big was going through the wreck of a fence-line, thumbing each sharp post into a soft grey lump and humming while it worked. It was a Slumpling: the Muddle poured into the shape of a demolition crew. And slung round its neck, worn as a prize by something with no idea what it was for, hung a True Edge.", next: "slump_fight" },
      slump_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Slumpling looked up from its vandalism without alarm. Things shaped like demolition seldom feel any.\n\n'CORNERS,' it said, in a voice like a wall coming down slowly. 'BAD FOR YOU. SHARP. I'LL TAKE 'EM OFF YER. NO CHARGE.'\n\n'We're keeping ours,' said Cubble, and set down the ruler, which is cube for rolling up sleeves it does not have.",
        enemy: { name: "The Slumpling", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck square and true, and a fistful of the Slumpling sheared off in a clean grey slab and thudded into the mud, edges intact for once.",
          strikeText: "A soft enormous fist came round like weather with weight behind it, and one of the party learned exactly how much give a Slump has in it. (More than a corner. Less than hoped.)",
          defeatText: "The Slumpling sagged all the way down, back into the ordinary shapeless mud it had come from, and lay there being a puddle, which suited everyone. The True Edge dropped from the dissolving cord with a grateful little slap." },
        victory: "slump_win", retreat: "slump_retreat" },
      slump_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "true_edge", text: "Cubble fished the True Edge out of the mud, wiped it thoughtfully on the nearest companion, and held it up: dead straight, honest to the last, and humming faintly as if relieved.\n\nThe party set the fence-posts upright as they went, squaring each one by eye, because some victories are grand and some are a dozen posts standing at attention again.\n\nComponent found: The True Edge.", next: "converge" },
      slump_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Slump settled the matter, as soft ground does, by simply being on the Slumpling's side. The party squelched back to firmer footing, down several biscuits of morale, and took the long dry way round with as much dignity as full boots allow.\n\nBehind them the Slumpling went back to its posts, prize swinging, thumbing the sharp off things because sharp things are the most satisfying to ruin. Cubble did not look back. Looking back, at a Slump, only encourages it to follow.", next: "converge" },
      /* ---------- ACT TWO: the road to Trueangle ---------- */
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again on firmer ground, as roads do when they've made their point, at a milestone that read TRUEANGLE: 5. The five was crisp, the corner of the 4 above it sharp as a reproach, and everyone chose to find that encouraging.\n\nAhead the land tilted down toward the Icefall Ledge, one long clean line of frozen white across the valley \u2014 the last straight thing, the glaziers said, between here and whatever was rounding off Trueangle.", next: "ledge_dice" },
      ledge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 6, target: 4, text: "The Icefall Ledge ran narrow and glass-slick, with only a scatter of true footholds someone had chipped long ago. There was nothing for it but to trust the leap. Roll 4 or more on a six-sided die to land square.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 4 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/2", "1/3", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 4, 5 and 6 all land you true. That is 3 outcomes out of 6, and 3/6 = 1/2. A coin-toss with a long cold drop attached. Reading the odds first steadies your nerve: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "ice_good", fail: "ice_miss" },
      ice_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "clear_ice", text: "Cubble landed on the true foothold with the tidy finality of a full stop, to a smatter of applause and one pointed silence from the drop, which had been hoping.\n\nAnd there in the ledge, frozen a century clear by a hundred honest winters, sat a Sliver of Clear Ice \u2014 so pure you could read a proof straight through it, and so obviously waiting to be a lens that it practically cleared its throat.\n\nComponent found: A Sliver of Clear Ice.", next: "level_check" },
      ice_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "Cubble judged the leap a shade short. The ledge received it with enthusiasm and slid it a bruising way down the glassy line before a snowbank agreed to stop it.\n\nBy the time Cubble had squared itself up again, the current of loose snow had swept every glint of clear ice far past reach. Some things give you one clean chance. The run went on, a little more muddled than before.", next: "level_check" },
      level_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udcd0", stat: 3, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found the party at the camp of an old surveyor, who sat glaring at a hillside through a spirit level.\n\n'This whole slope's gone out of true,' she said, 'and I can't for the life of me read WHY the water's running uphill in the north ditch. It's not a shovel job, it's a THINKING job \u2014 pressure, temperature, the way cold water sinks and warm rises. Somebody who knows their Science should work it out.'\n\nThe surveyor looked up hopefully. Surveyors' favours, as everyone knows, are worth having.",
        success: "level_fixed", fail: "level_stumped" },
      level_fixed: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udd2c", bonusStars: 2, text: "It came together the way good reasoning does: the ditch wasn't running uphill at all \u2014 the ground had sagged in the middle, so the far end only LOOKED higher, and the cold densest water was pooling at the true low point exactly as it should. Say it plainly and the whole hill stopped being a mystery and went back to being a hill.\n\nThe surveyor beamed and pressed an oilcloth parcel of hot bricks on the party, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      level_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf21\ufe0f", text: "The slope defeated everyone. Every explanation was wrong in a fresh and interesting way, until the surveyor gently took her level back and folded it away.\n\n'Never mind,' she said, kindly, in the way people are kind when they've stopped expecting much. 'The hill's been confused for years. It can stay confused a while longer.'\n\nThe kettle sang. Nobody quite met anyone's eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The surveyor's fire was warm and the night beyond it was not.\n\n'Trueangle's an hour on,' she said. 'Push through the dark by the frost-road and I'll owe you \u2014 surveyors pay their debts. Or bed down here by the bricks and go safe at dawn. Frost-road's chancy, mind. Chancier than usual, lately. Things out there that take the edge off a body.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll \u2014 but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the hot bricks are excellent." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The frost-road glittered treacherously under the moon. With the surveyor's lantern swinging ahead, you roll with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to keep the true path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Cubble brought the party into Trueangle before the moon had properly cleared the ridge, delivered the surveyor's sealed maps to the crooked box by the gate, and pocketed the promised favour: three bright stars, still warm, and a supper that put a whole heart's worth of courage back into everyone.\n\nA risk is fine, Cubble noted, cornerwise, provided you've measured the odds first and the odds have agreed to be measured.\n\nBonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end of the adventure, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The frost-road won. Cubble spent a bitter hour in a drift shaped exactly like a miscalculation, the maps got damp, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of postal condition.\n\nThe party limped the last mile in a silence with opinions in it. Good odds, Cubble reflected, wringing out one corner at a time, still lose sometimes. That is what makes them odds and not promises.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Cubble slept flat against the surveyor's cart and woke to a morning so crisp you could have ruled a line along it. The hot bricks had opinions about breakfast, all of them warm and correct.\n\nThe walk to Trueangle took exactly the hour promised, which Cubble found deeply reassuring. Sometimes the square move is the slow one. Expected value, Cubble told the party over the last of the tea, is allowed to be sensible.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The Tollgate of Trueangle stood where the road pinched narrow, taking its ancient toll of one true answer per traveller. But this morning the queue was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Slump-footpads had set up a plank across two barrels and a hand-daubed sign reading TOLE: ALL YER CORNERS. Behind her bolted shutters the real keeper glared like a kettle nearing the boil.\n\n'That isn't even spelled square,' said Cubble, which as war-cries go was at least accurate.",
        enemy: { name: "Slump Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck home like a set square, and one footpad lost its bulge and stood, briefly, in embarrassed straight lines before sagging apart.",
          strikeText: "A soft grey fist swept the plank aside like a bad bargain, and somebody in the party paid a toll they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad looked at the space where the first had been, weighed the plank, the barrels and its prospects, and slumped away of its own accord. The sign fell over. Nobody picked it up." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The keeper unbolted her shutters, then her door, then her scowl, in that order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, kicking the fallen sign into the drift. 'First time in two hundred years, so enjoy it. But nobody passes the Tollgate without answering \u2014 rules are rules, and squarest of all on a day when everything else is coming loose.' She slid across a slate that had plainly seen off generations.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The party fell back down the road with the footpads' soft laughter rolling after them. There followed a chilly quarter-hour in a snow-choked side lane that the lane enjoyed considerably more than anyone else.\n\nThey came at the Tollgate at last by the keeper's side wicket, cold and simmering. She let them into the porch, took a full inventory of their condition, and declined to comment on it. 'Bad morning,' she said. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering. Rules are rules, today of all days.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📐", text: "The slate was old, the chalk older, and the question written in the keeper's careful, unforgiving hand. 'Take your time,' she said, in the voice of someone counting it.", question: "The interior angles of a regular polygon add up to 1080\u00b0. How many sides does it have?", options: ["6", "7", "8", "9", "10"], answer: 2,
        hint: "The interior angles of a polygon with n sides add up to (n \u2212 2) \u00d7 180\u00b0. Set that equal to 1080 and solve for n.",
        solution: "For a polygon with n sides, the interior angles sum to (n \u2212 2) \u00d7 180\u00b0. So (n \u2212 2) \u00d7 180 = 1080, giving n \u2212 2 = 6, so n = 8. It is an octagon.",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the slate twice, sniffed in a way that in Tollgate dialect meant deep admiration, and stamped Cubble's face with the ancient seal, which read TRUE TO SPEC in very small letters.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, almost smiling, which startled a passing crow. 'And hurry. The town square could use someone square, and from what the crows say, it could use them yesterday.'", next: "trueangle" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, but not unkindly. She turned the slate round and worked the answer through, stage by careful stage, and made Cubble repeat it back twice, because in her experience twice is where knowing starts.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the party through the frozen side wicket \u2014 the long way round, past the ice-house, who could not believe their luck twice in one morning.", next: "trueangle" },
      /* ---------- ACT THREE: Trueangle ---------- */
      trueangle: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Trueangle at last: a town built by people who measured twice and cut once, where the streets met at right angles out of respect and even the roofs agreed on their pitch. It should have gleamed with edges.\n\nIt did not. A softness lay over the rooftops, thin as doubt, thickest around the Hall of Angles at the centre \u2014 pouring from its windows in slow grey ribbons, sagging the sharp town into curves. Shopkeepers stood in doorways watching their doorframes bow. Somewhere a church spire drooped like a wilting flower.\n\nCubble walked faster, all corners.", next: "sag_check" },
      sag_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 0, dc: 12,
        text: "The Hall of Angles' great doors sagged open on a draught that pushed a whole rack of measuring rods into the air, spinning \u2014 dozens of them, marked in inches, each one you'd need to catch and read and total before they clattered down out of order and the softness took them for good.\n\nThere was no time to be slow AND right. You had to add the marks as they flew, fast and exact, and call the total before the last one landed.\n\nSomebody quick with Arithmetic had better do the counting on the wing.",
        success: "sag_quick", fail: "sag_slow" },
      sag_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "A blur, a snatch, a run of sums done mid-air \u2014 and the rods came down stacked, squared and in perfect order, every inch-mark accounted for before the draught had finished being pleased with itself.\n\nUp at the lectern the Warden of Measures made a small approving noise, which by Warden standards was a ticker-tape parade.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      sag_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf42", text: "The rods came down the way autumn leaves do: everywhere, and wrong. It took the better part of an hour to re-sort them, checking every mark twice, and by the end the light was failing and the softness over the Hall had thickened a shade \u2014 as if the Muddle had been handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📦", text: "The Warden of Measures stood at a lectern the size of a garden shed. 'Prove you know a true solid when you meet one,' she said, in a voice like a ruler laid flat, 'and you may touch the Angle-Stone. The trouble is downstairs. But the truth is up here, and the truth is only for those who can measure it.'", question: "A cube has a total surface area of 150 square centimetres. What is its volume, in cubic centimetres?", options: ["100", "125", "150", "216", "64"], answer: 1,
        hint: "A cube has 6 identical square faces. Find the area of one face first, then the length of one edge.",
        solution: "A cube has 6 faces, so one face has area 150 \u00f7 6 = 25 cm\u00b2. Each edge is therefore \u221a25 = 5 cm. Volume = edge\u00b3 = 5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5 = 125 cubic centimetres.",
        good: "plumb_good", mid: "plumb_mid" },
      plumb_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "plumb_line", text: "The Warden blinked slowly, which for the Warden was wild applause.\n\nFrom a peg behind the lectern \u2014 'the exact vertical, if you know how to hang it, which apparently somebody finally does' \u2014 she lifted down a Plumb Line: a weighted cord that had shown nine generations of builders which way was truly down. 'It has never once lied about the vertical,' she said, laying it in Cubble's hands like a knighthood. 'It won't mind hanging true for something new.'\n\nComponent found: A Plumb Line.", next: "core_fight" },
      plumb_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Warden sighed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people guess, and chalked the full working on the lectern for Cubble to copy \u2014 every stage, in a hand so square it was practically an instruction manual.\n\n''Wrong once is how you get right forever,'' she said, quoting somebody, and there was no unkindness in it. But the Plumb Line stayed on its peg, hanging dead true for someone who could find it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Down in the main hall, something was leaning over the Angle-Stone itself. The Roundhead was bigger than its footpads and softer than its Slumps, and it was running a wet grey thumb along the town's master right angle, easing the sharp ninety degrees into a comfortable, useless curve.\n\nIt looked up at the party on the gallery stair with eyes like two smudged full stops.\n\n'CLOSED,' it said, 'FOR ROUNDING.'\n\n'You're softening a whole town,' said Cubble, and set down the ruler for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roundhead", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer landed like a plumb line finding true down, and the Roundhead lost a curve it had been fond of \u2014 the nearest doorframe visibly squared itself in relief.",
          strikeText: "The Roundhead's soft fist came down and the whole hall went briefly boneless. The flagstones turned to suggestions and somebody found the floor the slow, sinking way.",
          defeatText: "The Roundhead lost its last curve and simply stopped, edges first, softness last. Its final word was 'ROUNDABO\u2014', which pleased Cubble, because even its ending didn't square off." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Corner by corner, the town's angles crept back to true, like children who had been slouching and suddenly remembered posture. The grey ribbons stopped pouring from the windows. Outside, the church spire straightened with an audible, dignified creak.\n\nThe Warden of Measures appeared at the gallery rail, surveyed the re-sharpened hall, and inclined her head one exact degree, which witnesses agreed was unprecedented.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Roundhead's fist came down again and the hall went so boneless that the doors were the only true thing left, so the party used them.\n\nThey regrouped in a baker's doorway across the square, bruised, furious, and \u2014 after a short vote \u2014 fortified with buns. Then they took the back stair to the Angle-Stone gallery while the Roundhead slumped over the main floor below, softening on. Not every battle is won by winning it, Cubble noted grimly. Some are won by arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Hall glowed the forge of Anneal, smith of true instruments, who could weld a straight line back into a bent thing from twenty paces and had once repaired a broken symmetry, though she didn't like to talk about it.\n\n'A Setsquare Heart, is it?' she said, peering over spectacles that were themselves rather good lenses. 'Haven't made one since the Measurers' day. The True Edge, the Clear Ice, a Plumb Line \u2014 show me all three and I'll make you a thing worth trusting your corners to.'",
        forged: "The forge roared like an approving crowd. Anneal set the clear ice against the true edge, hung the whole square on the plumb line so it would always know which way was down, and cooled it hard.\n\nWith the Setsquare Heart, Cubble found it simply could not lean. Every step it took came out at exactly the angle it meant, and quickly too, because certainty is faster than doubt.\n\nThe Setsquare Heart is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Anneal laid the components on the anvil and counted them, then counted them again, which was polite of her because it did not take long.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Heart wants all three or it's just a wish with an edge on it. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the geometry.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "cubble_heart", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the Angle-Stone itself.\n\nCubble climbed the lectern and found the trouble at the master corner: a single right angle that had been told, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, that it was 'near enough' \u2014 and had been quietly softening ever since, the way anything does if you tell it that often enough. The rounding had spread from that one corner out through every angle that touched it, out of the Stone, and into the town.\n\nCubble scraped away the near enough. Cubble set the plumb line, drew it true with the cold compass, and made it exactly ninety degrees, no argument.\n\nThe whole Stone seemed to stand up straighter. Somewhere several streets away, a doorframe clicked, very quietly, back into square.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Trueangle threw a party with precisely square-cut cake, which the town agreed was the highest honour it knew how to pay. The mayor gave a speech at a perfect right angle to the crowd, the Warden of Measures was seen to almost dance, and the keeper of the Tollgate waived the toll for a second historic time.\n\nAt the end the town presented Cubble with the Frozen Proof: a single true theorem set in unmelting ice, small enough to carry and sharp enough to matter. Cubble walked home under a clear sky, checking the corners of the stars, and for once \u2014 the little cube was nearly sure of it \u2014 the stars felt squared away.",
        midText: "Trueangle was grateful, in a slightly rounded sort of way \u2014 the cake was cut at approximately right angles and the mayor's speech drifted off true twice. Still, the town's corners held, the spire stood up straight, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Warden pressed the Frozen Proof into Cubble's hands: a single true theorem set in unmelting ice, small enough to carry and sharp enough to matter. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes often are.' Cubble walked home already planning the next, tidier attempt \u2014 because a thing worth doing squarely is worth doing again.",
        grantItem: "cubble_proof" },
    },
  },
  countra: {
    id: "countra",
    title: "The Sums That Bred",
    tagline: "Reckonwell's spell-numbers have started multiplying in the night, and somebody who counts for a living had better cast them back.",
    items: [
      { id: "countra_wand",   name: "The Tally Wand",     stat: 0, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won with a sharp Arithmetic check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "countra_ledger", name: "The Ledger of Reasons",stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "countra_quill",  name: "The Quick Quill",    stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { spellwax: "A Cake of Spellwax", counting_ink: "A Dram of Counting-Ink", silver_nib: "The Silver Nib" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      /* ---------- ACT ONE: out of Spotshire ---------- */
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🐞", text: "Countra counted its own spots for fun. Seven on the left, seven on the right, and one on the very top that it counted twice, once going up and once coming down, because a spot that important deserved acknowledging both ways. Counting, for Countra, was not arithmetic. It was a kind of quiet spell you could cast on the whole untidy world to make it hold still and behave.\n\nSo when word came from Reckonwell \u2014 the town where every sum is cast aloud like a charm, where the bakers count their loaves into being \u2014 that the numbers had started BREEDING in the night, a single 6 becoming a dozen 6s by dawn, all of them wrong, Countra put on its pointed hat.\n\n'Bring me my good ink,' it said, 'and two who can keep a straight count. Loose numbers are how a town starves in a countinghouse full of bread.'", next: "spot_check" },
      spot_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u2728", stat: 0, dc: 10,
        text: "The lane out of Spotshire ran past a hedge full of loose spell-numbers, shed like leaves, most of them nonsense \u2014 but one small charm among them was still whole and true, a made thing worth keeping if only you could find it.\n\nThe trick was the counting. Each false number added up to something silly if you totted it honestly; the one that came out to a clean, sensible sum was the real one. Cast the wrong count and it would crumble to dust in the palm.\n\nSomebody quick with Arithmetic had better find the true one.",
        success: "found_wand", fail: "spot_miss" },
      spot_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf43", text: "The totals swam. Every number Countra tried added up to a different silliness than the time before, and the charm it finally chose broke apart into a puff of stale arithmetic and blew away down the lane.\n\n'It'll turn up in a sum somewhere,' said Countra, straightening its hat with dignity. Which was true, and no help at all, and Countra knew the difference better than most.\n\n(The Tally Wand stays lost \u2014 this time. Another run through this adventure may find it.)", next: "hedge_fight" },
      found_wand: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "countra_wand", text: "The count came out clean the first time and cleaner the second, and there it was among the dead numbers: a slender Tally Wand, its length notched in tens and units, warm from being made and never once miscounted.\n\nSomebody who reckoned for a living had carried this, and dropped it, and never dared count their way back for it. Countra tucked it behind one wing. The notches lined up with the satisfying certainty of a sum that comes out even.\n\nThe Tally Wand is yours. (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)", next: "hedge_fight" },
      hedge_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It was squatting on the milestone at the parish line: a Miscount, small and grey and smug, patiently turning the carved SIX MILES TO RECKONWELL into 'six, maybe seven, could be five, hard to say' by breathing on it.\n\nCountra set down the inkpot. A milestone that hedges is how travellers arrive nowhere, and Countra took nowhere personally.\n\n'That was a six this morning,' said Countra, in the tone one column of figures uses to correct another. The Miscount did not agree. The Miscount multiplied.",
        enemy: { name: "The Milestone Miscount", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed exact and a whole copy of the Miscount cancelled out with a small grey pop, like a number crossed off a list.",
          strikeText: "The Miscount doubled itself and the whole morning briefly refused to add up. Somewhere in the confusion one of the party sat down hard on a figure that turned out to be zero.",
          defeatText: "The last copy of the Miscount subtracted itself from existence, and the milestone said SIX again \u2014 firmly, and, Countra liked to think, gratefully." },
        victory: "hedge_win", retreat: "hedge_retreat" },
      hedge_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "The party dusted off while the milestone recovered its convictions. 'Two of these in a week,' Countra muttered, making a note. First the market tally at home breeding fours, now a milestone hedging its bets.\n\nSomething was loose in the valley, teaching numbers to lie and to breed \u2014 and Reckonwell, where every number is spoken and so every number matters, sat right at the end of the road it was walking.", next: "crossroads" },
      hedge_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "An orderly retreat, Countra informed the party, still adds up, provided you subtract nothing on the way. They subtracted nothing, going round by the bean field, which cost the best part of an hour and left a grey smudge on the morning that wouldn't total out.\n\nBehind them the Miscount settled back on its milestone, breeding sixes into maybes. Countra did not look back. Looking back, at a Miscount, only gives it more to copy.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road forked three ways under a signpost too busy arguing with its own arithmetic to point anywhere useful.\n\nThe left arm read THE COUNTINGHOUSE ROW, where they lend at interest and dare you to check the sums. The middle arm read THE ABACUS BRIDGE, whose beads you must cross by a lucky slide. The right arm read THE FOG OF FRACTIONS, of which the sign said only: NUMBERS GO MISSING HERE. AND COME BACK SMALLER.\n\nSomewhere down each road, the tinkers swore, a Cake of Spellwax \u2014 the sealing wax that holds a spell-number shut \u2014 was waiting to be earned. Spellwax, like trust, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Countinghouse Row", next: "row", preview: "Out-reckon a moneylender at their own game. A hard arithmetic puzzle guards the Spellwax." },
        { label: "Cross the Abacus Bridge", next: "bridge", preview: "Slide across the great beads of the Abacus Bridge. A dice challenge guards the Spellwax." },
        { label: "Wade the Fog of Fractions", next: "fog", preview: "The mist where numbers shrink. A Halver guards the Spellwax \u2014 this one is a fight." },
      ] },
      /* ---- route one: the countinghouse row ---- */
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Countinghouse Row was all abacuses clacking and coins counted twice for effect. 'FAIR RATES!' cried one lender, whose rates were self-taught. 'HONEST INTEREST!' cried another, holding a ledger that had clearly interested itself.\n\nAt the last window, sealing a stack of promissory charms, sat a Cake of Spellwax, red and ready. The lender, a magpie with an abacus in one claw and larceny in the other, watched Countra notice it and smiled the smile of someone hearing a purse clear its throat.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The wax? That's the good sealing stuff,' said the magpie. 'Here's a fair test, spotted one. My prize ladybird charm has the same number of spots on each of her two wings. If I cast three MORE spots onto each wing, she'd have twenty-six spots in all. Tell me how many she has right NOW, and the wax is yours. Get it wrong and it's countinghouse rates, which double.'", question: "A ladybird has the same number of spots on each of her two wings. With 3 more spots on each wing she would have 26 spots in total. How many spots does she have now?", options: ["14", "16", "18", "20", "22"], answer: 3,
        hint: "Let each wing have x spots now. Three more on each wing means (x + 3) per wing. Two wings give 2(x + 3) = 26.",
        solution: "Let each wing have x spots. With 3 more on each: 2(x + 3) = 26, so x + 3 = 13, giving x = 10. She now has 2 \u00d7 10 = 20 spots.",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "spellwax", text: "The magpie ran the sum on its abacus, then ran it again, and its face tried on several expressions before settling, badly, on respect.\n\n'Twenty, plain as debt,' it said. 'You'd count me out of house and home, you would. Take the wax. Take it before I lend you something.'\n\nComponent found: A Cake of Spellwax.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The magpie worked the true sum out on the back of a coin-wrapper, kindly and at length, the way people are kind when it also earns them a fee. Countinghouse rates, alas, are countinghouse rates: the charm took every coin Countra carried, and the wax stayed sealed on the counter.\n\n'Come back richer,' the magpie suggested, 'or sharper. Sharper's cheaper, in the long run.'", next: "converge" },
      /* ---- route two: the abacus bridge ---- */
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Abacus Bridge was strung across the gorge on a single great wire, threaded with beads the size of barrels, and you crossed it, said a jackdaw who had settled in to watch, by sliding bead to bead on the beat. Slide true and the beads carried you; slide wrong and the gorge, deep and indifferent, took the difference.\n\nCountra, who liked its counting on solid ground, eyed the wire. There was nothing for it but to trust the slide.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 4, text: "Roll 4 or more on a six-sided die to slide clean from bead to bead. The jackdaw has agreed to adjudicate, and to catch nothing.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 4 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/2", "1/3", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 4, 5 and 6 all carry you across. That is 3 outcomes out of 6, and 3/6 = 1/2. A coin-toss over a long drop. Reading the odds first steadies your slide: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "spellwax", text: "Countra slid bead to bead in perfect time, never once looking into the gorge, and stepped off onto the far anchor to a disappointed silence from the jackdaw, who had bet on a scream.\n\nWedged between the last two beads, dropped by someone less rhythmic, was a Cake of Spellwax, red and ready and only slightly gorge-dusted.\n\nComponent found: A Cake of Spellwax.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The fourth bead was exactly as unforgiving as the third had pretended not to be. Countra slid off, the gorge received it, and a patient updraught deposited it, ruffled, on a ledge a good way down and along.\n\nWhatever the bridge had been keeping between its last two beads, the bridge kept. Some crossings give you one clean slide and no more.", next: "converge" },
      /* ---- route three: the fog of fractions ---- */
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Fog of Fractions smelled of arithmetic left in the damp. Numbers drifted through it losing halves and quarters of themselves \u2014 an eight would wander in and stagger out a four, then a two, apologising all the way.\n\nHalf across, on a hummock of nearly-solid ground, something big was going through a sack of stolen sums, halving each one and pocketing the difference with a wet grey chuckle. It was a Halver: the Muddle poured into the shape of a very bad accountant. And slung round its neck, worn as a trophy by something with no idea what it was for, hung a Cake of Spellwax.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Halver looked up from its embezzling without alarm. Things shaped like bad accountants rarely feel any.\n\n'TOLL,' it said, in a voice like a sum going wrong slowly. 'HALF OF WHATEVER YOU'VE GOT. THEN HALF AGAIN. FAIR'S FAIR.'\n\n'That's not fair, that's a geometric series,' said Countra, and set down the inkpot, which is ladybird for rolling up sleeves.",
        enemy: { name: "The Halver", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer went in whole and undivided, and a full half of the Halver simply couldn't be halved any further and dropped off in a grey lump.",
          strikeText: "A soft grey fist swung round and briefly halved everyone's certainty. One of the party sat down in the mud, which tasted of long division done badly.",
          defeatText: "The Halver halved itself once too often, dwindled to a fraction of a fraction, and winked out entirely. The Spellwax fell from the dissolving cord with a plop that sounded, for wax, distinctly relieved." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "spellwax", text: "Countra fished the Spellwax out of the mud, wiped it on the nearest companion, and held it up: red, whole, and un-halved. The party emptied the Halver's sack at the next crooked post, sending twenty-nine shrunken sums off to be re-fattened wherever sums are mended.\n\nSome victories are grand and some are a sackful of numbers finally allowed to be their whole selves.\n\nComponent found: A Cake of Spellwax.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The fog settled the matter, as fog does, by simply being on the Halver's side. The party squelched back to firmer ground, down several biscuits of morale, and took the long way round the mist with as much dignity as full boots allow.\n\nBehind them the Halver went back to its sack, trophy swinging, halving the biggest numbers first because they last longest. Countra did not look back. Looking back, in a fog, only halves your resolve.", next: "converge" },
      /* ---------- ACT TWO: the road to Reckonwell ---------- */
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again on firmer ground, at a milestone that read RECKONWELL: 5. The five stood crisp and single, refusing to breed, and everyone chose to find that encouraging.\n\nAhead the land dropped toward the Ink Stream, one clean dark ruled line across the valley \u2014 the last honest measure, the tinkers said, between here and whatever was teaching numbers to multiply in Reckonwell.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 6, target: 2, text: "The Ink Stream ran shallow but quick, over stepping-stones set close together \u2014 easy footing, so long as you don't fumble the very first jump. Roll 2 or more on a six-sided die to start across cleanly.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 2 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["5/6", "2/3", "1/2", "1/3"], answer: 0, why: "Only a roll of 1 fails; 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 all succeed. That is 5 outcomes out of 6, so the chance is 5/6. Very good odds \u2014 but even one-in-six lands people in the ink. Reading the odds first steadies your nerve: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "ink_good", fail: "ink_miss" },
      ink_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "counting_ink", text: "Countra crossed the close-set stones with the tidy certainty of a sum coming out even, and there, caught in an eddy the ink had polished for a hundred years, sat a Dram of Counting-Ink \u2014 so true that any number written with it could not be persuaded to lie.\n\nComponent found: A Dram of Counting-Ink.", next: "reason_check" },
      ink_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "Countra fumbled the very first stone, which is the one nobody respects. The stream received it and rolled it a bumpy way down, past several interested minnows, before an eddy let it out, dripping and dignified.\n\nBy the time it climbed clear, the current had swept every glint of counting-ink far past reach. Some things give you one clean chance. The run went on, a little more muddled than before.", next: "reason_check" },
      reason_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83e\udde9", stat: 2, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found the party at a tinker's camp, where a kettle sang and a tinker sat scowling at a lockbox with three dials.\n\n'It's a REASONING lock,' she said. 'Three clues, and only one arrangement satisfies all three at once. I've turned these dials till my thumbs ache and I can't hold all three rules in my head together. It's not a hammer job, it's a THINKING job. Somebody with good Logic should crack it.'\n\nThe tinker looked up hopefully. Tinkers' favours, as everyone knows, are worth having.",
        success: "reason_solved", fail: "reason_stumped" },
      reason_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udd13", bonusStars: 2, text: "It fell open the way good reasoning does: two of the clues on their own left several possibilities, but the third ruled out all but one, and once you laid the three side by side the answer was the only thing that could survive them together. The dials clicked home.\n\nThe tinker beamed and pressed a parcel of travelling seedcake on the party, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      reason_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udd12", text: "The lock defeated everyone. Every arrangement satisfied two clues and cheerfully broke the third, until the tinker gently took the box back and set it aside.\n\n'Never mind,' she said, kindly, in the way people are kind when they've stopped expecting much. 'It's kept its secret nine years. It can keep it a while longer.'\n\nThe kettle sang. Nobody quite met anyone's eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The tinker's fire was warm and the road ahead was dark.\n\n'Reckonwell's an hour on,' she said. 'Carry my post through the night and I'll owe you \u2014 tinkers pay their debts. Or bed down by the kettle and go safe at dawn. Night road's chancy, mind. Chancier than usual, lately. Things on it that come apart into wrong numbers.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll \u2014 but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the seedcake is excellent." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night road twisted like a sum done in a hurry. With the tinker's lantern swinging ahead, you roll with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the true path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Countra brought the party into Reckonwell before the moon was properly up, posted the tinker's sealed letters into the crooked box by the gate, and pocketed the promised favour: three bright stars, still warm, and a hot supper that put a whole heart's worth of courage back into everyone.\n\nA risk is fine, Countra noted in the margin of its thoughts, provided you have counted the odds first and the odds have agreed to be counted.\n\nBonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end of the adventure, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The night road won. Countra spent a damp hour in a ditch shaped exactly like a carrying error, the letters got soggy, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of postal condition.\n\nThe party limped the last mile in a silence with opinions in it. The odds were good, Countra reflected, wringing out one wing at a time, but good odds still lose sometimes. That is what makes them odds and not promises.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Countra slept under the tinker's cart and woke to a morning so crisp you could have filed it under M. The kettle had opinions about breakfast, all of them correct.\n\nThe walk to Reckonwell took exactly the promised hour, which Countra found deeply reassuring. Sometimes the sensible spell is the slow one. Expected value, Countra told the party over the last of the tea, is allowed to be boring.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The Tollgate of Reckonwell stood where the road pinched narrow, taking its ancient toll of one right answer per traveller. But this morning the queue was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Miscount footpads had set a plank across two barrels and a hand-daubed sign reading TOLE: ALL YER NUMBERS, EVEN THE LITTLE ONES. Behind her bolted shutters the real keeper glared like a kettle nearing the boil.\n\n'That sign owes me an apostrophe,' said Countra, which as war-cries go was at least literate.",
        enemy: { name: "Miscount Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and one footpad's copies all cancelled at once, leaving it thin and singular before it faded.",
          strikeText: "A grey fist swept the plank like a bad estimate, and somebody in the party paid a toll they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad counted the space where the first had been, got a different total each time, panicked, and subtracted itself. The sign fell over. Nobody picked it up." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The keeper unbolted her shutters, then her door, then her expression, in that order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, sweeping the fallen sign into the ditch. 'First time in two hundred years, so enjoy it. But nobody passes without answering \u2014 rules are rules, especially on a day when everything else is coming loose and multiplying.' She slid across a slate that had seen off generations.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The party fell back down the road with the footpads' laughter rolling after them, doubling as it went. There followed an educational quarter-hour in a side lane the lane enjoyed far more than anyone else.\n\nThey came at the Tollgate at last by the keeper's side wicket, scratched and simmering. She took a full inventory of their condition and declined to comment. 'Bad morning,' she said. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering. Rules are rules, today of all days.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔢", text: "The slate was old, the chalk older, and the question written in the keeper's careful, unforgiving hand. 'Take your time,' she said, in the voice of someone counting it.", question: "What is the sum of all the whole numbers from 1 to 20 inclusive?", options: ["200", "210", "220", "190", "231"], answer: 1,
        hint: "Pair the numbers from the outside in: 1 + 20, 2 + 19, 3 + 18 ... Each pair makes the same total. How many pairs are there?",
        solution: "Pair them: 1 + 20 = 21, 2 + 19 = 21, and so on. There are 10 such pairs, each totalling 21, so the sum is 10 \u00d7 21 = 210. (The general rule is n(n + 1) \u00f7 2 = 20 \u00d7 21 \u00f7 2 = 210.)",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the slate twice, sniffed in a way that in Tollgate dialect meant deep admiration, and stamped Countra's wing with the ancient seal, which read PRECISELY CORRECT in very small letters.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, almost smiling, which startled a passing pigeon. 'And hurry. The Great Counting could use someone who counts, and from what the pigeons say, it could use them yesterday.'", next: "reckonwell" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, but not unkindly. She turned the slate round and worked the answer through, stage by careful stage, and made Countra repeat it back twice, because in her experience twice is where knowing starts.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the party through the side wicket \u2014 the long way round, past the granary, who could not believe their luck twice in one morning.", next: "reckonwell" },
      /* ---------- ACT THREE: Reckonwell ---------- */
      reckonwell: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Reckonwell at last: a town where every loaf, brick and coin is counted aloud into being, where children learn their numbers before their names. It should have hummed with tidy sums.\n\nIt did not. Numbers were breeding in the gutters \u2014 a 3 becoming a crowd of 3s, a 7 splitting into wrong 7s \u2014 and the grey ribbons of Muddle poured thickest from the Great Counting House at the town's heart, where the master ledger is spoken every dawn. Shopkeepers stood in doorways watching their tills disagree with themselves.\n\nCountra walked faster, hat first.", next: "reckon_check" },
      reckon_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 3, dc: 12,
        text: "The Counting House doors banged open on a gust that carried out a cloud of loose spell-numbers, spitting and fizzing \u2014 charms half-cast, unstable, each one about to either fix itself or go off. To calm them you had to know WHY they fizzed: which were too hot with excess energy, which too cold and needing a spark, and settle each the right way before the whole cloud went up.\n\nIt was not a counting job. It was a Science job, and a fast one.\n\nSomebody quick who understood how things settle and react had better calm the cloud.",
        success: "reckon_quick", fail: "reckon_slow" },
      reckon_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "A blur of the right cooling here, the right spark there \u2014 and the cloud of numbers settled all at once, fizzing done, each charm sitting quiet and stable in the air like a swarm remembering it was tame.\n\nUp at the lectern the Reckoner-in-Chief made a small approving noise, which by Reckoner standards was a ticker-tape parade.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      reckon_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf42", text: "The numbers fizzed faster than anyone could read them, going off one by one in little puffs of wrong arithmetic. It took the better part of an hour to settle the survivors, and by the end the light was failing and the Muddle over the House had thickened a shade \u2014 as if it had been handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📖", text: "The Reckoner-in-Chief stood at a lectern the size of a garden shed. 'Prove you can find a hidden number,' she said, in a voice like an index, 'and you may speak to the master ledger. The trouble is downstairs. But the truth is up here, and the truth is only for those who can reckon it.'", question: "A whole number leaves remainder 2 when divided by 3, remainder 3 when divided by 4, and remainder 4 when divided by 5. What is the smallest such number greater than 1?", options: ["57", "58", "59", "60", "119"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Notice that in every case the remainder is exactly one LESS than what you divided by. What does that tell you about the number plus 1?",
        solution: "Each remainder is one less than the divisor, so the number is 1 short of a multiple of 3, 4 and 5 all at once. That means (number + 1) is a multiple of 3, 4 and 5 together \u2014 a multiple of 60. The smallest such number bigger than 1 is 60 \u2212 1 = 59.",
        good: "nib_good", mid: "nib_mid" },
      nib_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "silver_nib", text: "The Reckoner blinked slowly, which for the Reckoner was wild applause.\n\nFrom the inkwell of the master ledger \u2014 'the exact place a true number is born, if you know how to look, which apparently somebody finally does' \u2014 she drew out the Silver Nib: the very nib every honest sum in Reckonwell had first been written with. 'It has never once written a lie,' she said, laying it in Countra's grasp like a knighthood. 'It won't mind writing something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Silver Nib.", next: "core_fight" },
      nib_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Reckoner sighed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people guess, and chalked the full working on the lectern for Countra to copy \u2014 every stage, in a hand so neat it was practically a lecture.\n\n''Wrong once is how you get right forever,'' she said, quoting somebody, and there was no unkindness in it. But the Silver Nib stayed in its well, waiting for someone who could find it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Down in the main hall, something was leaning over the master ledger. The Multiplier was bigger than its footpads and greyer than its Miscounts, and it was running a wet grey thumb down the columns, teaching each honest total to breed \u2014 one becoming two, two becoming ten, all of them wrong.\n\nIt looked up at the party on the gallery stair with eyes like two smudged zeroes.\n\n'CLOSED,' it said, 'FOR EXPANSION.'\n\n'You're breeding a whole town into nonsense,' said Countra, and set down the inkpot for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Multiplier", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer landed exact and undividable, and a whole brood of the Multiplier's copies cancelled at once \u2014 the nearest column of figures shivered and stood true.",
          strikeText: "The Multiplier's fist came down and the whole hall began to breed: two of every flagstone, ten of every shadow. Somebody found the floor among a crowd of wrong floors.",
          defeatText: "The Multiplier tried to copy itself out of danger, divided once too eagerly, and dwindled to nothing. Its final word was 'AGAIN, AGAI\u2014', which pleased Countra, because even its ending didn't repeat." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Column by column, the town's numbers stopped breeding and settled back to single honest totals, like children who had been showing off and suddenly remembered manners. The grey ribbons stopped pouring from the windows. Outside, a till cleared its throat and rang up exactly the right change.\n\nThe Reckoner-in-Chief appeared at the gallery rail, surveyed the reclaimed hall, and inclined her head one exact degree, which witnesses agreed was unprecedented.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Multiplier's fist came down again and the hall bred so many copies of itself that the doors were the only single thing left, so the party used them.\n\nThey regrouped in a baker's doorway across the square, bruised, furious, and \u2014 after a short vote \u2014 fortified with buns (one each; the baker counted). Then they took the back stair to the ledger gallery while the Multiplier bred over the main floor below. Not every battle is won by winning it, Countra noted grimly. Some are won by arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Counting House glowed the workshop of Scriven, maker of true instruments, who could re-point a blunt argument from twenty paces and had once repaired a broken promise, though she didn't like to talk about it.\n\n'A Quick Quill, is it?' she said, peering over spectacles that were themselves rather good lenses. 'Haven't made one since the Reckoners' day. Spellwax, Counting-Ink, a Silver Nib \u2014 show me all three and I'll make you a pen worth trusting your sums to.'",
        forged: "The forge roared like an approving crowd. Scriven set the Silver Nib into a shaft, sealed it with the Spellwax, charged it with the Counting-Ink, and cooled it fast.\n\nWith the Quick Quill, Countra found its sums arrived the instant it thought them, written true before the doubt could catch up. Speed, it turned out, is only certainty in a hurry.\n\nThe Quick Quill is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Scriven laid the components on the bench and counted them, then counted them again, which was polite of her because it did not take long.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Quill wants all three or it's just a wish with a point on it. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the arithmetic.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "countra_quill", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the master ledger itself.\n\nCountra climbed the lectern and found the trouble on the dawn line: a single total that had been counted, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, as 'about a dozen' \u2014 and had been quietly breeding copies of its own uncertainty ever since, the way anything does if you tell it that often enough. The wrongness had spread from that one total down every column that touched it, out of the book, and into the town.\n\nCountra crossed out the about. With the Quick Quill and the Silver Nib it wrote in the exact number, once and only once, and sealed it under Spellwax so it could never breed again.\n\nThe whole book seemed to sit up straighter. Somewhere several counters away, a column of figures went, very quietly, still.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Reckonwell threw a party with exactly enough cake for everyone and not one slice over, which the town counted twice and agreed was the highest honour it knew how to pay. The mayor gave a speech of precisely a hundred words, the Reckoner-in-Chief was seen to almost dance, and the keeper of the Tollgate waived the toll for a second historic time.\n\nAt the end the town presented Countra with the Ledger of Reasons: a small book in which every sum showed its working, so no number in it could ever tell a lie about how it got there. Countra walked home under a clear sky, counting the stars \u2014 once each, honestly \u2014 and for once the count came out even.",
        midText: "Reckonwell was grateful, in a slightly muddled way \u2014 the cake ran out at a rate nobody had quite predicted and the mayor's speech lost count of itself twice. Still, the town's numbers held single and honest, the tills rang true, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Reckoner pressed the Ledger of Reasons into Countra's grasp: a small book in which every sum shows its working. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes often are.' Countra walked home already planning the next, tidier attempt \u2014 because a thing worth reckoning is worth reckoning again.",
        grantItem: "countra_ledger" },
    },
  },
  zoomby: {
    id: "zoomby",
    title: "The Day the Hive Ran Slow",
    tagline: "Something is dragging Hivehallow to a crawl and rounding off its honeycomb, and only the fastest scout in the meadow can outrun it.",
    items: [
      { id: "zoomby_anther", name: "The Quicksilver Anther", stat: 4, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Speed. Won with a sharp Speed check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "zoomby_count",  name: "The Nectar Count",       stat: 0, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "zoomby_lens",   name: "The Honeycomb Lens",     stat: 1, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Geometry. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { beeswax: "A Cake of Beeswax", sun_amber: "A Drop of Sun-Amber", hex_mould: "The Hexagon Mould" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      /* ---------- ACT ONE: out of the Meadow ---------- */
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🐝", text: "Zoomby did everything at speed, including thinking, which it managed to do slightly before things happened so as not to waste the wait. Other bees dawdled at flowers, savouring. Zoomby had visited four and scouted a fifth before they'd settled. Fast, Zoomby held, was not the opposite of careful. Fast, done right, was careful arriving early.\n\nSo when the alarm reached the meadow \u2014 that Hivehallow, the great hive-city where everything runs to the second and every cell is a perfect six-sided marvel, had begun to RUN SLOW, its clocks dragging, its neat hexagons sagging into lazy circles overnight \u2014 Zoomby was airborne before the messenger finished.\n\n'Two of you, keep up if you can,' it called back. 'A hive that runs slow is a hive that starves by teatime. Scout ahead. Always scout ahead.'", next: "dash_check" },
      dash_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u26a1", stat: 4, dc: 10,
        text: "A gust had scattered a courier-bee's satchel across the fast air over the clover, and its contents were falling \u2014 papers, seeds, and one small bright thing that caught the light. Most of it was rubbish you could let drop. But that one bright thing was worth a dive, if you were quick enough to reach it before it hit the long grass and was lost.\n\nThere was no time to think twice. Fold the wings, pick the line, and GO.\n\nSomebody with real Speed had better make the dive.",
        success: "found_anther", fail: "dash_miss" },
      dash_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf3e", text: "Zoomby judged the dive a heartbeat late, which at that speed is a whole field. The bright thing vanished into the deep clover with a small final glint, somewhere in an acre of green that would not give it up.\n\n'It'll surface,' said Zoomby, pulling up in a tight, annoyed loop. Bright things did surface, eventually, which was true and no comfort, and Zoomby hated that it knew the difference.\n\n(The Quicksilver Anther stays lost \u2014 this time. Another run through this adventure may find it.)", next: "hedge_fight" },
      found_anther: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "zoomby_anther", text: "Zoomby caught it a hand's-breadth off the grass, pulled up hard, and opened its palm mid-climb: the Quicksilver Anther, a flower-stamen tipped in something that shivered like a held breath, so light it seemed to want to be already somewhere else.\n\nSome long-ago racer had carried this and dropped it in a gust and never been quick enough to catch it back. Zoomby tucked it under a wing. Everything felt suddenly a half-beat sooner.\n\nThe Quicksilver Anther is yours. (+1 Speed, kept forever.)", next: "hedge_fight" },
      hedge_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It was sitting on the meadow's mile-marker like tiredness given a shape: a Dawdle, small and grey and yawning, patiently smearing the crisp carved SIX MINUTES TO HIVEHALLOW into 'sixish, take your time, no rush'.\n\nZoomby braked hard, which it disliked on principle. A milestone that says no rush is how a hive misses the bloom, and Zoomby took missing the bloom personally.\n\n'That said SIX this morning,' said Zoomby, already circling for the angle. The Dawdle stretched, unbothered, and got slower.",
        enemy: { name: "The Meadow Dawdle", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer struck fast and sharp, and a whole slice of the Dawdle woke up, snapped into a crisp grey edge, and dissolved.",
          strikeText: "The Dawdle yawned and the whole morning went syrupy. For a horrible moment everything moved through treacle, and somebody's fast dodge arrived embarrassingly late.",
          defeatText: "The Dawdle slowed all the way down to a stop, which is what Dawdles are always secretly aiming at, and unravelled. The milestone said SIX MINUTES again, crisply, as if it had somewhere to be." },
        victory: "hedge_win", retreat: "hedge_retreat" },
      hedge_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Zoomby was already three lengths on before the Dawdle finished dissolving. 'Two of these this week,' it noted, banking. First the meadow sundial gone vague at home, now a milestone telling folk to take their time.\n\nSomething was loose in the valley, teaching quick things to drag and sharp things to sag \u2014 and Hivehallow, fastest and most six-sided of all, sat right at the end of the road it was crawling down.", next: "crossroads" },
      hedge_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "A fast retreat, Zoomby informed the party, is still progress if you pick the line well. It picked the line well, round by the fast air over the brook, which cost a maddening minute and left a grey drag on the morning that wouldn't shake off.\n\nBehind them the Dawdle settled back on its marker, smearing SIX into sometime. Zoomby did not look back. Looking back, at a Dawdle, only slows you to its speed.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road forked three ways beneath a signpost too sluggish to bother pointing.\n\nThe left arm read THE POLLEN MARKET, where they trade by weight and dare you to check the scales. The middle arm read THE UPDRAUGHT GAP, a gorge you cross on a lucky thermal. The right arm read THE STILL AIR, of which the sign said only: NOTHING MOVES HERE. NOT EVEN YOU. MIND THE DRAG.\n\nSomewhere down each road, the foragers swore, a Cake of Beeswax \u2014 pure comb-wax, the kind that holds a shape true \u2014 was waiting to be earned. Good beeswax, like a fast friend, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Pollen Market", next: "row", preview: "Out-reckon a pollen-trader at the scales. A hard rate puzzle guards the Beeswax." },
        { label: "Ride the Updraught Gap", next: "bridge", preview: "Catch a thermal across the gorge. A dice challenge guards the Beeswax." },
        { label: "Push through the Still Air", next: "fog", preview: "The dead air where speed goes to die. A Draggle guards the Beeswax \u2014 this one is a fight." },
      ] },
      /* ---- route one: the pollen market ---- */
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Pollen Market was all buzzing and the clatter of tiny scales. 'FAIR WEIGHT!' cried one trader, whose thumb rode the pan. 'HONEST MEASURE!' cried another, whose measure had clearly measured itself generously.\n\nAt the end stall, cooling on a slate between a jar of dubious honey and a very optimistic price-list, sat a Cake of Beeswax, pale gold and perfect. The trader, a wasp with a scoop in one hand and larceny in the other, watched Zoomby notice it and smiled the smile of someone hearing a purse buzz.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The wax? Best comb in the market,' said the wasp. 'Tell you what, quick one. My prize forager works like clockwork: she visits three flowers every five seconds, steady as you like, no breaks. Tell me how many flowers she works in two full minutes and the wax is yours. Get it wrong and it's market weight, which always rounds my way.'", question: "A bee visits 3 flowers every 5 seconds, at a steady rate. How many flowers does she visit in 2 minutes?", options: ["60", "72", "80", "48", "90"], answer: 1,
        hint: "First turn 2 minutes into seconds. Then work out how many 5-second chunks fit into that time, and multiply by 3 flowers per chunk.",
        solution: "Two minutes is 2 \u00d7 60 = 120 seconds. In 120 seconds there are 120 \u00f7 5 = 24 chunks of five seconds. Each chunk is 3 flowers, so 24 \u00d7 3 = 72 flowers.",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "beeswax", text: "The wasp checked the sum on its scales, then on its fingers, then gave up and admitted the number matched the market clock exactly.\n\n'Seventy-two, on the nose,' it buzzed, respect leaking through despite itself. 'You'd count me out of a living, you would. Take the wax. Take it before I try to sell you the slate too.'\n\nComponent found: A Cake of Beeswax.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The wasp worked the true rate out on the back of the price-list, kindly and slowly, the way people are kind when the delay is also billable. Market weight, alas, is market weight: the wax cost every grain of pollen Zoomby carried, and stayed cooling on its slate.\n\n'Come back heavier,' the wasp suggested, 'or faster in the head. Faster's cheaper, oddly enough.'", next: "converge" },
      /* ---- route two: the updraught gap ---- */
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Updraught Gap was a gorge with no bridge and no need of one, if you were a flier: a thermal rose from the sunny rock face, and you crossed by catching it just right, said a swift who had settled in to watch, hopefully. Catch the updraught and it lofted you clean over; miss the beat and the gorge, deep and windless, took the drop.\n\nZoomby, who trusted its wings but not the wind's timekeeping, eyed the shimmer of rising air. There was nothing for it but to commit to the launch.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 5, text: "The thermal came in strong pulses with dead patches between. Roll 5 or more on a six-sided die to launch on a strong pulse and clear the gap. The swift has agreed to adjudicate, and to look away at the crucial moment.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "1/3", "2/3", "1/6"], answer: 1, why: "Only 5 and 6 catch a strong pulse \u2014 that is 2 outcomes out of 6, and 2/6 = 1/3. Long odds over a long drop, so you'll want every edge. Reading the odds first lets you time the launch: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "beeswax", text: "Zoomby hit the pulse dead on the beat and was flung clear over the gorge like a thrown seed, tumbling to a showy landing on the far rim to a disappointed silence from the swift, who had bet on a plummet.\n\nCaught in a crevice on the sunny side, dropped by some flier with worse timing, sat a Cake of Beeswax, pale gold and warm from the rock.\n\nComponent found: A Cake of Beeswax.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "Zoomby launched into a dead patch between pulses, hung in the still air with the sudden clear knowledge that this had been a mistake, and dropped. A late, weak thermal caught it a good way down and coughed it out, ruffled, onto a ledge far below the crossing.\n\nWhatever the crevice had held on the sunny rim, the rim kept. Some launches give you one clean pulse and no more.", next: "converge" },
      /* ---- route three: the still air ---- */
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Still Air hung over the valley like a held yawn. Nothing moved in it \u2014 not the grass, not the light, not, worst of all, Zoomby, who found its fastest wingbeat dragged down to a heavy, dreamlike crawl. Speed simply didn't work here.\n\nHalf across the dead zone, on a patch of ground that resented being walked on, something big was hunched over a stolen honeycomb, thumbing each crisp six-sided cell into a soft useless blob and chuckling a low, syrupy chuckle. It was a Draggle: the Muddle poured into the shape of pure delay. And slung round its neck, worn as a prize by something with no idea what it was for, hung a Cake of Beeswax.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Draggle looked up from its vandalism without hurry. Things shaped like delay never feel any.\n\n'SLOW DOWN,' it said, in a voice like honey pouring in winter. 'NO HURRY. STAY. STAY A WHILE. STAY FOREVER, EH?'\n\n'We're on a clock,' said Zoomby, and set its stance, which is bee for rolling up sleeves it hasn't got, in the still air, where even that took effort.",
        enemy: { name: "The Draggle", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck through the treacle fast and clean, and a slab of the Draggle finally MOVED, tore loose, and fell away twitching.",
          strikeText: "A slow enormous arm came round with the awful patience of a closing door, and one of the party found that dodging, in still air, is mostly a good intention.",
          defeatText: "The Draggle slowed itself past the point of moving at all, which is a Draggle's one true wish and its undoing, and set solid, then crumbled. The Beeswax dropped from the sagging cord with a small quick thud, glad to be going somewhere." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "beeswax", text: "The instant the Draggle fell, the air let go, and Zoomby snapped back to full speed with a grateful whir. It scooped up the Beeswax, wiped it on the nearest companion, and held it up: pale gold, firm, and holding its edges like it meant them.\n\nThe party re-shaped the stolen comb's blobbed cells back into honest hexagons as they left, one crisp six-sided cell at a time. Some victories are grand and some are a honeycomb allowed to be a honeycomb again.\n\nComponent found: A Cake of Beeswax.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The still air settled the matter, as still air does, by being entirely on the Draggle's side. The party slogged back to living wind, down several beats of morale, and took the long fast way round with as much dignity as a crawl allows.\n\nBehind them the Draggle went back to its comb, prize swinging slowly, blobbing the sharpest cells first because they take the longest to ruin. Zoomby did not look back. Looking back, in still air, only drags you further under.", next: "converge" },
      /* ---------- ACT TWO: the road to Hivehallow ---------- */
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again where the wind picked up, at a milestone that read HIVEHALLOW: 5. The five was crisp and quick-cornered, refusing to sag, and everyone chose to find that encouraging.\n\nAhead the land tilted down toward the Quickwater, one clean fast line of stream across the valley \u2014 the last thing still moving at a proper clip, the foragers said, between here and whatever was dragging Hivehallow to a halt.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 6, target: 3, text: "The Quickwater ran fast over a scatter of stepping-stones, most of them steady, a couple of them sly. Roll 3 or more on a six-sided die to cross at speed without a slip. The current is watching with interest.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 3 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 3, 4, 5 and 6 all keep your footing \u2014 that is 4 outcomes out of 6, and 4/6 = 2/3. Good odds, though a third of the time the fast water wins. Reading the odds first steadies your crossing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "amber_good", fail: "amber_miss" },
      amber_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "sun_amber", text: "Zoomby skipped the fast stones in a blur, quick as a dropped stitch, and landed dry on the far bank. There, snagged where the current had polished it for a hundred summers, glowed a Drop of Sun-Amber \u2014 a bead of hardened light so clear it bent the whole meadow small and sharp when you squinted through it.\n\nComponent found: A Drop of Sun-Amber.", next: "shape_check" },
      amber_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "Zoomby trod on one of the sly stones, which rolled with malicious glee. The Quickwater took it, whisked it a bracing way downstream past several amused fish, and let it out, dripping, on a shingle bank well below the crossing.\n\nBy the time it wrung out its wings, the current had carried every glint of amber far past reach. Some crossings give you one clean chance. The run went on, a little more muddled than before.", next: "shape_check" },
      shape_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udcd0", stat: 1, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found the party at the camp of an old comb-wright, who sat glaring at a half-built honeycomb frame.\n\n'The cells won't tessellate,' she grumbled. 'I've a frame to fill and I can't for the life of me work out the angle the cells must meet at so they fit with no gaps and no overlaps. It's not a wax job, it's a SHAPE job. Somebody who knows their Geometry should reckon it.'\n\nThe comb-wright looked up hopefully. A comb-wright's favour, as everyone knows, is worth having.",
        success: "shape_solved", fail: "shape_stumped" },
      shape_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udcd0", bonusStars: 2, text: "It came together the way good geometry does: three hexagons meet at every point, and since the angles round a point must total three hundred and sixty, each cell's corner had to be a hundred and twenty degrees \u2014 which is exactly a regular hexagon's angle, which is exactly why bees chose it. Say it plainly and the frame filled itself, gapless and true.\n\nThe comb-wright beamed and pressed a parcel of travelling honeycake on the party, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      shape_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udcd0", text: "The angles defeated everyone. Every guess left a gap or forced an overlap, until the comb-wright gently took the frame back and set it aside.\n\n'Never mind,' she said, kindly, in the way people are kind when they've stopped expecting much. 'Bees managed it without the sums for a million years. The frame can wait.'\n\nThe kettle sang. Nobody quite met anyone's eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The comb-wright's fire was warm and the sky ahead was going dark fast.\n\n'Hivehallow's an hour on,' she said. 'Fly my urgent post through the night and I'll owe you \u2014 comb-wrights pay their debts. Or bed down by the kettle and go safe at dawn. Night air's chancy, mind. Chancier than usual, lately. Things out there that slow a flier right down and don't let go.'", options: [
        { label: "Fly on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll \u2014 but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the honeycake is excellent." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night air was thick and treacherous, full of slow patches. With the comb-wright's glow-lantern lighting the fast lanes, you fly with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the quick path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Zoomby threaded the fast lanes and brought the party over Hivehallow's wall before the moon had properly cleared the ridge, dropped the comb-wright's sealed post into the crooked box by the gate, and pocketed the promised favour: three bright stars, still warm, and a hot supper that put a whole heart's worth of courage back into everyone.\n\nA risk is fine, Zoomby noted, banking for the gate, provided you've clocked the odds first and the odds have agreed to be clocked.\n\nBonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end of the adventure, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The night air won. Zoomby hit a slow patch it hadn't scouted, wallowed a wretched hour going nowhere, the post got dew-soaked, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of postal condition.\n\nThe party limped the last mile in a silence with opinions in it. Good odds, Zoomby reflected, shaking out its wings, still lose sometimes. That is what makes them odds and not promises.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Zoomby slept clamped to the comb-wright's cart, wings twitching through dreams of racing, and woke to a morning so crisp the air practically hummed. The kettle had opinions about breakfast, all of them correct.\n\nThe flight to Hivehallow took exactly the hour promised, which Zoomby found deeply reassuring, and only slightly disappointing. Sometimes the fast move is the patient one. Expected value, Zoomby told the party over the last of the tea, is allowed to sleep in.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The Gate of Hivehallow stood where the flightpath pinched narrow, taking its ancient toll of one right answer per traveller. But this morning the queue was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Draggle footpads had strung a sticky web across the gap and hung a hand-daubed sign reading TOLE: ALL YER SPEED. WHERE'S THE FIRE? Behind her bolted shutters the real gatekeeper glared like a kettle nearing the boil.\n\n'There's always a fire,' said Zoomby, 'that's rather the point,' which as war-cries go was at least a philosophy.",
        enemy: { name: "Draggle Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck fast through the sticky air, and one footpad lost its grip on the slowness and sped up helplessly into nothing.",
          strikeText: "A gluey grey arm swept the flightpath like a closing shutter, and somebody in the party paid a toll of speed they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad watched the space where the first had been, tried to hurry away, discovered it had forgotten how, and simply stopped. The web sagged. Nobody untangled it." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The gatekeeper unbolted her shutters, then her door, then her frown, in that order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, tearing down the sticky sign. 'First time in two hundred years, so enjoy it. But nobody passes the Gate without answering \u2014 rules are rules, and quickest of all on a day when the whole hive's gone slow.' She slid across a slate that had plainly outlasted generations.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The party peeled back from the web with the footpads' slow laughter oozing after them. There followed a gummy quarter-hour in a side-lane that the lane, being in no hurry, enjoyed enormously.\n\nThey came at the Gate at last by the keeper's side wicket, sticky and simmering. She took a full inventory of their condition and declined to comment. 'Bad morning,' she said. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering. Rules are rules, today of all days.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⬡", text: "The slate was old, the chalk older, and the pattern chalked on it in the keeper's careful hand: a growing honeycomb, ring by ring. 'The comb grows so,' she said. 'Give me the sixth number, and mind you don't rush it wrong.'", question: "A honeycomb grows outward in rings, and the total number of cells goes: 1, 7, 19, 37, 61, ... What is the next (sixth) number in the pattern?", options: ["85", "91", "97", "127", "73"], answer: 1,
        hint: "Look at the gaps between the numbers: 7 \u2212 1 = 6, 19 \u2212 7 = 12, 37 \u2212 19 = 18 ... The gaps themselves grow by a steady amount each time.",
        solution: "The differences are 6, 12, 18, 24, and each grows by 6, so the next difference is 30. The fifth number is 61, so the sixth is 61 + 30 = 91. (Each new ring adds one more group of six cells than the last.)",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the slate twice, sniffed in a way that in Gatekeeper dialect meant deep admiration, and stamped Zoomby's wing with the ancient seal, which read UP TO SPEED in very small letters.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, almost smiling, which startled a passing dragonfly. 'And hurry \u2014 you're good at that. The Great Clock could use someone quick, and from what the dragonflies say, it could use them yesterday.'", next: "hivehallow" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, but not unkindly. She turned the slate round and walked the pattern through, ring by ring, and made Zoomby say the rule back twice, because in her experience twice is where knowing starts.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the party through the side wicket \u2014 the long way round, past the drone-barracks, who could not believe their luck twice in one morning.", next: "hivehallow" },
      /* ---------- ACT THREE: Hivehallow ---------- */
      hivehallow: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Hivehallow at last: a city of a thousand perfect hexagons stacked gold to the sky, where everything runs to the second and the very streets are cells. It should have blurred with speed.\n\nIt did not. A slowness lay over the combs like syrup, thickest around the Clock Tower at the centre, where the Great Clock keeps the whole hive to time \u2014 grey ribbons of Muddle pouring from its face, dragging the city to a crawl, rounding the sharp cells into soft useless circles. Foragers hung stalled in mid-air. A honey-cart had been crossing the same square, the locals said, since Tuesday.\n\nZoomby flew faster, out of pure defiance.", next: "clock_check" },
      clock_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 2, dc: 12,
        text: "The Clock Tower's door hung open on a jammed mechanism, and the great gears had seized in a tangle \u2014 a dozen cogs locked wrong, and only one exact sequence of which to turn first, second, third would free the escapement without stripping the teeth. Force them in the wrong order and the whole Clock would grind itself to ruin.\n\nIt was not a speed job, for once. It was a puzzle of order and consequence, and it had to be reasoned out cold.\n\nSomebody with sharp Logic had better work out the sequence.",
        success: "clock_quick", fail: "clock_slow" },
      clock_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u2699\ufe0f", text: "Zoomby traced the tangle backward from the escapement \u2014 that cog can only move once this one's clear, which can't shift till THAT one turns \u2014 and read off the one order that worked. Turn, turn, turn, and the gears unlocked in a cascade, each freeing the next exactly as reasoned.\n\nUp on the platform the Keeper of the Clock made a small approving noise, which by Clock-Keeper standards was a ticker-tape parade.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      clock_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf42", text: "The gears defeated everyone. Every order that looked right jammed harder two cogs later, until the Keeper gently reached past and eased them free by long, weary habit.\n\nIt cost the better part of an hour, and by the end the light was failing and the slowness over the tower had thickened a shade \u2014 as if the Muddle had been handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🏃", text: "The Keeper of the Clock stood on a platform among the huge slow gears. 'Prove you understand speed \u2014 REALLY understand it,' she said, in a voice like a well-oiled tick, 'and you may touch the Great Clock. The trouble is above us. But the truth is here, and the truth is only for those who can time it.'", question: "A worker bee flies straight out to a flower at 12 metres per second, then straight back along the same path at 8 metres per second. What is her average speed for the whole round trip?", options: ["10 m/s", "9.6 m/s", "8 m/s", "12 m/s", "11.2 m/s"], answer: 1,
        hint: "It is NOT just (12 + 8) \u00f7 2. Average speed is total distance \u00f7 total time. Pick an easy distance for one leg, say 24 metres, and work out the time for each leg.",
        solution: "Say one leg is 24 m. Out: 24 \u00f7 12 = 2 s. Back: 24 \u00f7 8 = 3 s. Total distance = 48 m, total time = 5 s. Average speed = 48 \u00f7 5 = 9.6 m/s. She spends longer at the slower speed, so the average is dragged below 10.",
        good: "hex_good", mid: "hex_mid" },
      hex_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "hex_mould", text: "The Keeper's antennae stood straight up, which for the Keeper was wild applause.\n\nFrom a bracket beside the escapement \u2014 'the exact tool that stamps a true cell, if you know how to hold it, which apparently somebody finally does' \u2014 she lifted down the Hexagon Mould: the iron form every honest cell in Hivehallow had first been pressed against. 'It has never once stamped a wonky cell,' she said, laying it in Zoomby's grasp like a knighthood. 'It won't mind shaping something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Hexagon Mould.", next: "core_fight" },
      hex_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Keeper sighed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people just average the two numbers, and chalked the full working on the platform for Zoomby to copy \u2014 every stage, in a hand so precise it ticked.\n\n''Wrong once is how you get right forever,'' she said, quoting somebody, and there was no unkindness in it. But the Hexagon Mould stayed on its bracket, waiting for someone who could find it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Up on the clock face itself, something was draped across the great minute hand, holding it back. The Standstill was bigger than its footpads and heavier than its Draggles, and it was pressing a slow grey palm to the mechanism, easing the whole hive toward a full and final stop.\n\nIt turned two vast slow eyes on the party climbing the escapement.\n\n'RESSST,' it said, drawing the word out until it nearly stopped mid-syllable. 'EVERYTHING. STOPSSS. EVENTUALLY. WHY. FIGHT. IT.'\n\n'Because the bloom won't wait,' said Zoomby, and set its wings for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Standstill", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck fast as a snapped thread, and the Standstill lost its grip on a whole span of the clock \u2014 the minute hand jumped forward a notch with an eager tick.",
          strikeText: "The Standstill pressed down and time itself went thick as cold honey. Every wingbeat took an age, and somebody's dodge is, technically, still in progress.",
          defeatText: "The Standstill slowed itself to the absolute stop it had always craved, achieved it, and in achieving it simply ceased, the way a held breath does. Its final word was 'FINALLL\u2014', which pleased Zoomby, because it never got to the Y." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Notch by notch, the Great Clock picked up its proper pace, and all across Hivehallow the syrup thinned and the city sped back to life \u2014 foragers unstalling mid-air, the honey-cart finally, gloriously, finishing its crossing to a small ironic cheer. The grey ribbons stopped pouring from the clock face. The rounded cells snapped back to crisp gold hexagons with a sound like a thousand tiny agreements.\n\nThe Keeper of the Clock appeared at the platform rail, surveyed the quickening hive, and gave a single approving tick, which witnesses agreed was unprecedented.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Standstill pressed down again and time went so thick that the door was the only thing still willing to move, so the party used it.\n\nThey regrouped in a comb-doorway across the square, bruised, furious, and \u2014 after a short vote \u2014 fortified with honeycake. Then they took the outer ladder to the clock face while the Standstill draped itself over the hands above. Not every battle is won by winning it, Zoomby noted grimly. Some are won by arriving anyway, even slowly.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Clock Tower glowed the workshop of Facet, maker of true lenses, who could grind a clear view out of a foggy one from twenty paces and had once repaired a broken perspective, though she didn't like to talk about it.\n\n'A Honeycomb Lens, is it?' she said, peering over spectacles that were themselves rather good lenses. 'Haven't made one since the old Keepers' day. Beeswax, Sun-Amber, the Hexagon Mould \u2014 show me all three and I'll grind you a lens worth trusting your eye to.'",
        forged: "The forge roared like an approving crowd. Facet melted the Sun-Amber clear, framed it in Beeswax, and pressed the whole thing true in the Hexagon Mould so it would always see the honest shape of things.\n\nWith the Honeycomb Lens, Zoomby found every angle and edge leapt into focus, sharp and countable, sagging shapes snapping true the moment it looked at them. Seeing clearly, it turned out, is half of getting the geometry right.\n\nThe Honeycomb Lens is yours! (+1 Geometry, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Facet laid the components on the bench and counted them, then counted them again, which was polite of her because it did not take long.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Lens wants all three or it's just a wish you can squint through. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor are the hexagons.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "zoomby_lens", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the Great Clock itself.\n\nZoomby scaled the escapement and found the trouble at the heart of the mechanism: a single tooth on the master cog that had been filed down, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, to run 'near enough' to time \u2014 and had been quietly dragging ever since, the way anything does if you let it slack off often enough. The slowness had spread from that one worn tooth through every gear that touched it, out of the Clock, and into the hive.\n\nZoomby scouted the fault, sighted it true through the Honeycomb Lens, and re-cut the tooth to an exact, unarguable point. No near enough.\n\nThe whole Clock seemed to draw a sharp breath and quicken. Somewhere several combs away, a stalled forager shot forward as if released from a held moment, which it was.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Hivehallow threw a party at record speed and finished it early, which the hive agreed was the highest honour it knew how to pay. The queen gave a speech of exactly the right length, the Keeper of the Clock was seen to almost dance (a brisk, ticking sort of dance), and the gatekeeper waived the toll for a second historic time.\n\nAt the end the hive presented Zoomby with the Nectar Count: a running tally, always exact, of every bloom in the valley and the best fast line between them. Zoomby flew home the long way for once, unhurried under a clear sky, and found \u2014 to its considerable surprise \u2014 that it rather enjoyed arriving on time instead of early.",
        midText: "Hivehallow was grateful, in a slightly draggy sort of way \u2014 the party started late and the queen's speech ran on past its proper length. Still, the Clock ticked true, the cells stood crisp and six-sided, the honey-cart got across, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Keeper pressed the Nectar Count into Zoomby's grasp: a running tally, always exact, of every bloom in the valley. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes often are.' Zoomby flew home already plotting a faster, cleaner line for next time \u2014 because a thing worth doing quickly is worth doing quickly again.",
        grantItem: "zoomby_count" },
    },
  },
  pebble: {
    id: "pebble",
    title: "The Wall That Wouldn't",
    tagline: "Cairnmoot's ancient wall has started forgetting how to stand up, and the smallest stone in the company intends to remind it.",
    items: [
      { id: "pebble_level", name: "The Plumb-True Level", stat: 1, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Geometry. Won with a sharp Geometry check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "pebble_key",   name: "The Keystone Riddle",  stat: 2, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "pebble_heart", name: "The Ballast Heart",    stat: 3, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Science. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { cornerstone: "A Dressed Cornerstone", plumbcoil: "A Coil of Plumb-Line", seal: "The Founder's Seal" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🪨", text: "Pebble was two and three-eighth inches tall, counting the pride, and he had never once in his life been anything other than exactly where he meant to be. This is a rarer talent than it sounds. Most things, given the chance, drift. Pebble held.\n\nSo when word reached Little Reckoning that the great wall of Cairnmoot had started to forget itself overnight, stones losing their footing, whole courses sagging like a sentence trailing off, Pebble was on the road before the porridge cooled.\n\n'A wall's just a lot of small certainties leaning on each other,' he said, which was almost a whole speech for Pebble. 'Take the certainty out and it's a landslide with regrets. Little Reckoning reckons. Let's go.'", next: "level_check" },
      level_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udcd0", stat: 1, dc: 10,
        text: "A cartload of dressed stone had shed its load down the banking, and among the honest rectangular blocks lay one mason's tool worth having, half-buried where the scree had taken it.\n\nThe trick was reading the true from the tumbled: the good block sat square to the world, its edges meeting at clean right angles, while the frost-cracked ones only pretended. Dig at the wrong block and the whole slope would shift and bury the lot.\n\nSomebody with an eye for Geometry had better pick the square one.",
        success: "found_level", fail: "level_miss" },
      level_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\udea8", text: "The angles all looked plausible from above, which is the trouble with scree; every block promises to be square until you trust it. The slope shifted, the tool slid deeper, and the banking closed over it with a sound like a shrug.\n\n'It'll fetch up at the bottom, true side down,' said Pebble, and did not fall over, because he was not laughing. (The Plumb-True Level stays lost this time. Another run may find it.)", next: "post_fight" },
      found_level: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "pebble_level", text: "The square block held firm under Pebble's small determined weight, and tucked beneath it, dropped by some long-gone mason, lay the Plumb-True Level: a brass tube of trapped liquid with a bubble that only ever sat dead-centre when a thing was truly flat.\n\nA level does not argue and cannot be flattered. Pebble approved of it enormously. The bubble settled to the middle as if pleased to be carried by someone who cared where the middle was.\n\nThe Plumb-True Level is yours. (+1 Geometry, kept forever.)", next: "post_fight" },
      post_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It clung to the parish waystone like damp: a Totter, small and grey, patiently rocking the stone loose in its socket so it would point true one moment and drunkenly askew the next.\n\nPebble planted his feet. A waystone that can't decide which way is up is how a whole road loses its nerve.\n\n'That stood straight since my great-grandstone's day,' said Pebble, and set himself square. The Totter wobbled harder, which is a Totter's whole argument.",
        enemy: { name: "The Waystone Totter", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed plumb and level, and a whole side of the Totter stopped wobbling, went briefly solid, and dropped away.",
          strikeText: "The Totter set the ground rocking underfoot, and for a sickening moment nothing in the company was sure which way down was. Somebody sat, hard, on the newly uncertain grass.",
          defeatText: "The Totter rocked itself right off its own centre of gravity, which is the one thing a Totter must never do, and toppled into nothing. The waystone settled true and pointed, firmly, one way only." },
        victory: "post_win", retreat: "post_retreat" },
      post_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Pebble checked the waystone with his new level. Dead true. 'Second one this week,' he said. First the village stocks gone wobbly at home, now a waystone losing its nerve.\n\nSomething was abroad in the country teaching solid things to doubt their footing, and Cairnmoot, all footing and nothing else, sat squarely in its path.", next: "crossroads" },
      post_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "A short strategic step back, Pebble announced, is still standing your ground, only elsewhere. They took the elsewhere by the low path, which cost a slow half-hour and left the morning feeling faintly untrustworthy underfoot.\n\nBehind them the Totter went back to rocking the waystone loose. Pebble did not look back. A stone that looks back, he held, is a stone thinking about rolling.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road split three ways under a waysign that had given up committing to any of them.\n\nThe left arm read THE MASON'S YARD, where they dress stone and dare you to fault the cut. The middle arm read THE CLAPPER BRIDGE, great slabs laid over the beck that you cross by nerve. The right arm read THE SCREE, of which the sign said only: LOOSE GROUND. NOTHING SITS STILL. MIND YOUR FOOTING.\n\nSomewhere down each road, the masons swore, a Dressed Cornerstone waited to be earned. A true cornerstone, like a true friend, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Mason's Yard", next: "row", preview: "Out-reckon a stone-dresser at the cut. A hard geometry puzzle guards the Cornerstone." },
        { label: "Cross the Clapper Bridge", next: "bridge", preview: "Cross the great slabs over the beck. A dice challenge guards the Cornerstone." },
        { label: "Climb the Scree", next: "fog", preview: "The loose slope where nothing sits still. A Crumble guards the Cornerstone. This one is a fight." },
      ] },
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Mason's Yard rang with mallets and boasting. 'SQUARE AS JUDGEMENT!' cried one dresser, over a block that leaned. 'TRUE ON ALL SIX FACES!' cried another, whose sixth face was a matter of faith.\n\nAt the far bench, cooling under a wet sack, sat a Dressed Cornerstone, every angle a clean ninety. The master dresser, a badger with a chisel in one paw and a swindle in the other, watched Pebble notice it and smiled the smile of one who hears a purse settle.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The cornerstone? Best in the yard,' said the badger. 'Settle a wager, small one. I'm laying a triangular buttress: one block on the top course, two on the next, three on the next, straight down to a bottom course of twelve. Tell me the total number of blocks and it's yours. Guess wrong and you buy it by the course, which I count generously.'", question: "A triangular stack has 1 block on the top row, 2 on the next, 3 on the next, and so on down to a bottom row of 12. How many blocks are there in total?", options: ["66", "72", "78", "84", "144"], answer: 2,
        hint: "You need 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 12. Pair them from the ends: 1 + 12, 2 + 11, 3 + 10 ... each pair is the same total.",
        solution: "Add 1 + 2 + ... + 12. Pairing from the outside: 1 + 12 = 13, 2 + 11 = 13, and so on gives 6 pairs of 13, so 6 \u00d7 13 = 78. (The rule is n(n + 1) \u00f7 2 = 12 \u00d7 13 \u00f7 2 = 78.)",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "cornerstone", text: "The badger counted it on its claws, ran out of claws, tried again on the bench in chalk, and arrived, grudging, at seventy-eight.\n\n'Right to the block,' it admitted. 'You'd dress me out of trade, you would. Take the cornerstone. Take it before I offer you an apprenticeship.'\n\nComponent found: A Dressed Cornerstone.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The badger worked the true total out on the bench, slowly and helpfully, which cost by the minute as well as the course. Yard rates being yard rates, the buttress took every coin Pebble carried and the cornerstone stayed under its sack.\n\n'Come back with a straighter head,' the badger advised. 'Straight's cheaper than sorry, in stone.'", next: "converge" },
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Clapper Bridge was three great slabs laid slab-to-slab over the loud brown beck, and you crossed it, said a dipper bobbing on a rock, by nerve alone. The slabs were true but the water lied about how far down it was, and a stone that hesitates on a clapper bridge is a stone the beck gets to keep.\n\nPebble, who trusted stone implicitly and water not at all, eyed the crossing. There was nothing for it but to go, and go certain.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 10, target: 3, text: "Roll 3 or more on a ten-sided die to cross the slabs sure-footed. The dipper has agreed to keep score, and to fish out nothing.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 3 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["3/5", "4/5", "7/10", "1/2"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10 all carry you across \u2014 that is 8 outcomes out of 10, and 8/10 = 4/5. Strong odds, but one time in five the beck wins. Reading the odds first steadies your footing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "cornerstone", text: "Pebble crossed slab to slab without a flicker of doubt, small and sure as a full stop, and stepped off dry to a disappointed bob from the dipper, who had counted on a splash.\n\nWedged under the last slab, dropped by some less certain crosser, sat a Dressed Cornerstone, true on every face and cold from the beck.\n\nComponent found: A Dressed Cornerstone.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The middle slab was exactly as far across as it looked, which Pebble briefly doubted, and doubt on a clapper bridge is a full step wrong. The beck took him, rolled him cheerfully downstream, and deposited him in a shallows with his dignity floating just out of reach.\n\nWhatever the last slab had been keeping, it kept. Some crossings give you one sure step and no more.", next: "converge" },
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Scree was a whole hillside of loose stone that had forgotten the point of staying put. It slid where it liked and settled nowhere, and even Pebble found his certainty tested, which was novel and unwelcome.\n\nHalf up the slope, on a boulder that was itself thinking about moving, something big was working the hillside looser, thumbing each settled stone into a wobble and chuckling gravel. It was a Crumble: the Muddle poured into the shape of pure subsidence. And balanced on its head, worn like a crown by a thing with no idea what a crown was for, sat a Dressed Cornerstone.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Crumble looked down without alarm. Things shaped like landslides feel very little until the very end.\n\n'NOTHING LASSSTS,' it said, in a voice like a wall giving way one stone at a time. 'ALL FALLS DOWN. SIT WITH ME. SLIDE A WHILE.'\n\n'Some things last on purpose,' said Pebble, and dug his small feet into the shifting slope, which is stone for standing his ground.",
        enemy: { name: "The Crumble", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck solid and square, and a whole face of the Crumble stopped sliding, set hard for one honest moment, and sheared away.",
          strikeText: "The Crumble sent the scree rushing, and the ground simply left. One of the company went down the slope a painful distance before the hillside remembered how to hold.",
          defeatText: "The Crumble slid itself apart faster than it could hold together, which is the fate every landslide is secretly racing toward, and settled at last into ordinary still stone. The Cornerstone rolled off its crumbling head and fetched up neatly at Pebble's feet, as if reporting for duty." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "cornerstone", text: "Pebble set the Cornerstone upright, checked it with his level (dead true), and then, because he could not help himself, spent a patient quarter-hour settling the worst of the loosened scree back into stable courses. A hillside is only a wall that has given up, he reasoned, and reasoned it back.\n\nComponent found: A Dressed Cornerstone.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The scree sided with the Crumble, as loose ground always will, and carried the company back down in an undignified rush. They regrouped on firm turf, short a good deal of skin and morale, and took the long stable path around.\n\nBehind them the Crumble went back to loosening the hill, crown wobbling. Pebble did not look back. Looking back on a slope, he noted, is how you learn what your feet are doing without you.", next: "converge" },
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again on bedrock, at a milestone reading CAIRNMOOT: 5, and the five stood so unshakably vertical that everyone felt steadier just looking at it.\n\nAhead the land rose toward the Old Quarry, one clean cliff-face of true-cut rock, the last thing standing dead straight, the masons said, between here and whatever was talking Cairnmoot's wall out of standing.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 8, target: 5, text: "A rockfall had left the quarry path blocked but for a run of stepping-blocks across the gap, half of them sound and half of them loose. Roll 5 or more on an eight-sided die to pick the sound blocks and cross. The drop is watching with professional interest.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair eight-sided die?", options: ["3/8", "1/2", "5/8", "1/4"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 5, 6, 7 and 8 all find sound footing \u2014 that is 4 outcomes out of 8, and 4/8 = 1/2. An even gamble over a long drop. Reading the odds first steadies the crossing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "seal_good", fail: "seal_miss" },
      seal_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "plumbcoil", text: "Pebble read the sound blocks by the way they sat and crossed without a wobble. In a cleft on the far side, coiled where a mason had cached it a lifetime ago, lay a Coil of Plumb-Line: a weighted cord that had never once lied about which way was truly down.\n\nComponent found: A Coil of Plumb-Line.", next: "logic_check" },
      seal_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "Pebble trusted a block that looked sound and wasn't; loose stone is an excellent liar. It tipped, he slid, and a merciful ledge caught him a good way down and along, jarred but whole.\n\nBy the time he climbed back the way was too dark to search the cleft. Some crossings give you one sound step. The run went on, a shade more muddled.", next: "logic_check" },
      logic_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83e\udde9", stat: 2, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found a wandering wall-warden slumped by her fire, glaring at a ring of standing stones.\n\n'It's a REASONING puzzle, this ring,' she said. 'Each stone's carved with a clue about the others, and only one is the true keystone \u2014 the rest are decoys, and I can't hold all the clues in my head at once to tell which. It's not a shoulder job, it's a thinking job. Somebody with good Logic should crack it.'\n\nShe looked up hopefully. A warden's favour is a thing worth carrying.",
        success: "logic_solved", fail: "logic_stumped" },
      logic_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udd13", bonusStars: 2, text: "It came clear the way good reasoning does: two clues on their own left three possible keystones, but the third clue quietly ruled out all but one, and once you laid them side by side the true stone was the only survivor. It rang, softly, when Pebble touched it.\n\nThe warden beamed and pressed a parcel of hard bread and harder cheese on the company, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      logic_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\udea8", text: "The clues defeated everyone; every candidate keystone satisfied two carvings and cheerfully broke a third. The warden took the burden back with the patience of someone who has been out-argued by a ring of rocks before.\n\n'Never mind,' she said kindly. 'The ring's kept its counsel a thousand years. It'll keep a night more.' The fire settled. Nobody quite met an eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The warden's fire was warm and the moor beyond it was not.\n\n'Cairnmoot's an hour on,' she said. 'Carry my warning to the wall-keeper through the dark and I'll owe you \u2014 wardens pay their debts. Or bed down here and go safe at dawn. Night moor's chancy, mind, chancier than usual, with things about that unsettle a body's footing.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll, but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the hard cheese is, against all odds, excellent." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night moor was all hidden dips and shifting peat. With the warden's lantern picking out the firm ground, you cross with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the true path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Pebble picked the firm ground stone by stone and brought the company to Cairnmoot's gate before the moon had properly risen, posted the warden's warning to the crooked box, and pocketed the favour: three warm stars and a supper that put a whole heart's worth of grit back into everyone.\n\nA risk is sound, Pebble held, once you've weighed the odds and the odds have agreed to be weighed. Bonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The moor won. Pebble went hip-deep into a peat hag shaped exactly like overconfidence, the warning got sodden, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of legibility.\n\nThe company squelched the last mile in a pointed silence. Good odds still lose sometimes, Pebble reflected, wringing out. That is precisely what keeps them odds and not promises.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Pebble slept flat against a boulder, which suited him, and woke to a moor so crisp the horizon looked ruled. The hard cheese proved, against all reasonable expectation, superb.\n\nThe walk took the promised hour exactly, which Pebble found deeply satisfactory. Sometimes the solid move is the slow one. Expected value, he told the company, is allowed to keep its feet dry.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The gate of Cairnmoot stood in a gap in the very wall they'd come to save, taking its old toll of one true answer per traveller. This morning the gap was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Crumble footpads had shoved a leaning barricade across it and daubed a sign: TOLE: ALL YER STANDING. Behind her bolted shutters the wall-keeper glared like a kettle at the boil.\n\n'A barricade that's already falling over,' observed Pebble, 'is barely a barricade at all,' which as war-cries go was at least structurally sound.",
        enemy: { name: "Crumble Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck plumb and true, and one footpad lost its balance for good, teetered, and came down in a heap of grey nothing.",
          strikeText: "A landslide of a fist swept the barricade wide, and somebody in the company paid a toll of footing they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad watched the first fall, tried to lean somewhere safer, discovered it had nothing to lean on, and subsided. The barricade slid down after it. Nobody rebuilt it." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The wall-keeper unbolted her shutters, her door and her scowl in that order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, kicking the barricade into the ditch. 'First time in an age. But nobody passes without answering \u2014 wall's rules, and firmest of all on a day the wall itself's gone soft.' She slid across a slate worn smooth by generations.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The company fell back with the footpads' rockslide laughter behind them, and spent a gritty quarter-hour in a hollow the hollow enjoyed rather more than they did.\n\nThey reached the gate at last by the keeper's side wicket, scuffed and simmering. She inventoried their state and said nothing about it. 'Bad morning,' she allowed. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📐", text: "The slate was old, the chalk worn to a nub, the question in the keeper's blunt sure hand. 'Take your time,' she said, in the tone of someone timing it.", question: "Each interior angle of a regular polygon is 150\u00b0. How many sides does it have?", options: ["10", "11", "12", "15", "8"], answer: 2,
        hint: "At each corner, the interior angle and the exterior angle sit on a straight line, so they add to 180\u00b0. The exterior angles of any polygon add up to 360\u00b0.",
        solution: "Each exterior angle = 180\u00b0 \u2212 150\u00b0 = 30\u00b0. The exterior angles of any polygon total 360\u00b0, so the number of sides = 360\u00b0 \u00f7 30\u00b0 = 12. It is a dodecagon.",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the slate, sniffed her deepest sniff of respect, and stamped Pebble with the old seal, which read SOUND in one small firm letter more than it needed.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, almost warmly, 'and hurry. The Keystone's gone, and a wall without its keystone is just a very slow avalanche. From what the crows say, it's slowing already.'", next: "cairnmoot" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, not unkindly, turned the slate about and walked the reasoning through corner by corner, then had Pebble say it back twice, because twice is where knowing sets.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the company through the side wicket, the long way round, past the byre, who could not believe their luck twice in a morning.", next: "cairnmoot" },
      cairnmoot: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Cairnmoot at last: a town that was really one enormous wall with houses leaning gratefully against it, built by folk who believed, correctly, that anything worth having is worth building to last.\n\nBut the wall was losing heart. A grey softness lay along its courses, thickest at the Keep at its centre, where the founding Keystone had held the whole ring true for a thousand years \u2014 and grey ribbons of Muddle now poured from the Keep's arch, coaxing stone after stone to lose its footing. Householders stood in their doors watching their lintels sag.\n\nPebble walked faster, small and grim and absolutely upright.", next: "found_check" },
      found_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 4, dc: 12,
        text: "The Keep's arch was shedding its voussoirs \u2014 the wedge-stones of the arch itself \u2014 one by one, and each falling stone had to be caught and set back into its exact slot before the next let go and the whole arch came down. There was no time to set them SLOWLY; the arch was unstitching faster than a careful hand could work.\n\nIt was pure speed and sure hands, catch and set, catch and set, before the keystone slot gaped too wide to bridge.\n\nSomebody quick had better hold the arch up by hand.",
        success: "found_quick", fail: "found_slow" },
      found_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "A blur of small sure movements \u2014 catch, seat, catch, seat \u2014 and the voussoirs went back faster than they could fall, until the arch stood whole again on Pebble's stubborn insistence, humming with held load.\n\nUp on the parapet the Master of the Wall made a low approving rumble, which by her standards was a standing ovation.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      found_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\udea8", text: "The stones fell faster than any hand could set them, and it took the better part of an hour of frantic propping to stop the arch collapsing at all. By the end the light had gone and the softness had crept a shade further along the wall, as if handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📦", text: "The Master of the Wall stood by the empty keystone slot. 'Prove you understand how a true shape holds itself,' she said, in a voice like set mortar, 'and you may set the founding stone. The trouble is below. But the truth is here, and the truth is only for those who can measure it.'", question: "A square has exactly the same perimeter as a rectangle measuring 10 cm by 4 cm. What is the area of the square, in cm\u00b2?", options: ["40", "49", "56", "64", "28"], answer: 1,
        hint: "First find the rectangle's perimeter. The square has the same perimeter shared equally over its four sides.",
        solution: "The rectangle's perimeter is 2 \u00d7 (10 + 4) = 28 cm. The square shares this over 4 equal sides, so each side is 28 \u00f7 4 = 7 cm. Area = 7 \u00d7 7 = 49 cm\u00b2.",
        good: "seal_forge_good", mid: "seal_forge_mid" },
      seal_forge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "seal", text: "The Master's stony face shifted a whole degree, which for her was jubilation.\n\nFrom a niche beside the slot \u2014 'the founder's own mark, for one who's earned the right to it, which apparently is you' \u2014 she lifted the Founder's Seal: the iron stamp that had blessed the true-laying of every stone in Cairnmoot. 'It has never once sealed a crooked course,' she said, setting it in Pebble's small grip. 'It won't mind blessing something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Founder's Seal.", next: "core_fight" },
      seal_forge_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Master sighed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people forget to find the perimeter first, and chalked the full working on the slot's rim for Pebble to copy, stage by stage, in a hand like set courses.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, quoting somebody. But the Founder's Seal stayed in its niche, for a hand that could earn it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "In the Keep's heart, something huge was leaning against the load-bearing pillar, easing it out of true a hair at a time. The Undermine was bigger than its footpads and slower than its Crumbles, and where its grey weight pressed, the whole wall above sagged toward the long fall.\n\nIt turned two heavy eyes on the company at the stair.\n\n'EVERYTHING. SETTLESSS,' it said. 'GIVE IT TIME. GIVE IT ME.'\n\n'Not this wall,' said Pebble, all two-and-three-eighth inches of him, and planted his feet for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Undermine", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck true as a keystone home, and the Undermine lost its purchase on a whole span \u2014 the pillar above creaked gratefully back toward vertical.",
          strikeText: "The Undermine leaned, and the floor sagged with it. For a heartbeat the whole Keep tilted, and somebody found the sloping ground the hard way.",
          defeatText: "The Undermine leaned its full weight for the killing push and, finding the pillar suddenly true and unyielding, overbalanced into its own subsidence and was gone. Its last word was 'SETTLLL\u2014', which pleased Pebble, since it never got to settle." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Course by course the wall took heart, stones remembering their footing all along the ring, and the grey ribbons stopped pouring from the arch. Outside, a sagging lintel creaked back level with a sound like relief. The Keep stood square.\n\nThe Master of the Wall appeared at the parapet, surveyed her steadied ring, and dipped her head one deliberate inch, which the town agreed was without precedent.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Undermine leaned again and the Keep tilted so alarmingly that the doorway was briefly the only trustworthy line in it, so the company used it.\n\nThey rallied in a lee of good masonry, bruised and furious and, after a short vote, fortified with the warden's hard cheese. Then they took the outer stair while the Undermine bore down on the pillar below. Not every fight is won by winning it, Pebble noted grimly. Some are won by staying upright and arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Keep glowed the forge of Anvilla, maker of true things, who could weight a wobble out of anything from twenty paces and had once rebalanced a broken argument, though she didn't discuss it.\n\n'A Ballast Heart, is it?' she said, over spectacles that were themselves rather good levels. 'Not made one since the founders' day. A Cornerstone, a Coil of Plumb-Line, the Founder's Seal \u2014 bring me all three and I'll make you a thing that cannot be talked off its centre.'",
        forged: "The forge roared its approval. Anvilla set the cornerstone plumb by the line, sealed its truth with the founder's mark, and cooled it slow and heavy.\n\nWith the Ballast Heart, Pebble found he understood WHY a thing stays standing \u2014 the pull of the world, the spread of the load, where the weight wants to go \u2014 and could set anything true by feel. Knowing how forces balance, it turns out, is half of standing firm.\n\nThe Ballast Heart is yours! (+1 Science, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Anvilla laid the components out and counted them, which was quick.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Heart wants all three or it's a wish with weight on it. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the geometry.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "pebble_heart", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the empty keystone slot at the crown of the founding arch.\n\nPebble climbed to it and found the trouble: the old keystone had been set, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, a hair off true \u2014 'close enough,' they'd have said \u2014 and a wall built on a keystone a hair off true will hold for a thousand years and then, all at once, remember it was wrong. The doubt had spread from that one stone through every course that leaned on it, out of the Keep and into the town.\n\nPebble set the founding stone dead true by level and line, blessed it with the Seal, and seated it home with a small, final, satisfied grunt. No close enough.\n\nThe whole wall seemed to draw itself up. Somewhere along the ring, a sagging course rose, very quietly, back into line.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Cairnmoot held a feast on top of its own wall, which the town considered the highest and most literal honour available. The mayor gave a speech standing dead upright, the Master of the Wall was seen to almost smile, and the gate-keeper waived the toll a second historic time.\n\nAt the end they gave Pebble the Keystone Riddle: the founding puzzle whose solving first raised the wall, carved small enough to carry. Pebble walked home under a steady sky, and laughed once at something Countra said, and fell over to the left, as he always did, and got up again, as he always did, because that is the whole of Pebble in one small motion.",
        midText: "Cairnmoot was grateful in a slightly off-plumb way \u2014 the feast started late and the mayor's speech listed gently to one side. Still, the wall stood, the courses held, the lintels came level, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Master pressed the Keystone Riddle into Pebble's grip: the founding puzzle, carved small. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes are.' Pebble walked home already planning a squarer attempt, because a thing worth doing soundly is worth doing again \u2014 and Little Reckoning, after all, reckons.",
        grantItem: "pebble_key" },
    },
  },
  squarby: {
    id: "squarby",
    title: "The Tiling That Came Undone",
    tagline: "Every floor in Tessellon has started leaving gaps, and the truest square in the world is not going to stand for it.",
    items: [
      { id: "squarby_setsq", name: "The Master Set-Square", stat: 1, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Geometry. Won with a sharp Geometry check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "squarby_tally",  name: "The Tiler's Tally",     stat: 0, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "squarby_dash",   name: "The Snapline",          stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { true_tile: "A Perfectly True Tile", chalk: "A Stick of Blue Chalk", gnomon: "The Bronze Gnomon" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🟥", text: "Squarby had four equal sides and four right angles and not one single regret about any of them. Circles, in Squarby's private opinion, were showing off. A square you could TRUST. A square you could tile a whole world with and never leave a gap, which is more than roundness has ever managed.\n\nSo when the distress-tile arrived from Tessellon \u2014 the city paved end to end in perfect patterns, where the floors themselves are the art \u2014 reporting that the tilings had begun to come undone overnight, gaps opening between tiles that had fit flush for centuries, Squarby was already squaring up.\n\n'A gap,' said Squarby, with feeling, 'is just a place where somebody stopped caring. Fetch my set-square. We are going to care at it until it closes.'", next: "square_check" },
      square_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u2712\ufe0f", stat: 1, dc: 10,
        text: "A tinker's tray of geometry tools had spilled across the road, and among the bent protractors and lying rulers lay one true instrument worth the stooping \u2014 if you could tell it from the frauds.\n\nThe test was the corners: the honest tool held a dead-square right angle, while the frauds were a fraction open or shut, the kind of error that tiles a floor beautifully and wrongly. Choose a bent one and it would throw every angle it touched.\n\nSomebody with an eye for Geometry had better pick the true one.",
        success: "found_setsq", fail: "square_miss" },
      square_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udcd0", text: "The corners all looked square to a careless glance, which is exactly how bad tools survive. Squarby chose one a hair open, felt the wrongness a beat too late, and by the time the tinker's cart trundled off the true instrument had gone with it, boxed among the frauds.\n\n'It'll square up in someone's hands eventually,' said Squarby, unconvinced. (The Master Set-Square stays lost this time. Another run may find it.)", next: "post_fight" },
      found_setsq: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "squarby_setsq", text: "The true tool announced itself the instant Squarby's corner met its corner: a perfect, uncompromising fit, angle to angle, with the quiet click of two things that agree completely. The Master Set-Square, its right angle honest to the last second of arc.\n\nSome tinker had carried this and let it slip and never found a corner true enough to know it again. Squarby did. The set-square rode home against Squarby's own edge like it had come back to family.\n\nThe Master Set-Square is yours. (+1 Geometry, kept forever.)", next: "post_fight" },
      post_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It had settled into the crossroads paving like spilt tea: a Gap, small and grey, patiently widening the joint between two flagstones that had sat flush since the road was young.\n\nSquarby bristled all four corners. A joint that opens is how a whole pavement unravels, one gap inviting the next.\n\n'Those stones FIT,' said Squarby, in the appalled tone of a creature watching someone dog-ear a book. The Gap, unrepentant, widened.",
        enemy: { name: "The Paving Gap", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed flush and exact, and a whole edge of the Gap snapped shut, two stones kissing true before the grey crumbled away.",
          strikeText: "The Gap yawned wider and the paving lurched, joints opening underfoot. Somebody put a boot straight through where solid ground had been a moment before.",
          defeatText: "The Gap closed on itself entirely, having nothing left to be a gap between, and simply ceased. The two flagstones sat flush again, and the pavement let out a small settling sigh." },
        victory: "post_win", retreat: "post_retreat" },
      post_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Squarby ran an edge along the closed joint. Flush. Perfect. 'Second gap this week,' it said darkly. First the market-square paving at home, now a crossroads coming apart.\n\nSomething was travelling the roads teaching fitted things to stop fitting, and Tessellon, whose every surface was a fit, lay right at the end of its route.", next: "crossroads" },
      post_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "A brief measured withdrawal, Squarby maintained, is not a gap in one's resolve so much as a deliberate spacing. They spaced themselves deliberately round by the verge, losing a slow half-hour and a little faith in the ground.\n\nBehind them the Gap went back to prising stones apart. Squarby did not look back. Looking back at a Gap, it reasoned, only gives it your other side to work on.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road forked three ways beneath a signpost whose own joints had loosened alarmingly.\n\nThe left arm read THE TILE-YARD, where they fire and cut and dare you to fault the fit. The middle arm read THE STEPPING TILES, great glazed slabs across the mere. The right arm read THE FRACTURE, of which the sign said only: NOTHING MEETS HERE. ALL EDGES PART. MIND THE GAPS.\n\nSomewhere down each road, the tilers swore, a Perfectly True Tile waited to be earned. A true tile, like a true word, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Tile-Yard", next: "row", preview: "Out-reckon a tile-cutter at the count. A hard tiling puzzle guards the True Tile." },
        { label: "Cross the Stepping Tiles", next: "bridge", preview: "Cross the glazed slabs over the mere. A dice challenge guards the True Tile." },
        { label: "Enter the Fracture", next: "fog", preview: "The broken ground where all edges part. A Sunderling guards the True Tile. This one is a fight." },
      ] },
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Tile-Yard roared with kilns and salesmanship. 'FLUSH EVERY TIME!' bawled one cutter, over a batch that plainly wasn't. 'NO GAPS OR YOUR GROATS BACK!' cried another, who had never once given groats back.\n\nAt the end kiln, cooling on a rack, sat a Perfectly True Tile, square to the atom. The master cutter, a stoat with a scoring-knife in one paw and a scheme in the other, watched Squarby notice it and smiled the smile of a purse being sized up.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The true tile? That's showroom stock,' said the stoat. 'Answer me this and it's yours, corner-boy. Picture my display: one big square tile ruled into a five-by-five grid of little squares. A customer asks how many squares are in it \u2014 ALL of them, every size, the little ones AND the bigger squares you can trace across the grid. Give me the total or pay showroom rates, which I round up handsomely.'", question: "A large square is ruled into a 5\u00d75 grid of small squares. Counting squares of every size (1\u00d71, 2\u00d72, 3\u00d73, 4\u00d74 and the whole 5\u00d75), how many squares are there in total?", options: ["25", "50", "55", "30", "225"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Count each size separately. There are 5\u00d75 = 25 of the 1\u00d71 squares. How many 2\u00d72 squares fit? Their top-left corner can sit in a 4\u00d74 grid of positions, so 16. Keep going.",
        solution: "Count by size: 1\u00d71 \u2192 5\u00d75 = 25; 2\u00d72 \u2192 4\u00d74 = 16; 3\u00d73 \u2192 3\u00d73 = 9; 4\u00d74 \u2192 2\u00d72 = 4; 5\u00d75 \u2192 1. Total = 25 + 16 + 9 + 4 + 1 = 55.",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "true_tile", text: "The stoat traced squares on the display with a claw, lost count twice, started over, and finally hissed 'fifty-five' with the air of one conceding a duel.\n\n'Every size, right enough. You'd cut me out of my own showroom. Take the tile. Take it before I make you foreman.'\n\nComponent found: A Perfectly True Tile.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The stoat walked the true count through, size by size, kindly and billably. Showroom rates being showroom rates, the display's price took every coin Squarby had and the true tile stayed on its rack.\n\n'Come back sharper,' the stoat advised, scoring a fresh tile. 'Sharp cuts cheaper than blunt, in this trade.'", next: "converge" },
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Stepping Tiles were great glazed slabs set across the mere, beautiful and treacherous, and you crossed them, said a moorhen, by trusting the pattern. The true slabs bore your weight; the cracked ones only shone as if they would, and the mere below was cold and patient and deep.\n\nSquarby, who trusted patterns above almost everything, studied the glaze. There was nothing for it but to step, and step believing.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 12, target: 5, text: "Roll 5 or more on a twelve-sided die to read the pattern and cross the sound slabs. The moorhen keeps score and will fish out nobody.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair twelve-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/3", "7/12", "3/4"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11 and 12 all land you true \u2014 that is 8 outcomes out of 12, and 8/12 = 2/3. Good odds, though a third of the time it's the cold mere. Reading the odds first steadies your step: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "true_tile", text: "Squarby read the glaze slab by slab and crossed without a wrong step, to a let-down cluck from the moorhen. Wedged under the last slab, dropped by some crosser who misread the pattern, sat a Perfectly True Tile, square and cold and gleaming.\n\nComponent found: A Perfectly True Tile.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "One slab shone exactly like a sound one and was not; a cracked glaze is a lovely liar. Squarby went through with a glassy crunch and a very cold splash, and paddled squarely to the near bank, out-argued by a tile for the first time in its life.\n\nWhatever the far slab kept, it kept. Some crossings give one right step and no more.", next: "converge" },
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Fracture was ground that had given up on togetherness. Everything in it had come apart at the seams \u2014 flagstones, field-walls, the very soil crazed into separating plates \u2014 and walking it meant leaping widening gaps that opened as you looked.\n\nAt the centre, on a plinth splitting slowly down the middle, something big was going along a mosaic prising each tessera from its neighbour and flicking it away. It was a Sunderling: the Muddle poured into the shape of pure coming-apart. And set into its brow, worn like a jewel by a thing with no notion of fit, gleamed a Perfectly True Tile.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Sunderling looked up from its unpicking without alarm. Things shaped like coming-apart rarely hold together enough to worry.\n\n'EVERYTHING. PARTSSS,' it said, in a voice like a seam giving way. 'NOTHING REALLY TOUCHES. WHY PRETEND? LET GO.'\n\n'Because a floor that lets go is a hole,' said Squarby, and squared its stance, which is square for rolling up sleeves it is far too rectangular to possess.",
        enemy: { name: "The Sunderling", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck flush and whole, and a whole seam of the Sunderling was forced to MEET itself \u2014 it fused shut and sheared away.",
          strikeText: "The Sunderling pulled the ground apart underfoot, and a gap yawned where footing had been. Somebody dropped a nasty distance into the widening seam before scrambling clear.",
          defeatText: "The Sunderling came apart at every seam at once, which is a Sunderling's dearest wish and its undoing, and scattered into grit too fine to gap. The True Tile fell from its splitting brow and landed flush on a flat rock, as if choosing to fit." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "true_tile", text: "Squarby retrieved the True Tile and, unable to walk past a broken mosaic, spent a patient while fitting the loosened tesserae back edge to edge until the pattern ran unbroken again. A floor healed is a small argument won against everything coming apart.\n\nComponent found: A Perfectly True Tile.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Fracture sided with the Sunderling, opening gaps faster than the company could clear them, and drove them back to whole ground short of skin and cheer. They took the long unbroken way round.\n\nBehind them the Sunderling went back to its mosaic, jewel gleaming, prising the tightest joints first because they resist most sweetly. Squarby did not look back. A gap, it held, only widens for an audience.", next: "converge" },
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again on a stretch of paving so flush you could not have slid a hair between the stones, at a milestone reading TESSELLON: 5 in tiles that had not shifted a whisker.\n\nAhead the land ran down to the Glasswater, one clean unbroken sheet across the valley, the last surface still perfectly joined, the tilers said, between here and whatever was un-fitting Tessellon.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 6, target: 6, text: "The Glasswater had frozen to a single pane, sound almost everywhere \u2014 but one hidden flaw ran across it, and only a true sixth-sense for a fault-line would cross clean. Roll a 6 on a six-sided die to read the pane and cross without cracking it.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling exactly a 6 on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "1/3", "1/6", "2/6"], answer: 2, why: "Only one face out of six is a 6, so the chance is 1/6. These are hard odds \u2014 you'll want the edge. Reading the odds first shows just how much you need that bonus: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "chalk_good", fail: "chalk_miss" },
      chalk_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "chalk", text: "Against the long odds Squarby read the pane's one true seam and crossed it without a crack. Frozen into the ice mid-sheet, dropped by a tiler with a steadier hand than luck, lay a Stick of Blue Chalk \u2014 the kind that snaps a line so straight the eye can rest on it.\n\nComponent found: A Stick of Blue Chalk.", next: "count_check" },
      chalk_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The odds were always long and this time they landed long. Squarby found the flaw the hard way, the pane gave with a crack like a dropped rule, and the Glasswater took it for a brisk cold swim to the shallows.\n\nWhatever the ice held, it kept. Long odds are long precisely so they can do this. The run went on, a shade more muddled.", next: "count_check" },
      count_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83e\uddee", stat: 0, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found a tile-merchant by his fire, surrounded by crates and despair.\n\n'It's the RECKONING beat me,' he said. 'A commission of tiles, an awkward price per hundred, breakages to subtract, a discount that only bites past a threshold \u2014 and I cannot for the life of me total the bill without it coming out different every time. It's not a lifting job, it's an arithmetic job. Somebody quick with numbers should tot it.'\n\nHe looked up hopefully. A merchant's favour keeps.",
        success: "count_solved", fail: "count_stumped" },
      count_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udcb0", bonusStars: 2, text: "It fell out cleanly once taken in order: count the sound tiles, price them per hundred, subtract the breakages, THEN take the discount off the part above the threshold and not a tile below it. Written straight, the bill came out the same twice running, which delighted the merchant more than the money.\n\nHe pressed a parcel of provisions on the company, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      count_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\uddfe", text: "The bill defeated everyone; the discount and the threshold conspired to give a different total each attempt, until the merchant folded the ledger away with the resignation of a man long at war with his own arithmetic.\n\n'Never mind,' he said kindly. 'It's been almost-right for years. It'll be almost-right a night more.' The fire ticked. Nobody quite met an eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The merchant's fire was warm and the dark outside was not.\n\n'Tessellon's an hour on,' he said. 'Run my urgent order-book through the night and I'll owe you \u2014 merchants pay their debts. Or bed down here and go safe at dawn. Night road's chancy, chancier than usual, with things about that part a body from its footing.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll, but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the provisions are surprisingly good." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night road was cracked and treacherous, seams opening in the dark. With the merchant's lantern showing the whole slabs, you cross with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the true path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Squarby crossed slab to whole slab and brought the company to Tessellon's gate before moonrise, filed the merchant's order-book in the crooked box, and pocketed the favour: three warm stars and a supper that fitted a whole heart's worth of nerve back into everyone.\n\nA risk is sound, Squarby held, once the odds are squared away and the odds have agreed to be squared. Bonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The night road won. Squarby dropped a corner into a seam shaped exactly like overconfidence, the order-book tore along a fold, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of condition.\n\nThe company limped the last mile in an edged silence. Good odds lose sometimes, Squarby reflected, dusting off. That is exactly what makes them odds and not promises.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Squarby slept flush against a flat stone, perfectly content, and woke to a morning ruled straight to the horizon. The provisions were, surprisingly, a delight.\n\nThe walk took the promised hour to the second, which Squarby found deeply agreeable. Sometimes the fitted move is the unhurried one. Expected value, it told the company, keeps its corners.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Tessellon's gate stood in a great tiled arch, taking its old toll of one true answer per traveller. This morning the arch was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Sunderling footpads had jammed a splitting hoarding across it, daubed TOLE: ALL YER EDGES. Behind her bolted shutters the gate-keeper glared like a kettle at the boil.\n\n'A hoarding coming apart at the joins,' said Squarby, 'is not a barrier, it is a demonstration of my point,' which as war-cries go was at least on brand.",
        enemy: { name: "Sunderling Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck flush, and one footpad's seams were forced to meet all at once \u2014 it clenched shut and crumbled.",
          strikeText: "A splitting fist parted the arch and somebody in the company paid a toll of footing they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad tried to part from the danger, parted from itself instead, and scattered. The hoarding fell into its own gaps. Nobody reassembled it." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The gate-keeper unbolted shutters, door and scowl in order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, kicking the split hoarding aside. 'First in an age. But nobody passes without answering \u2014 gate's rules, and tightest of all on a day the whole city's coming ungrouted.' She slid across a slate worn glassy by use.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The company fell back with the footpads' unpicking laughter behind, and spent a fractured quarter-hour in a side-court that came apart around them enthusiastically.\n\nThey reached the gate by the side wicket, scuffed and simmering. The keeper took stock of them and said nothing. 'Bad morning,' she allowed. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📐", text: "The slate was old, the chalk worn, the question in the keeper's exact hand. 'Take your time,' she said, timing it.", question: "A square tile has an area of 144 cm\u00b2. Seven of these tiles are laid in a single straight row, edge to edge, to make a path. What is the total length of the path, in cm?", options: ["72", "84", "96", "1008", "168"], answer: 1,
        hint: "First find the side length of one tile from its area. Then seven tiles in a row lie side by side along that length.",
        solution: "One tile has area 144 cm\u00b2, so its side is \u221a144 = 12 cm. Seven tiles in a row give a length of 7 \u00d7 12 = 84 cm. (The 1008 answer is the mistake of multiplying the AREA by 7.)",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the slate, sniffed her sniff of respect, and stamped Squarby FLUSH in tidy letters.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, nearly warm, 'and hurry. The Founding Floor's coming up, tile by tile, and a city that loses its founding pattern loses the plot. From what the pigeons say, it's most of the way to plotless.'", next: "tessellon" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, not unkindly, turned the slate and walked it through \u2014 area to side to length \u2014 then had Squarby say it back twice, because twice is where knowing sets.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the company through the side wicket, the long way, past the grout-works, who could not believe their luck twice in a morning.", next: "tessellon" },
      tessellon: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Tessellon at last: a city where the floors are the glory, every square and street a pattern that runs unbroken from wall to wall, laid by folk who held that a gap anywhere is an insult everywhere.\n\nBut the patterns were failing. A grey unravelling ran through the tilings, widest at the Hall of Floors at the centre, where the Founding Floor \u2014 the first true tessellation, from which every other took its measure \u2014 was lifting tile by tile, grey ribbons of Muddle pouring from the gaps. Citizens stood in doorways watching their mosaics come apart like sentences losing their words.\n\nSquarby marched faster, all four corners set.", next: "fit_check" },
      fit_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 4, dc: 12,
        text: "The Hall's antechamber floor was throwing its tiles: tesserae springing loose and flying up in a glittering storm, and each one had to be snatched from the air and pressed back into its exact place before the gap beside it spread and the whole floor let go at once. There was no time to place them CAREFULLY; they were coming loose faster than a slow hand could seat them.\n\nPure speed and a true eye, snatch and seat, snatch and seat.\n\nSomebody quick had better hold the floor together by hand.",
        success: "fit_quick", fail: "fit_slow" },
      fit_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "A blur of snatching and seating, each tile home in its slot the instant before its neighbour could gap, and the floor held, whole and flush, on Squarby's sheer refusal to let it part.\n\nUp on the dais the Keeper of Floors made a low approving note, which by her standards was rapturous.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      fit_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\udde9", text: "The tiles flew loose faster than any hand could seat them, and it took the better part of an hour of scrambling just to keep the floor from letting go entirely. By the end the light had failed and the unravelling had crept a shade further, as if handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📦", text: "The Keeper of Floors stood at the edge of the empty founding slot. 'Prove you know how true shapes fit,' she said, in a voice like a laid course, 'and you may set the founding tile. The trouble is below. But the truth is here, and the truth is only for those who can measure it.'", question: "Inside a square of side 10 cm, a second square is drawn with each of its corners touching a side of the big square, 3 cm from a corner of the big square. What is the area of the tilted inner square, in cm\u00b2?", options: ["40", "49", "58", "74", "100"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Each corner splits a side of the big square into 3 cm and 7 cm. One side of the tilted inner square is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs 3 and 7. You want the square of that length.",
        solution: "Each corner of the inner square cuts a side into 3 cm and 7 cm, so a side of the inner square is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs 3 and 7. Its length squared is 3\u00b2 + 7\u00b2 = 9 + 49 = 58, and the area of a square is exactly (side)\u00b2, so the area is 58 cm\u00b2.",
        good: "gnomon_good", mid: "gnomon_mid" },
      gnomon_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "gnomon", text: "The Keeper's composed face opened a fraction, which for her was delight.\n\nFrom a bracket by the slot \u2014 'the founder's own gnomon, for a corner true enough to deserve it, which is you' \u2014 she lifted the Bronze Gnomon: the pin whose shadow had squared the very first tile to true north and true noon. 'It has never once cast a crooked shadow,' she said, setting it in Squarby's keeping. 'It won't mind squaring something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Bronze Gnomon.", next: "core_fight" },
      gnomon_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Keeper sighed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people forget that a square's area is just its side squared, and chalked the working on the slot's rim, stage by stage, in a hand like a set course.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, quoting somebody. But the Bronze Gnomon stayed on its bracket, for a corner that could earn it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "In the Hall's heart, something vast was drawn across the Founding Floor, prising up its master tile. The Unmortar was bigger than its footpads and greyer than its Sunderlings, and everywhere its grey hand passed, the pattern lifted and drifted and forgot how to lie flat.\n\nIt turned two blank eyes on the company at the stair.\n\n'NOTHING FITSSS,' it said. 'NOT REALLY. NOT FOREVER. GIVE UP THE GRID.'\n\n'The grid is the point,' said Squarby, and squared up for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Unmortar", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck flush and exact, and the Unmortar lost its grip on a whole quarter of the floor \u2014 the tiles snapped back into pattern with a sound like a held chord resolving.",
          strikeText: "The Unmortar swept the pattern loose, and the floor became a scatter of drifting tiles underfoot. Somebody went down into the shifting gap between them.",
          defeatText: "The Unmortar tried to lift the master tile free and found it, and the whole floor, suddenly locked flush and immovable; over-reaching, it came apart in the effort and was gone. Its last word was 'UNGRI\u2014', which pleased Squarby, since it never finished coming apart." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Quarter by quarter the Founding Floor settled flush, and all across Tessellon the patterns drew tight again, tile finding tile in a spreading hush of small perfect fits. The grey ribbons stopped pouring. Outside, a half-unravelled mosaic knitted itself whole with a ripple like a smoothed cloth.\n\nThe Keeper of Floors appeared on the dais, surveyed the reclaimed pattern, and inclined her head one exact degree, which the city agreed was unheard of.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Unmortar swept again and the floor became such a raft of drifting tiles that the doorway was the only fixed thing, so the company took it.\n\nThey rallied in a flush-tiled alcove, bruised and furious and, after a short vote, fortified with the merchant's provisions. Then they came back by the dais stair while the Unmortar prised at the master tile below. Not every fight is won by winning it, Squarby noted grimly. Some are won by refusing to come apart and arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Hall glowed the workshop of Kiln, maker of true things, who could fire the wobble out of any shape from twenty paces and had once mended a broken pattern, though she wouldn't dwell on it.\n\n'A Snapline, is it?' she said, over spectacles ground square. 'Not made one since the founders' day. A True Tile, a Stick of Blue Chalk, the Bronze Gnomon \u2014 bring me all three and I'll make you a line so fast and so straight nothing can drift off it.'",
        forged: "The forge roared approval. Kiln squared the true tile by the gnomon's shadow, charged the snapline with the blue chalk, and drew it taut and quick.\n\nWith the Snapline, Squarby found it could lay a dead-straight line across a whole city faster than doubt could travel, tiles falling true in its wake. Straightness, at speed, turns out to be its own kind of quickness.\n\nThe Snapline is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Kiln laid the components out and counted them, which took no time.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Snapline wants all three or it's a wish on a string. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the geometry.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "squarby_dash", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the empty founding slot at the heart of the Floor.\n\nSquarby knelt to it and found the trouble: the first tile had been set, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, a whisper out of square \u2014 'near enough,' they'd have shrugged \u2014 and a whole city tiled off a founding tile a whisper out of square will run true for centuries and then, all at once, discover the error in every joint at the same moment. The gap had spread from that one tile through every pattern that took its line, out of the Hall and into the streets.\n\nSquarby squared the founding tile to true north by the gnomon, snapped its edges dead straight with the line, and pressed it home flush, with a small ferocious satisfaction. No near enough.\n\nThe whole Floor seemed to draw tight. Somewhere across the city, a gaping mosaic closed, very quietly, without a seam.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Tessellon threw a feast laid out on its own Founding Floor, which the city held to be the highest honour it could offer a guest. The mayor gave a speech aligned exactly to the grid, the Keeper of Floors was seen to almost smile, and the gate-keeper waived the toll a second historic time.\n\nAt the end they gave Squarby the Tiler's Tally: the reckoning-frame that had counted true every tile in the founding, so no floor laid by it could ever come up a tile short. Squarby went home over paving so flush it could have wept, if squares wept, which they do not, being far too composed for it.",
        midText: "Tessellon was grateful in a slightly gappy way \u2014 the feast set out a little uneven and the mayor's speech drifted off the grid twice. Still, the floors held, the patterns ran unbroken, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Keeper pressed the Tiler's Tally into Squarby's keeping: the founding reckoning-frame. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes are.' Squarby went home already planning a flusher attempt, because a thing worth doing true is worth doing again.",
        grantItem: "squarby_tally" },
    },
  },
  frostcal: {
    id: "frostcal",
    title: "The Melting of the Measures",
    tagline: "In Rime, where medicine is measured to the last grain, the measures have started running warm and vague. A healer who counts exactly is the only cure.",
    items: [
      { id: "frostcal_spoon", name: "The Exact Measure",  stat: 0, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won with a sharp Arithmetic check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "frostcal_draught",name: "The Steady Draught", stat: 3, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Science. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "frostcal_star",  name: "The Sixfold Star",    stat: 1, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Geometry. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { truecold: "A Phial of True Cold", salt: "A Grain of Fixing-Salt", mould: "The Sixfold Mould" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "❄️", text: "Frostcal had six arms of frost radiating from one still centre, each the exact mirror of the others, and she healed the way she was built: by exactness. A remedy is only medicine while it stays precise. One grain too much and it is a poison; one grain too little and it is a disappointment. The whole art is in the counting.\n\nSo when the plea came from Rime \u2014 the cold apothecary-town where every draught is measured to the grain against the Cold Ledger \u2014 that the measures themselves had started running warm and vague, spoons melting their marks, doses coming out 'about right' and making people 'about better', Frostcal packed her scales.\n\n'About better is another way of saying still ill,' she said, cold and kind at once. 'Fetch the good scales. We are going to be exact at this until it heals.'", next: "measure_check" },
      measure_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83e\uddee", stat: 0, dc: 10,
        text: "A courier's case of measuring spoons had spilt across the frozen road, and among the warped and re-melted ones lay a single true measure worth the cold fingers \u2014 if you could read which.\n\nA true spoon's marks divide its bowl into exact, even steps; the melted ones step unevenly, a fraction out and then a fraction more. Add the steps up and only the true spoon totals clean. Choose a warped one and every dose it pours will drift.\n\nSomebody quick with Arithmetic had better read the marks and pick the true spoon.",
        success: "found_spoon", fail: "measure_miss" },
      measure_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\udd44", text: "The marks all looked even to a cold and hurried eye, which is how bad spoons get trusted. Frostcal added the steps a beat too fast, chose one whose totals limped, and by the time she saw the drift the courier's sled had gathered the case and gone.\n\n'It will pour for somebody, wrongly, and they will wonder why they never quite recover,' she said, which was the bleakest thing anyone had said all morning. (The Exact Measure stays lost this time. Another run may find it.)", next: "post_fight" },
      found_spoon: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "frostcal_spoon", text: "The true spoon's marks added clean the first time and cleaner the second: exact even steps, bowl to brim, no drift anywhere in the count. The Exact Measure, silver and cold and honest.\n\nSome apothecary had carried this and lost it in the snow and never trusted their doses again. Frostcal folded it into her kit, and something in her sixfold symmetry settled, the way a thing does when it meets another thing that also refuses to be approximate.\n\nThe Exact Measure is yours. (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)", next: "post_fight" },
      post_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It sat astride the dose-post at the parish line \u2014 the public marker giving the safe measure of willowbark for a fever \u2014 a Thaw, small and grey and warm, patiently melting the crisp 'THREE GRAINS' into 'a few grains, more or less, to taste'.\n\nFrostcal's frost-arms stiffened. A dose-post that says 'to taste' is how a fever becomes a funeral.\n\n'Three grains,' said Frostcal, in a voice you could have kept ice in. 'Precisely three.' The Thaw only radiated more warmth, which is a Thaw's whole cruelty.",
        enemy: { name: "The Dose-Post Thaw", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed crisp and cold, and a whole side of the Thaw froze solid, edges sharpening for one clean instant before crumbling to frost.",
          strikeText: "The Thaw breathed warm and the world went runny; marks blurred, measures ran, and somebody's careful count melted mid-number into a helpless 'roughly'.",
          defeatText: "The Thaw warmed itself past the point of holding any shape at all, which is where warmth ends, and evaporated. The dose-post read THREE GRAINS again, sharp enough to trust your life to." },
        victory: "post_win", retreat: "post_retreat" },
      post_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Frostcal reset the dose-post's marks with a cold fingertip. Exactly three. 'Second one this week,' she said. First the infirmary scales gone soft at home, now a dose-post melting its numbers.\n\nSomething was moving through the country warming exact things into vague ones, and Rime, whose every remedy lived or died by an exact grain, sat right in its thaw-line.", next: "crossroads" },
      post_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "A measured step back, Frostcal noted, is not a dilution of resolve so much as a lower dose of it. They took the lower dose by the frozen ditch-path, losing a slow half-hour and a little trust in their own arithmetic.\n\nBehind them the Thaw went back to melting the dose-post warm. Frostcal did not look back. Looking back at a Thaw only warms you to its way of thinking.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road forked three ways under a signpost whose letters were beginning, ominously, to drip.\n\nThe left arm read THE APOTHECARY ROW, where they compound and measure and dare you to fault the dose. The middle arm read THE ICE-LEDGES, a stair of frozen shelves across the ravine. The right arm read THE FEVER-FEN, of which the sign said only: WARM GROUND. THINGS RUN HERE. MIND YOUR MEASURES.\n\nSomewhere down each road, the apothecaries swore, a Phial of True Cold waited to be earned. True cold, like a true friend, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Apothecary Row", next: "row", preview: "Out-reckon a compounder at the mixing bench. A hard mixture puzzle guards the True Cold." },
        { label: "Cross the Ice-Ledges", next: "bridge", preview: "Cross the frozen shelves over the ravine. A dice challenge guards the True Cold." },
        { label: "Wade the Fever-Fen", next: "fog", preview: "The warm ground where all things run. A Swelter guards the True Cold. This one is a fight." },
      ] },
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Apothecary Row steamed with a hundred little burners and boasts. 'EXACT TO THE GRAIN!' cried one compounder, over a dose plainly to the pinch. 'NEVER A DROP OUT!' cried another, dripping.\n\nAt the last bench, sealed under a cold-cap, stood a Phial of True Cold, its contents so still they were nearly solid. The master compounder, a marten with a pipette in one paw and a fiddle in the other, watched Frostcal notice it and smiled the smile of a purse being weighed.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The True Cold? That's my prize stock,' said the marten. 'Settle a compounder's wager, frost. I'm blending a cooling draught: two litres of spring-water at sixty degrees mixed with three litres of meltwater at ten degrees, no heat lost to the room. Tell me the temperature of the blend and the phial is yours. Miss it and you buy the batch at bench rates, which run warm and dear.'", question: "Two litres of water at 60\u00b0C are mixed with three litres of water at 10\u00b0C, with no heat lost. What is the temperature of the mixture?", options: ["25\u00b0C", "30\u00b0C", "35\u00b0C", "36\u00b0C", "40\u00b0C"], answer: 1,
        hint: "This is a weighted average: the 3 litres pull harder than the 2 litres. Total the heat as (litres \u00d7 temperature) for each, add them, and divide by the total litres.",
        solution: "Weighted average: (2\u00d760 + 3\u00d710) \u00f7 (2 + 3) = (120 + 30) \u00f7 5 = 150 \u00f7 5 = 30\u00b0C. It sits closer to 10\u00b0 than to 60\u00b0 because there is more of the cold water.",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "truecold", text: "The marten checked it on the bench thermometer, blinked at the reading, and conceded thirty degrees with the grace of a creature that would rather have overcharged.\n\n'Dead on the mark. You'd compound me out of trade. Take the phial. Take it before I offer you a partnership.'\n\nComponent found: A Phial of True Cold.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The marten worked the true blend out on the bench, warmly and helpfully, which cost by the drop as well as the minute. Bench rates being bench rates, the batch took every coin Frostcal carried and the phial stayed under its cap.\n\n'Come back cooler in the head,' the marten advised. 'Cool heads compound cheaper, in this trade.'", next: "converge" },
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Ice-Ledges were a stair of frozen shelves down the ravine wall, and you crossed them, said a shivering wren, by picking the sound ones. The thick ledges held; the thin ones only glittered as if they would, and the ravine below was a long cold way down.\n\nFrostcal, who read ice the way others read faces, studied the shelves. There was nothing for it but to trust her eye and go.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 8, target: 7, text: "The ledges are mostly thin; only the very thickest will bear you. Roll 7 or more on an eight-sided die to find sound ice. The wren keeps score from a safe crevice.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 7 or more on a fair eight-sided die?", options: ["1/8", "1/4", "3/8", "1/2"], answer: 1, why: "Only 7 and 8 bear your weight \u2014 that is 2 outcomes out of 8, and 2/8 = 1/4. Hard odds over a hard drop, so every edge counts. Reading the odds first steadies your footing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "truecold", text: "Against the odds Frostcal read the thick ice true and crossed ledge to sound ledge. Frozen into a shelf on the far wall, dropped by some crosser with a warmer eye, sat a Phial of True Cold, still as stopped time.\n\nComponent found: A Phial of True Cold.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The odds were thin as the ice, and this time both gave way. Frostcal trusted a shelf that glittered and shouldn't have; it cracked, she dropped, and a snowbank caught her a jarring way down.\n\nWhatever the far wall held, it kept. Hard odds are hard precisely so they can do this. The run went on, a shade more muddled.", next: "converge" },
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Fever-Fen breathed warm and wrong, and everything in it ran: icicles wept, marked stones lost their numbers, and Frostcal felt her own precise edges begin, alarmingly, to soften.\n\nAt its heart, on a hummock steaming gently, something big was going along a rack of phials warming each until its measure ran and its label smeared. A Swelter: the Muddle poured into the shape of a fever. And clutched to its melting chest, worn like a locket by a thing with no notion of dosage, glinted a Phial of True Cold, holding out against the warmth.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Swelter turned without alarm. Things shaped like fever rarely worry; they make you do the worrying.\n\n'WARMER,' it sighed, in a voice like a poultice gone off. 'LOOSER. EASIER. WHY MEASURE? MEASURING'S SO COLD.'\n\n'Cold keeps things true,' said Frostcal, and set her frost-arms wide, which is snowflake for rolling up sleeves arranged in perfect sixfold order.",
        enemy: { name: "The Swelter", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck crisp and cold, and a whole side of the Swelter froze true for one honest instant, marks and edges sharp, before shearing away.",
          strikeText: "The Swelter breathed and the fen ran hotter; measures melted, and one of the company felt their careful counting go warm and slippery and slide away.",
          defeatText: "The Swelter warmed itself out of all shape and simply steamed off, the way a fever finally breaks. The Phial of True Cold dropped from its melting chest and landed unmelted in the frost, still keeping perfect time." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "truecold", text: "Frostcal recovered the Phial and, being a healer, could not leave the fen's warmed remedies to poison whoever found them; she chilled each running phial back to true measure and re-inked its smeared label before moving on. A dose set right is a life not yet lost.\n\nComponent found: A Phial of True Cold.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The fen sided with the Swelter, running hotter the harder they pushed, and drove the company back to cold clean air, wrung out and low on cheer. They took the long chill way round.\n\nBehind them the Swelter went back to its rack, locket glinting, warming the coldest phials first because they resist most satisfyingly. Frostcal did not look back. Looking back at a Swelter only lets it warm your resolve.", next: "converge" },
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again on ground gone hard with honest frost, at a milestone reading RIME: 5 in numbers sharp enough to cut.\n\nAhead the land dropped toward the Still Pool, one clean sheet frozen dead true, the last exact surface, the apothecaries said, between here and whatever was thawing Rime's measures.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 10, target: 2, text: "The Still Pool had frozen almost everywhere sound \u2014 only one thin patch waited to swallow the careless. Roll 2 or more on a ten-sided die to cross the good ice. The frost is watching, kindly.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 2 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["9/10", "4/5", "1/2", "7/10"], answer: 0, why: "Only a roll of 1 fails; 2 through 10 all cross safe \u2014 that is 9 outcomes out of 10, so 9/10. Excellent odds, but even one-in-ten finds the thin patch. Reading the odds first steadies the crossing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "salt_good", fail: "salt_miss" },
      salt_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "salt", text: "Frostcal crossed the good ice sure-footed, and there, frozen into the pool where a hundred winters had cured it pure, lay a Grain of Fixing-Salt \u2014 the salt that stops a thing running warm ever again, that holds a measure fixed forever.\n\nComponent found: A Grain of Fixing-Salt.", next: "science_check" },
      salt_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The one-in-ten patch is exactly where Frostcal's foot came down; good odds do occasionally do this. The ice gave, the pool took her for a short freezing dip, and let her out gasping in the shallows.\n\nWhatever the pool held, it kept. The run went on, a shade more muddled.", next: "science_check" },
      science_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udd2c", stat: 3, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found a field-surgeon at her fire, glaring at a patient's chart.\n\n'It's a SCIENCE puzzle, this fever,' she said. 'It rises and falls in a way I can't read \u2014 something about how the body sheds heat, how the medicine cools it, why the temperature keeps rebounding. It's not a bandage job, it's a reasoning-about-the-world job. Somebody who knows their Science should work out why.'\n\nShe looked up hopefully. A surgeon's favour is worth carrying.",
        success: "science_solved", fail: "science_stumped" },
      science_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\uddea", bonusStars: 2, text: "It came clear the way good science does: the fever rebounded because the draught worked too fast, and a body sheds heat slower than medicine can force it, so it overshot and rallied. Give it gently, in steady doses, and the temperature came down and stayed. Say it plainly and the chart stopped being a mystery.\n\nThe surgeon beamed and pressed a parcel of dried rations on the company, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      science_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf21\ufe0f", text: "The fever defeated everyone; every theory explained the rise and got the fall wrong, until the surgeon took the chart back with the patience of someone long at odds with a stubborn body.\n\n'Never mind,' she said kindly. 'It's puzzled better than you. It'll puzzle a night more.' The fire settled. Nobody quite met an eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The surgeon's fire was warm and the night beyond it bitter.\n\n'Rime's an hour on,' she said. 'Carry my urgent case-notes through the dark and I'll owe you \u2014 surgeons pay their debts. Or bed down here and go safe at dawn. Night road's chancy, chancier than usual, with things about that warm the sense right out of a body.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll, but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the dried rations are better than they look." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night road ran through warm fog that hid the safe path. With the surgeon's lantern burning cold and clear, you cross with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the true path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Frostcal read the cold clear path through the warm fog and brought the company to Rime's gate before moonrise, filed the surgeon's case-notes in the crooked box, and pocketed the favour: three cold-bright stars and a supper that measured a whole heart's worth of resolve back into everyone.\n\nA risk is sound, Frostcal held, once the odds are weighed and the odds have agreed to hold still. Bonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The warm fog won. Frostcal lost the cold path in a pocket of thaw shaped exactly like overconfidence, the case-notes ran and smeared, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of legibility.\n\nThe company trudged the last mile in a chilled silence. Good odds still lose sometimes, Frostcal reflected. That is exactly what makes them odds and not promises.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Frostcal slept in the cold clean air, which suited her crystalline constitution, and woke to a morning sharp enough to shave with. The dried rations proved unexpectedly excellent.\n\nThe walk took the promised hour exactly, which Frostcal found deeply reassuring. Sometimes the exact move is the unhurried one. Expected value, she told the company, likes to keep its head cold.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Rime's gate stood in a frost-carved arch, taking its old toll of one true answer per traveller. This morning the arch was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Swelter footpads had strung a dripping curtain across it, daubed TOLE: ALL YER EXACTNESS. Behind her bolted shutters the gate-keeper glared like a kettle at the boil, which was ironic and she knew it.\n\n'A curtain that's melting,' said Frostcal, 'is scarcely a curtain,' which as war-cries go was at least clinically accurate.",
        enemy: { name: "Swelter Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck cold and exact, and one footpad froze true for an instant, its warmth arrested, before it cracked and fell.",
          strikeText: "A warm wet fist swept the curtain wide, and somebody in the company paid a toll of exactness they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad tried to warm away from the cold and, warming, lost the last of its shape and steamed off. The curtain dripped itself to nothing. Nobody re-hung it." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The gate-keeper unbolted shutters, door and scowl in order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, kicking the melted curtain aside. 'First in an age. But nobody passes without answering \u2014 gate's rules, and coldest of all on a day the whole town's running warm.' She slid across a slate frosted at the edges.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The company fell back with the footpads' warm laughter behind, and spent a clammy quarter-hour in a side-lane the thaw enjoyed rather more than they did.\n\nThey reached the gate by the side wicket, damp and simmering. The keeper took stock of them and said nothing. 'Bad morning,' she allowed. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧊", text: "The slate was frost-rimed, the chalk cold, the question in the keeper's exact hand. 'Take your time,' she said, timing it.", question: "A flask is 3/4 full. After 6 decilitres are poured out, it is exactly 1/2 full. What is the full capacity of the flask, in decilitres?", options: ["18", "20", "24", "30", "12"], answer: 2,
        hint: "The 6 decilitres poured out is the difference between 3/4 full and 1/2 full. What single fraction of the flask is that?",
        solution: "Going from 3/4 full to 1/2 full is a drop of 3/4 \u2212 1/2 = 1/4 of the flask. So 1/4 of the capacity is 6 dl, which means the full capacity is 6 \u00d7 4 = 24 dl.",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the slate, sniffed a frosty sniff of respect, and stamped Frostcal EXACT in letters cut sharp.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, nearly warm, and caught herself. 'Nearly. And hurry. The Cold Ledger's melting, and a town whose every dose lives in that book has hours, not days. From what the crows say, fewer hours than it had.'", next: "rime" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, not unkindly, turned the slate and walked the fractions through, then had Frostcal say it back twice, because twice is where knowing freezes solid.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the company through the side wicket, the long way, past the ice-house, who could not believe their luck twice in a morning.", next: "rime" },
      rime: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Rime at last: a town of blue shadows and cold clean rooms, where every ailment has its exact remedy written in the Cold Ledger and measured to the grain, kept by folk who know that precision is the only mercy medicine has.\n\nBut the measures were melting. A grey warmth lay over the streets, thickest at the Infirmary at the centre, where the Cold Ledger held every true dose \u2014 and grey ribbons of Muddle poured from its doors, warming exact grains into vague pinches. Apothecaries stood helpless as their scales drifted and their doses ran to 'about'.\n\nFrostcal swept faster, cold to her still centre.", next: "count_check" },
      count_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 2, dc: 12,
        text: "The Infirmary's dispensary was in uproar: the Cold Ledger's loose dose-cards had scattered in the warm draught, and each had to be read and sorted back into the one correct order \u2014 by ailment, by strength, by which must never be shelved beside which \u2014 before the warmth smeared their ink for good. There was no time to do it SLOWLY; the cards were smearing as they lay.\n\nReasoning at speed: read the logic of the order, sort, and be right the first time.\n\nSomebody with sharp Logic had better set the dispensary in order.",
        success: "count_quick", fail: "count_slow" },
      count_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "A blur of reading and sorting \u2014 this before that, this never beside that \u2014 and the dose-cards went back into flawless order the instant before their ink could run, the whole dispensary set right on Frostcal's cold clear reasoning.\n\nUp at the lectern the Keeper of the Ledger made a small approving breath that fogged in the cold, which by her standards was applause.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      count_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf42", text: "The cards smeared faster than they could be read, and it took the better part of an hour to sort even the survivors, checking each twice. By the end the light had failed and the warmth had crept a shade further through the Infirmary, as if handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔢", text: "The Keeper of the Ledger stood at a lectern cut from a single block of ice. 'Prove you can hold an exact quantity in your head,' she said, in a voice like a frozen bell, 'and you may touch the Cold Ledger. The trouble is below. But the truth is here, and the truth is only for those who can count it.'", question: "A snowflake charm doubles its number of ice-spikes every minute. After 8 minutes it has 256 spikes. After how many minutes did it have exactly 32 spikes?", options: ["4", "5", "6", "3", "8"], answer: 1,
        hint: "Doubling makes the count go 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, ... Work BACKWARDS from 256, halving each minute: 256, 128, 64, 32.",
        solution: "At minute 8 there are 256. Halving back: minute 7 \u2192 128, minute 6 \u2192 64, minute 5 \u2192 32. So it had 32 spikes at minute 5. (Counting up from 1 at minute 0 agrees: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 lands on minute 5.)",
        good: "mould_good", mid: "mould_mid" },
      mould_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "mould", text: "The Keeper's still face crystallised into something like joy.\n\nFrom a niche of blue ice \u2014 'the founder's own mould, for a mind exact enough to deserve it, which is you' \u2014 she lifted the Sixfold Mould: the form that had cast the first true snowflake, six arms in perfect symmetry. 'It has never once cast a lopsided flake,' she said, laying it in Frostcal's cold hands. 'It won't mind shaping something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Sixfold Mould.", next: "core_fight" },
      mould_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Keeper breathed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people count forwards and lose their place, and chalked the halving out on the ice for Frostcal to copy, minute by minute.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, quoting somebody. But the Sixfold Mould stayed in its niche, for a mind that could reach it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "In the Infirmary's cold heart, something vast lay draped across the Cold Ledger, breathing warm into its spine. The Fever-King was bigger than its footpads and warmer than its Swelters, and everywhere its heat reached, the true doses ran together into one meaningless smear.\n\nIt turned two glowing eyes on the company at the stair.\n\n'WARMTH,' it sighed. 'COMFORT. NO MORE COLD COUNTING. LET THE NUMBERS SLEEP.'\n\n'The numbers are what keep people alive,' said Frostcal, and set her six frost-arms for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Fever-King", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck cold and exact, and the Fever-King lost its warmth over a whole page \u2014 the doses beneath froze back into sharp true figures.",
          strikeText: "The Fever-King breathed and the Infirmary ran hot; measures melted together, and somebody's exact count dissolved into a fevered, useless 'roughly'.",
          defeatText: "The Fever-King poured all its warmth into one last thaw and, spent, had no shape left to cool into; it steamed away entirely. Its last word was 'WARMLLL\u2014', which pleased Frostcal, since even its ending failed to hold together." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Page by page the Cold Ledger froze back to exactness, and all across Rime the measures set true again, scales settling, doses sharpening from 'about' to precisely-so. The grey warmth withdrew. Outside, a melting dose-post refroze its numbers with a crackle like a held breath released.\n\nThe Keeper of the Ledger appeared at the lectern, surveyed the recovered doses, and dipped her head one exact degree, which the town agreed was without precedent.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Fever-King breathed again and the Infirmary ran so warm that the cold doorway was the only exact edge left, so the company took it.\n\nThey rallied in a chilled store-room, wrung out and furious and, after a short vote, fortified with the surgeon's rations. Then they came back by the cold stair while the Fever-King smeared the Ledger below. Not every fight is won by winning it, Frostcal noted. Some are won by staying cold and arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Infirmary glowed \u2014 coldly, this being Rime \u2014 the workshop of Rimewright, maker of true things, who could freeze the wobble out of any measure from twenty paces and had once re-set a broken symmetry, though she wouldn't dwell on it.\n\n'A Sixfold Star, is it?' she said, over spectacles rimed with frost. 'Not made one since the founders' day. A Phial of True Cold, a Grain of Fixing-Salt, the Sixfold Mould \u2014 bring me all three and I'll make you a star that can never be warmed out of true.'",
        forged: "The forge blazed cold. Rimewright poured the True Cold into the Sixfold Mould, fixed it forever with the grain of salt, and set it hard and symmetrical.\n\nWith the Sixfold Star, Frostcal found she could see the true SHAPE of a thing at a glance \u2014 its symmetry, its proportion, whether its parts balanced \u2014 and set any crooked form to rights by eye. Knowing the geometry of a thing, it turns out, is half of keeping it exact.\n\nThe Sixfold Star is yours! (+1 Geometry, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Rimewright laid the components out and counted them, which was quick.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Star wants all three or it's a wish with points on it. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the arithmetic.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "frostcal_star", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the Cold Ledger's founding page.\n\nFrostcal turned to it and found the trouble: the very first remedy had been measured, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, as 'a good pinch' \u2014 and a town that keeps its doses in a book founded on 'a good pinch' will heal true for a thousand years and then, all at once, warm into vagueness together. The thaw had spread from that one soft measure down every dose that followed, out of the book and into the streets.\n\nFrostcal scraped away the good pinch. With the Exact Measure she wrote the true dose, to the grain, and fixed it with the salt so it could never run warm again. No good pinch.\n\nThe whole Ledger seemed to frost over, clean and sharp. Somewhere across the town, a melted dose-post refroze, very quietly, to exactly three grains.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Rime held a feast in the cold clear air, every dish measured to the guest, which the town considered the highest honour it had. The mayor gave a speech of exactly the right length, the Keeper of the Ledger was seen to almost thaw, and the gate-keeper waived the toll a second historic time.\n\nAt the end they gave Frostcal the Steady Draught: a remedy that never overshoots and never rebounds, measured true and fixed forever. She went home under a sky sharp with stars, counting them once each, exactly, and finding, for once, that the count of everything came out whole.",
        midText: "Rime was grateful in a slightly runny way \u2014 the feast came out a touch uneven and the mayor's speech drifted off its measure twice. Still, the doses set true, the scales held, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Keeper pressed the Steady Draught into Frostcal's cold hands: a remedy that never overshoots. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes are.' Frostcal went home already planning an exacter attempt, because a thing worth measuring true is worth measuring again.",
        grantItem: "frostcal_draught" },
    },
  },
  loopy: {
    id: "loopy",
    title: "The Argument That Broke",
    tagline: "In Coilhaven the great Spiral of Reason has come uncoiled, and nothing follows from anything any more. Only a snail who thinks in careful loops can wind it back.",
    items: [
      { id: "loopy_shell",  name: "The Syllogism Shell", stat: 2, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Logic. Won with a sharp Logic check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "loopy_tally",  name: "The Countertally",    stat: 0, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "loopy_spiral", name: "The Golden Spiral",   stat: 1, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Geometry. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { premise: "A True Premise", thread: "A Thread of Reason", stamp: "The Q.E.D. Stamp" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🐌", text: "Loopy thought slowly, which people mistook for thinking badly right up until the moment Loopy was proved right, whereupon they mistook it for luck. In fact Loopy thought in loops: take a thing you know, and a thing that follows from it, and a thing that follows from THAT, winding the argument round and round its shell until the conclusion sat snug at the centre where nothing could argue it out again.\n\nSo when the summons spiralled in from Coilhaven \u2014 the city built around the great Spiral of Reason, where every law and every truth is one long chain of because \u2014 saying the Spiral had come UNCOILED overnight, that nothing followed from anything and the courts were handing down verdicts by coin-toss, Loopy set out at once. At Loopy's pace, which is to say: eventually, and unstoppably.\n\n'If nothing follows,' said Loopy, 'then anything can. And anything is a very dangerous thing to let into a city. Come along. Mind the pace.'", next: "syllogism_check" },
      syllogism_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83e\udde9", stat: 2, dc: 10,
        text: "A scholar's dropped satchel had scattered its argument-cards along the verge, most of them cut loose from their reasoning and now saying nothing at all. But one small chain of cards still held together, premise leading to premise leading to a true conclusion, and coiled inside it lay something worth the keeping.\n\nThe trick was to follow the chain: only one arrangement of the cards made every step follow honestly from the last, and only that arrangement revealed the prize. Pick a broken chain and it would lead you nowhere and shut.\n\nSomebody with good Logic had better follow the reasoning to its centre.",
        success: "found_shell", fail: "syllogism_miss" },
      syllogism_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf43", text: "The chains all looked plausible if you didn't check every link, which is exactly how bad arguments win. Loopy followed one a step too fast, took a because that didn't, and the chain came apart in the paw and led, as broken chains do, precisely nowhere.\n\n'It will coil itself up again somewhere true,' said Loopy, without much conviction. (The Syllogism Shell stays lost this time. Another run may follow the reasoning to it.)", next: "post_fight" },
      found_shell: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "loopy_shell", text: "Link by link the true chain held, each because bearing the weight of the next, and at its centre it opened on the prize: the Syllogism Shell, a small spiral shell that hummed, faintly, whenever a thought inside it reached a sound conclusion.\n\nSome scholar had reasoned their way to this and then lost the thread and never found the chain again. Loopy did, slowly, and the shell settled into the curve of Loopy's own, one spiral nesting in another, and both of them, quietly, correct.\n\nThe Syllogism Shell is yours. (+1 Logic, kept forever.)", next: "post_fight" },
      post_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It had wrapped itself around the wayside proof-post \u2014 the public marker that reasons out which turning leads where \u2014 a Non-Sequitur, small and grey, patiently snipping the becauses out of the argument so the post now read 'LEFT FOR COILHAVEN, THEREFORE FISH'.\n\nLoopy stopped, which for Loopy took a moment to become visible. A proof-post whose reasons don't connect is how a traveller ends up, logically, in a hedge.\n\n'That does not follow,' said Loopy, with the quiet menace of a creature to whom this is the gravest possible accusation. The Non-Sequitur, in reply, followed from nothing harder.",
        enemy: { name: "The Non-Sequitur", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed and one link of the argument snapped true again, a because re-connecting with an almost audible click as a slice of the Non-Sequitur simply stopped making sense and dissolved.",
          strikeText: "The Non-Sequitur loosed a fact from its reason, and for a moment nothing anyone knew led anywhere. Somebody concluded, mid-step, that the ground was optional, and tested it.",
          defeatText: "The Non-Sequitur reached a conclusion that followed from nothing whatsoever, including itself, and vanished up its own logic. The proof-post read LEFT FOR COILHAVEN, BECAUSE THAT IS WHERE THE ROAD GOES, which was at least honest work." },
        victory: "post_win", retreat: "post_retreat" },
      post_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Loopy checked the post's reasoning link by link. Sound throughout. 'Second one this week,' Loopy observed. First the town by-laws gone non-sequitur at home, now a proof-post concluding fish.\n\nSomething was abroad snipping the becauses out of the world, and Coilhaven, which was nothing BUT becauses wound into a spiral, sat right at the end of its trail.", next: "crossroads" },
      post_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "A step back, Loopy reasoned, does not break the chain of one's resolve provided one keeps hold of why one is stepping. They kept hold of it, going round by the long verge, which cost a slow half-hour and left the morning feeling faintly like a premise nobody had checked.\n\nBehind them the Non-Sequitur went back to snipping reasons. Loopy did not look back. Looking back, Loopy held, is only sound if it follows from looking forward first.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road forked three ways under a signpost whose reasoning had come badly unlinked.\n\nThe left arm read THE DEBATE ROW, where they argue for coin and dare you to out-reason them. The middle arm read THE STEPPING-STONES OF THEN, a chain of stones you cross in strict sequence. The right arm read THE MAZE OF MAYBE, of which the sign said only: NOTHING FOLLOWS HERE. ALL PATHS FORK. MIND YOUR REASONS.\n\nSomewhere down each road, the scholars swore, a True Premise waited to be earned \u2014 a starting-point you could actually build on. A true premise, like a true friend, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Debate Row", next: "row", preview: "Out-reason a sophist at their own game. A hard logic puzzle guards the True Premise." },
        { label: "Cross the Stepping-Stones of Then", next: "bridge", preview: "Cross the stones in strict sequence. A dice challenge guards the True Premise." },
        { label: "Enter the Maze of Maybe", next: "fog", preview: "The forking ground where nothing follows. A Muddlehead guards the True Premise. This one is a fight." },
      ] },
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Debate Row rang with argument-for-hire. 'WATERTIGHT LOGIC!' cried one sophist, over a case full of holes. 'EVERY CONCLUSION GUARANTEED!' cried another, guaranteeing nothing.\n\nAt the last stall, held down by a paperweight of pure reason, lay a True Premise, plain and solid and quietly load-bearing. The master sophist, a magpie with a debating-badge on one wing and a con in the other, watched Loopy notice it and smiled the smile of a purse being reasoned open.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The premise? That's the good load-bearing stock,' said the magpie. 'Out-reason me and it's yours, spiral-brain. A snail sets out to climb a ten-metre well. Each DAY it climbs three metres; each NIGHT it slips back two. On which day does it FIRST reach the top? Answer true, or you argue by my rates, which never conclude in your favour.'", question: "A snail climbs a 10-metre well. Each day it climbs 3 m; each night it slips back 2 m, ending each full day 1 m higher. On which day does it FIRST reach the top?", options: ["7", "8", "9", "10", "11"], answer: 1,
        hint: "The net gain is only 1 m per full day \u2014 BUT the snail reaches the top DURING a day's climb, before it can slip back. Track its height at the START of each day's climb.",
        solution: "At the start of each day the snail is 0, 1, 2, ... metres up (one more each morning). It climbs 3 m during the day. It first touches 10 m when its morning start plus 3 reaches 10, i.e. start = 7, which is the morning of day 8. So it escapes on day 8, before that night's slip.",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "premise", text: "The magpie followed the reasoning, tried to find the slip in it, slipped instead, and conceded 'day eight' with the sour face of a debater out-debated.\n\n'The last climb doesn't count backward. Sound. You'd argue me out of my own stall. Take the premise. Take it before I concede anything else.'\n\nComponent found: A True Premise.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The magpie walked the true reasoning through, day by day, helpfully and at debating rates. Its rates being what they were, the argument cost every coin Loopy carried and the premise stayed under its paperweight.\n\n'Come back with a tighter chain,' the magpie advised. 'Tight reasoning argues cheaper, in this trade.'", next: "converge" },
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Stepping-Stones of Then were a chain of stones across the gorge, each carved with a step of an argument, and you crossed them, said a heron, in strict order \u2014 this one, THEN that one, because the second followed from the first. Step out of sequence and the logic broke and the stone with it.\n\nLoopy, who never did anything out of sequence, studied the chain. There was nothing for it but to follow the reasoning across.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 10, target: 8, text: "The gorge wind kept snatching the stones out of sequence, and only a strong, lucky read keeps the chain in order. Roll 8 or more on a ten-sided die to hold the sequence across. The heron adjudicates and catches nothing.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 8 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["1/5", "3/10", "2/5", "1/2"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 8, 9 and 10 hold the sequence \u2014 that is 3 outcomes out of 10, so 3/10. Hard odds over a long drop, so you'll want the edge. Reading the odds first steadies your footing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "premise", text: "Loopy held the sequence stone by stone, each step following the last, and reached the far side without a broken link, to a let-down croak from the heron. Wedged under the last stone, dropped by some crosser who lost the thread, lay a True Premise, plain and solid and load-bearing.\n\nComponent found: A True Premise.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The gorge wind flipped two stones out of order and Loopy, following honestly, followed them wrong; a broken sequence is a broken bridge. The gorge took Loopy for a slow tumbling descent to a ledge below, unhurt and out of order.\n\nWhatever the last stone kept, it kept. Some crossings give one sound sequence and no more.", next: "converge" },
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Maze of Maybe forked wherever you looked. Every path split into two, and each of those into two more, none of them following from the last, so that reasoning your way through was like arguing with a creature that changes its premise every time you blink.\n\nAt its heart, on a fork within a fork, something big was going along a wall of sound arguments cutting the becauses out of each and cackling at the wreckage. A Muddlehead: the Muddle poured into the shape of a bad-faith debater. And balanced on its brow, worn like a trophy by a thing that had never followed anything, sat a True Premise, stubbornly still meaning something.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Muddlehead turned without alarm. Things shaped like bad-faith argument never worry; worrying would require a position.\n\n'WHY THOUGH,' it said, in a voice like a goalpost being moved. 'DOESN'T FOLLOW. PROVE IT. NOTHING PROVES ANYTHING. RELAX.'\n\n'Some things prove things,' said Loopy, and drew into the mouth of the shell, which is snail for squaring up, only rounder and more considered.",
        enemy: { name: "The Muddlehead", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck and a whole argument snapped back into sound order, because leading to because \u2014 a slab of the Muddlehead simply failed to follow and fell away.",
          strikeText: "The Muddlehead moved its premise mid-sentence, and for a moment nothing anyone believed led anywhere useful. Somebody deduced their footing away entirely.",
          defeatText: "The Muddlehead demanded proof that proof exists, received it, could not argue with it, and had by its own rules to conclude it did not itself follow \u2014 and so, logically, ceased. The True Premise rolled off its brow and came to rest, still true." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "premise", text: "Loopy retrieved the True Premise and, unable to leave sound arguments in ruins, spent a patient while re-linking the wall's severed becauses until the reasoning ran unbroken again. A restored argument is a small, quiet victory over everything that says nothing follows.\n\nComponent found: A True Premise.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The maze sided with the Muddlehead, forking faster the harder they reasoned, and drove the company back to solid ground, out of patience and low on cheer. They took the one path that actually followed to somewhere.\n\nBehind them the Muddlehead went back to its wall, trophy balanced, cutting the soundest arguments first because they resist most sweetly. Loopy did not look back. Looking back at a Muddlehead only gives it a new premise to abandon.", next: "converge" },
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again at a milestone reading COILHAVEN: 5, and the 5 was reasoned out beneath it in a tidy chain of becauses that actually held, which everyone found steadying.\n\nAhead the land wound down toward the Reasoning Falls, one clean unbroken cascade of logic-water, the last argument still following from itself, the scholars said, between here and whatever had uncoiled Coilhaven.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 6, target: 3, text: "The Reasoning Falls ran fast over stepping-stones, most sound, a couple that only seemed to follow. Roll 3 or more on a six-sided die to reason your way across at speed. The current is listening with interest.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 3 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 3, 4, 5 and 6 all follow soundly \u2014 that is 4 outcomes out of 6, and 4/6 = 2/3. Good odds, though a third of the time the reasoning gives way. Reading the odds first steadies your crossing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "thread_good", fail: "thread_miss" },
      thread_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "thread", text: "Loopy crossed stone to sound stone, each following the last, and there in an eddy the logic-water had polished for a century lay a Thread of Reason \u2014 the red thread you follow through any argument to be sure it never doubles back or drops a link.\n\nComponent found: A Thread of Reason.", next: "count_check" },
      thread_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "One stone only seemed to follow; a plausible non-sequitur is the hardest kind to catch mid-stride. Loopy trusted it, the reasoning gave, and the Falls carried Loopy for a brisk logical tumble to the shallows.\n\nWhatever the eddy held, it kept. The run went on, a shade more muddled.", next: "count_check" },
      count_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83e\uddee", stat: 0, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found a travelling accountant at her fire, glaring at a column of figures.\n\n'It's the RECKONING, not the reasoning, that's beaten me tonight,' she said. 'A tally of tolls and tithes that ought to sum the same forwards and backwards, and it won't. It's not a logic job, it's an arithmetic job \u2014 pure counting, done exactly. Somebody quick with numbers should tot it.'\n\nShe looked up hopefully. An accountant's favour keeps its value.",
        success: "count_solved", fail: "count_stumped" },
      count_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udcb0", bonusStars: 2, text: "It came out clean once totalled in careful order, forwards to check the backwards, every figure carried and none dropped. The column summed the same both ways, which pleased the accountant more than the money did.\n\nShe beamed and pressed a parcel of provisions on the company, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      count_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\uddfe", text: "The column defeated everyone; it summed one way going down and another coming up, and no one could find the dropped figure, until the accountant folded the ledger with the patience of one long at war with her own arithmetic.\n\n'Never mind,' she said kindly. 'It's been almost-right for years. A night more won't hurt.' The fire ticked. Nobody quite met an eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The accountant's fire was warm and the dark beyond it was not.\n\n'Coilhaven's an hour on,' she said. 'Carry my urgent accounts through the night and I'll owe you \u2014 accountants pay their debts, to the penny. Or bed down here and go safe at dawn. Night road's chancy, chancier than usual, with things about that leave a body unable to say why it did anything.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll, but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the provisions are surprisingly good." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night road forked in the dark where the logic had frayed. With the accountant's lantern showing which turnings actually followed, you go with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the true path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Loopy followed the sound turnings through the frayed dark and brought the company to Coilhaven's gate before moonrise, filed the accountant's ledgers in the crooked box, and pocketed the favour: three bright stars and a supper that reasoned a whole heart's worth of resolve back into everyone.\n\nA risk follows soundly, Loopy held, once the odds are reasoned out and the odds have agreed to be reasoned. Bonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The frayed dark won. Loopy took a turning that seemed to follow into a ditch shaped exactly like overconfidence, the ledgers got sodden, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of condition.\n\nThe company trudged the last mile in a pointed silence. Good odds still lose sometimes, Loopy reflected. That is precisely what makes them odds and not conclusions.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Loopy slept coiled in the shell, which is the most reasonable way to sleep, and woke to a morning that followed clearly from the night before. The provisions were, on reflection, a delight.\n\nThe walk took the promised hour, at Loopy's pace, which meant leaving a little early, which Loopy found deeply logical. Sometimes the sound move is the slow one. Expected value, Loopy told the company, is in no hurry.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Coilhaven's gate stood in a spiral arch, taking its old toll of one sound answer per traveller. This morning the arch was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Muddlehead footpads had strung an argument across it that proved, at length and to nobody's satisfaction, that the toll was ALL YER REASONS. Behind her bolted shutters the gate-keeper glared like a kettle at the boil.\n\n'Your proof has a hole in step two,' said Loopy, 'and step four, and it concludes fish,' which as war-cries go was at least a peer review.",
        enemy: { name: "Muddlehead Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck and one footpad's argument was forced to follow soundly to its own end, which was nowhere; it concluded itself out of existence.",
          strikeText: "A footpad shifted its premise mid-swing and somebody in the company paid a toll of reasons they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad demanded to know why it should stop existing, was shown exactly why, could not fault the logic, and stopped. The argument fell over. Nobody rebuilt it." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The gate-keeper unbolted shutters, door and scowl in order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, kicking the fallen argument aside. 'First in an age. But nobody passes without answering \u2014 gate's rules, and soundest of all on a day the whole city's stopped following.' She slid across a slate worn smooth by generations of because.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The company fell back with the footpads' circular laughter behind, and spent an exasperating quarter-hour in a side-lane that led, non-sequitur by non-sequitur, everywhere but out.\n\nThey reached the gate by the side wicket, footsore and simmering. The keeper took stock of them and said nothing. 'Bad morning,' she allowed. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧠", text: "The slate was old, the chalk worn, the puzzle in the keeper's careful hand. 'Take your time,' she said, timing it.", question: "Three snails race. Ada does not finish last. Bo does not finish first. Cy finishes ahead of Ada. In what order do they finish, from first to last?", options: ["Cy, Ada, Bo", "Ada, Cy, Bo", "Cy, Bo, Ada", "Bo, Cy, Ada", "Ada, Bo, Cy"], answer: 0,
        hint: "Start with the strongest clue: Cy finishes ahead of Ada, so Cy is before Ada. Then use 'Ada is not last' to place Ada, and check Bo is not first.",
        solution: "Cy is ahead of Ada, and Ada is not last, so Ada must be in the middle and Cy first \u2014 which leaves Bo last. Check: Bo is not first \u2713, Ada is not last \u2713, Cy beats Ada \u2713. Order: Cy, Ada, Bo.",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the reasoning, sniffed her sniff of respect, and stamped Loopy SOUND in one firm letter.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, nearly warm, 'and hurry. The Spiral's most of the way uncoiled, and a city whose every law is one long because becomes, without it, one long anything-goes. From what the crows say, it's most of the way to anything.'", next: "coilhaven" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, not unkindly, turned the slate and walked the deduction through clue by clue, then had Loopy say it back twice, because twice is where knowing coils tight.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the company through the side wicket, the long way, past the archive, who could not believe their luck twice in a morning.", next: "coilhaven" },
      coilhaven: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Coilhaven at last: a city wound in a single vast spiral, street following street inward to the Court of Reason at the centre, built by folk who held that a city is only an argument you can live in, and had better be a sound one.\n\nBut the argument was breaking. A grey unravelling ran along the spiral, worst at the Court, where the great Spiral of Reason held every law in one unbroken chain of because \u2014 and grey ribbons of Muddle poured from its doors, snipping the links, so verdicts came by coin-toss and nobody could say why anything was so. Citizens stood in doorways unable to remember the reason for their own front steps.\n\nLoopy pressed inward, faster, which is to say at a slightly less deliberate crawl.", next: "spiral_check" },
      spiral_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 4, dc: 12,
        text: "The Court's antechamber was a storm of loose argument-links whirling in the draught, and each had to be snatched from the air and clipped back into its chain \u2014 this because before that therefore \u2014 before it blew away and the whole proof was lost. There was no time to reason it SLOWLY; the links were scattering as fast as thought.\n\nIt was Loopy's own art, but at a speed Loopy was not built for: catch, place, catch, place.\n\nSomebody quick had better re-link the chain before it blows apart.",
        success: "spiral_quick", fail: "spiral_slow" },
      spiral_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "Loopy moved faster than Loopy had ever moved, which was still not fast but was, this once, fast ENOUGH \u2014 catch, clip, catch, clip \u2014 and the chain held, link to link, the proof saved by a snail hurrying for the first time in its life.\n\nUp at the bench the Chief Reasoner made a small approving hum, which by her standards was an ovation.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      spiral_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf42", text: "The links scattered faster than even Loopy could clip them, and it took the better part of an hour to save even half the chain. By the end the light had failed and the unravelling had crept a shade further along the Spiral, as if handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔢", text: "The Chief Reasoner stood at a bench shaped like the inward turn of a shell. 'Prove you can see where a pattern is GOING,' she said, in a voice like a proof reaching its end, 'and you may touch the Spiral. The trouble is within. But the truth is here, and the truth is only for those who can follow it out.'", question: "Look at the pattern 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, 42, ... where each term is the previous plus the next even number. What is the 10th term?", options: ["90", "100", "110", "120", "132"], answer: 2,
        hint: "The differences go 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, ... each term is n \u00d7 (n + 1): 1\u00d72 = 2, 2\u00d73 = 6, 3\u00d74 = 12. What is the 10th?",
        solution: "Each term is n \u00d7 (n + 1): the 1st is 1\u00d72 = 2, the 2nd 2\u00d73 = 6, the 3rd 3\u00d74 = 12, and so on. The 10th term is 10 \u00d7 11 = 110.",
        good: "stamp_good", mid: "stamp_mid" },
      stamp_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "stamp", text: "The Chief Reasoner's careful face reached a conclusion very like delight.\n\nFrom a niche at the shell's heart \u2014 'the founder's own stamp, for a mind that can follow a pattern to its end, which is you' \u2014 she lifted the Q.E.D. Stamp: the seal pressed at the close of every sound proof in Coilhaven's history. 'It has never once sealed a thing that did not follow,' she said, laying it in Loopy's keeping. 'It won't mind sealing something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Q.E.D. Stamp.", next: "core_fight" },
      stamp_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Chief Reasoner breathed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people add the wrong difference, and chalked the pattern out term by term for Loopy to follow.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, quoting somebody. But the Q.E.D. Stamp stayed in its niche, for a mind that could follow the pattern out unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "At the Spiral's heart, something vast was coiled the wrong way round the founding chain, pulling its links apart one because at a time. The Great Non-Sequitur was bigger than its footpads and greyer than its Muddleheads, and everywhere it uncoiled, the city's reasons came loose and drifted free of their conclusions.\n\nIt turned two eyes that followed from nothing on the company at the stair.\n\n'WHY,' it said, and did not make it a question. 'NOTHING FOLLOWS. NOTHING NEED. LET IT ALL COME LOOSE.'\n\n'Then let me show you what follows from THAT,' said Loopy, and set the shell for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Great Non-Sequitur", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck and a whole coil of the founding chain snapped back into sound order, because gripping because \u2014 the Great Non-Sequitur lost its hold on a span and reeled.",
          strikeText: "The Great Non-Sequitur uncoiled a length of reason, and for a breath nothing in the Court led anywhere; somebody concluded the floor away and met it regardless.",
          defeatText: "The Great Non-Sequitur, pressed to say what followed from its own existence, found that nothing did, including it, and by that very logic came apart. Its last word was 'THEREFOR\u2014', which pleased Loopy, since it never reached the conclusion." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Coil by coil the Spiral wound back true, links gripping links all along its length, and across Coilhaven the reasons reconnected to their conclusions, verdicts finding their becauses, citizens remembering why. The grey ribbons stopped pouring. Outside, a coin-toss verdict rewound itself into an argument that actually held.\n\nThe Chief Reasoner appeared at the bench, surveyed the re-coiled Spiral, and inclined her head one exact degree, which the city agreed followed soundly from the occasion.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Great Non-Sequitur uncoiled again and so much reason came loose that the doorway was the only thing that still followed from anything, so the company took it.\n\nThey rallied in a well-reasoned alcove, wrung out and furious and, after a short and logical vote, fortified with the accountant's provisions. Then they came back by the inner stair while the thing uncoiled the founding chain below. Not every fight is won by winning it, Loopy noted. Some are won by keeping hold of the thread and arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "At the Spiral's heart glowed the workshop of Cogent, maker of true things, who could re-link a broken argument from twenty paces and had once mended a shattered conclusion, though she wouldn't dwell on it.\n\n'A Golden Spiral, is it?' she said, over spectacles ground to follow the light. 'Not made one since the founders' day. A True Premise, a Thread of Reason, the Q.E.D. Stamp \u2014 bring me all three and I'll wind you a spiral that always comes out where it means to.'",
        forged: "The forge roared its agreement. Cogent set the True Premise at the centre, wound the Thread of Reason outward in the one perfect curve, and sealed the whole with the Q.E.D. Stamp.\n\nWith the Golden Spiral, Loopy found it could see the SHAPE of a sound argument \u2014 how it should curve, where it should close, whether its parts were in true proportion \u2014 and could lay out any proof so cleanly the eye followed it like a road. The geometry of reasoning, it turns out, is half of getting it right.\n\nThe Golden Spiral is yours! (+1 Geometry, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Cogent laid the components out and counted them, which followed quickly.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Spiral wants all three or it's a wish that won't close. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the logic.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "loopy_spiral", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the founding link at the heart of the Spiral.\n\nLoopy wound inward to it and found the trouble: the very first because had been forged, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, as 'well, obviously' \u2014 and a city whose every law hangs from a founding because of 'well, obviously' will reason true for a thousand years and then, all at once, discover it never checked the first link at all. The break had spread from that one lazy step out through every argument that leaned on it, out of the Court and into the streets.\n\nLoopy prised out the well, obviously, followed the true reason to its root with the Thread, set it sound, and sealed it with the Stamp. No obviously.\n\nThe whole Spiral seemed to draw tight and hum. Somewhere out in the city, a verdict that had come by coin-toss quietly rewound itself into a reason.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Coilhaven held a feast that followed, course by course, from the one before, which the city considered the highest honour reason could pay a guest. The mayor gave a speech that reached a conclusion, the Chief Reasoner was seen to almost laugh, and the gate-keeper waived the toll a second historic time, and could prove it was justified.\n\nAt the end they gave Loopy the Countertally: the reckoning-frame that had counted true every citizen and coin in the city's founding, so no argument built on it could ever come up a figure short. Loopy went home at its own pace, in no hurry at all, following the road because the road, soundly, followed to home.",
        midText: "Coilhaven was grateful in a slightly circular way \u2014 the feast doubled back on itself once and the mayor's speech reached its conclusion twice. Still, the Spiral held, the laws followed, the verdicts had reasons, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Chief Reasoner pressed the Countertally into Loopy's keeping: the founding reckoning-frame. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes are.' Loopy went home already reasoning out a tighter attempt, because a thing worth concluding is worth concluding again.",
        grantItem: "loopy_tally" },
    },
  },
  tritip: {
    id: "tritip",
    title: "The City That Lost Its Bearings",
    tagline: "Trigon fixes every street by sighting three towers, and the sightlines have gone to fog. Only the sharpest triangle alive can re-fix the city before it forgets where it is.",
    items: [
      { id: "tritip_eye",   name: "The Theodolite Eye", stat: 1, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Geometry. Won with a sharp Geometry check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "tritip_book",  name: "The Bearing-Book",   stat: 2, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "tritip_line",  name: "The Swift Sightline", stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { alidade: "A Brass Alidade", chain: "A Surveyor's Chain", trigstone: "The Founding Trig-Stone" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🔺", text: "Tritip had three sides, three points and no patience for anything blunt. A triangle, Tritip held, is the only honest shape: the one figure that cannot be pushed out of true without breaking, which is why bridges are full of them and cowards are not. Give Tritip three fixed points and it could tell you exactly where a fourth thing stood, to the inch, from a mile off. This is called triangulation, and Tritip considered it the finest thing three straight lines had ever done together.\n\nSo when the alarm sighted in from Trigon \u2014 the surveyor-city where every street's position is fixed by sighting the three great towers, so nothing is ever lost \u2014 that the sightlines had gone to fog overnight and the city was, impossibly, misplacing its own streets, Tritip sharpened all three points and went.\n\n'A city that can't sight its towers,' said Tritip, 'is a city that will wake up somewhere it didn't mean to be. Bring the good instruments. We are going to fix its position by force if we must.'", next: "sight_check" },
      sight_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udcd0", stat: 1, dc: 10,
        text: "A surveyor's overturned pack had spilt its sighting-tools across the road, and among the bent sights and lying levels lay one true instrument worth the stooping \u2014 if you could tell it from the frauds.\n\nThe test was the angles: a true sight held its cross-hairs at a perfect ninety, so the bearings it read came out square; the frauds sat a degree off, the kind of error that puts a whole street in the wrong place. Choose a bent one and every position it fixed would drift.\n\nSomebody with an eye for Geometry had better pick the true sight.",
        success: "found_eye", fail: "sight_miss" },
      sight_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\udded", text: "The cross-hairs all looked square to a hurried glance, which is how bad sights survive. Tritip chose one a degree off, felt the drift a beat too late, and by the time the surveyor's runner had gathered the pack and gone, the true sight had gone with it.\n\n'It will fix somebody's position wrongly, and they will build a wall a foot from where they meant,' said Tritip grimly. (The Theodolite Eye stays lost this time. Another run may find it.)", next: "post_fight" },
      found_eye: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "tritip_eye", text: "The true sight declared itself the instant Tritip squinted through it: cross-hairs dead square, the far tower snapping into a point so exact it could be trusted with a life. The Theodolite Eye, brass gone green with weather and honest to the last second of arc.\n\nSome surveyor had carried this and lost it and never fixed a true bearing since. Tritip did, at once. The Eye rode against Tritip's sharpest point like a blade returned to a swordsmith.\n\nThe Theodolite Eye is yours. (+1 Geometry, kept forever.)", next: "post_fight" },
      post_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It had draped itself over the parish trig-post \u2014 the sighting-marker every surveyor takes a bearing from \u2014 a Blunt, small and grey, patiently dulling the post's sharp sighting-notch into a soft grey nub you couldn't take a true angle on.\n\nTritip levelled all three points. A trig-post you can't sight is how a whole survey drifts a degree, and a degree, a mile off, is a disaster.\n\n'That notch was sharp this morning,' said Tritip, in the tone of a blade noticing rust. The Blunt, unrepentant, went blunter.",
        enemy: { name: "The Trig-Post Blunt", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer struck to a point, and a whole edge of the Blunt was forced sharp again, keen for one clean instant, before it flaked away.",
          strikeText: "The Blunt smeared the world soft, and for a moment no angle in sight would hold true; somebody sighted a firm footing that had gone as vague as everything else, and missed it.",
          defeatText: "The Blunt dulled itself past the point of having any point at all, which is the end of every Blunt, and smeared into nothing. The trig-post's notch stood sharp again, keen enough to hang a whole city's bearings on." },
        victory: "post_win", retreat: "post_retreat" },
      post_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Tritip took a test bearing off the re-sharpened post. True to the second. 'Second one this week,' Tritip noted. First the boundary sights gone soft at home, now a parish trig-post dulled to a nub.\n\nSomething was travelling the country blunting the sharp things a survey depends on, and Trigon, whose whole existence hung on sharp bearings, sat right at the end of its line.", next: "crossroads" },
      post_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "A sharp step back, Tritip maintained, is still an advance if you take the bearing first. They took the bearing and the low road, losing a slow half-hour and a degree of confidence in the ground's honesty.\n\nBehind them the Blunt went back to dulling the post. Tritip did not look back. Looking back, Tritip held, only gives a Blunt your other edge to work on.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road forked three ways under a signpost whose arms had been dulled to vague grey paddles.\n\nThe left arm read THE SURVEYORS' ROW, where they sell instruments and dare you to fault the reading. The middle arm read THE TRIANGLE BRIDGE, a truss of girders you cross by the sound ones. The right arm read THE FLATLANDS, of which the sign said only: NO ANGLES HERE. ALL POINTS DULLED. MIND YOUR BEARINGS.\n\nSomewhere down each road, the surveyors swore, a Brass Alidade waited to be earned \u2014 the sighting-rule a bearing is read along. A true alidade, like a true friend, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Surveyors' Row", next: "row", preview: "Out-reckon an instrument-seller at the angles. A hard triangle puzzle guards the Alidade." },
        { label: "Cross the Triangle Bridge", next: "bridge", preview: "Cross the truss by the sound girders. A dice challenge guards the Alidade." },
        { label: "Cross the Flatlands", next: "fog", preview: "The dulled ground where no angle holds. A Rounder guards the Alidade. This one is a fight." },
      ] },
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Surveyors' Row rang with tapping rules and tall claims. 'TRUE TO THE MINUTE!' cried one seller, over a sight that read to the vaguely. 'NEVER A DEGREE OUT!' cried another, out by several.\n\nAt the last stall, on a velvet cloth, lay a Brass Alidade, its edge a perfect straight line. The master seller, a stoat with a protractor in one paw and a swindle in the other, watched Tritip notice it and smiled the smile of a purse being sighted.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The alidade? Finest edge in the Row,' said the stoat. 'Settle a surveyor's wager, points. I'm laying out a triangular plot whose three angles come in the ratio two to three to four. Tell me the size of the LARGEST angle and the rule is yours. Miss it and you buy at Row rates, which I set at a generous angle to your purse.'", question: "The three angles of a triangle are in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. What is the size of the largest angle?", options: ["60\u00b0", "72\u00b0", "80\u00b0", "90\u00b0", "100\u00b0"], answer: 2,
        hint: "The three angles of any triangle add to 180\u00b0. The ratio 2 : 3 : 4 means 2 + 3 + 4 = 9 equal parts make up that 180\u00b0. Find one part first.",
        solution: "The parts total 2 + 3 + 4 = 9, and the angles total 180\u00b0, so one part is 180 \u00f7 9 = 20\u00b0. The largest angle is 4 parts: 4 \u00d7 20\u00b0 = 80\u00b0.",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "alidade", text: "The stoat checked it on its protractor, tried to find a degree of slack, found none, and conceded eighty degrees with the grace of a creature that would rather have overcharged.\n\n'Dead on the angle. You'd survey me out of my own stall. Take the alidade. Take it before I make you a partner.'\n\nComponent found: A Brass Alidade.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The stoat worked the true angle out on its slate, helpfully and by the minute of arc. Row rates being Row rates, the plot took every coin Tritip carried and the alidade stayed on its velvet.\n\n'Come back with a sharper eye,' the stoat advised. 'Sharp reads cheaper than blunt, in this trade.'", next: "converge" },
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Triangle Bridge was a truss of iron triangles across the gorge, and you crossed it, said a wagtail, by trusting the sound girders. The true triangles held any weight; the ones the Blunt had softened only looked as if they would, and the gorge below was long and unforgiving.\n\nTritip, who loved a truss the way others love a sunset, studied the ironwork. There was nothing for it but to read the sound triangles and go.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 10, target: 5, text: "About half the girders still ring true. Roll 5 or more on a ten-sided die to pick the sound triangles and cross. The wagtail keeps score from a safe rail.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "3/5", "2/5", "7/10"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10 all find sound iron \u2014 that is 6 outcomes out of 10, and 6/10 = 3/5. Better than even, but not by much over a long drop. Reading the odds first steadies your footing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "alidade", text: "Tritip read the truss triangle by triangle and crossed on the sound iron alone, to a disappointed flick from the wagtail. Wedged in a joint on the far side, dropped by some crosser who trusted a soft girder, lay a Brass Alidade, its edge still perfectly straight.\n\nComponent found: A Brass Alidade.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "One girder rang almost true and wasn't; a softened triangle is a subtle liar. Tritip trusted it, the joint gave, and the gorge took Tritip for a jarring slide to a ledge below.\n\nWhatever the far joint held, it kept. Some crossings give one sound reading and no more.", next: "converge" },
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Flatlands had been dulled until nothing in them held an angle: fences met at vague grey smears, the horizon itself had lost its edge, and Tritip's own three points began, alarmingly, to feel soft.\n\nAt the centre, on a mound worn round, something big was going along a line of survey-markers thumbing each sharp point into a useless nub and humming grey. A Rounder: the Muddle poured into the shape of pure bluntness. And balanced on its smooth head, worn like a crown by a thing that had never had a point, sat a Brass Alidade, holding its straight edge against all the softness.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Rounder turned without alarm. Things shaped like bluntness rarely feel the point of anything.\n\n'SO SHARP,' it said, in a voice like a chisel gone soft. 'SO TIRING, ALL THOSE EDGES. LET ME TAKE THE POINTS OFF. YOU'LL FEEL SO MUCH ROUNDER.'\n\n'A triangle with no points is just a smudge with ideas,' said Tritip, and set all three points forward, which is triangle for drawing steel.",
        enemy: { name: "The Rounder", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck to a keen point and a whole face of the Rounder was forced sharp \u2014 it held an angle for one honest instant before shearing off.",
          strikeText: "The Rounder smeared the ground soft and a whole bearing came loose; one of the company sighted its footing, found the footing had no edge left to stand on, and dropped.",
          defeatText: "The Rounder rounded itself so thoroughly it had no point of any kind left, including a point in continuing, and smeared away. The Alidade slid off its smooth head and landed edge-down, sharp and true, as if relieved." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "alidade", text: "Tritip recovered the Alidade and, unable to leave a survey in ruins, spent a patient while re-cutting the dulled markers back to sharp true points so the next surveyor could sight them. A re-sharpened bearing is a small hard victory against everything gone vague.\n\nComponent found: A Brass Alidade.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Flatlands sided with the Rounder, softening every edge the harder they pushed, and drove the company back to ground that still held an angle, blunted in spirit and low on cheer. They took the long sharp way round.\n\nBehind them the Rounder went back to its markers, crown smooth, dulling the sharpest first because they resist most sweetly. Tritip did not look back. Looking back at a Rounder, Tritip knew, only lets it take the edge off your resolve.", next: "converge" },
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again at a milestone reading TRIGON: 5, the 5 cut so sharp it looked ready to draw blood, which everyone found bracing.\n\nAhead the land rose toward the Sighting Ridge, one clean sharp skyline you could take a true bearing off, the last un-blunted edge, the surveyors said, between here and whatever was fogging Trigon's sightlines.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 8, target: 5, text: "A landslip had left only a scatter of sound foot-holds across the ridge gap, half of them crumbling. Roll 5 or more on an eight-sided die to pick the firm ones and cross. The drop takes a professional interest.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair eight-sided die?", options: ["3/8", "1/2", "5/8", "1/4"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 5, 6, 7 and 8 all find firm rock \u2014 that is 4 outcomes out of 8, and 4/8 = 1/2. An even gamble over a hard drop. Reading the odds first steadies the crossing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "chain_good", fail: "chain_miss" },
      chain_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "chain", text: "Tritip read the firm rock and crossed clean, and there, coiled in a cleft where a surveyor had cached it a lifetime past, lay a Surveyor's Chain \u2014 the measuring chain, every link a known length, that has never once stretched or lied about a distance.\n\nComponent found: A Surveyor's Chain.", next: "logic_check" },
      chain_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "One hold looked firm and crumbled the instant it bore weight; loose rock is a patient liar. Tritip slid a jarring distance to a lower ledge, whole but out of position.\n\nWhatever the cleft held, it kept. The run went on, a shade more muddled.", next: "logic_check" },
      logic_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83e\udde9", stat: 2, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found a retired mapmaker at his fire, glaring at three overlapping surveys.\n\n'It's a REASONING knot, this,' he said. 'Three old maps, each with a clue about which is the true one and which two are copies, and only one story fits all three clues at once. It's not a chain-and-sight job, it's a thinking job. Somebody with good Logic should reason out which map to trust.'\n\nHe looked up hopefully. A mapmaker's favour is worth carrying, and worth more than most.",
        success: "logic_solved", fail: "logic_stumped" },
      logic_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udd13", bonusStars: 2, text: "It came clear the way good reasoning does: two clues alone left two maps in the running, but the third ruled one out cold, and once the three were laid side by side only the true map survived them all. It matched the land exactly.\n\nThe mapmaker beamed and pressed a parcel of provisions on the company, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      logic_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f", text: "The maps defeated everyone; each candidate satisfied two clues and broke the third, until the mapmaker rolled them up with the patience of a man long out-argued by his own charts.\n\n'Never mind,' he said kindly. 'They've disagreed for years. A night more won't settle them.' The fire ticked. Nobody quite met an eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The mapmaker's fire was warm and the dark beyond it was not.\n\n'Trigon's an hour on,' he said. 'Run my urgent charts through the night and I'll owe you \u2014 mapmakers pay their debts, and know exactly where you live. Or bed down here and go safe at dawn. Night road's chancy, chancier than usual, with things about that blunt a body's sense of direction.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll, but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the provisions are surprisingly good." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night road wandered where the bearings had fogged. With the mapmaker's lantern lighting the true line, you cross with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the true path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Tritip held the true line through the fogged dark and brought the company to Trigon's gate before moonrise, filed the mapmaker's charts in the crooked box, and pocketed the favour: three sharp-bright stars and a supper that fixed a whole heart's worth of nerve back into everyone.\n\nA risk is sound, Tritip held, once you've sighted the odds and the odds have agreed to hold their bearing. Bonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The fogged dark won. Tritip lost the true line at a fork shaped exactly like overconfidence, the charts got sodden, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of legibility.\n\nThe company trudged the last mile in a pointed silence. Good odds still lose sometimes, Tritip reflected. That is precisely what makes them odds and not bearings.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Tritip slept braced in a corner, which suited its geometry, and woke to a morning with a horizon sharp enough to sight off. The provisions proved unexpectedly excellent.\n\nThe walk took the promised hour exactly, which Tritip found deeply satisfactory. Sometimes the sharp move is the unhurried one. Expected value, Tritip told the company, keeps its edge either way.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Trigon's gate stood in a triangular arch, taking its old toll of one true answer per traveller. This morning the arch was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Rounder footpads had propped a dulled hoarding across it, daubed TOLE: ALL YER ANGLES. Behind her bolted shutters the gate-keeper glared like a kettle at the boil.\n\n'A hoarding with no corners,' said Tritip, 'won't hold a straight line long enough to stop anyone,' which as war-cries go was at least a structural observation.",
        enemy: { name: "Rounder Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck to a point, and one footpad's edges were forced sharp all at once \u2014 it held one keen angle and then flaked apart.",
          strikeText: "A dulled fist smeared the arch soft, and somebody in the company paid a toll of angles they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad tried to round away from the danger and, rounding, lost the last point it had, including the point of resisting, and smeared off. The hoarding sagged. Nobody re-propped it." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The gate-keeper unbolted shutters, door and scowl in order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, kicking the dulled hoarding aside. 'First in an age. But nobody passes without answering \u2014 gate's rules, and sharpest of all on a day the whole city's gone soft in the bearings.' She slid across a slate worn keen by generations.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The company fell back with the footpads' soft laughter behind, and spent a directionless quarter-hour in a side-lane whose corners had all been smeared away.\n\nThey reached the gate by the side wicket, scuffed and simmering. The keeper took stock of them and said nothing. 'Bad morning,' she allowed. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📐", text: "The slate was old, the chalk keen, the question in the keeper's exact hand. 'Take your time,' she said, timing it.", question: "A triangle has sides of length 9 cm, 12 cm and 15 cm. What is its area, in cm\u00b2?", options: ["54", "60", "66", "90", "108"], answer: 0,
        hint: "Check whether this is a right-angled triangle first: does 9\u00b2 + 12\u00b2 equal 15\u00b2? If so, two of the sides are the base and height.",
        solution: "9\u00b2 + 12\u00b2 = 81 + 144 = 225 = 15\u00b2, so it is right-angled with the two shorter sides at 90\u00b0. Area = \u00bd \u00d7 base \u00d7 height = \u00bd \u00d7 9 \u00d7 12 = 54 cm\u00b2.",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the working, sniffed her sniff of respect, and stamped Tritip TRUE in one sharp letter.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, nearly warm, 'and hurry. The Founding Trig-Stone's fogging over, and a city that loses the stone all its bearings start from loses itself entire. From what the crows say, it's most of the way to lost.'", next: "trigon" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, not unkindly, turned the slate and walked the Pythagoras through, then had Tritip say it back twice, because twice is where knowing sharpens.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the company through the side wicket, the long way, past the drawing-office, who could not believe their luck twice in a morning.", next: "trigon" },
      trigon: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Trigon at last: a city of sharp bastions and sighting-towers, where every street's exact position is fixed by triangulating the three great towers, laid out by folk who held that to be certain of where you stand is the beginning of everything else.\n\nBut the city was losing its place. A grey fog lay over the sightlines, thickest at the Central Tower, where the Founding Trig-Stone \u2014 the one fixed point every bearing in the city is measured from \u2014 sat at the heart of the survey. Grey ribbons of Muddle poured from the tower, blurring the sightlines, so streets drifted on the maps and householders argued about which road their own door opened onto.\n\nTritip advanced faster, all three points levelled.", next: "fix_check" },
      fix_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 0, dc: 12,
        text: "The Tower's survey-room was in chaos: the master bearing-cards had scattered in the draught, each a column of angle-figures that had to be caught, its degrees and minutes totalled true, and filed in order before the fog smeared its ink for good. There was no time to add them SLOWLY; the cards were smearing where they lay.\n\nIt was fast, exact arithmetic under pressure: catch, total the bearing, file, and be right the first time.\n\nSomebody quick with Arithmetic had better tot the bearings on the wing.",
        success: "fix_quick", fail: "fix_slow" },
      fix_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "A blur of snatching and totalling \u2014 degrees, minutes, carried clean, filed in order \u2014 and the bearing-cards went back true the instant before the fog could smear them, the whole survey saved on Tritip's fast exact reckoning.\n\nUp at the great table the Master Surveyor made a sharp approving click, which by her standards was rapture.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      fix_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf42", text: "The bearings smeared faster than they could be totalled, and it took the better part of an hour to save even the survivors, checking each twice. By the end the light had failed and the fog had crept a shade further over the sightlines, as if handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📐", text: "The Master Surveyor stood at a table ruled in fine angles. 'Prove you understand how angles inside a triangle behave,' she said, in a voice like a bearing read true, 'and you may touch the Trig-Stone. The trouble is below. But the truth is here, and the truth is only for those who can measure it.'", question: "In triangle ABC, angle A = 50\u00b0. The bisectors of angles B and C meet at point I inside the triangle. What is the size of angle BIC?", options: ["100\u00b0", "110\u00b0", "115\u00b0", "120\u00b0", "130\u00b0"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Angles B and C add to 180\u00b0 \u2212 50\u00b0 = 130\u00b0. The bisectors use only HALF of each, so in triangle BIC the two base angles add to half of 130\u00b0. Then use the angle sum of triangle BIC.",
        solution: "B + C = 180\u00b0 \u2212 50\u00b0 = 130\u00b0. The bisectors give half of each, so in triangle BIC the two angles at B and C add to 130\u00b0 \u00f7 2 = 65\u00b0. Therefore angle BIC = 180\u00b0 \u2212 65\u00b0 = 115\u00b0. (In general angle BIC = 90\u00b0 + A\u00f72.)",
        good: "trig_good", mid: "trig_mid" },
      trig_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "trigstone", text: "The Master Surveyor's keen face sharpened into delight.\n\nFrom a plinth by the survey-table \u2014 'the founding stone's own template, for a mind sharp enough to deserve it, which is you' \u2014 she lifted the Founding Trig-Stone: the first fixed point, from which the whole city had once been triangulated true. 'It has never once given a false bearing,' she said, laying it in Tritip's keeping. 'It won't mind fixing something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Founding Trig-Stone.", next: "core_fight" },
      trig_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Master Surveyor sighed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people forget to halve the bisected angles, and chalked the working out step by step for Tritip to copy.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, quoting somebody. But the Founding Trig-Stone stayed on its plinth, for a mind that could earn it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "At the Tower's heart, something vast was draped over the Founding Trig-Stone, smearing its sharp sighting-facets soft. The Great Blur was bigger than its footpads and greyer than its Rounders, and everywhere its dull weight lay, the city's bearings came loose and the streets on the master map began, quietly, to wander.\n\nIt turned two edgeless eyes on the company at the stair.\n\n'NO POINTS,' it slurred. 'NO EDGES. NO KNOWING WHERE. SO RESTFUL, NOT KNOWING.'\n\n'Knowing where you are is the whole job,' said Tritip, and levelled all three points for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Great Blur", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck to a keen point, and the Great Blur lost its dulling grip on a whole quarter of the survey \u2014 the streets snapped back to their true places on the map with a click of certainty.",
          strikeText: "The Great Blur smeared the room soft, and a whole set of bearings came loose; somebody sighted their footing and found it had drifted to somewhere else entirely.",
          defeatText: "The Great Blur dulled itself so completely it lost even the edge that made it a thing rather than a fog, and dispersed. Its last word was 'ROUGHLLL\u2014', which pleased Tritip, since even its ending refused to come to a point." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Quarter by quarter the survey sharpened, streets snapping back to their true positions all across the master map, and out in the city the sightlines cleared, tower answering to tower, everyone suddenly certain again of which road they stood on. The grey ribbons stopped pouring. Outside, a drifting street settled back onto its true line with an audible click.\n\nThe Master Surveyor appeared at the table, surveyed the re-fixed city, and inclined her head one exact degree \u2014 measured, naturally \u2014 which the city agreed was unheard of.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Great Blur smeared again and so many bearings came loose that the doorway was the only edge left to sight by, so the company took it.\n\nThey rallied in a sharp-cornered alcove, wrung out and furious and, after a short vote, fortified with the mapmaker's provisions. Then they came back by the tower stair while the Blur dulled the Trig-Stone below. Not every fight is won by winning it, Tritip noted. Some are won by keeping your point and arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "At the Tower's foot glowed the workshop of Keening, maker of true edges, who could put a point back on anything from twenty paces and had once re-sharpened a broken resolve, though she wouldn't dwell on it.\n\n'A Swift Sightline, is it?' she said, over spectacles ground to a fine edge. 'Not made one since the founders' day. A Brass Alidade, a Surveyor's Chain, the Founding Trig-Stone \u2014 bring me all three and I'll make you a sightline that finds its mark before the fog can rise.'",
        forged: "The forge roared its approval. Keening set the alidade's edge along the chain's true length, fixed both to the Trig-Stone's certain point, and drew the whole to a keen fast line.\n\nWith the Swift Sightline, Tritip found it could take a true bearing in the blink of an eye, fixing any position faster than doubt could fog it. A sharp sightline, at speed, is its own kind of quickness.\n\nThe Swift Sightline is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Keening laid the components out and counted them, which took no time.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Sightline wants all three or it's a wish you can't aim. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the geometry.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "tritip_line", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the Founding Trig-Stone at the heart of the survey.\n\nTritip climbed to it and found the trouble: the very first bearing had been sighted, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, a half-degree off \u2014 'near enough for the founding,' they'd have shrugged \u2014 and a city triangulated from a founding bearing a half-degree off will hold true for a thousand years and then, all at once, find every street a stride from where the map swears it is. The drift had spread from that one soft sighting through every bearing that referenced it, out of the Tower and into the streets.\n\nTritip re-sighted the founding line through the Theodolite Eye, measured it true with the Chain, and cut the Stone's facet sharp and exact. No near enough.\n\nThe whole survey seemed to snap into focus. Somewhere across the city, a wandering street settled, very quietly, back onto its true bearing.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Trigon held a feast laid out in a perfect triangle, which the city held to be the highest honour geometry could pay a guest. The mayor gave a speech aligned exactly to the three towers, the Master Surveyor was seen to almost smile, and the gate-keeper waived the toll a second historic time, and logged its coordinates.\n\nAt the end they gave Tritip the Bearing-Book: the master record of every true sightline in the city, so no position taken from it could ever drift. Tritip went home along a road it could have sighted blindfolded, certain to the inch, at every step, of exactly where it stood \u2014 which is, for a triangle, a kind of happiness.",
        midText: "Trigon was grateful in a slightly off-bearing way \u2014 the feast came out a touch skewed and the mayor's speech drifted off the towers twice. Still, the sightlines cleared, the streets held their places, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Master Surveyor pressed the Bearing-Book into Tritip's keeping: the master record of true sightlines. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes are.' Tritip went home already planning a sharper attempt, because a thing worth fixing true is worth fixing again.",
        grantItem: "tritip_book" },
    },
  },
  sparkfin: {
    id: "sparkfin",
    title: "The Stilling of the Tides",
    tagline: "Tidehold runs on currents timed to the science of the sea, and the currents have gone slack and wrong. Only a fish who reads water like a book can set the tides true.",
    items: [
      { id: "sparkfin_reader", name: "The Current-Reader", stat: 3, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Science. Won with a sharp Science check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "sparkfin_fin",    name: "The Swift Current",   stat: 4, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Speed. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "sparkfin_net",    name: "The Tally-Net",       stat: 0, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { livingwater: "A Vial of Living Water", kelpline: "A Strand of Kelp-Line", tidestone: "The Tide-Stone" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🐠", text: "Sparkfin read water the way scholars read books, and thought most scholars had the worse deal. Water told you everything if you knew how to feel it: which way the current meant to go, where the cold deep welled up, how the pressure changed with depth, why a warm layer sat on a cold one and refused to mix. To Sparkfin the sea was one enormous experiment that had been running, beautifully, for longer than anyone could count, and every current in it made perfect sense.\n\nSo when the distress came surging up from Tidehold \u2014 the tide-city whose canals and mills and message-currents all run to the exact science of the sea \u2014 that the tides had gone slack and wrong overnight, currents stilling, the whole flow of the place seizing up, Sparkfin was already swimming.\n\n'A city runs on its currents like blood runs in a body,' said Sparkfin, cutting the water. 'Still the flow and the whole thing goes cold. Come on. We swim against this.'", next: "current_check" },
      current_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udd2c", stat: 3, dc: 10,
        text: "A tide-reader's kit had spilled from a wrecked coracle and drifted down through the shallows, and among the fouled and useless gauges one true instrument still worked \u2014 if you could tell which by how the water moved around it.\n\nThe test was science, not luck: the true gauge sat in the flow without disturbing it, reading the current clean, while the broken ones set up little false eddies of their own that lied about the water. Read the eddies wrong and you'd grab a gauge that fibbed.\n\nSomebody who understood how water moves had better pick the true gauge.",
        success: "found_reader", fail: "current_miss" },
      current_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf0a", text: "The eddies all looked much alike to a hurried eye, which is how bad gauges pass. Sparkfin read the flow a beat too fast, chose one that set up its own sly little current, and by the time the mistake was clear the tide had rolled the whole kit off into the deep.\n\n'It'll read the sea wrongly for somebody, and they'll mistime a tide and lose a boat,' said Sparkfin grimly. (The Current-Reader stays lost this time. Another run may find it.)", next: "post_fight" },
      found_reader: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "sparkfin_reader", text: "The true gauge announced itself by doing nothing at all to the water: the current slid past it clean and honest, and its needle read the flow exactly. The Current-Reader, brass and coral and true to the last ripple.\n\nSome tide-reader had carried this and lost it to a wreck and never trusted a tide since. Sparkfin did, at once, and tucked it beneath a fin, and the sea around it seemed to move a little more truthfully, the way water does near a thing that refuses to lie about it.\n\nThe Current-Reader is yours. (+1 Science, kept forever.)", next: "post_fight" },
      post_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It had settled over the channel-marker at the parish reach \u2014 the post that tells boats which way the safe current runs \u2014 a Slack, small and grey and still, patiently stilling the water around the marker so the current-arrow drooped into 'flows whichever, take your chances'.\n\nSparkfin's fins flared. A channel-marker that says 'whichever' is how a boat meets a rock.\n\n'That current ran true this morning,' said Sparkfin, in the tone of a swimmer who takes the sea personally. The Slack, unmoved, stilled harder.",
        enemy: { name: "The Channel Slack", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer struck like a fresh current, and a whole side of the Slack was forced to FLOW again, running clean for one honest instant before dissolving.",
          strikeText: "The Slack stilled the water dead, and for a moment nothing moved as it should; somebody kicked hard for footing and the stilled water simply refused to push back.",
          defeatText: "The Slack stilled itself so completely it stopped moving in any sense at all, which is the end of every Slack, and settled into nothing. The channel-marker's arrow ran true again, pointing one clear way for the tide to go." },
        victory: "post_win", retreat: "post_retreat" },
      post_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Sparkfin checked the marker against the Reader. Current true and strong. 'Second one this week,' Sparkfin noted. First the mill-race gone slack at home, now a channel-marker drooping.\n\nSomething was moving through the waters stilling the currents that everything depended on, and Tidehold, whose every canal and mill ran on the flow, sat right at the end of the slackening tide.", next: "crossroads" },
      post_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "A drift backward, Sparkfin allowed, is not a stilling of resolve so much as riding a different current for a while. They rode the long slack channel round, losing a slow half-hour and a little faith in the water.\n\nBehind them the Slack went back to stilling the marker. Sparkfin did not look back. Looking back, in a still current, only lets the stillness catch up.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The channel forked three ways beneath a marker whose arrows had gone slack and grey.\n\nThe left arm read THE FISHMONGERS' REACH, where they trade by weight and dare you to fault the scales. The middle arm read THE STEPPING SHOALS, sandbars you cross between the waves. The right arm read THE DEAD CALM, of which the marker said only: NO CURRENT HERE. THE WATER STANDS. MIND YOUR FLOW.\n\nSomewhere down each way, the tide-readers swore, a Vial of Living Water waited to be earned \u2014 water that will not go slack. Living water, like a true friend, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Fishmongers' Reach", next: "row", preview: "Out-reckon a monger at the scales. A hard ratio puzzle guards the Living Water." },
        { label: "Cross the Stepping Shoals", next: "bridge", preview: "Cross the sandbars between the waves. A dice challenge guards the Living Water." },
        { label: "Enter the Dead Calm", next: "fog", preview: "The stilled water where nothing flows. A Doldrum guards the Living Water. This one is a fight." },
      ] },
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Fishmongers' Reach churned with haggling and the slap of scales. 'HONEST WEIGHT!' cried one monger, thumb on the pan. 'TRUE MEASURE, EVERY CATCH!' cried another, measuring generously to itself.\n\nAt the last stall, kept cold under weed, stood a Vial of Living Water, its contents swirling of their own accord. The master monger, an otter with a scoop in one paw and a scam in the other, watched Sparkfin notice it and smiled the smile of a purse being weighed.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The Living Water? That's my prize stock,' said the otter. 'Settle a monger's wager, fins. I mix fish-feed of flakes and pellets in the ratio five to three by weight. To make six point four kilograms of feed, how many kilograms of PELLETS do I need? Answer true, or you buy at Reach rates, which weigh heavy on your side and light on mine.'", question: "A feed mixes flakes and pellets in the ratio 5 : 3 by weight. How many kilograms of pellets are needed to make 6.4 kg of feed?", options: ["2.0", "2.4", "3.0", "3.6", "4.0"], answer: 1,
        hint: "The ratio 5 : 3 makes 5 + 3 = 8 equal parts in total. Find the weight of one part first, then take the 3 parts that are pellets.",
        solution: "Total parts = 5 + 3 = 8. One part = 6.4 \u00f7 8 = 0.8 kg. Pellets are 3 parts: 3 \u00d7 0.8 = 2.4 kg.",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "livingwater", text: "The otter checked it on the scales, tried to shave a tenth off in its own favour, failed, and conceded two point four with the grace of a creature that would rather have overcharged.\n\n'Right to the tenth. You'd weigh me out of my own reach. Take the water. Take it before I make you a partner.'\n\nComponent found: A Vial of Living Water.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The otter worked the true weight out on the wet slate, helpfully and by the kilo. Reach rates being Reach rates, the feed took every coin Sparkfin carried and the vial stayed under its weed.\n\n'Come back sharper in the head,' the otter advised. 'Sharp weighs cheaper than slow, in this trade.'", next: "converge" },
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Stepping Shoals were sandbars that surfaced between the waves, and you crossed them, said a tern wheeling overhead, by timing the sets. Cross on the lull and the bars held you; misjudge and the next wave took the difference, and the deep water beyond did the rest.\n\nSparkfin, who could feel a wave-set coming through the water long before it broke, studied the swell. There was nothing for it but to time it and go.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 10, target: 6, text: "The waves come in sets, and only the lull between them bares the bars. Roll 6 or more on a ten-sided die to catch the lull and cross. The tern keeps score and dives for nobody.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 6 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/5", "3/5", "3/10"], answer: 0, why: "Rolls of 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10 all catch the lull \u2014 that is 5 outcomes out of 10, and 5/10 = 1/2. An even gamble against the sea. Reading the odds first steadies your timing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "livingwater", text: "Sparkfin felt the lull coming and crossed bar to bar between the sets, dry-finned and unhurried, to a let-down cry from the tern. Caught in a rock-pool on the far bar, dropped by some crosser who mistimed the swell, stood a Vial of Living Water, still swirling.\n\nComponent found: A Vial of Living Water.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "Sparkfin read the set a beat wrong, which even good readers do; a rogue wave hides inside an honest lull. It broke early, took Sparkfin off the bar, and rolled it a bracing way down-current to the shallows.\n\nWhatever the far pool held, it kept. Some crossings give one clean lull and no more.", next: "converge" },
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Dead Calm was water that had forgotten how to move. It hung thick and grey and still, and Sparkfin's own strong strokes bogged down in it, every kick swallowed by the stillness.\n\nAt its heart, on a stalled current-wheel, something big hunched over a rack of tide-charts, stilling the drawn currents on each until they drooped into meaningless grey. A Doldrum: the Muddle poured into the shape of dead water. And clutched to its motionless chest, worn like a locket by a thing that had never flowed, glinted a Vial of Living Water, swirling on in defiance of all the stillness.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Doldrum turned without haste. Things shaped like dead calm never hurry; hurrying would require a current.\n\n'STILLNESS,' it breathed, in a voice like a stagnant pool. 'PEACE. NO MORE PUSHING AGAINST THE FLOW. THERE IS NO FLOW. REST.'\n\n'A sea that stops flowing is a sea that dies,' said Sparkfin, and set its fins, which is fish for squaring up, only faster and considerably wetter.",
        enemy: { name: "The Doldrum", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck through the dead water like a fresh spring tide, and a whole side of the Doldrum was forced to move, tore loose in the current, and washed away.",
          strikeText: "The Doldrum stilled the water solid, and one of the company kicked with all its strength against water that simply would not push back, and went nowhere useful.",
          defeatText: "The Doldrum stilled itself past the last possibility of motion and, being now perfectly at rest in every sense, ceased to be a thing that could act at all. The Vial dropped from its still chest and its water swirled brighter, glad of the company." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "livingwater", text: "Sparkfin recovered the Vial and, being a reader of tides, could not leave the Doldrum's stilled charts to mislead the next navigator; it set each drawn current flowing true again before swimming on. A current re-read true is a boat not yet wrecked.\n\nComponent found: A Vial of Living Water.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Dead Calm sided with the Doldrum, thickening the harder they pushed, and drove the company back to living water, worn out and low on cheer. They rode the long flowing way round.\n\nBehind them the Doldrum went back to its charts, locket glinting, stilling the swiftest currents first because they resist most satisfyingly. Sparkfin did not look back. Looking back, in dead water, only lets the stillness settle in your fins.", next: "converge" },
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three ways met again in a channel running fresh and true, at a marker reading TIDEHOLD: 5 in a current so brisk the 5 seemed to swim.\n\nAhead the water dropped toward the Racewater, one clean fast run of true current, the last living flow, the tide-readers said, between here and whatever was stilling Tidehold's tides.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 6, target: 5, text: "The Racewater ran fast over a scatter of stepping-rocks, most sound, some slick with weed. Roll 5 or more on a six-sided die to cross the swift current sure-finned. The race is watching with interest.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "1/3", "2/3", "1/6"], answer: 1, why: "Only 5 and 6 keep your footing on the slick rocks \u2014 that is 2 outcomes out of 6, and 2/6 = 1/3. Hard odds in a fast current, so every edge counts. Reading the odds first steadies your crossing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "kelp_good", fail: "kelp_miss" },
      kelp_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "kelpline", text: "Against the odds Sparkfin caught the swift current clean and crossed the race, and there, snagged where the water had cured it strong for a hundred tides, lay a Strand of Kelp-Line \u2014 a cord grown, not made, that never frays and never lies about a depth.\n\nComponent found: A Strand of Kelp-Line.", next: "science_check" },
      kelp_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The odds were hard and this time they broke hard; a weed-slick rock is a fast liar. Sparkfin lost its footing, the race took it for a brisk tumbling run, and let it out gasping in a backwater.\n\nWhatever the current held, it kept. Hard odds are hard precisely so they can do this. The run went on, a shade more muddled.", next: "science_check" },
      science_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udd2c", stat: 4, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found a harbour-pilot at her fire, glaring at a tide-table she couldn't outrun.\n\n'It's a SPEED puzzle, this,' she said \u2014 'reading the water FAST. The tide's turning quicker than I can plot it, and by the time I've worked out where the current will be, it's already somewhere else. It's not a slow-and-careful job, it's a quick-and-right job. Somebody FAST who knows the water should call the turn.'\n\nShe looked up hopefully. A pilot's favour is worth carrying.",
        success: "science_solved", fail: "science_stumped" },
      science_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", bonusStars: 2, text: "Sparkfin read the turning tide on the wing, faster than it could shift \u2014 the current here, next it'll be THERE, then THERE \u2014 and called each turn a half-beat before it happened, plotting the whole tide before it could get away. Speed and water-sense together, which is Sparkfin's whole self.\n\nThe pilot beamed and pressed a parcel of provisions on the company, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      science_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf0a", text: "The tide outran everyone; every plot was right for the water as it HAD been and wrong for the water as it was, until the pilot rolled the table away with the patience of one long outpaced by the sea.\n\n'Never mind,' she said kindly. 'The tide's faster than all of us. It'll outrun us a night more.' The fire settled. Nobody quite met an eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The pilot's fire was warm and the dark water beyond it was not.\n\n'Tidehold's an hour on,' she said. 'Run my urgent tide-warnings through the night and I'll owe you \u2014 pilots pay their debts. Or bed down here and go safe at dawn. Night water's chancy, chancier than usual, with things about that still a body dead in the water.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll, but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the provisions are surprisingly good." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night water was treacherous with slack patches. With the pilot's lantern showing the living current, you swim with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the true flow.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Sparkfin rode the living current through the slack dark and brought the company to Tidehold's gate before moonrise, filed the pilot's warnings in the crooked box, and pocketed the favour: three sea-bright stars and a supper that flowed a whole heart's worth of nerve back into everyone.\n\nA risk is sound, Sparkfin held, once the odds are read and the odds have agreed to keep flowing. Bonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The slack dark won. Sparkfin bogged down in a still patch shaped exactly like overconfidence, the warnings got waterlogged past reading, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of condition.\n\nThe company swam the last stretch in a pointed silence. Good odds still lose sometimes, Sparkfin reflected. That is precisely what makes them odds and not tides.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Sparkfin slept in the gentle current, which suited it entirely, and woke to a morning with the tide running fresh and clean. The provisions proved unexpectedly excellent.\n\nThe swim took the promised hour exactly, which Sparkfin found deeply reassuring. Sometimes the fast move is the unhurried one. Expected value, Sparkfin told the company, is happy to drift when drifting is right.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Tidehold's gate stood in a great sea-arch, taking its old toll of one true answer per traveller. This morning the arch was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Doldrum footpads had strung a stilled net across the channel, daubed TOLE: ALL YER CURRENT. Behind her bolted shutters the harbour-keeper glared like a kettle at the boil.\n\n'A net in dead water,' said Sparkfin, 'won't catch a fish that's actually moving,' which as war-cries go was at least seaworthy.",
        enemy: { name: "Doldrum Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck like a spring tide, and one footpad was forced to flow, lost its stillness all at once, and washed apart.",
          strikeText: "A stilled fist dropped through the dead water, and somebody in the company paid a toll of current they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad tried to still itself away from the danger and, stilling, lost the last motion it had, including the motion of resisting, and settled into nothing. The net went slack. Nobody re-strung it." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The harbour-keeper unbolted shutters, door and scowl in order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, hauling the slack net aside. 'First in an age. But nobody passes without answering \u2014 harbour's rules, and truest of all on a day the whole city's gone slack.' She slid across a slate cured smooth by the sea.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The company fell back with the footpads' still laughter behind, and spent a becalmed quarter-hour in a backwater the dead water enjoyed rather more than they did.\n\nThey reached the gate by the side wicket, waterlogged and simmering. The keeper took stock of them and said nothing. 'Bad morning,' she allowed. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🌊", text: "The slate was sea-cured, the chalk damp, the question in the keeper's steady hand. 'Take your time,' she said, timing it.", question: "Sound travels through water at 1500 metres per second. A dolphin sends out a click and hears its echo return from the seabed 2 seconds later. How deep is the seabed, in metres?", options: ["750", "1500", "3000", "1000", "1250"], answer: 1,
        hint: "The sound travels DOWN to the seabed and back UP again in those 2 seconds, so it covers twice the depth. Find the total distance first.",
        solution: "In 2 seconds the sound covers 1500 \u00d7 2 = 3000 m in total. But that is the round trip \u2014 down and back \u2014 so the depth is half of it: 3000 \u00f7 2 = 1500 m.",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the working, sniffed a briny sniff of respect, and stamped Sparkfin TRUE in wave-cut letters.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, nearly warm, 'and hurry. The Tide-Heart's stilling, and a city that runs on its currents has hours, not days, once the heart of them stops. From what the gulls say, fewer hours than it had.'", next: "tidehold" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, not unkindly, turned the slate and walked the there-and-back through, then had Sparkfin say it back twice, because twice is where knowing runs deep.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the company through the side wicket, the long way, past the boatyard, who could not believe their luck twice in a morning.", next: "tidehold" },
      tidehold: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Tidehold at last: a city of canals and tide-mills and message-currents, where everything moves on water timed to the exact science of the sea, built by folk who held that a city, like a fish, is only truly alive while something is flowing through it.\n\nBut the flow was failing. A grey stillness lay over the canals, thickest at the Tide-Mill at the centre, where the Tide-Heart \u2014 the great valve that draws the true tide in and out and drives the whole city \u2014 had begun to seize. Grey ribbons of Muddle poured from its housing, stilling the currents, so mills stopped mid-turn and messages drifted nowhere and the water in the streets stood dead and grey.\n\nSparkfin surged faster, cutting the slack.", next: "count_check" },
      count_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 2, dc: 12,
        text: "The Tide-Mill's control-room was a tangle: the great sluice-gates had jammed in the wrong sequence, and only one exact order of which to open first, second, third would restart the Tide-Heart without flooding the lower city. Force them in the wrong order and the whole flow would surge back and drown the works. There was no time to trial each order slowly; the water was already backing up.\n\nIt was a puzzle of order and consequence, reasoned out cold and fast.\n\nSomebody with sharp Logic had better work out the sequence of the gates.",
        success: "count_quick", fail: "count_slow" },
      count_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u2699\ufe0f", text: "Sparkfin reasoned the sluices backward from the Tide-Heart \u2014 that gate can't open till this one's clear, which can't lift till THAT one drops \u2014 and read off the one safe order. Open, open, open, and the Heart drew its first true breath of tide in days.\n\nUp on the gantry the Keeper of the Tide made a low approving surge, which by her standards was an ovation.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      count_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf0a", text: "Every order Sparkfin tried backed the water up worse two gates later, and it took the better part of an hour of careful trial to restart the Heart at all. By the end the light had failed and the stillness had crept a shade further through the canals, as if handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧪", text: "The Keeper of the Tide stood at a console of brass valves. 'Prove you understand how a mixture truly works,' she said, in a voice like a turning tide, 'and you may touch the Tide-Heart. The trouble is within. But the truth is here, and the truth is only for those who can reckon it.'", question: "A tank of brine is 20% salt by weight. You add 200 grams of pure water and stir. Now it is 12% salt by weight. What was the original weight of the brine, in grams?", options: ["240", "300", "360", "400", "250"], answer: 1,
        hint: "The WEIGHT of salt does not change when you add water \u2014 only the total weight does. Write the salt as 20% of the original, and also as 12% of (original + 200), and set them equal.",
        solution: "Let the original brine weigh x g. The salt is 0.20x g, and it stays the same. After adding 200 g of water: 0.20x = 0.12(x + 200). So 0.20x = 0.12x + 24, giving 0.08x = 24, so x = 300 g.",
        good: "tide_good", mid: "tide_mid" },
      tide_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "tidestone", text: "The Keeper's tidal face swelled into delight.\n\nFrom a niche in the valve-housing \u2014 'the founder's own Tide-Stone, for a mind that reads a mixture true, which is you' \u2014 she lifted the Tide-Stone: the weighted heart-stone that had set the true rhythm of the tides since the city's founding. 'It has never once mistimed a tide,' she said, laying it in Sparkfin's keeping. 'It won't mind driving something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Tide-Stone.", next: "core_fight" },
      tide_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Keeper sighed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people forget that the salt stays put, and chalked the working out for Sparkfin to copy, line by line.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, quoting somebody. But the Tide-Stone stayed in its niche, for a mind that could reckon it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "At the Mill's heart, something vast lay coiled over the Tide-Heart itself, stilling its great pulse. The Great Slack was bigger than its footpads and greyer than its Doldrums, and everywhere its dead weight pressed, the city's currents seized and the water stood.\n\nIt turned two still eyes on the company at the gantry stair.\n\n'STILLNESS,' it breathed. 'AT LAST. NO MORE TIRESOME TIDES. LET THE WATER SLEEP FOREVER.'\n\n'Sleeping water is a dead sea,' said Sparkfin, and set its fins for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Great Slack", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck like a spring flood, and the Great Slack lost its grip on a whole reach of the works \u2014 the Tide-Heart surged and a quarter of the city's currents leapt back to life.",
          strikeText: "The Great Slack stilled the water solid, and somebody kicked for the surface against a sea that had simply stopped, and hung there, going nowhere.",
          defeatText: "The Great Slack stilled the last of itself into perfect motionlessness and, being now entirely at rest, had no motion left with which to exist, and dispersed. Its last word was 'CALMLLL\u2014', which pleased Sparkfin, since even its ending failed to carry through." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Reach by reach the Tide-Heart pulsed back to life, and all across Tidehold the currents leapt and ran, mills catching, messages surging away, the dead grey water flushing bright and living again. The stillness withdrew. Outside, a stalled tide-mill spun back up with a joyful rush like a held breath let go.\n\nThe Keeper of the Tide appeared at the gantry, surveyed the living city, and dipped her head one exact degree, which the city agreed was without precedent.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Great Slack stilled the works again so completely that the doorway was the only thing water still moved past, so the company took it.\n\nThey rallied in a flowing side-canal, wrung out and furious and, after a short vote, fortified with the pilot's provisions. Then they came back by the gantry stair while the Slack stilled the Heart below. Not every fight is won by winning it, Sparkfin noted. Some are won by keeping moving and arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "At the Mill's foot glowed \u2014 improbably, over water \u2014 the workshop of Weir, maker of true flows, who could set a stilled current running from twenty paces and had once restarted a stopped heart, though she wouldn't dwell on it.\n\n'A Tally-Net, is it?' she said, over spectacles beaded with spray. 'Not made one since the founders' day. A Vial of Living Water, a Strand of Kelp-Line, the Tide-Stone \u2014 bring me all three and I'll knot you a net that counts its catch true every time.'",
        forged: "The forge roared over the rush of water. Weir knotted the kelp-line into a net by the Tide-Stone's true rhythm and charged every knot with living water.\n\nWith the Tally-Net, Sparkfin found it could count a whole shoal at a glance, reckon a catch, total a tide's worth of figures in an eye-blink, every number landing exact. Reading the water, it turned out, had taught it to reckon fast and true.\n\nThe Tally-Net is yours! (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Weir laid the components out and counted them, which was quick.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Net wants all three or it's a wish with holes in it. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the science.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "sparkfin_net", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the Tide-Heart itself.\n\nSparkfin swam to it and found the trouble: the founding tide had been set, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, to run 'roughly on the turn' \u2014 and a city driven by a Tide-Heart set to run 'roughly' will flow true for a thousand years and then, all at once, slacken into stillness together. The seizing had spread from that one lazy setting out through every current that took its rhythm, out of the Mill and into the streets.\n\nSparkfin read the true tide through the Current-Reader, measured its depth with the Kelp-Line, and set the Tide-Heart's rhythm exact by the Tide-Stone. No roughly on the turn.\n\nThe whole city seemed to draw one long breath of moving water. Somewhere down a stilled canal, a dead current stirred, very quietly, and began to run.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Tidehold held a feast that floated down the canals course by course, which the city considered the highest honour flowing water could pay a guest. The mayor gave a speech that ran to exactly the turn of the tide, the Keeper of the Tide was seen to almost dance (a rolling, tidal dance), and the harbour-keeper waived the toll a second historic time.\n\nAt the end they gave Sparkfin the Swift Current: a personal current that carries you exactly where you mean to go, exactly when. Sparkfin swam home the long way for the pleasure of it, riding the living tide, faster and truer than it had ever swum, and thought that a sea that flows is the finest experiment anyone had ever left running.",
        midText: "Tidehold was grateful in a slightly sluggish way \u2014 the feast drifted a touch off the turn and the mayor's speech ran slack twice. Still, the currents ran, the mills turned, the water lived, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Keeper pressed the Swift Current into Sparkfin's keeping: a current that carries you true. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes are.' Sparkfin swam home already plotting a swifter, cleaner run, because a thing worth setting flowing is worth setting flowing again.",
        grantItem: "sparkfin_fin" },
    },
  },
  hunchik: {
    id: "hunchik",
    title: "The Case of the Missing Because",
    tagline: "In Deducton every truth is worked backward from its clues, and the clues have come loose from their causes. Only a hedgehog who never stops asking why can solve the city itself.",
    items: [
      { id: "hunchik_lens", name: "The Deduction Lens", stat: 2, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Logic. Won with a sharp Logic check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "hunchik_plan", name: "The Plan-Drawer",    stat: 1, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Geometry. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "hunchik_kit",  name: "The Forensic Flask",  stat: 3, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Science. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { fact: "A Fixed Fact", motive: "A True Motive", casebook: "The Founding Casebook" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🦔", text: "Hunchik had a quill for every question and asked most of them out loud. A fact, Hunchik held, is a lonely and useless thing until you find the WHY behind it: a footprint means nothing until you ask who walked, a broken cup means nothing until you ask what shook the shelf. Work backward from the clue to its cause and the world confesses. Hunchik had solved a great many small mysteries this way and a few large ones, chiefly by refusing, ever, to stop at the first because.\n\nSo when the case came in from Deducton \u2014 the detective-city where every truth is deduced backward from its evidence, so nothing stays hidden \u2014 that the clues had come loose from their causes overnight, footprints leading to no one, effects with no cause, coincidences breeding in the streets like flies, Hunchik packed the lens and went, bristling.\n\n'A city where nothing causes anything,' said Hunchik, 'is a city where anything can happen and no one can say why. Bring the good glass. We are going to ask this thing why until it answers.'", next: "deduce_check" },
      deduce_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udd0d", stat: 2, dc: 10,
        text: "A detective's dropped evidence-case had scattered its cards along the road, most of them clues cut loose from any conclusion and now pointing nowhere. But one small run of cards still held its chain of reasoning, clue leading to inference leading to a sound conclusion, and clipped inside it lay something worth the finding.\n\nThe test was to follow the deduction: only one reading of the cards let every clue point honestly to the next, and only that reading opened the case. Read it wrong and it led to a dead end and locked.\n\nSomebody with good Logic had better deduce the true chain.",
        success: "found_lens", fail: "deduce_miss" },
      deduce_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf43", text: "The chains all seemed to point somewhere if you didn't test each inference, which is exactly how false leads flourish. Hunchik followed one a step too fast, took a clue that pointed nowhere, and the case dead-ended and shut just as the detective's runner gathered the scattered cards and hurried off.\n\n'It will point somebody at the wrong suspect,' said Hunchik grimly, 'and the real one will walk.' (The Deduction Lens stays lost this time. Another run may deduce its way to it.)", next: "post_fight" },
      found_lens: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "hunchik_lens", text: "Clue by clue the true chain held, each pointing honestly to the next, and at its end it opened on the prize: the Deduction Lens, a small bright glass through which any clue showed the cause behind it, plain as a signature.\n\nSome detective had reasoned their way to this and then lost the thread and never closed a case since. Hunchik did, carefully, and set the lens to one eye, and the whole roadside seemed to arrange itself into evidence, every fact quietly admitting what had caused it.\n\nThe Deduction Lens is yours. (+1 Logic, kept forever.)", next: "post_fight" },
      post_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🐟", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It hung about the parish notice-post \u2014 the board where the constable pins which way the trouble went \u2014 a Red Herring, small and grey and fishy, patiently unpinning each true clue and pinning up distractions, so the board now read 'SUSPECT FLED NORTH, OR POSSIBLY IT WAS THE WEATHER, OR A DOG'.\n\nHunchik's quills rose. A notice-board full of red herrings is how the wrong soul gets arrested.\n\n'That board pointed one clear way this morning,' said Hunchik, in the tone of a detective who takes a false lead as a personal insult. The Red Herring, unrepentant, grew fishier.",
        enemy: { name: "The Red Herring", emoji: "🐟", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer struck home, and a whole side of the Red Herring was forced to point at its true cause \u2014 it held one honest lead for an instant before flaking away like old fish.",
          strikeText: "The Red Herring flung up a cloud of coincidence, and for a moment every clue pointed everywhere at once; somebody chased a false lead straight into a wall.",
          defeatText: "The Red Herring produced one distraction too many, distracted even itself, forgot what it was doing there, and simply wasn't. The notice-board read SUSPECT FLED NORTH, BECAUSE THAT IS WHERE THE FOOTPRINTS GO, which was proper detective work." },
        victory: "post_win", retreat: "post_retreat" },
      post_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Hunchik re-pinned the board and read it back through the lens. Every clue pointing true. 'Second one this week,' Hunchik noted. First the parish records gone to coincidence at home, now a notice-board breeding red herrings.\n\nSomething was abroad cutting clues loose from their causes, and Deducton, whose whole life was reasoning backward from evidence to truth, sat right at the end of its trail.", next: "crossroads" },
      post_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🐟", text: "A step back, Hunchik reasoned, does not sever one's resolve from its cause provided one remembers why one stepped. Hunchik remembered, and took the long way by the ditch, losing a slow half-hour and a little trust in the tidiness of the world.\n\nBehind them the Red Herring went back to breeding distractions. Hunchik did not look back. Looking back at a Red Herring, Hunchik knew, only gives it a fresh coincidence to wave.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The road forked three ways beneath a signpost whose arms pointed, unhelpfully, at three unrelated things each.\n\nThe left arm read THE WITNESS ROW, where they sell testimony and dare you to catch the lie. The middle arm read THE INFERENCE BRIDGE, a span you cross by the sound planks of reasoning. The right arm read THE FOG OF COINCIDENCE, of which the sign said only: NOTHING CAUSES ANYTHING HERE. ALL IS CHANCE. MIND YOUR WHYS.\n\nSomewhere down each road, the detectives swore, a Fixed Fact waited to be earned \u2014 one thing you could be certain of. A fixed fact, like a true friend, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Witness Row", next: "row", preview: "Out-deduce a witness at their own game. A hard logic puzzle guards the Fixed Fact." },
        { label: "Cross the Inference Bridge", next: "bridge", preview: "Cross by the sound planks of reasoning. A dice challenge guards the Fixed Fact." },
        { label: "Enter the Fog of Coincidence", next: "fog", preview: "The chance-ridden fog where nothing has a cause. A Confounder guards the Fixed Fact. This one is a fight." },
      ] },
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Witness Row was loud with sworn statements for hire. 'GOSPEL TRUTH!' cried one witness, shiftily. 'SAW IT WITH MY OWN EYES, PROBABLY!' cried another, who plainly hadn't.\n\nAt the last stall, weighed down by a paperweight of solid certainty, lay a Fixed Fact, plain and heavy and unbudgeable. The master witness, a weasel with a bible in one paw and a fib in the other, watched Hunchik notice it and smiled the smile of a purse being cross-examined.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The Fixed Fact? That's my sworn stock,' said the weasel. 'Solve a witness-box wager, quills. Four suspects gave statements about who took the tarts. Ada said \u201cBo did it.\u201d Bo said \u201cI did not.\u201d Cy said \u201cI did not.\u201d Dot said \u201cAda did it.\u201d Exactly ONE of them is lying. Name the thief and the fact is yours. Miss it and you testify at my rates, which never favour the witness.'", question: "Ada: \u201cBo did it.\u201d Bo: \u201cI didn't.\u201d Cy: \u201cI didn't.\u201d Dot: \u201cAda did it.\u201d Exactly one statement is a lie. Who took the tarts?", options: ["Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Dot"], answer: 0,
        hint: "Try each suspect as the thief and count how many statements would be lies. You want the suspect for whom exactly ONE statement comes out false.",
        solution: "Suppose Ada is the thief. Then Ada's \u201cBo did it\u201d is a lie; Bo's \u201cI didn't\u201d is true; Cy's \u201cI didn't\u201d is true; Dot's \u201cAda did it\u201d is true. That is exactly one lie \u2014 it fits. Every other suspect makes two or more statements false, so the thief is Ada.",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "fact", text: "The weasel checked the count of lies, tried to find a second reading, found none, and conceded 'Ada' with the grace of a creature that would rather have muddied the water.\n\n'One lie, one thief. Airtight. You'd cross-examine me out of my own row. Take the fact. Take it before I confess to something.'\n\nComponent found: A Fixed Fact.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The weasel walked the true reasoning through, suspect by suspect, helpfully and at witness rates. Its rates being what they were, the testimony cost every coin Hunchik carried and the fact stayed under its paperweight.\n\n'Come back with a sharper eye,' the weasel advised. 'Sharp deduction testifies cheaper, in this trade.'", next: "converge" },
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Inference Bridge was a span of planks across the gorge, each carved with a step of reasoning, and you crossed it, said a magpie, by the sound ones. A plank whose inference held bore your weight; one that only LOOKED to follow gave way, and the gorge below asked no further questions.\n\nHunchik, who tested every inference by habit, studied the planks. There was nothing for it but to read the sound steps and go.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 12, target: 9, text: "Most of the planks are plausible but unsound; only the truly valid steps will bear you. Roll 9 or more on a twelve-sided die to find the sound inferences and cross. The magpie keeps score from a safe rail.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 9 or more on a fair twelve-sided die?", options: ["1/4", "1/3", "3/8", "5/12"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 9, 10, 11 and 12 hold \u2014 that is 4 outcomes out of 12, and 4/12 = 1/3. Hard odds over a long drop, so you'll want the edge. Reading the odds first steadies your footing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "fact", text: "Hunchik tested each plank's inference and crossed on the sound ones alone, to a disappointed rattle from the magpie. Wedged under the far post, dropped by some crosser who trusted an invalid step, sat a Fixed Fact, plain and heavy and true.\n\nComponent found: A Fixed Fact.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "One plank's inference looked valid and wasn't; a plausible fallacy is the hardest kind to catch mid-stride. Hunchik trusted it, the plank gave, and the gorge took Hunchik for a jarring slide to a ledge below.\n\nWhatever the far post held, it kept. Some crossings give one sound step and no more.", next: "converge" },
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Fog of Coincidence hung thick and grey, and inside it nothing caused anything: doors slammed with no wind, footprints appeared with no walker, and every clue Hunchik found led, maddeningly, to pure chance.\n\nAt its heart, on a heap of unrelated evidence, something big was going along a wall of solved cases and cutting each clue loose from its cause, cackling at the coincidences it left. A Confounder: the Muddle poured into the shape of pure chance. And balanced on its formless head, worn like a trophy by a thing that had never caused a single effect, sat a Fixed Fact, stubbornly still certain.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Confounder turned without concern. Things shaped like coincidence never worry; worrying would imply a cause.\n\n'JUST CHANCE,' it burbled, in a voice like a dropped deck of cards. 'NO WHY. NO WHO. THINGS JUST HAPPEN. ISN'T THAT A RELIEF?'\n\n'Nothing just happens,' said Hunchik, and raised every quill, which is hedgehog for objecting, strenuously and with points.",
        enemy: { name: "The Confounder", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck and a whole span of loose clues snapped back onto their true causes \u2014 a slab of the Confounder was forced to MEAN something and, unable to bear it, fell away.",
          strikeText: "The Confounder threw up a fog of coincidence, and for a moment no clue caused anything; somebody deduced a safe path that turned out, by pure chance, to be a cliff.",
          defeatText: "The Confounder generated a coincidence so vast it coincidentally undid itself, which it found deeply ironic for the half-second it had left. The Fixed Fact rolled off its head and came to rest, still certain of exactly one thing." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "fact", text: "Hunchik retrieved the Fixed Fact and, unable to leave solved cases in ruins, spent a patient while re-attaching the wall's loosened clues to their true causes so the next detective could read them. A clue restored to its cause is a small hard win against a world of chance.\n\nComponent found: A Fixed Fact.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The fog sided with the Confounder, breeding coincidences the harder they pushed, and drove the company back to ground where cause still met effect, out of patience and low on cheer. They took the one path that led somewhere for a reason.\n\nBehind them the Confounder went back to its wall, trophy balanced, cutting the best-solved cases loose first because they resist most sweetly. Hunchik did not look back. Looking back at a Confounder only hands it another coincidence.", next: "converge" },
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three roads met again at a milestone reading DEDUCTON: 5, the 5 deduced beneath it from a tidy line of clues that actually pointed to it, which everyone found steadying.\n\nAhead the land dipped toward the Evidence Stream, one clean run of water where every clue still lay beside its cause, the last honest scene, the detectives said, between here and whatever had cut Deducton's causes loose.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 8, target: 2, text: "The Evidence Stream had firm stepping-stones nearly all across \u2014 only one lay loose. Roll 2 or more on an eight-sided die to cross the sound stones. The current takes a professional interest.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 2 or more on a fair eight-sided die?", options: ["7/8", "3/4", "1/2", "5/8"], answer: 0, why: "Only a roll of 1 finds the loose stone; 2 through 8 all cross safe \u2014 that is 7 outcomes out of 8, so 7/8. Excellent odds, but one-in-eight still slips. Reading the odds first steadies the crossing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "motive_good", fail: "motive_miss" },
      motive_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "motive", text: "Hunchik crossed the sound stones sure-footed, and there in an eddy where the stream had preserved it clean lay a True Motive \u2014 the real reason behind a thing, the one that actually explains it rather than merely seeming to.\n\nComponent found: A True Motive.", next: "geo_check" },
      motive_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The one-in-eight stone is precisely where Hunchik's paw came down; good odds do occasionally do this. The stone rolled, the stream took Hunchik for a brisk dunplanned wade, and let it out spluttering in the shallows.\n\nWhatever the eddy held, it kept. The run went on, a shade more muddled.", next: "geo_check" },
      geo_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83d\udccf", stat: 1, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found a scene-of-crime officer at her fire, glaring at a jumble of measurements.\n\n'It's a GEOMETRY job, this scene,' she said. 'I've the distances and the angles between where everything lay, and I need them drawn to scale into one true plan before the trail's cold \u2014 where each thing stood, how the lines of sight ran, what could reach what. It's not a deducing job yet, it's a drawing-it-true job. Somebody with an eye for shape and scale should plot it.'\n\nShe looked up hopefully. A scene officer's favour is worth carrying.",
        success: "geo_solved", fail: "geo_stumped" },
      geo_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f", bonusStars: 2, text: "It came together the way good draughtsmanship does: the distances scaled clean, the angles closed true, and the whole scene resolved into one plan you could stand inside \u2014 and the moment it was drawn to scale, one sight-line no witness had noticed lay plainly open.\n\nThe scene officer beamed and pressed a parcel of provisions on the company, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      geo_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udcd0", text: "The scene defeated everyone; the distances wouldn't scale and the angles refused to close, until the officer rolled up the draft with the patience of one long at odds with a stubborn set of measurements.\n\n'Never mind,' she said kindly. 'The scene's out-drawn better than you. It'll keep a night more.' The fire ticked. Nobody quite met an eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The officer's fire was warm and the dark beyond it was not.\n\n'Deducton's an hour on,' she said. 'Run my urgent case-notes through the night and I'll owe you \u2014 scene officers pay their debts, and note everything. Or bed down here and go safe at dawn. Night road's chancy, chancier than usual, with things about that leave a body unable to reason its way home.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll, but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the provisions are surprisingly good." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night road forked where the causes had come loose. With the officer's lantern showing which turning actually led on, you go with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the true path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Hunchik reasoned out the true turning at each fork and brought the company to Deducton's gate before moonrise, filed the officer's case-notes in the crooked box, and pocketed the favour: three sharp stars and a supper that reasoned a whole heart's worth of nerve back into everyone.\n\nA risk has a cause and an odds, Hunchik held, and once you've deduced both and the odds have agreed to hold, it is a sound bet. Bonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The loose dark won. Hunchik took a turning that seemed to follow into a ditch shaped exactly like overconfidence, the case-notes got sodden, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of condition.\n\nThe company trudged the last mile in a bristling silence. Good odds still lose sometimes, Hunchik reflected. That is precisely what makes them odds and not conclusions.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Hunchik slept rolled in a defensive ball, which is the most logical way for a hedgehog to sleep, and woke to a morning that followed clearly from the night. The provisions proved, on inspection, a delight.\n\nThe walk took the promised hour exactly, which Hunchik found deeply reasonable. Sometimes the sound move is the slow one. Expected value, Hunchik told the company, is in no hurry to be proved right.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Deducton's gate stood in a keystone arch, taking its old toll of one true answer per traveller. This morning the arch was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Confounder footpads had strung a web of red herrings across it that proved, by sheer coincidence, that the toll was ALL YER CERTAINTY. Behind her bolted shutters the gate-keeper glared like a kettle at the boil.\n\n'Your proof is nothing but coincidences,' said Hunchik, 'and coincidences aren't causes,' which as war-cries go was at least admissible.",
        enemy: { name: "Confounder Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck and one footpad's coincidences were forced back onto their true causes, leaving it with nothing to stand on; it flaked apart.",
          strikeText: "A footpad flung a coincidence, and somebody in the company paid a toll of certainty they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad demanded to know why it should stop existing, was shown the exact cause, could not wave it away as chance, and ceased. The web of herrings fell slack. Nobody re-strung it." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The gate-keeper unbolted shutters, door and scowl in order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, sweeping the dead herrings aside. 'First in an age. But nobody passes without answering \u2014 gate's rules, and soundest of all on a day the whole city's stopped adding up.' She slid across a slate worn smooth by generations of deduction.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The company fell back with the footpads' scattered laughter behind, and spent a baffling quarter-hour in a side-lane where every turning was a coincidence and none a cause.\n\nThey reached the gate by the side wicket, footsore and simmering. The keeper took stock of them and said nothing. 'Bad morning,' she allowed. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔢", text: "The slate was old, the chalk worn, the riddle in the keeper's careful hand. 'Take your time,' she said, timing it.", question: "A two-digit number has a tens digit that is three times its units digit. Reversing its two digits makes it 54 smaller. What is the number?", options: ["31", "62", "93", "84", "72"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Call the units digit u; then the tens digit is 3u. Reversing a two-digit number changes it by 9 \u00d7 (tens digit \u2212 units digit). Set that equal to 54.",
        solution: "Reversing changes a two-digit number by 9 \u00d7 (tens \u2212 units). That change is 54, so tens \u2212 units = 6. With tens = 3 \u00d7 units: 3u \u2212 u = 6, so 2u = 6, u = 3 and the tens digit is 9. The number is 93 (and 93 \u2212 39 = 54 \u2713).",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the working, sniffed her sniff of respect, and stamped Hunchik SOUND in one firm letter.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, nearly warm, 'and hurry. The Founding Casebook's coming loose, and a city that reasons every truth backward from that book becomes, without it, one long coincidence. From what the crows say, it's most of the way to chance.'", next: "deducton" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, not unkindly, turned the slate and walked the digit-reversing through, then had Hunchik say it back twice, because twice is where knowing sets hard.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the company through the side wicket, the long way, past the records-office, who could not believe their luck twice in a morning.", next: "deducton" },
      deducton: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Deducton at last: a city of lamplit offices and reasoning-rooms, where every truth is deduced backward from its evidence and no wrong stays hidden, built by folk who held that a city is only as safe as its willingness to ask why.\n\nBut the whys were failing. A grey confusion lay over the streets, thickest at the Central Bureau, where the Founding Casebook \u2014 the master record that ties every effect in the city to its true cause \u2014 was coming unbound. Grey ribbons of Muddle poured from its doors, cutting clues loose from causes, so constables arrested coincidences and citizens blamed the weather for everything and no one could say why a single thing had happened.\n\nHunchik pressed inward, quills up, asking why of everything.", next: "count_check" },
      count_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 0, dc: 12,
        text: "The Bureau's evidence-room was in uproar: the case-ledgers' running totals had scrambled in the draught \u2014 tallies of clues, counts of witnesses, sums that had to balance \u2014 and each had to be caught and re-totalled true and filed before the confusion smeared the figures for good. There was no time to add them SLOWLY; the numbers were scrambling as they lay.\n\nIt was fast, exact arithmetic under pressure: catch, total, file, and be right the first time.\n\nSomebody quick with Arithmetic had better tot the ledgers on the wing.",
        success: "count_quick", fail: "count_slow" },
      count_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "A blur of catching and totalling \u2014 clues counted, witnesses summed, every figure carried clean \u2014 and the case-ledgers went back true the instant before the confusion could smear them, the whole evidence-room saved on Hunchik's fast exact reckoning.\n\nUp at the master desk the Chief Inspector made a small approving grunt, which by her standards was applause.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      count_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf42", text: "The figures scrambled faster than they could be totalled, and it took the better part of an hour to save even the survivors, checking each twice. By the end the light had failed and the confusion had crept a shade further through the Bureau, as if handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧠", text: "The Chief Inspector stood at a desk stacked with closed cases. 'Prove you can reason out truth from liars and truth-tellers,' she said, in a voice like a case snapping shut, 'and you may touch the Casebook. The trouble is below. But the truth is here, and the truth is only for those who can deduce it.'", question: "On an island each person is a knight (always tells the truth) or a knave (always lies). You meet two, X and Y. X says: \u201cAt least one of us is a knave.\u201d What are X and Y?", options: ["X is a knight, Y is a knave", "X is a knave, Y is a knight", "Both are knights", "Both are knaves", "Cannot be determined"], answer: 0,
        hint: "Test the idea that X is a knave (a liar). If X's statement were false, what would that say about how many knaves there are \u2014 and does that contradict X being a knave?",
        solution: "If X were a knave, X's statement would be false, meaning NEITHER is a knave \u2014 but that contradicts X being a knave. So X must be a knight, and the statement is true. Since a knight tells the truth, at least one is a knave, and it cannot be X, so Y is the knave. X is a knight, Y is a knave.",
        good: "casebook_good", mid: "casebook_mid" },
      casebook_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "casebook", text: "The Chief Inspector's sharp face closed on something like delight.\n\nFrom a locked drawer \u2014 'the founder's own Casebook, for a mind that can reason a liar into the light, which is you' \u2014 she lifted the Founding Casebook: the first record that had ever tied an effect to its true cause. 'It has never once closed a case on the wrong soul,' she said, laying it in Hunchik's keeping. 'It won't mind proving something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Founding Casebook.", next: "core_fight" },
      casebook_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Chief Inspector sighed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people take a liar at their word, and reasoned the knight-and-knave through for Hunchik, step by step.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, quoting somebody. But the Founding Casebook stayed in its drawer, for a mind that could deduce it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "In the Bureau's heart, something vast lay draped across the Founding Casebook, cutting each entry loose from its cause. The Great Coincidence was bigger than its footpads and greyer than its Confounders, and everywhere its formless weight lay, effect drifted free of cause and the whole city became a heap of things that had merely happened.\n\nIt turned two causeless eyes on the company at the stair.\n\n'NO REASON,' it said, and it was not a complaint. 'NO CAUSE. NO CULPRIT. JUST THINGS, HAPPENING, FOREVER. GIVE UP ASKING WHY.'\n\n'Why,' said Hunchik, 'is the only question worth asking,' and raised every quill for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Great Coincidence", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck and a whole chapter of the Casebook snapped shut on its true causes \u2014 effect bound back to cause \u2014 and the Great Coincidence lost its grip on a quarter of the city and reeled.",
          strikeText: "The Great Coincidence loosed a storm of chance, and for a breath nothing in the Bureau had a cause; somebody reasoned out a safe step that a coincidence turned, at the last instant, into a drop.",
          defeatText: "The Great Coincidence, pressed to name the cause of its own existence, found there was none \u2014 which by its own rule made it mere chance, and mere chance, pressed hard enough, simply doesn't. Its last word was 'BECAUS\u2014', which pleased Hunchik, since it never reached the reason." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Chapter by chapter the Casebook bound shut on its true causes, and across Deducton effect snapped back to cause: constables released their coincidences, citizens remembered what had actually happened, and every clue in the city pointed, once more, at its real culprit. The grey confusion withdrew. Outside, a wrongly-blamed rainstorm was quietly exonerated.\n\nThe Chief Inspector appeared at the desk, surveyed the re-bound city, and inclined her head one exact degree, which the Bureau agreed had no precedent and, now, a clear cause.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Great Coincidence loosed so much chance that the doorway was the only thing left with a reason to be where it was, so the company took it.\n\nThey rallied in a well-lit alcove, wrung out and furious and, after a short vote, fortified with the officer's provisions. Then they came back by the inner stair while the thing cut the Casebook loose below. Not every fight is won by winning it, Hunchik noted. Some are won by refusing to stop asking why and arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Bureau glowed the workshop of Sable, maker of true instruments, who could bind an effect back to its cause from twenty paces and had once mended a shattered certainty, though she wouldn't dwell on it.\n\n'A Forensic Flask, is it?' she said, over spectacles that magnified. 'Not made one since the founders' day. A Fixed Fact, a True Motive, the Founding Casebook \u2014 bring me all three and I'll blow you a flask that shows the cause behind any effect you pour into it.'",
        forged: "The forge flared. Sable suspended the Fixed Fact in the flask, drew the True Motive through it like a reagent, and sealed the working against the Casebook's certain page.\n\nWith the Forensic Flask, Hunchik found it could test any effect and read out its cause \u2014 what shook the shelf, what left the print, what really happened \u2014 the way a chemist reads a reaction. Deduction, it turned out, is only Science with better questions.\n\nThe Forensic Flask is yours! (+1 Science, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Sable laid the components out and counted them, which took no time.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Flask wants all three or it's a wish you can't test. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the logic.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "hunchik_kit", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the founding entry at the heart of the Casebook.\n\nHunchik turned to it and found the trouble: the city's very first case had been closed, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, with the verdict 'must have been SOMETHING' \u2014 and a city that reasons every truth backward from a founding case closed on 'something' will deduce true for a thousand years and then, all at once, find that nothing causes anything and never did. The confusion had spread from that one lazy verdict out through every case that cited it, out of the Bureau and into the streets.\n\nHunchik struck out the 'something', worked the true cause back from the founding clue with the lens, tested it in the Flask till it proved, and closed the case on the real culprit at last. No must have been something.\n\nThe whole Casebook seemed to snap shut, satisfied. Somewhere out in the city, a coincidence quietly confessed to being a cause.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Deducton held a feast where every dish was served with the reason it had been chosen, which the city considered the highest honour reason could pay a guest. The mayor gave a speech that reached a verdict, the Chief Inspector was seen to almost smile, and the gate-keeper waived the toll a second historic time and filed the cause for the record.\n\nAt the end they gave Hunchik the Plan-Drawer: the founding instrument that had mapped every scene in the city true to scale, so no deduction taken from it could ever be led astray. Hunchik went home asking why of every hedgerow, and finding, for once, that everything had a good answer.",
        midText: "Deducton was grateful in a slightly circumstantial way \u2014 the feast came with two reasons that turned out to be coincidences and the mayor's verdict was appealed twice. Still, the causes bound to their effects, the cases closed on the guilty, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Chief Inspector pressed the Plan-Drawer into Hunchik's keeping: the founding scene-mapping instrument. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes are.' Hunchik went home already reasoning out a tighter case, because a thing worth proving is worth proving again.",
        grantItem: "hunchik_plan" },
    },
  },
  burrowl: {
    id: "burrowl",
    title: "The Warren That Wouldn't Add Up",
    tagline: "Tallywarren keeps every grain counted to the last, and the counts have started slipping. Only a burrower who reckons true can make the whole city add up again.",
    items: [
      { id: "burrowl_frame", name: "The Counting-Frame", stat: 0, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won with a sharp Arithmetic check early in the adventure." },
      { id: "burrowl_book",  name: "The Order-Book",      stat: 2, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "burrowl_gauge", name: "The Grain-Gauge",     stat: 3, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Science. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
    ],
    components: { grain: "A True Grain", weight: "A Balanced Weight", bushel: "The Founding Bushel" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🐛", text: "Burrowl counted in its sleep and got it right. Down in the dark where a burrower spends its days, counting is not a hobby but a survival: how many grains laid by, how many mouths to the spring, how many tunnels dug and how many yet to dig. Get the count wrong and a warren starves in a good year or hoards itself sick in a bad one. So Burrowl reckoned everything, twice, and had never in a long digging life mistaken a total, which is a rarer boast than it sounds.\n\nSo when the alarm burrowed up from Tallywarren \u2014 the granary-city where every grain is counted into the Great Tally, so no one ever wants \u2014 that the counts had started SLIPPING overnight, stores adding to nonsense, the same sack tallying three different totals, the whole city unable to say what it had, Burrowl oiled the counting-frame and went.\n\n'A warren that can't count its grain,' said Burrowl, 'is a warren that will feast itself empty and then starve wondering why. Bring the good frame. We are going to make this city add up if we have to count it grain by grain.'", next: "count_check" },
      count_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83e\uddee", stat: 0, dc: 10,
        text: "A tallyman's spilled kit lay strewn along the tunnel, and among the warped frames and lying scales one true counting-frame remained \u2014 if you could tell it from the ones that had started slipping.\n\nThe test was pure reckoning: the true frame totalled the same forwards and backwards, every bead honest, while the slipped ones dropped a count one way and gained it the other. Add along a bad frame and you'd trust a total that drifts.\n\nSomebody quick with Arithmetic had better pick the true frame.",
        success: "found_frame", fail: "count_miss" },
      count_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\udeb1", text: "The frames all totalled plausibly if you didn't check both ways, which is how slipped frames keep working. Burrowl added along one a beat too fast, missed the drift, and by the time the tallyman's runner had gathered the kit and scurried off, the true frame had gone with it.\n\n'It will count wrong for somebody, and a warren will lay by too little,' said Burrowl grimly. (The Counting-Frame stays lost this time. Another run may reckon its way to it.)", next: "post_fight" },
      found_frame: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83e\ude84", grantItem: "burrowl_frame", text: "The true frame proved itself at once: totalled the same forwards and backwards, every bead sliding honest, no drift anywhere in the count. The Counting-Frame, worn smooth by a lifetime of true sums.\n\nSome tallyman had carried this and lost it and never trusted a total since. Burrowl did, immediately, and the beads settled under its touch into rows that meant exactly what they said.\n\nThe Counting-Frame is yours. (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)", next: "post_fight" },
      post_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It hunched over the parish tally-post \u2014 the marker that counts out the safe ration per family this season \u2014 a Miscount, small and grey, patiently making the numbers slip, so the post now read 'RATION: 4 SACKS, OR 7, OR POSSIBLY NONE, HARD TO SAY'.\n\nBurrowl's segments bristled. A tally-post that can't hold a number is how a family lays by nothing for winter.\n\n'That post counted clean this morning,' said Burrowl, in the tone of an accountant who has found an error on purpose. The Miscount, unrepentant, slipped a few more.",
        enemy: { name: "The Tally-Post Miscount", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed true, and a whole side of the Miscount was forced to hold its number \u2014 the count stood firm for one honest instant before crumbling.",
          strikeText: "The Miscount made the numbers slip, and for a moment nobody could hold a total; somebody counted their footing, lost count halfway, and stumbled.",
          defeatText: "The Miscount slipped so many numbers it lost count of itself, reached a total of nothing, and honoured it by ceasing to exist. The tally-post read RATION: 4 SACKS, firmly, which was a number a family could plan a winter around." },
        victory: "post_win", retreat: "post_retreat" },
      post_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Burrowl re-counted the post on the frame, twice, both ways. Total held. 'Second one this week,' Burrowl noted. First the home granary-books gone to nonsense, now a parish tally-post slipping its ration.\n\nSomething was tunnelling through the country making numbers slip, and Tallywarren, whose whole life was the exact count of its grain, sat right at the end of its dig.", next: "crossroads" },
      post_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "A step back, Burrowl reckoned, does not subtract from one's resolve provided one keeps a true count of why. Burrowl kept the count and took the low tunnel, losing a slow half-hour and a little faith in the honesty of numbers.\n\nBehind them the Miscount went back to slipping the post. Burrowl did not look back. Looking back at a Miscount, Burrowl knew, only lets it slip your own count while you're distracted.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The tunnel forked three ways beneath a signpost whose numbers were quietly rearranging themselves.\n\nThe left arm read THE GRANARY ROW, where they trade by the sack and dare you to fault the count. The middle arm read THE ROOT-BRIDGE, a lattice of roots you cross by the sound ones. The right arm read THE SLIPWAYS, of which the sign said only: NO COUNT HOLDS HERE. NUMBERS SLIP. MIND YOUR TOTALS.\n\nSomewhere down each way, the tallymen swore, a True Grain waited to be earned \u2014 one grain you could be certain of. A true grain, like a true friend, is never simply lying about.", options: [
        { label: "Take the Granary Row", next: "row", preview: "Out-reckon a grain-factor at the count. A hard number puzzle guards the True Grain." },
        { label: "Cross the Root-Bridge", next: "bridge", preview: "Cross by the sound roots. A dice challenge guards the True Grain." },
        { label: "Enter the Slipways", next: "fog", preview: "The slipping dark where no count holds. A Fumbler guards the True Grain. This one is a fight." },
      ] },
      row: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "The Granary Row rang with the pour of grain and the patter of factors. 'COUNTED TRUE, EVERY SACK!' cried one, thumb on the scale. 'NEVER A GRAIN SHORT!' cried another, several grains short.\n\nAt the last stall, kept under a glass bell, lay a True Grain, single and golden and somehow heavier than a grain should be. The master factor, a mole with a scoop in one paw and a swindle in the other, watched Burrowl notice it and smiled the smile of a purse being weighed light.", next: "row_puzzle" },
      row_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "'The True Grain? That's my prize stock,' said the mole. 'Settle a factor's wager, digger. I've a heap of grain I count out in fives and have two left over, and count out in sevens and have three left over. What's the SMALLEST heap bigger than ten that does both? Answer true, or you buy at Row rates, which always leave me the remainder.'", question: "A number leaves remainder 2 when divided by 5, and remainder 3 when divided by 7. What is the smallest such number greater than 10?", options: ["12", "17", "22", "24", "31"], answer: 1,
        hint: "List the numbers above 10 that leave remainder 2 when divided by 5: 12, 17, 22, 27, ... Now check which of THOSE also leaves remainder 3 when divided by 7.",
        solution: "Numbers leaving remainder 2 on division by 5: 12, 17, 22, 27, ... Test each against division by 7: 12 leaves 5, 17 leaves 3 \u2713. So 17 is the smallest that fits both (17 = 3\u00d75 + 2 and 17 = 2\u00d77 + 3).",
        good: "row_good", mid: "row_mid" },
      row_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "grain", text: "The mole checked it against its own heap, twice, tried to find a smaller answer, found none, and conceded 'seventeen' with the grace of a creature that would rather have kept the remainder.\n\n'Both counts, dead on. You'd reckon me out of my own row. Take the grain. Take it before I owe you interest.'\n\nComponent found: A True Grain.", next: "converge" },
      row_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The mole worked the true number out on its counting-board, helpfully and by the grain. Row rates being Row rates, the reckoning cost every coin Burrowl carried and the grain stayed under its bell.\n\n'Come back with a sharper count,' the mole advised. 'Sharp reckoning trades cheaper than slow, in this row.'", next: "converge" },
      bridge: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "The Root-Bridge was a lattice of great roots across the sinkhole, and you crossed it, said a shrew, by the sound ones. A living root bore your weight; one the Miscount had hollowed only looked as if it would, and the sinkhole below counted no survivors.\n\nBurrowl, who could feel a hollow root through a paw the way it could feel a wrong total, studied the lattice. There was nothing for it but to read the sound roots and go.", next: "bridge_dice" },
      bridge_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 2, text: "Nearly all the roots are sound; only one in six is hollowed through. Roll 2 or more on a six-sided die to find the living roots and cross. The shrew keeps score from a safe knot.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 2 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["5/6", "2/3", "1/2", "3/4"], answer: 0, why: "Only a roll of 1 finds the hollow root; 2 through 6 all bear you \u2014 that is 5 outcomes out of 6, so 5/6. Fine odds, but one-in-six still gives way. Reading the odds first steadies your footing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "bridge_good", fail: "bridge_miss" },
      bridge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "grain", text: "Burrowl read the lattice root by root and crossed on the living ones alone, to a let-down squeak from the shrew. Lodged in a root-fork on the far side, dropped by some crosser who trusted a hollow one, lay a True Grain, golden and certain.\n\nComponent found: A True Grain.", next: "converge" },
      bridge_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The one-in-six hollow root is exactly the one Burrowl trusted; good odds do occasionally do this. The root gave, and the sinkhole took Burrowl for a jarring slide to a ledge below.\n\nWhatever the far fork held, it kept. Some crossings give one sound count and no more.", next: "converge" },
      fog: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Slipways were tunnels where no count would hold: mark ten paces and arrive at seven, count your steps and lose the total halfway, watch a heap of grain tally differently each time you looked. Burrowl felt its own reliable count begin, alarmingly, to slip.\n\nAt the dead centre, on a spill of miscounted grain, something big was going along a rack of tally-sticks making each one's notches slip, and chuckling at the nonsense left behind. A Fumbler: the Muddle poured into the shape of a lost count. And balanced on its shapeless head, worn like a crown by a thing that had never held a number, sat a True Grain, stubbornly still counting as one.", next: "fog_fight" },
      fog_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The Fumbler turned without hurry. Things shaped like a lost count never worry; worrying would mean keeping track.\n\n'DON'T COUNT,' it mumbled, in a voice like beads dropped and scattered. 'WHY COUNT? SOME. ENOUGH. ROUGHLY. NUMBERS ARE SO TIRING. LET THEM SLIP.'\n\n'Numbers are how a warren lives to spring,' said Burrowl, and squared its segments, which is grub for rolling up all its sleeves at once.",
        enemy: { name: "The Fumbler", emoji: "👹", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a whole rack of slipped tallies snapped back to their honest totals \u2014 a slab of the Fumbler was forced to add up and, unable to, fell away.",
          strikeText: "The Fumbler made the numbers slip, and for a moment no count in the tunnel held; somebody counted out a safe number of steps and arrived, miscounted, at a wall.",
          defeatText: "The Fumbler slipped so many counts it could not tally even itself, arrived at a total of zero, and respected the result by not existing. The True Grain rolled off its head and came to rest, still and singular and exactly one." },
        victory: "fog_win", retreat: "fog_retreat" },
      fog_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "grain", text: "Burrowl recovered the True Grain and, unable to leave a rack of tallies in nonsense, spent a patient while re-cutting each slipped tally-stick back to its true notch-count so the next tallyman could trust it. A count set right is a winter a warren survives.\n\nComponent found: A True Grain.", next: "converge" },
      fog_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥾", text: "The Slipways sided with the Fumbler, slipping counts faster the harder they pushed, and drove the company back to tunnels where a number would hold, worn out and low on cheer. They took the one way whose paces they could still count true.\n\nBehind them the Fumbler went back to its rack, crown balanced, slipping the truest tallies first because they resist most sweetly. Burrowl did not look back. Looking back at a Fumbler only lets it slip the count you were keeping.", next: "converge" },
      converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three ways met again at a milestone reading TALLYWARREN: 5, and the 5 was counted out beneath it in five honest notches that stayed five however you looked, which everyone found steadying.\n\nAhead the tunnels sloped toward the Reckoning Stream, one clean run of water where every pebble stayed the number it was, the last true count, the tallymen said, between here and whatever was slipping Tallywarren's totals.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💎", die: 10, target: 4, text: "The Reckoning Stream had firm stepping-stones over most of its width, a few undercut. Roll 4 or more on a ten-sided die to count the sound stones and cross. The current keeps its own tally.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 4 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["3/5", "7/10", "1/2", "4/5"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10 all find firm stone \u2014 that is 7 outcomes out of 10, so 7/10. Good odds, though three times in ten a stone gives. Reading the odds first steadies your crossing: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "weight_good", fail: "weight_miss" },
      weight_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💎", grantComponent: "weight", text: "Burrowl counted the sound stones and crossed sure-footed, and there in a still pool where the stream had trued it over a century lay a Balanced Weight \u2014 the reference weight that every honest scale is set against, that has never once weighed heavy or light.\n\nComponent found: A Balanced Weight.", next: "logic_check" },
      weight_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "One stone counted sound and was undercut; a hidden hollow is a quiet liar. Burrowl trusted it, it tipped, and the stream took Burrowl for a brisk unplanned wade to the shallows.\n\nWhatever the pool held, it kept. The run went on, a shade more muddled.", next: "logic_check" },
      logic_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\ud83e\udde9", stat: 2, dc: 11,
        text: "Dusk found a retired quartermaster at his fire, glaring at three conflicting stores-reports.\n\n'It's a REASONING knot, this,' he said. 'Three storehouses, three reports, and each has a clue about which report is the honest one and which two are cooked. Only one story fits all three clues. It's not a counting job, it's a thinking job. Somebody with good Logic should reason out which storehouse to trust.'\n\nHe looked up hopefully. A quartermaster's favour is worth carrying, and it keeps.",
        success: "logic_solved", fail: "logic_stumped" },
      logic_solved: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udd13", bonusStars: 2, text: "It came clear the way good reasoning does: two clues alone left two reports in the running, but the third ruled one out flat, and once all three clues were laid together only the honest storehouse survived. Its count matched the grain on the floor exactly.\n\nThe quartermaster beamed and pressed a parcel of provisions on the company, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 \u2b50)", next: "camp_choice" },
      logic_stumped: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83d\udc4b", text: "The reports defeated everyone; each candidate satisfied two clues and broke the third, until the quartermaster folded them away with the patience of a man long outwitted by his own stores.\n\n'Never mind,' he said kindly. 'They've disagreed for seasons. A night more won't reconcile them.' The fire ticked. Nobody quite met an eye.", next: "camp_choice" },
      camp_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "The quartermaster's fire was warm and the dark tunnel beyond it was not.\n\n'Tallywarren's an hour on,' he said. 'Run my urgent stores-orders through the night and I'll owe you \u2014 quartermasters pay their debts, to the grain. Or bed down here and go safe at dawn. Night dig's chancy, chancier than usual, with things about that slip a body's count of where it is.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll, but you roll with advantage. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up one heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward. You arrive rested, and the provisions are surprisingly good." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The night dig branched where the counts had slipped. With the quartermaster's lantern letting you count the true turnings, you go with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to hold the true path.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher \u2014 what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fall short. Each falls short half the time, so both fail 1/2 \u00d7 1/2 = 1/4 of the time \u2014 meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage is powerful, and now you know exactly how powerful. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Burrowl counted the true turnings through the slipping dark and brought the company to Tallywarren's gate before moonrise, filed the quartermaster's orders in the crooked box, and pocketed the favour: three bright stars and a supper that counted a whole heart's worth of nerve back into everyone.\n\nA risk is a sum, Burrowl held, and once you've reckoned the odds and the odds have agreed to stay reckoned, it is a sum worth doing. Bonus: +3 \u2b50 at the end, and one heart restored.", next: "guard_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The slipping dark won. Burrowl miscounted a turning shaped exactly like overconfidence into a dead-end, the orders got sodden, and the favour was politely withdrawn on grounds of condition.\n\nThe company dug the last stretch in a pointed silence. Good odds still lose sometimes, Burrowl reflected. That is precisely what makes them odds and not totals.", next: "guard_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "Burrowl slept curled in a snug side-burrow, which is the most sensible way for a grub to sleep, and woke to a morning it could count on. The provisions proved, once tallied, a delight.\n\nThe dig took the promised hour exactly, which Burrowl found deeply satisfying. Sometimes the true move is the unhurried one. Expected value, Burrowl told the company, comes out the same whether you rush the count or not.", next: "guard_fight" },
      guard_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Tallywarren's gate stood in a great root-arch, taking its old toll of one true answer per traveller. This morning the arch was under new and worse management.\n\nTwo Fumbler footpads had strung a slipping abacus across it that totalled, differently each time, that the toll was ALL YER COUNTS. Behind her bolted shutters the gate-keeper glared like a kettle at the boil.\n\n'Your abacus won't hold the same total twice,' said Burrowl, 'so it can't have added up a toll at all,' which as war-cries go was at least numerate.",
        enemy: { name: "Fumbler Footpads", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and one footpad's slipping beads were forced to a fixed total \u2014 it held the number a moment, could not bear the certainty, and fell apart.",
          strikeText: "A footpad slipped the count, and somebody in the company paid a toll of totals they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second footpad tried to slip its own count away from the danger, slipped past zero into a total that made no sense even to it, and stopped. The abacus went slack. Nobody re-strung it." },
        victory: "guard_win", retreat: "guard_retreat" },
      guard_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The gate-keeper unbolted shutters, door and scowl in order.\n\n'Toll's waived for pest control,' she said, sweeping the slipping abacus aside. 'First in an age. But nobody passes without answering \u2014 gate's rules, and truest of all on a day the whole warren's stopped adding up.' She slid across a slate worn smooth by generations of sums.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The company fell back with the footpads' scattered laughter behind, and spent a disorienting quarter-hour in a side-tunnel whose length would not stay counted.\n\nThey reached the gate by the side wicket, footsore and simmering. The keeper took stock of them and said nothing. 'Bad morning,' she allowed. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering.' Out came the slate.", next: "guard_puzzle" },
      guard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔢", text: "The slate was old, the chalk worn, the sum in the keeper's careful hand. 'Take your time,' she said, timing it.", question: "A warren lays by grain across a week: 3 sacks on Monday, 5 on Tuesday, 7 on Wednesday, and so on, adding 2 more each day through Sunday. How many sacks in the whole week?", options: ["49", "56", "63", "70", "77"], answer: 2,
        hint: "The daily amounts are 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15 \u2014 seven numbers. You can pair the first and last (3 + 15), or use: number of days \u00d7 the average of the first and last day.",
        solution: "The seven days give 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15. Their average is (3 + 15) \u00f7 2 = 9, and there are 7 days, so the total is 9 \u00d7 7 = 63 sacks. (Adding directly: 3+5+7+9+11+13+15 = 63.)",
        good: "guard_good", mid: "guard_mid" },
      guard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the sum, sniffed her sniff of respect, and stamped Burrowl TRUE in one firm figure.\n\n'Go on through,' she said, nearly warm, 'and hurry. The Founding Tally's coming loose, and a warren that counts every grain into that one book becomes, without it, a heap of guesses. From what the crows say, it's most of the way to guessing.'", next: "tallywarren" },
      guard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The keeper shook her head, not unkindly, turned the slate and walked the series-sum through, then had Burrowl say it back twice, because twice is where a count sets firm.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and waved the company through the side wicket, the long way, past the seed-store, who could not believe their luck twice in a morning.", next: "tallywarren" },
      tallywarren: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Tallywarren at last: a city of tunnels and granaries and counting-halls, where every grain is tallied into the Great Tally so no burrow ever wants, dug by folk who held that a city is only as safe as its willingness to count what it has, honestly, to the last grain.\n\nBut the counts were slipping. A grey confusion lay over the halls, thickest at the Central Granary, where the Founding Tally \u2014 the master reckoning that every store in the city is counted against \u2014 was coming unstrung. Grey ribbons of Muddle poured from its vault, slipping the totals, so granaries reported famine and glut in the same breath and no burrow could say whether it faced a feast or a winter.\n\nBurrowl dug inward, faster, frame at the ready.", next: "speed_check" },
      speed_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "\u23f3", stat: 4, dc: 12,
        text: "The Granary's counting-hall was in chaos: a great spill of grain was pouring from a burst bin, and it had to be caught, scooped and counted into measures FAST \u2014 before it drained away down the slip-tunnels and the day's whole store was lost and uncountable. There was no time to count it SLOWLY, grain by careful grain; it had to be measured true at speed.\n\nQuick hands and a quicker count: scoop, measure, tally, keep pace with the pour.\n\nSomebody FAST had better measure the spill before it's gone.",
        success: "speed_quick", fail: "speed_slow" },
      speed_quick: { type: "story", emoji: "\u26a1", text: "Burrowl moved faster than a grub has any right to \u2014 scoop, level, tally, scoop \u2014 and measured the whole spill true into the bins the instant before the last of it could drain away, the day's store saved and counted on Burrowl's quick sure reckoning.\n\nUp on the counting-gantry the Keeper of the Tally made a brisk approving click, which by her standards was an ovation.", next: "core_puzzle" },
      speed_slow: { type: "story", emoji: "\ud83c\udf42", text: "The grain drained faster than it could be measured, and it took the better part of an hour to save and count even what was left, checking each measure twice. By the end the light had failed and the confusion had crept a shade further through the granary, as if handed exactly the hour it wanted.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "core_puzzle", setMid: true },
      core_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🌾", text: "The Keeper of the Tally stood at a great counting-board. 'Prove you can reckon a share out true,' she said, in a voice like a total struck clean, 'and you may touch the Founding Tally. The trouble is in the vault. But the truth is here, and the truth is only for those who can count it.'", question: "Grubs are sharing a store of grain. If each grub takes 6 measures, 8 measures are left over. If each grub takes 9 measures, they are 7 measures short. How many grubs are there?", options: ["3", "4", "5", "6", "7"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Let the number of grubs be g. Write the total store two ways: as 6g + 8 (six each, 8 left) and as 9g \u2212 7 (nine each, 7 short). Set them equal.",
        solution: "The store equals 6g + 8 and also 9g \u2212 7. Setting them equal: 6g + 8 = 9g \u2212 7, so 15 = 3g, giving g = 5 grubs. (Check: 6\u00d75 + 8 = 38 and 9\u00d75 \u2212 7 = 38 \u2014 the store is 38 measures.)",
        good: "bushel_good", mid: "bushel_mid" },
      bushel_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎯", grantComponent: "bushel", text: "The Keeper's brisk face broke into something like delight.\n\nFrom a niche in the counting-board \u2014 'the founder's own measure, for a mind that reckons a share out true, which is you' \u2014 she lifted the Founding Bushel: the first true measure, against which every store in the city had once been counted. 'It has never once measured false,' she said, laying it in Burrowl's keeping. 'It won't mind measuring something new.'\n\nComponent found: The Founding Bushel.", next: "core_fight" },
      bushel_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Keeper sighed the sigh of one who has watched a great many people guess at a share, and worked the two-count reckoning through for Burrowl, line by line.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, quoting somebody. But the Founding Bushel stayed in its niche, for a mind that could reckon it unaided.", next: "core_fight" },
      core_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "In the granary's vault, something vast lay heaped over the Founding Tally, slipping its every total. The Great Miscount was bigger than its footpads and greyer than its Fumblers, and everywhere its slipping weight lay, the city's counts came loose and the stores tallied to famine and glut at once.\n\nIt turned two uncountable eyes on the company at the vault stair.\n\n'DON'T COUNT,' it slurred. 'ENOUGH. SOME. PLENTY. WHO KNOWS. WHO NEEDS TO. LET THE TALLY SLIP.'\n\n'A warren that stops counting is a warren that starves surprised,' said Burrowl, and set its segments for the last time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Great Miscount", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a whole ledger of the Tally snapped back to its honest totals \u2014 the Great Miscount lost its grip on a quarter of the city's stores and reeled.",
          strikeText: "The Great Miscount slipped the counts, and for a breath no total in the vault held; somebody counted out a safe number of steps and, miscounted, found one too few beneath them.",
          defeatText: "The Great Miscount slipped so many totals it lost its own count entirely, reckoned itself down to nothing, and by the honesty of that final sum ceased to be. Its last word was 'ROUGHLLL\u2014', which pleased Burrowl, since even its ending refused to total." },
        victory: "core_win", retreat: "core_retreat" },
      core_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Ledger by ledger the Founding Tally struck true, and across Tallywarren the counts came home: granaries reported honest stores, families knew what they had, and every grain in the city sat, once more, in a total that held. The grey confusion withdrew. Outside, a tally-post that had read famine-and-glut settled, very quietly, on a number a burrow could live by.\n\nThe Keeper of the Tally appeared at the gantry, surveyed the re-struck city, and dipped her head one exact degree, which the warren agreed was, by any count, unprecedented.", next: "forge" },
      core_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Great Miscount slipped so many totals that the vault door was the only thing that still counted as one thing, so the company took it.\n\nThey rallied in a snug side-burrow, wrung out and furious and, after a short vote, fortified with the quartermaster's provisions. Then they came back by the vault stair while the Miscount slipped the Tally below. Not every fight is won by winning it, Burrowl noted. Some are won by keeping your count and arriving anyway.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Off the vault glowed the workshop of Grist, maker of true measures, who could set a slipped count straight from twenty paces and had once re-struck a shattered resolve, though she wouldn't dwell on it.\n\n'A Grain-Gauge, is it?' she said, over spectacles fine enough to count grains through. 'Not made one since the founders' day. A True Grain, a Balanced Weight, the Founding Bushel \u2014 bring me all three and I'll make you a gauge that reads the true measure of anything, no matter how it's slipping.'",
        forged: "The forge roared its total. Grist set the True Grain in the Founding Bushel, trued it against the Balanced Weight, and struck the whole into one honest gauge.\n\nWith the Grain-Gauge, Burrowl found it could read the true measure of any real thing \u2014 the actual weight, the actual volume, the actual count behind the claim \u2014 the way a scientist trusts the instrument over the guess. Counting, it turned out, is only Science with the grains still in it.\n\nThe Grain-Gauge is yours! (+1 Science, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "Grist laid the components out and counted them, which took her no time at all.\n\n'Short,' she said, not unkindly, tapping each gap. 'A Gauge wants all three or it's a wish you can't weigh. Come back with the full set. The forge isn't going anywhere, and nor is the arithmetic.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "burrowl_gauge", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the founding entry at the heart of the Great Tally.\n\nBurrowl dug down to it and found the trouble: the city's very first store had been counted, once, long ago, by somebody in a hurry, as 'enough, near enough' \u2014 and a warren that counts every grain against a founding total of 'near enough' will reckon true for a thousand years and then, all at once, find that no count holds and never quite did. The slipping had spread from that one lazy total out through every tally that leaned on it, out of the vault and into the streets.\n\nBurrowl scratched out the near enough, re-counted the founding store true against the Bushel with the frame, twice, both ways, and struck the total exact. No near enough.\n\nThe whole Tally seemed to settle and hold. Somewhere out in the city, a slipping tally-post steadied, very quietly, on a number that stayed put.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "Tallywarren held a feast where every guest's share was counted out exactly equal, which the warren considered the highest honour a count could pay a guest. The mayor gave a speech of precisely reckoned length, the Keeper of the Tally was seen to almost smile, and the gate-keeper waived the toll a second historic time and entered it, correctly, in the book.\n\nAt the end they gave Burrowl the Order-Book: the founding record of how every count in the city fits with every other, so no tally taken from it could ever fall out of order. Burrowl dug home counting the roots as it passed, and finding, for once, that the count of everything came out whole and stayed there.",
        midText: "Tallywarren was grateful in a slightly approximate way \u2014 the feast came out a share short and a share over and the mayor's speech was recounted twice. Still, the tallies held, the granaries knew their stores, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate the Keeper pressed the Order-Book into Burrowl's keeping: the founding record of how the counts fit together. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes are.' Burrowl dug home already reckoning a truer attempt, because a thing worth counting right is worth counting again.",
        grantItem: "burrowl_book" },
    },
  },

  /* ---------------- UNCOMMON-TIER ADVENTURE (bigger, dual-branch) ---------------- */
  multimoo: {
    id: "multimoo",
    title: "The Herd That Would Not Multiply",
    tagline: "Every meadow in the valley should have doubled its flock by the old midsummer reckoning. Instead the numbers just sit there, refusing to grow — and only a cow who keeps her times tables in her bones is going to find out why.",
    items: [
      { id: "multimoo_tally",    name: "The Drover's Tally-Stick",   stat: 0, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won by counting straight in the tall grass at the very start of the journey." },
      { id: "multimoo_herdbook", name: "The Herd-Book of True Counting", stat: 2, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "multimoo_crook",    name: "The Multiplying Crook",      stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components gathered along three different roads." },
    ],
    components: { valley_bell: "The Valley Bell", mill_gear: "The Millpond Gear", copse_horn: "The Copse Horn" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      /* ========================= ACT ONE: out of the Long Byre ========================= */
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🐄", text: "Multimoo had never once lost count of anything. Not the slats in a fence (forty-one, and the forty-first was wonky), not the buttercups in the top field (variable, but always exactly countable), and certainly not the herd — every calf, every cousin, every third-removed relation who'd wandered in from a neighbouring valley and simply never left.\n\nSo when the drovers came down from the high pastures white about the muzzle, saying the flocks had stopped multiplying — that the old midsummer reckoning, when every well-kept herd doubled clean as a coin-flip, had come and gone and left the numbers exactly where they'd started — Multimoo did not wait to be asked twice.\n\n'Get the halters,' said Multimoo, already counting the ones on the peg. 'Three. Good. That's enough for company.'", next: "tally_check" },
      tally_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔔", stat: 0, dc: 10,
        text: "The lane out of the Long Byre ran through grass gone to seed, and somewhere in it, half-buried, something brass caught the early light. Then something else did. A whole scatter of somethings, the way a dropped bag of bells scatters when nobody picks it up in time.\n\nMultimoo had seen this before: a drover's kit, spilled and abandoned in a hurry, glinting in exact proportion — roughly one bell in six worth stooping for, the rest just grass-wet brass with nothing behind it. The trick was never the stooping. The trick was working out which sixth.\n\nSomebody sharp with Arithmetic had better do the counting before the dew finished the job of hiding them.", success: "tally_found", fail: "tally_missed" },
      tally_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🔔", grantItem: "multimoo_tally", text: "The count came out clean — thirty bells, five worth a look, one worth the stoop — and the stoop landed true: a drover's tally-stick, notches worn glassy by a thumb that had never once miscounted a head of stock.\n\nMultimoo turned it over. Good balance. True notches. Whoever dropped this had been exact right up until the moment they weren't, and that was a small worrying thought to carry into a valley where the maths itself had stopped working.\n\nThe Drover's Tally-Stick is yours. (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)", next: "plank_dice" },
      tally_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🔔", text: "The count wobbled. Thirty bells, or thirty-one? The sixth kept sliding, the stoop landed on a lump of quartz that only looked promising, and whatever tally-stick had really been down there stayed down there, keeping its own counsel.\n\n'It'll turn up on someone else's reckoning,' Multimoo said, which was true, unhelpful, and delivered with the full dignity of a cow who intended to say it again later, possibly to the tally-stick itself, if they ever met.", next: "plank_dice" },
      plank_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌉", die: 20, target: 14, text: "A drainage ditch cut the lane clean across, bridged by a single plank laid down by someone who trusted planks more than the situation strictly warranted. Roll 14 or more on a twenty-sided die to cross it without a wobble.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 14 or more on a fair twenty-sided die?", options: ["7/20", "1/2", "13/20", "3/10"], answer: 0, why: "The rolls 14 through 20 all succeed — that's 7 values out of 20, so 7/20. A little worse than a third, which is exactly the kind of plank that deserves respect. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your hooves: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "plank_good", fail: "plank_miss" },
      plank_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🌾", text: "Multimoo went over in four neat steps, weight placed dead centre each time, and didn't so much as glance at the ditch on the way past. The party followed in single file, considerably less serenely.", next: "gate_fight" },
      plank_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "💦", text: "The plank had opinions about being trusted with four passengers instead of one, and voiced them by tipping Multimoo into eight inches of ditch water at the least dignified possible moment. The halter came out muddy. The morning did not improve immediately.", next: "gate_fight" },
      gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It was sitting on the parish boundary stone, small and grey and thoroughly pleased with itself: a Smudge, gently blurring the carved tally of the Long Byre's own herd from SIXTY into 'sixty-ish, give or take, who's counting.'\n\n'I am counting,' said Multimoo, setting down the halters. 'That is rather the whole point of me.'",
        enemy: { name: "The Tally Smudge", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed clean, and a corner of the Smudge sloughed away in a puff of corrected grey.",
          strikeText: "The Smudge billowed forward and the morning went briefly approximate; somebody in the party lost track of their own feet for a worrying half-second.",
          defeatText: "The Smudge came apart with an embarrassed little pop, and the boundary stone said SIXTY again — firm, carved, unblurred." },
        victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
      gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "The party dusted themselves off while the boundary stone recovered its convictions.\n\n'That's the third one this month,' Multimoo noted, filing it away with the other worrying facts. Small grey scouts, sitting on exact numbers to see what gave. And somewhere ahead, presumably, whatever was sending them.", next: "crossroads" },
      gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "There is no shame in an orderly withdrawal, Multimoo informed the party, provided everyone agrees afterwards on how many of them there still were. There were still three. This was confirmed twice.\n\nThey went the long way round by the hedge, which took the better part of an hour and left a smear of grey on the morning that did not brush off easily. Behind them, the Smudge settled back onto the boundary stone, smug as fog on a window.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "Beyond the boundary stone the lane split three ways, at a signpost that had clearly been having an argument with itself for years.\n\nThe left arm pointed to the WIDE MEADOW, where the flocks grazed in their hundreds and the counting was all about groups and grouping. The middle arm pointed to MILLPOND, where the water-wheel turned the valley's grain and its gearing turned rather stranger sums. The right arm pointed into the DEEP COPSE, where deer and stranger things left their tracks in ratios nobody quite trusted.\n\nSomewhere down each road — tinkers' gossip agreed on this much — a Valley Bell was waiting to be earned: the bell that used to hang at the old midsummer gathering, before the reckoning stopped working.", options: [
        { label: "Cross the Wide Meadow", next: "meadow_1", preview: "A grouping puzzle about a flock that used to multiply. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Visit Millpond", next: "mill_1", preview: "A dice challenge on the old mill wheel. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Wade into the Deep Copse", next: "copse_1", preview: "A fight with something that has been eating the deer-counts. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
      ] },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: MEADOW ========================= */
      meadow_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🌾", setRoute: "meadow", text: "The Wide Meadow rolled out in every direction, grazed short and even, dotted with sheep arranged — Multimoo noted with satisfaction — in genuinely countable clumps. At its centre stood the old midsummer gathering post, hung with a bare hook where a bell should have been.\n\nAn old shepherd sat beside it, worrying at a scrap of slate. 'Flock's meant to treble every season, near enough, since I were a lamb,' she said. 'Started this year at four. Reckon it should be well past two hundred head by now. It isn't. Work out where it ought to be and where it stalled, and the Bell's back on its hook for you.'", next: "meadow_puzzle" },
      meadow_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🐑", text: "The shepherd tapped the slate. 'Four sheep. Trebles every season, regular as the moon. Tell me: after how many WHOLE seasons does the flock FIRST pass two hundred head?'", question: "A flock starts at 4 sheep and trebles every season. After how many whole seasons does it first exceed 200 sheep?", options: ["3", "4", "5", "6", "7"], answer: 1,
        hint: "List the flock size season by season: 4, then ×3 each time. Stop as soon as you pass 200.",
        solution: "Season 0: 4. Season 1: 12. Season 2: 36. Season 3: 108. Season 4: 324. The flock first passes 200 after 4 whole seasons — it should be deep into the hundreds by now, not stuck at four.",
        good: "meadow_good", mid: "meadow_mid" },
      meadow_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔔", grantComponent: "valley_bell", text: "The shepherd checked the working twice, nodded once, and lifted the Valley Bell down off her own belt, where she'd been keeping it safe rather than let it hang somewhere a Smudge could get at it.\n\n'Four years past due for two hundred head, and sat on four the whole time,' she said, turning the bell over. 'Something's holding the treble still. You didn't just do my sum, cow. You told me how frightened I ought to be.'\n\nComponent found: the Valley Bell.", next: "meadow_subchoice" },
      meadow_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The working slipped — a season miscounted here, a treble applied twice there — and the shepherd walked it through from the top, patiently, the way people are patient when they've stopped expecting better.\n\n'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' she said, and did not lift the Bell down after all. It stayed on her belt, out of reach, for this visit at least.", next: "meadow_subchoice" },
      meadow_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🐑", text: "'Two more things before you go on,' said the shepherd, squinting at the sky. 'A ewe's gone missing over the far rise, and I could use a second pair of eyes counting the flock's true shape before I report it to the reeve. Can't do both — pick one, I'll manage the other myself.'", options: [
        { label: "Help count the flock's shape (a check)", next: "meadow_flock_check", preview: "A Geometry check: judge how the flock is spaced and grouped." },
        { label: "Chase down the missing ewe (a fight)", next: "meadow_runaway_fight", preview: "A scuffle with whatever spooked her into running." },
      ] },
      meadow_flock_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "📐", stat: 1, dc: 11,
        text: "The flock had drifted into a rough grid across the field, rows against columns, the way grazing animals do when nobody's herding them on purpose. The reeve would want the shape reported properly — rows, columns, the lot — not just a headcount guessed at from the gate.\n\nSomebody with a good eye for Geometry had better pace it out.", success: "meadow_flock_good", fail: "meadow_flock_bad" },
      meadow_flock_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📐", bonusStars: 2, text: "The grid came out clean — eleven rows, nine columns, not a sheep out of place in the reckoning — and the shepherd copied the figure into her ledger with real relief. 'Reeve likes a tidy grid,' she said, pressing a little purse of stars into Multimoo's hoof. 'Tidier than my nerves, this week.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      meadow_flock_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The grid kept sliding — a row here counted twice, a straggler there counted not at all — and by the time a number was agreed on, nobody entirely trusted it. The shepherd took it anyway, with a shrug that said she'd take what she could get this year.", next: "converge1" },
      meadow_runaway_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "👹", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The missing ewe hadn't wandered. She'd been chased — by a Roughling Strayling, fog packed loosely into the shape of something with too many legs for a sheep to feel comfortable near, currently herding her toward the tree line with slow, patient malice.\n\n'That's not yours,' said Multimoo, in the tone one uses on a dog that has picked up the wrong stick.",
        enemy: { name: "A Roughling Strayling", emoji: "👹", hits: 2, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer landed true, and a leg's worth of the Strayling unravelled into ordinary mist.",
          strikeText: "A fog-limb swept low and someone in the party found themselves briefly, unnervingly airborne.",
          defeatText: "The Strayling came apart into scattered weather, and the ewe trotted straight back to the flock as if nothing at all had happened, because as far as she was concerned, nothing had." },
        victory: "meadow_runaway_win", retreat: "meadow_runaway_retreat" },
      meadow_runaway_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🐑", text: "The shepherd counted the ewe back into the flock with visible relief, then counted the whole flock twice more, just to be sure, which Multimoo considered an entirely reasonable response to the morning so far.", next: "converge1" },
      meadow_runaway_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Strayling won the tug of fog-and-nerve, and drove the ewe off into the tree line before anyone could stop it. The shepherd took the loss the way shepherds take losses: hard, briefly, and then back to work, because the rest of the flock still needed minding.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: MILLPOND ========================= */
      mill_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", setRoute: "millpond", text: "Millpond's great wheel turned the way it always had, groaning gently, throwing up a steady rhythm of splash and creak that had probably been keeping the miller's family sane for four generations. The trouble, said the miller, meeting them at the sluice gate, wasn't the wheel. It was the gearing behind it, which was meant to turn the whole valley's grain reckoning and had lately started turning nothing much at all.\n\n'Wheel's fine. Sums behind it aren't. Same trouble as everyone's having, one way or another.' She nodded at the sluice. 'Time the gate right and I'll owe you the loan of the Bell that used to ring the wheel's own midsummer count.'", next: "mill_dice" },
      mill_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎡", die: 8, target: 6, text: "Roll 6 or more on an eight-sided die to time the sluice gate with the wheel's turn and keep the flow steady.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 6 or more on a fair eight-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "3/8", "5/8", "1/4"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 6, 7 and 8 succeed — 3 outcomes out of 8, so 3/8. Just over a third: the gate is fussy, and rightly so, it moves a lot of water. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your grip: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "mill_good", fail: "mill_miss" },
      mill_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔔", grantComponent: "valley_bell", text: "The gate dropped at exactly the right instant, the wheel caught the flow clean, and somewhere in the gearing a bell that hadn't rung in longer than the miller could remember gave one clear, startled chime.\n\n'There she is,' said the miller, fishing the Valley Bell out from where it had been rattling loose in the gear housing for who knew how long. 'Rings true when the timing's true. Hasn't rung at all this whole season, which tells you something.'\n\nComponent found: the Valley Bell.", next: "mill_subchoice" },
      mill_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "💦", setMid: true, text: "The gate dropped a heartbeat late, the flow lurched, and the wheel juddered through a rough half-turn that sprayed everybody standing anywhere near the sluice. Whatever was loose in the gearing stayed loose, unrung, for this visit at least.", next: "mill_subchoice" },
      mill_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "⚙️", text: "The miller wiped her hands on her apron. 'While you're here — I could use a second opinion on two things, and only the time for one. Grain sacks need weighing out proper for the toll, or the wheel's drive-gears need steadying before the next big turn. Your pick.'", options: [
        { label: "Weigh out the grain sacks (a puzzle)", next: "mill_weigh_puzzle", preview: "An Arithmetic puzzle about wheat and barley sacks." },
        { label: "Steady the drive-gears (a check)", next: "mill_wheel_check", preview: "A Science check to keep the gearing from juddering apart." },
      ] },
      mill_weigh_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🌾", text: "'Forty sacks came off the cart today,' said the miller, 'wheat and barley mixed, and I know the whole lot weighed eight hundred and twelve kilograms exactly, because the cart-scale doesn't lie. Wheat sacks run twenty-three kilos, barley seventeen. Toll-collector wants the wheat count specifically. Work it out?'", question: "A mill has 40 sacks in total — some wheat (23 kg each), some barley (17 kg each) — weighing 812 kg altogether. How many sacks of wheat are there?", options: ["18", "20", "22", "24", "26"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Let w be wheat sacks and b be barley sacks. Then w + b = 40 and 23w + 17b = 812. Substitute b = 40 − w into the second equation.",
        solution: "From w + b = 40, b = 40 − w. Substituting: 23w + 17(40 − w) = 812, so 23w + 680 − 17w = 812, giving 6w = 132, so w = 22. Check: 22 wheat and 18 barley: 23×22 + 17×18 = 506 + 306 = 812. ✓",
        good: "mill_weigh_good", mid: "mill_weigh_mid" },
      mill_weigh_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "The miller checked it against the toll-book and found the figures matched to the sack. 'Twenty-two wheat, eighteen barley,' she said, unable quite to keep the surprise out of her voice. 'That's the fastest that toll's balanced in a year.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      mill_weigh_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The sacks came out wrong twice before the miller took over, working the pairs of numbers through by hand until one finally fit both totals. 'Takes practice, this,' she said, not unkindly, restacking the sacks herself.", next: "converge1" },
      mill_wheel_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔬", stat: 3, dc: 11,
        text: "The drive-gears ran a hair's breadth out of true, chattering against each other in a way that would strip a tooth off one of them within the week if nobody intervened. Fixing it meant reading the whole mechanism at speed — which gear drove which, and in which direction — and adjusting before the next big turn of the wheel.\n\nSomebody sharp with Science had better have a look.", success: "mill_wheel_good", fail: "mill_wheel_bad" },
      mill_wheel_good: { type: "story", emoji: "⚙️", bonusStars: 2, text: "The chatter smoothed out to a contented, steady clunk, and the miller actually laughed with relief. 'That's another season out of these gears, easy,' she said, and meant it as the considerable compliment it was. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      mill_wheel_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The adjustment held for about four turns of the wheel before the chatter crept straight back, no better and no worse than before anyone had touched it. 'It'll last the week,' said the miller, in the voice of someone who has decided not to think about next week yet.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: DEEP COPSE ========================= */
      copse_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🌲", setRoute: "copse", text: "The Deep Copse smelled of wet bark and old leaf-mould, and the deer-tracks in it refused to add up the way deer-tracks should. A forester crouched over a patch of churned mud, counting hoofprints with a stick and getting a different answer every time she tried.\n\n'Something's in here eating the count,' she said, without looking up. 'Not the deer — the NUMBER of the deer. Whatever's doing it is wearing the old midsummer bell round its neck like a trophy, and hasn't the faintest idea what it's for.'", next: "copse_fight" },
      copse_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🦌", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It stood on a hummock in a clearing: an Antler Roughling, fog packed hard into something part stag and part argument, chewing thoughtfully on a mouthful of trail markers. Round its neck, on a cord, hung a Valley Bell, worn as a trophy by something with no notion what it was for.\n\n'TRESPASS,' it said, in a voice like branches cracking underfoot. 'COUNT AGAIN. WRONG.'\n\n'I intend to,' said Multimoo, 'and correctly.'",
        enemy: { name: "The Antler Roughling", emoji: "🦌", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a tine's worth of the Roughling sloughed away into drifting fog.",
          strikeText: "An antler swept low and wide, and someone in the party learned exactly how much a face full of wet bracken can sting.",
          defeatText: "The Roughling dissolved into ordinary forest damp, and the bell dropped from its unravelling neck into the leaf-mould with a small, grateful chime." },
        victory: "copse_win", retreat: "copse_retreat" },
      copse_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🔔", grantComponent: "valley_bell", text: "Multimoo fished the Valley Bell out of the leaf-mould and wiped it clean on the nearest sleeve, which happened to be its own. Sturdy, true, and — now that the Roughling wasn't wearing it as a trophy — clearly meant to hang somewhere it could be heard.\n\n'That's the fourth deer-count this month that thing's eaten,' said the forester, pocketing her tally stick with new confidence. 'Glad someone finally disagreed with it properly.'\n\nComponent found: the Valley Bell.", next: "copse_subchoice" },
      copse_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", setMid: true, text: "The Roughling held the clearing and the bell both, and the party fell back through the bracken considerably worse for wear. The forester took the news with a weary nod; this, her face said, was becoming a familiar shape of morning.", next: "copse_subchoice" },
      copse_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🌲", text: "The forester straightened up, brushing leaf-mould off her knees. 'Two more things I could use a hand with, if you've the time for one. A calf's tracks lead off toward the old quarry and I'd rather know where before dark. Or there's a stand of trees marked in bark-notches I've never managed to make sense of — might be a boundary count, might be older than that. Pick one.'", options: [
        { label: "Track the missing calf (a check)", next: "copse_track_check", preview: "A Speed check — the trail is faint and getting fainter." },
        { label: "Read the bark-notches (a puzzle)", next: "copse_bark_puzzle", preview: "A counting puzzle in a repeating pattern of tracks." },
      ] },
      copse_track_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⚡", stat: 4, dc: 11,
        text: "The trail was already fading — a bent stem here, a print already softening at the edges there — and daylight wasn't going to wait around for a careful survey. Whoever went after it needed to move fast and read faster.\n\nSomebody quick had better take the lead.", success: "copse_track_good", fail: "copse_track_bad" },
      copse_track_good: { type: "story", emoji: "⚡", bonusStars: 2, text: "The trail held together just long enough — a footprint here, a broken twig there — right up to a hollow where the calf stood, unbothered, chewing bark as if it had never been lost at all. The forester's relief was worth more than the stars, though she pressed both on Multimoo anyway. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      copse_track_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The trail thinned out to nothing by a stream crossing, and the light went with it not long after. The forester would have to try again at first light, alone this time, and said so without complaint, which somehow made it worse.", next: "converge1" },
      copse_bark_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🌳", text: "The bark-notches ran in a strict repeating pattern along a whole stand of trees: two deer-shaped notches, then three rabbit-shaped ones, over and over, the forester said, 'as far back as anyone's bothered to count.' She'd counted eighty of them, notches of both kinds together, and given up trying to separate them by eye.", question: "Notches repeat in a pattern of 2 deer-notches then 3 rabbit-notches, over and over. There are 60 notches in total. How many are deer-notches?", options: ["20", "22", "24", "26", "28"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Each repeat of the pattern uses 2 + 3 = 5 notches. How many repeats fit into 60 notches, and how many deer-notches does each repeat contain?",
        solution: "Each repeat is 5 notches long (2 deer + 3 rabbit). 60 ÷ 5 = 12 repeats. Each repeat has 2 deer-notches, so there are 12 × 2 = 24 deer-notches in total.",
        good: "copse_bark_good", mid: "copse_bark_mid" },
      copse_bark_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'Twenty-four,' the forester repeated, checking it against her own careful re-count. 'Bang on. That's a boundary mark, that pattern — someone set it a long time ago, and it still holds.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      copse_bark_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The pattern kept slipping between deer and rabbit on the recount, and the forester walked the notches herself, tapping each one aloud, until the true split finally settled. 'Easy to lose the rhythm,' she admitted. 'Everyone does, first time.'", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT THREE: shared, all lanes reconverge ========================= */
      converge1: { type: "story", emoji: "🔔", text: "Whichever road had brought them there, the party arrived at the old midsummer gathering post with a Valley Bell in hand and a fair idea, by now, that this was not the only piece of the reckoning gone missing.\n\nAhead, the land dropped toward two more landmarks the tinkers swore by: the Millpond's outer gearhouse, where a second bell's worth of mechanism was said to be jammed solid, and a stand of old horn-trees in the Copse where a third had reportedly been swallowed whole by a root system with ideas above its station.", next: "gear_dice" },
      gear_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "⚙️", die: 10, target: 7, text: "The outer gearhouse door was seized shut, its locking gear frozen mid-turn. Roll 7 or more on a ten-sided die to force it round without shearing a tooth off the mechanism.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 7 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["3/10", "1/2", "2/5", "7/10"], answer: 2, why: "The rolls 7, 8, 9 and 10 succeed — 4 outcomes out of 10, so 4/10 = 2/5. A little less than even, which is about right for a gear that's been seized all season. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your push: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "gear_good", fail: "gear_miss" },
      gear_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔧", grantComponent: "mill_gear", text: "The gear gave all at once, with a groan like a held breath finally let out, and behind it sat the Millpond Gear — the mechanism's own reckoning-piece, exactly where it should never have been able to get stuck at all.\n\nComponent found: the Millpond Gear.", next: "horn_check" },
      gear_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", setMid: true, text: "The gear held fast, sheared a tooth for its trouble, and the door stayed exactly as seized as it had been before anyone leaned on it. Whatever mechanism was jammed behind it would have to wait for a stronger shoulder, another day.", next: "horn_check" },
      horn_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧠", stat: 2, dc: 12,
        text: "Deep among the horn-trees, a root system had grown up through something buried, swallowing it a fraction more each season the way roots do when nobody's watching closely enough. A faint, muffled chime came from somewhere underneath, bouncing oddly off the trunks — the echo alone wouldn't say which root, or how deep.\n\nSomebody with sharp Logic had better work out where the sound was really coming from, not just where it seemed to be.", success: "horn_good", fail: "horn_bad" },
      horn_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎺", grantComponent: "copse_horn", text: "The echo gave itself away in the end — bounced twice off the near trunk, arriving late, which meant the true source sat further back and lower than it first seemed. A little careful digging turned up the Copse Horn, root-marked but whole, the last of the three.\n\nComponent found: the Copse Horn.", next: "tinker_scene" },
      horn_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The reasoning went round in circles along with the echo, and the root system kept its secret a while longer. Whatever was buried under those horn-trees would have to be found by someone with fresher ears, another visit.", next: "tinker_scene" },
      tinker_scene: { type: "story", emoji: "🏕️", text: "Dusk found the party at a tinker's camp on the valley road, where a kettle sang and a widdershins clock ticked determinedly the wrong way, on the grounds that time should be mended whichever direction it happened to be going.\n\n'Gear train's in bits,' said the tinker, nodding at a disassembled tangle on the workbench. 'Three wheels, meshed all wrong, and I'm blowed if I can see the true order of them without doing the sum properly. Not a screwdriver job. A THINKING job.'", next: "tinker_puzzle" },
      tinker_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚙️", text: "The tinker laid the three gears out in a row. 'First gear turns eight times for every three turns of the second. Second turns five times for every two turns of the third. Third gear's turned ninety times today, near as the wear on it shows. How many times has the FIRST gear turned, all told?'", question: "In a three-gear train, the first gear turns 8 times for every 3 turns of the second, and the second turns 5 times for every 2 turns of the third. If the third gear turns 90 times, how many times does the first gear turn?", options: ["500", "550", "600", "650", "700"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Find how many times the second gear turns for the third gear's 90 turns, then use the first ratio on that result.",
        solution: "From gear2 : gear3 = 5 : 2, gear2 = (5/2) × 90 = 225 turns. From gear1 : gear2 = 8 : 3, gear1 = (8/3) × 225 = 600 turns.",
        good: "tinker_good", mid: "tinker_mid" },
      tinker_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "The gears clicked into their true order on the first try, meshing clean all the way down the train. The tinker beamed and pressed a parcel of travelling biscuits on the party, plus the favour, plus interest. (+2 ⭐)", next: "night_choice" },
      tinker_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🕰️", text: "The ratios tangled on the first two attempts before falling into place on the third, by which point the tinker had long since stopped watching and started quietly re-boiling the kettle. 'Gets easier,' she said, kindly, which was probably true.", next: "night_choice" },
      night_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏕️", text: "'Village proper's an hour on from here,' said the tinker, banking the fire. 'Push on through the dark and carry my post, and I'll owe you a favour worth having. Or sleep here by the kettle and go safe at dawn. Night road's chancy this season — chancier than usual, if the gossip's to be believed.'", options: [
        { label: "Press on through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A d6 roll with ADVANTAGE. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up a heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward, but the kettle is genuinely excellent." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 6, target: 5, advantage: true, text: "The night road wound like a sum done in a hurry. With the tinker's lantern swinging ahead, roll with ADVANTAGE: roll the six-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 5 or more to keep your footing in the dark.", predict: { question: "One roll of this die succeeds (5 or more) a third of the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher — what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/3", "5/9", "2/3", "4/9"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fail. Each fails 2/3 of the time, so both fail 2/3 × 2/3 = 4/9 of the time — meaning you succeed the other 5/9 of the time. Advantage turns a rough third into better-than-even odds. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Multimoo brought the party into the village before the moon was properly up, posted the tinker's letters into the crooked box by the gate, and pocketed the promised favour: three bright stars and a hot supper that put a whole heart's worth of courage back into everybody. Bonus: +3 ⭐ and one heart restored.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", setMid: true, text: "The night road won this time. Multimoo spent an undignified spell in a bramble patch shaped exactly like a bad decision, the letters got scuffed, and the favour was quietly withdrawn on grounds of postal condition.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "The party slept by the tinker's fire and woke to a morning so crisp it practically squeaked. The widdershins clock struck six going backwards, which everyone by now agreed still counted, and the walk to the village took exactly the hour promised. Sometimes the boring move is the right one.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      ambush_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪓", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The village toll-bridge stood where the valley narrowed, taking its usual toll of one correct sum per traveller — except this morning it was being run by the wrong management. Roughling Drovers had set up a hand-painted sign reading TOLE: EVRYTHING, while the real toll-keeper glared from behind a bolted shutter.\n\n'That's not even a real word,' said Multimoo, which as declarations of war go was at least accurate.",
        enemy: { name: "Roughling Drovers", emoji: "🪓", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and one of the Drovers thinned noticeably, the way fog does when told firmly what time it actually is.",
          strikeText: "A grey fist swept the toll-plank aside, and somebody in the party paid a toll they had not agreed to.",
          defeatText: "The second Drover looked at the space where the first had been, considered its career prospects, and dissolved on its own initiative. The sign fell in the ditch. Nobody picked it up." },
        victory: "ambush_win", retreat: "ambush_retreat" },
      ambush_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The toll-keeper unbolted his shutter, then his expression, in that order. 'Waived, for pest control,' he said, sweeping the fallen sign aside with one professional boot. 'But nobody crosses without answering. Rules are rules, especially on days like this.' He slid a slate across the counter.", next: "tollhouse_puzzle" },
      ambush_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The party fell back down the road with the Drovers' laughter rolling after them, and came at the bridge eventually by the toll-keeper's side path, scratched and simmering. 'Bad morning,' he said. 'It's going round. But nobody crosses without answering.' Out came the slate anyway.", next: "tollhouse_puzzle" },
      tollhouse_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🛒", text: "'Take your time,' said the toll-keeper, in the voice of someone counting it.", question: "A tollhouse charges 4 groats per cart and 7 groats per wagon. In one day it counts exactly 25 vehicles in total, for a total of 142 groats. How many wagons passed through?", options: ["12", "13", "14", "15", "16"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Let c be carts and w be wagons. Then c + w = 25 and 4c + 7w = 142. Substitute c = 25 − w into the money equation.",
        solution: "c + w = 25, so c = 25 − w. Then 4(25 − w) + 7w = 142, giving 100 − 4w + 7w = 142, so 3w = 42, meaning w = 14. Check: 11 carts and 14 wagons: 4×11 + 7×14 = 44 + 98 = 142. ✓",
        good: "toll_good", mid: "toll_mid" },
      toll_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The toll-keeper checked the slate twice, made a small noise that in toll-keeper dialect meant real admiration, and waved the party straight through. 'Go on,' he said, almost smiling. 'And hurry — from what the carters say, the village could use someone who counts like that.'", next: "act4_intro" },
      toll_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The toll-keeper shook his head, not unkindly, and worked the true sum out step by step on the slate, making the party repeat it back before he'd wave them through the side gate. 'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' he said, and meant it.", next: "act4_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FOUR: second branch — a return visit, route-dependent bonus ========================= */
      act4_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🗺️", text: "Word in the village was that the valley's reckoning hadn't broken all at once. It had come apart at three specific seams — Meadow, Millpond, Copse — the very three the party had already walked. Going back to whichever one they knew best now, properly forewarned, might turn up something a first, hurried pass had missed.", next: "act4_choice" },
      act4_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🔁", text: "'Only time for one more proper look before the trail goes cold,' said the village elder, spreading a hand-drawn map on the table. 'Meadow, Millpond, or Copse — wherever you already know your way around ought to go easiest.'", options: [
        { label: "Return to the Wide Meadow", next: "act4_meadow", preview: "A Geometry check — much easier if you already know the Meadow." },
        { label: "Return to Millpond", next: "act4_millpond", preview: "A dice roll — much easier if you already know Millpond." },
        { label: "Return to the Deep Copse", next: "act4_copse", preview: "A fight — much easier if you already know the Copse." },
      ] },
      act4_meadow: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "📐", stat: 1, dc: 13, routeBonus: { route: "meadow", amount: 3 },
        text: "The Meadow's boundary stones had shifted a hand's width here and there over the years, throwing the whole field's true area into doubt — exactly the kind of resurvey that goes twice as fast for someone who's already walked every furlong of the place once today.\n\nSomebody sharp with Geometry had better resurvey the boundary properly.", success: "act4_meadow_good", fail: "act4_meadow_bad" },
      act4_meadow_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📐", bonusStars: 3, text: "The resurvey came out clean and, for anyone who'd walked the Meadow already that day, almost embarrassingly quick — the ground was familiar underfoot in a way that made every measurement fall into place. The shepherd's reeve accepted the new boundary without a single query. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_meadow_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The stones refused to agree with themselves however carefully they were paced out, and the resurvey went down as provisional, pending someone with fresher legs and a clearer head.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_millpond: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎡", die: 12, target: 8, routeBonus: { route: "millpond", amount: 3 },
        text: "The sluice gate needed timing once more, this time against the wheel's full-speed turn rather than its gentle morning one — a much fussier business, though considerably less fussy for hooves that already knew the gate's particular sticking point. Roll 8 or more on a twelve-sided die.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 8 or more on a fair twelve-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "5/12", "2/3", "1/3"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 8 through 12 succeed — 5 outcomes out of 12, so 5/12. Just under half, which feels about right for a gate this particular about its timing. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your hand: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "act4_mill_good", fail: "act4_mill_bad" },
      act4_mill_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎡", bonusStars: 3, text: "The gate dropped at exactly the right instant against the wheel's full pace, and the whole mechanism settled into a smoother rhythm than the miller had heard from it in months. She pressed a purse of stars into Multimoo's hoof without being asked twice. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_mill_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "💦", text: "The full-speed timing proved a rougher business than the morning's gentle version, and the gate dropped just late enough to send a fresh spray over everybody nearby. The miller took it in good humour, mostly.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_copse: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌲", qLevel: 3, routeBonus: { route: "copse", amount: 3 },
        text: "A second Roughling had moved into the clearing where the Antler one had fallen — smaller, greyer, packed into the shape of dense undergrowth: a Thicket Smudge, already blurring the forester's fresh tally of the horn-trees. Ground that had already been walked once that day was, at least, ground the party knew how to move through fast.\n\n'Not again,' said Multimoo, without much surprise.",
        enemy: { name: "The Thicket Smudge", emoji: "🌲", hits: 3, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and a tangle of the Smudge's undergrowth-shape came apart into ordinary drifting leaf-mould.",
          strikeText: "A branch-shaped limb caught someone across the shoulder, and the whole clearing went briefly, unhelpfully approximate.",
          defeatText: "The Thicket Smudge dissolved into scattered leaves, and the horn-tree tally held steady at last, unblurred." },
        victory: "act4_copse_win", retreat: "act4_copse_retreat" },
      act4_copse_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌲", bonusStars: 3, text: "The forester re-checked her tally against the now-settled trees and found it held, clean, for the first time in weeks. 'Whatever you did the first time through here,' she said, 'it's clearly still working.' (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_copse_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Thicket Smudge held the clearing this time, and the tally stayed blurred a while longer. The forester sighed the sigh of someone who has started to suspect this is simply how the season is going to go.", next: "act5_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FIVE: reconverge, forge, finale ========================= */
      act5_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🏘️", text: "Whatever the second visit had turned up, the trail now led, unmistakably, to the old Reckoning Hall at the heart of the valley — the building where the midsummer count had always been struck, and where, judging by the grey ribbons pouring gently from its windows, something had gone very wrong indeed.\n\nMultimoo checked the halters. Still three. This was, by now, an entirely automatic reflex.", next: "captain_fight" },
      captain_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Inside, something large was leaning over the Hall's great ledger of herds, smearing every entry into 'thereabouts' with a wet grey thumb. The Herd Captain was bigger than its Smudges and greyer than its Roughlings, and it looked up with eyes like un-inked full stops.\n\n'CLOSED,' it said. 'FOR RECKONING.'\n\n'You're rounding down an entire valley's herds,' said Multimoo, setting down the halters for good this time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Herd Captain", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer landed like an audit, and the Captain lost an edge it had clearly been fond of.",
          strikeText: "The Captain's fist came down and the whole hall went approximate; the flagstones were suddenly only mostly underfoot.",
          defeatText: "The Captain went the way of all fog when the sun means it: edges first, opinions last. Its final word was 'THEREABO—', which pleased Multimoo more than it probably should have." },
        victory: "captain_win", retreat: "captain_retreat" },
      captain_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Entry by entry, the ledger's herd-counts crept back into their true places, and the grey ribbons stopped pouring from the windows. Somewhere outside, a flock that had been stuck on four for four seasons took its first proper step toward the two hundred it was owed.", next: "forge" },
      captain_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Captain's fist came down again and the hall went so approximate that the doors were the only certain thing left, so the party used them, regrouped in the square, and came back in by the side stair while the Captain glowered over the main floor below, smearing on.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Reckoning Hall stood the workshop of a smith who could true a bent gear from twenty paces and had once, reportedly, argued a broken promise back into shape. 'A Multiplying Crook, is it,' she said, peering over half-moon spectacles. 'Valley Bell, Millpond Gear, Copse Horn — show me all three, and I'll make you a crook worth leaning on.'",
        forged: "The forge roared like an approving crowd. The smith set the bell, the gear and the horn together into a crook's curved head, and hung the whole thing on a cord fit for a working drover's shoulder.\n\nHeld up against a flock, the Multiplying Crook simply told you, at a glance, exactly how many were there — no recount required, ever.\n\nThe Multiplying Crook is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "The smith counted the components on her bench, then counted them again, which was polite of her, because it didn't take long.\n\n'Short,' she said, tapping each gap. 'A crook wants all three or it's just a stick with opinions. Come back with the full set. Forge isn't going anywhere.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "multimoo_crook", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the great ledger itself.\n\nMultimoo found the trouble on a page near the back: a single entry that had been told, once, long ago, 'close enough' — and had been quietly refusing to multiply ever since, the way anything does if you tell it that often enough. The rot had spread down every column that touched it, out of the ledger and into every meadow, millpond and copse in the valley.\n\nMultimoo crossed out the roughly. Multimoo wrote in the exactly, in a hoofprint-steady hand, and underlined it twice.\n\nSomewhere in the valley, a flock that had been stuck on four for four seasons finally, audibly, began to multiply.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "The valley threw a midsummer gathering to make up for the one it had missed, with exactly the right number of everything, which everybody agreed was the highest compliment a valley could pay itself. The shepherd's flock had already trebled twice by the time the bell — properly hung again — rang the reckoning in.\n\nAt the end of it, the valley pressed the Herd-Book of True Counting into Multimoo's keeping: the record of every count, in order, that would never again let a 'roughly' creep in unchallenged. Multimoo walked home counting the stars, and found, for once, that the count came out whole and stayed there.",
        midText: "The valley was grateful, in a slightly muddled way — the gathering ran a touch short on everything and the reckoning bell rang a beat late. Still, the flocks were multiplying again, the mills were turning true, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate, the valley elder pressed the Herd-Book of True Counting into Multimoo's keeping anyway. 'You got here,' she said. 'The route was scenic. Routes often are.' Multimoo walked home already planning a tidier attempt, because a count worth getting right is worth getting right again.",
        grantItem: "multimoo_herdbook" },
    },
  },

  owlgorith: {
    id: "owlgorith",
    title: "The Archive of Answers That Argue",
    tagline: "Every riddle ever solved in the Owl Archive is meant to stay solved, forever, full stop. Lately, though, the old answers have started arguing with each other in the small hours of the night — and only an owl who thinks in strict logic is going to find out why.",
    items: [
      { id: "owlgorith_loupe", name: "The Archivist's Loupe", stat: 2, how: "found", desc: "+1 Logic. Won by counting carefully through drifting dust and old paper, right at the start of the journey." },
      { id: "owlgorith_index",  name: "The Index of True Shelves", stat: 3, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Science. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "owlgorith_quill",  name: "The Reckoning Quill", stat: 1, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Geometry. Forged from three components gathered along three different reading-rooms." },
    ],
    components: { brass_bookend: "The Brass Bookend", silver_ribbon: "The Silver Ribbon", iron_key: "The Iron Key" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      /* ========================= ACT ONE ========================= */
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🦉", text: "Owlgorithm had never once left a riddle half-solved. Not the one about the bridge and the fox and the very particular chicken, not the one about the two doors and the two guards, and certainly not the seventeen-volume set on prime numbers that the Archive's founder had left, unfinished, four hundred years ago out of what everyone agreed was simple spite.\n\nSo when the night-clerks reported that the Archive's own ANSWERS had started disagreeing with each other — the same riddle giving a different solution depending on which shelf you asked — Owlgorithm did not wait to be asked twice.\n\n'Bring lanterns,' said Owlgorithm, already three steps down the spiral stair. 'And something to write on. This is going to need working through properly.'", next: "loupe_check" },
      loupe_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔍", stat: 2, dc: 10,
        text: "The lane to the Archive ran through drifts of old paper, blown loose from some upper window, settling in the corners like snow that had given up on being clean. Somewhere in it, a lens caught the lamplight — then another glint, then a whole scatter of them, brass and glass among the drifts.\n\nOwlgorithm had seen this before: about one glint in seven was a genuine lens, the rest just foil scraps and old spectacles catching the light the same way. The trick was never the looking. The trick was counting sevenths correctly before the draught rearranged everything.\n\nSomebody sharp with Logic had better do the counting.", success: "loupe_found", fail: "loupe_missed" },
      loupe_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🔍", grantItem: "owlgorith_loupe", text: "The count came out clean, and the seventh glint turned out to be exactly what it promised: an archivist's loupe, brass-ringed, the glass inside it worn perfectly smooth by a thousand careful readings.\n\nOwlgorithm turned it over once. Whoever had owned this had clearly never once misread a footnote. That was either a very good sign or a very worrying one, this deep into a mystery about answers going wrong.\n\nThe Archivist's Loupe is yours. (+1 Logic, kept forever.)", next: "dust_dice" },
      loupe_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🔍", text: "The count wobbled — six glints, or seven? — and the one that mattered slid under a drift before the sevenths could be pinned down. Owlgorithm made a note of the failure, which is itself a kind of logic, and pressed on into the Archive.", next: "dust_dice" },
      dust_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "📜", die: 20, target: 17, text: "A cascade of loose index cards blocked the stairwell, decades of cross-references shaken free by some argument higher up. Roll 17 or more on a twenty-sided die to pick a clean path through without scattering them further.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 17 or more on a fair twenty-sided die?", options: ["1/5", "1/4", "3/10", "2/5"], answer: 0, why: "The rolls 17 through 20 all succeed — that's 4 outcomes out of 20, and 4/20 = 1/5. A narrow path indeed. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your talons: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "dust_good", fail: "dust_miss" },
      dust_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📇", text: "Owlgorithm picked a path through the drifted cards without disturbing a single cross-reference, which the Archive itself seemed to appreciate: a faint, grateful rustle followed all the way down the stairwell.", next: "gate_fight" },
      dust_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "💨", text: "The cards went everywhere. Owlgorithm spent an undignified ten minutes re-filing footnotes by lamplight, muttering the whole while about the general uselessness of gravity.", next: "gate_fight" },
      gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It sat on the Archive's own threshold stone, small and grey and pleased with itself: a Smudge, gently blurring the carved shelf-number from SEVEN into 'seven-ish, could be six, who's counting.'\n\n'I am counting,' said Owlgorithm, setting down the lantern. 'That is, in point of fact, the whole point of me.'",
        enemy: { name: "The Footnote Smudge", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed clean, and a corner of the Smudge sloughed away into corrected grey mist.",
          strikeText: "The Smudge billowed forward and, for one worrying moment, every number in sight went approximate at once.",
          defeatText: "The Smudge came apart with an embarrassed little pop, and the threshold stone said SEVEN again — carved, certain, unblurred." },
        victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
      gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "Owlgorithm dusted off and stepped over the (now correctly numbered) threshold. Small grey scouts, sitting on exact numbers to see what gave — and somewhere deeper in the Archive, whatever was sending them.", next: "crossroads" },
      gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "There is no shame in an orderly withdrawal, provided it is properly filed afterwards. It was filed. Owlgorithm went in by the side door instead, trailing a faint smear of grey that took the rest of the night to shake off.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏛️", text: "Just past the threshold, the Archive split three ways under a sign that had clearly been re-lettered several times by several different hands.\n\nThe left passage led to the READING ROOM, where the riddle-scrolls were kept and cross-checked. The centre passage led to the MAP ROOM, where the shelf-plans themselves were drawn and redrawn. The right passage led to the VAULT, where the oldest and touchiest answers were kept under lock.\n\nSomewhere down each passage — the night-clerks agreed on this much — a Brass Bookend was waiting to be earned, the very bookend that used to hold the Archive's founding index steady.", options: [
        { label: "Search the Reading Room", next: "reading_1", preview: "A number-riddle guards the Bookend. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Search the Map Room", next: "map_1", preview: "A dice challenge guards the Bookend. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Search the Vault", next: "vault_1", preview: "A fight guards the Bookend. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
      ] },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: READING ROOM ========================= */
      reading_1: { type: "story", emoji: "📖", setRoute: "reading", text: "The Reading Room was hushed in the particular way that only rooms full of unread riddles can manage. On the central lectern sat a scroll that had clearly been troubling the night-clerks: a number-riddle, unsolved, its answer supposedly the key to the Brass Bookend's hiding place.", next: "reading_puzzle" },
      reading_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📜", text: "The scroll read: 'I am a number less than 100. I am a multiple of 7. My digits add to 9. What am I?'", question: "A number is less than 100, a multiple of 7, and its digits add up to 9. What is the number?", options: ["49", "56", "63", "70", "91"], answer: 2,
        hint: "List the multiples of 7 under 100, then add up the digits of each one until you find a match.",
        solution: "The multiples of 7 under 100 are 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, 42, 49, 56, 63, 70, 77, 84, 91, 98. Their digit sums are 7, 5, 3, 10, 8, 6, 13, 11, 9, 7, 14, 12, 10, 17. Only 63 has a digit sum of 9 (6+3=9).",
        good: "reading_good", mid: "reading_mid" },
      reading_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔖", grantComponent: "brass_bookend", text: "The lectern clicked open at exactly '63', and there inside sat the Brass Bookend, engraved with a founding date nobody in the room could quite agree on, which felt appropriate.\n\nComponent found: the Brass Bookend.", next: "reading_subchoice" },
      reading_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The lectern refused every wrong guess with a small disapproving click, and by the time '63' was finally reached the Bookend's hiding place had already reset itself for the next visitor. Some locks are patient like that.", next: "reading_subchoice" },
      reading_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "📚", text: "A night-clerk hurried over. 'Two things, if you've a moment. Something's been rustling in the reference stacks that isn't a draught. Or I could use a second opinion on whether a very old cross-reference actually holds up. Pick one — I'll manage the other.'", options: [
        { label: "Investigate the rustling (a fight)", next: "reading_moth_fight", preview: "Something has been eating footnotes." },
        { label: "Check the old cross-reference (a check)", next: "reading_hush_check", preview: "A Science check on a very particular kind of old evidence." },
      ] },
      reading_moth_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🦋", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The rustling turned out to be a Roughling Silverfish, fog packed loosely into something with far too many legs for comfort, currently working its way through an entire shelf of footnotes with visible satisfaction.\n\n'Those are load-bearing,' said Owlgorithm, which is not a sentence that gets said about footnotes nearly often enough.",
        enemy: { name: "A Roughling Silverfish", emoji: "🦋", hits: 2, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a leg's worth of the Silverfish unravelled into ordinary dust.",
          strikeText: "Something many-legged brushed past in the dark, and somebody in the party made a noise they would later deny making." },
        victory: "reading_moth_win", retreat: "reading_moth_retreat" },
      reading_moth_win: { type: "story", emoji: "📖", text: "The Silverfish dissolved into scattered dust, and the footnotes it had been eating were, mercifully, mostly the boring ones. The night-clerk counted the survivors with real relief.", next: "converge1" },
      reading_moth_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Silverfish won the shelf for the night, and finished off a footnote that several scholars would sorely miss come morning. The night-clerk took the loss with the weary calm of someone who has filed this exact complaint before.", next: "converge1" },
      reading_hush_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔬", stat: 3, dc: 11,
        text: "The cross-reference in question claimed that two entirely different riddles, catalogued a century apart, were secretly the same riddle wearing different words. Testing that claim properly meant checking the actual structure underneath the wording, not just the surface phrasing.\n\nSomebody sharp with Science had better verify it.", success: "reading_hush_good", fail: "reading_hush_bad" },
      reading_hush_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "The structures matched exactly, once the surface wording was stripped away — the cross-reference held. The clerk practically glowed with vindication and pressed a small purse of stars into Owlgorithm's wing. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      reading_hush_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The structures very nearly matched but not quite, and the old cross-reference had to be quietly withdrawn. 'Close doesn't count in an Archive,' the clerk said, without much bitterness, refiling it.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: MAP ROOM ========================= */
      map_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🗺️", setRoute: "map", text: "The Map Room held the Archive's own floor-plan, redrawn so many times over the centuries that nobody entirely trusted the current version. A cartographer-clerk stood beside a locked drawer, visibly nervous. 'Bookend's in here. Drawer's rigged to a rather old mechanism. Timing's everything.'", next: "map_dice" },
      map_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🔐", die: 12, target: 10, text: "Roll 10 or more on a twelve-sided die to time the drawer's release mechanism correctly.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 10 or more on a fair twelve-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "1/3", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 2, why: "The rolls 10, 11 and 12 succeed — 3 outcomes out of 12, and 3/12 = 1/4. A fussy old mechanism, and rightly cautious about it. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your grip: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "map_good", fail: "map_miss" },
      map_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔖", grantComponent: "brass_bookend", text: "The drawer released with a satisfying click precisely on time, and there inside — wedged between two truly ancient maps — sat the Brass Bookend.\n\nComponent found: the Brass Bookend.", next: "map_subchoice" },
      map_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", setMid: true, text: "The timing landed a half-second late, and the drawer jammed itself shut out of pure mechanical spite. Whatever it was guarding stayed guarded, for this visit at least.", next: "map_subchoice" },
      map_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🧭", text: "The cartographer-clerk wrung their hands. 'Two more things, if you've the patience. There's a numbering puzzle on the new floor-plan I can't work out, or the compass rose itself needs resetting true. Pick one.'", options: [
        { label: "Solve the floor-plan puzzle (a puzzle)", next: "map_grid_puzzle", preview: "A grid-numbering puzzle about how the plan snakes across the floor." },
        { label: "Reset the compass rose (a check)", next: "map_compass_check", preview: "A Geometry check to true the compass again." },
      ] },
      map_grid_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🗺️", text: "'The new floor-plan numbers the shelving in a snake,' said the clerk, tracing it with one claw. 'Ten shelves to a row, left to right along the first row, then right to left along the next, back and forth, on and on. I've lost track of which shelf sits directly behind which.'", question: "Shelves are numbered 1-100 in a snake pattern, 10 per row: row 1 goes left-to-right (1-10), row 2 right-to-left (11-20), row 3 left-to-right (21-30), and so on. Which shelf number is directly behind (in the next row from) shelf 23?", options: ["36", "37", "38", "39", "40"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Work out which column shelf 23 sits in during its own row, then find the shelf in that same column in the next row — remembering the next row runs in the opposite direction.",
        solution: "Row 3 (shelves 21-30) runs left-to-right, so shelf 23 is the 3rd shelf along (column 3). Row 4 (shelves 31-40) runs right-to-left, so its column 1 is shelf 40, column 2 is 39, column 3 is 38. Directly behind shelf 23 is shelf 38.",
        good: "map_grid_good", mid: "map_grid_mid" },
      map_grid_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'Thirty-eight, exactly,' said the clerk, checking it against the physical shelving itself. 'That's the first time anyone's worked that out without just walking the whole row to check.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      map_grid_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The snake pattern kept doubling back in the working, and the clerk eventually walked the actual shelves to settle it by counting. 'Easy to lose the thread,' they admitted. 'Everyone does, on the first try.'", next: "converge1" },
      map_compass_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "📐", stat: 1, dc: 11,
        text: "The compass rose inlaid in the Map Room floor had drifted a few careful degrees off true, throwing every bearing taken from it slightly wrong. Resetting it meant sighting three separate reference points and finding where they genuinely agreed.\n\nSomebody sharp with Geometry had better do the sighting.", success: "map_compass_good", fail: "map_compass_bad" },
      map_compass_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📐", bonusStars: 2, text: "The three sightings agreed to the exact degree, and the compass rose clicked back to true with a small satisfied sound. The clerk breathed out properly for the first time all night. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      map_compass_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The three sightings disagreed by just enough to matter, and the compass stayed a few degrees off true. 'It'll do for tonight,' the clerk said, in the voice of someone who has decided not to think about tomorrow's bearings yet.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: VAULT ========================= */
      vault_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🔒", setRoute: "vault", text: "The Vault held the Archive's oldest and touchiest answers, the ones nobody was quite willing to let go of even when they'd stopped making sense. Guarding the door — and, worse, guarding the Brass Bookend inside — was something that had clearly been standing there a very long time.", next: "vault_fight" },
      vault_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🗝️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "A Roughling Custodian stood fused to the doorframe, fog packed hard into the shape of an official who has never once approved a request on the first attempt.\n\n'RETURN LATER,' it said, in a voice like a stamp coming down on a form. 'CONSULT AGAIN NEVER.'\n\n'That's not even proper grammar,' said Owlgorithm, which as declarations of war go was at least accurate.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Custodian", emoji: "🗝️", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer landed like a returned form, correctly filled, and a corner of the Custodian's officious bulk sloughed away.",
          strikeText: "A grey stamp-hand came down hard, and somebody in the party found out exactly what being officially declined feels like." },
        victory: "vault_win", retreat: "vault_retreat" },
      vault_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🔖", grantComponent: "brass_bookend", text: "The Custodian dissolved into ordinary bureaucratic fog, and the Vault door swung open on the Brass Bookend, sitting exactly where the oldest ledger said it should be.\n\nComponent found: the Brass Bookend.", next: "vault_subchoice" },
      vault_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", setMid: true, text: "The Custodian held the doorframe and, by extension, the Vault. The party fell back, filed a mental complaint, and resolved to try the paperwork again another night.", next: "vault_subchoice" },
      vault_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🔑", text: "Inside, a junior clerk looked up nervously. 'While you're here — the inner lock's sticking, and there's a keeper's combination-riddle nobody's cracked in years. Time for one, I'm afraid.'", options: [
        { label: "Free the sticking lock (a check)", next: "vault_lock_check", preview: "A Speed check — the mechanism won't wait." },
        { label: "Crack the combination-riddle (a puzzle)", next: "vault_combo_puzzle", preview: "A logic-deduction puzzle from three keepers." },
      ] },
      vault_lock_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⚡", stat: 4, dc: 11,
        text: "The inner lock had a habit of re-seizing if you didn't work it through fast enough, faster than careful thought really allowed for. This one called for quick hands over quick reasoning.\n\nSomebody fast had better take it on.", success: "vault_lock_good", fail: "vault_lock_bad" },
      vault_lock_good: { type: "story", emoji: "⚡", bonusStars: 2, text: "The lock gave way in one clean motion, faster than it had any right to. The junior clerk looked almost disappointed not to get to watch a longer struggle. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      vault_lock_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The lock seized halfway and stayed seized, stubborn as only very old mechanisms can be. It would need a locksmith, and possibly an apology.", next: "converge1" },
      vault_combo_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔢", text: "The junior clerk produced a note, three lines in three different hands. 'Three keepers set this combination between them, ages ago. Nobody wrote down the actual number — only this.' The note read: 'The first keeper's digit is double the second's. The second keeper's digit is triple the third's. The third keeper's digit is 1.'", question: "Three keepers set a 3-digit combination. The first keeper's digit is double the second's. The second keeper's digit is triple the third's. The third keeper's digit is 1. What is the combination (first, second, third in order)?", options: ["631", "621", "641", "613", "316"], answer: 0,
        hint: "Work from the keeper you know for certain (the third), then use each clue in turn to build up to the first.",
        solution: "The third keeper's digit is 1. The second is triple that: 3×1 = 3. The first is double the second: 2×3 = 6. Reading first, second, third in order: 6, 3, 1 — the combination is 631.",
        good: "vault_combo_good", mid: "vault_combo_mid" },
      vault_combo_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'631,' breathed the junior clerk, trying it against the old lock plate. It turned on the first attempt. 'Nobody has cracked that in this clerk's living memory.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      vault_combo_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The chain of clues tangled twice before falling into place, and by the time '631' was reached the clerk had long since fetched a spare candle for the wait. 'Gets easier with practice,' they said, kindly.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT THREE: shared ========================= */
      converge1: { type: "story", emoji: "🔖", text: "Whichever room had given up its Brass Bookend, Owlgorithm returned to the Archive's central hall with a growing certainty that this was not the only piece of the founding index gone missing.\n\nTwo more landmarks awaited, the night-clerks agreed: a length of Silver Ribbon that used to mark the true middle of the great catalogue, and an Iron Key that opened the one shelf nobody currently remembered the contents of.", next: "ribbon_dice" },
      ribbon_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎗️", die: 10, target: 8, text: "The catalogue's spine had come loose from its binding, and only a steady claw could slot it back without tearing the old paper. Roll 8 or more on a ten-sided die.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 8 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/5", "3/10", "7/10"], answer: 2, why: "The rolls 8, 9 and 10 succeed — 3 outcomes out of 10, so 3/10. A properly narrow chance, and rightly so for paper this old. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your claw: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "ribbon_good", fail: "ribbon_miss" },
      ribbon_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🎗️", grantComponent: "silver_ribbon", text: "The spine slotted back cleanly, and from between the very middle pages fell the Silver Ribbon — the catalogue's own bookmark, worn soft, that had marked its true centre for longer than anyone currently on staff.\n\nComponent found: the Silver Ribbon.", next: "key_check" },
      ribbon_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", setMid: true, text: "The spine slipped at the last moment and the old paper tore a little at the edge — nothing fatal, but the ribbon stayed lost somewhere in the loose pages, unfound this visit.", next: "key_check" },
      key_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧠", stat: 2, dc: 12,
        text: "Nobody could quite remember which shelf the Iron Key actually opened, only that it was somewhere in the older stacks, mislabelled at some point in the last century by someone with dreadful handwriting. Working it out meant reasoning through decades of shelf-moves, not just searching by eye.\n\nSomebody with sharp Logic had better trace it.", success: "key_good", fail: "key_bad" },
      key_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🗝️", grantComponent: "iron_key", text: "The chain of shelf-moves untangled cleanly, decade by decade, and led straight to a drawer nobody had opened in memory: the Iron Key, exactly where the reasoning said it had to be.\n\nComponent found: the Iron Key.", next: "scribe_scene" },
      key_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The reasoning tangled somewhere around the third decade of shelf-moves, and the trail went cold. The Iron Key stayed wherever the dreadful old handwriting had left it, unrecovered.", next: "scribe_scene" },
      scribe_scene: { type: "story", emoji: "🖋️", text: "Deep in the Archive, a scribe sat hunched over a disassembled writing-mechanism, three brass gears laid out on a cloth. 'Reckoning engine's in bits,' the scribe said, not looking up. 'Gears mesh in a ratio nobody's written down properly. Not a screwdriver job. A THINKING job.'", next: "scribe_puzzle" },
      scribe_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔢", text: "'I'm after the smallest number,' said the scribe, 'that leaves remainder 3 when divided by 4, AND remainder 5 when divided by 6. The gears won't mesh until we've named it properly.'", question: "What is the smallest positive number that leaves a remainder of 3 when divided by 4, and a remainder of 5 when divided by 6?", options: ["11", "15", "17", "23", "29"], answer: 0,
        hint: "List numbers that leave remainder 3 when divided by 4 (3, 7, 11, 15, ...), then check each one against the second condition.",
        solution: "Numbers leaving remainder 3 when divided by 4: 3, 7, 11, 15, 19, 23... Checking each against 'remainder 5 when divided by 6': 3÷6 rem 3, 7÷6 rem 1, 11÷6 rem 5 ✓. The smallest such number is 11.",
        good: "scribe_good", mid: "scribe_mid" },
      scribe_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'Eleven,' said the scribe, setting the gears to mesh at last. They clicked together clean and true. 'Fastest that's ever been named correctly in this workshop.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "night_choice" },
      scribe_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🕰️", text: "The list of candidates ran rather longer than strictly necessary before '11' turned up, and the scribe waited it out with visible patience, re-checking the cloth-laid gears all the while.", next: "night_choice" },
      night_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🕯️", text: "'The reading room proper is an hour's flight on,' said the scribe, banking the workshop candle. 'Fly the night route and carry this workshop's post, and there's a favour in it. Or wait for the Archive to properly open at dawn. Night route's chancy this season, mind.'", options: [
        { label: "Fly the night route (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A d8 roll with ADVANTAGE. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up a heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward, but the reading room by daylight is genuinely pleasant." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 8, target: 6, advantage: true, text: "The night route wound between rooftops like a sum done in a hurry. Roll with ADVANTAGE: roll the eight-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 6 or more to hold a true course in the dark.", predict: { question: "One roll of this die succeeds (6 or more) a little over a third of the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher — what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 3/8", "39/64", "1/2", "5/8"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fail. Each fails 5/8 of the time, so both fail 5/8 × 5/8 = 25/64 — meaning you succeed the other 39/64 of the time. Advantage turns just-over-a-third into a genuine majority. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Owlgorithm held true course over the rooftops and delivered the scribe's post into the reading room's own letterbox before the moon had properly set. The promised favour — three bright stars, and a warm meal that put a whole heart's courage back into the party. Bonus: +3 ⭐ and one heart restored.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", setMid: true, text: "The night route won this time. Owlgorithm came down in an unfamiliar courtyard, feathers thoroughly ruffled, the post rather worse for the detour, and the promised favour quietly withdrawn.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "The party waited out the dark hours in the workshop, and the reading room opened at a properly civilised hour, sunlight falling clean across every shelf. Sometimes the boring choice is simply the right one.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      ambush_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪶", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The Archive's own toll-desk, which normally charged one correctly-answered riddle per visitor, was being run by the wrong management this morning: two Roughling Clerks had set up behind it with a hand-lettered sign reading FEE: EVRYTHING, while the real desk-keeper glared from behind a bolted grille.\n\n'That's not even spelled right,' said Owlgorithm, which as declarations of war go was, again, accurate.",
        enemy: { name: "Roughling Clerks", emoji: "🪶", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and one of the Clerks thinned noticeably, the way fog does when corrected firmly.",
          strikeText: "A grey stamp came down hard on the counter, and somebody in the party paid a fee they hadn't agreed to." },
        victory: "ambush_win", retreat: "ambush_retreat" },
      ambush_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The real desk-keeper unbolted the grille, then their expression, in that order. 'Waived, for pest control,' they said, sweeping the false sign into the bin. 'But nobody passes without a proper riddle answered. Rules are rules, especially this week.'", next: "archive_puzzle" },
      ambush_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The party fell back down the corridor with the Clerks' stamping still ringing behind them, and came at the desk eventually by the side stair, scratched and simmering. 'Bad morning,' said the keeper. 'Going round. But nobody passes without answering.'", next: "archive_puzzle" },
      archive_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📜", text: "The desk-keeper slid a slate across. 'Take your time,' they said, in the voice of someone counting it.", question: "Riddle-scrolls cost 4 sickles each and map fragments cost 9 sickles each. Owlgorithm buys 15 items in total for exactly 95 sickles. How many map fragments were bought?", options: ["5", "6", "7", "8", "9"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Let s be scrolls and m be fragments. Then s + m = 15 and 4s + 9m = 95. Substitute s = 15 − m into the second equation.",
        solution: "s + m = 15, so s = 15 − m. Then 4(15 − m) + 9m = 95, giving 60 − 4m + 9m = 95, so 5m = 35, meaning m = 7. Check: 8 scrolls and 7 fragments: 4×8 + 9×7 = 32 + 63 = 95. ✓",
        good: "archive_good", mid: "archive_mid" },
      archive_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the slate twice, made a small approving noise, and waved the party straight through. 'Go on,' they said. 'And hurry — the Archive could use someone who counts like that.'", next: "act4_intro" },
      archive_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The keeper shook their head, not unkindly, and worked the true sum out step by step, making the party repeat it back before waving them through the side gate. 'Wrong once is how you get right forever,' they said.", next: "act4_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FOUR: second branch ========================= */
      act4_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🗺️", text: "Word among the clerks was that the Archive's trouble hadn't started all at once — it had come apart at three specific seams: the Reading Room, the Map Room, the Vault. The very three rooms already searched once, in a hurry. A second, calmer look at whichever was already familiar might turn up what a first pass had missed.", next: "act4_choice" },
      act4_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🔁", text: "'Only time for one more proper look before the trail goes cold,' said the head clerk. 'Reading Room, Map Room, or Vault — wherever you already know your way around ought to go easiest.'", options: [
        { label: "Return to the Reading Room", next: "act4_reading", preview: "A Science check — much easier if you already know the Reading Room." },
        { label: "Return to the Map Room", next: "act4_map", preview: "A dice roll — much easier if you already know the Map Room." },
        { label: "Return to the Vault", next: "act4_vault", preview: "A fight — much easier if you already know the Vault." },
      ] },
      act4_reading: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔬", stat: 3, dc: 13, routeBonus: { route: "reading", amount: 3 },
        text: "A second, calmer pass through the Reading Room turned up an inconsistency in the shelving records — exactly the kind of check that goes twice as fast for someone who already knows every scroll's usual place.\n\nSomebody sharp with Science had better verify it properly this time.", success: "act4_reading_good", fail: "act4_reading_bad" },
      act4_reading_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔬", bonusStars: 3, text: "The inconsistency resolved cleanly under a second, familiar look — ground already walked once that morning made every check faster the second time round. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_reading_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The inconsistency refused to resolve however carefully it was checked, and the matter was logged as provisional, pending fresher eyes.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_map: { type: "dice", emoji: "🗺️", die: 20, target: 13, routeBonus: { route: "map", amount: 3 },
        text: "The Map Room's drawer mechanism needed timing once more, this time against a much fussier secondary catch — though considerably less fussy for claws that already knew its particular sticking point. Roll 13 or more on a twenty-sided die.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 13 or more on a fair twenty-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/5", "3/5", "1/4"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 13 through 20 succeed — 8 outcomes out of 20, so 8/20 = 2/5. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your grip: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "act4_map_good", fail: "act4_map_bad" },
      act4_map_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🗺️", bonusStars: 3, text: "The secondary catch released clean on the first proper attempt, and the cartographer-clerk nearly wept with relief, pressing a full purse of stars into Owlgorithm's wing without being asked. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_map_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The secondary catch proved rougher than the morning's version, and jammed a hair's breadth short of releasing. The clerk took it in good humour, mostly.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_vault: { type: "combat", emoji: "🔒", qLevel: 3, routeBonus: { route: "vault", amount: 3 },
        text: "A second Roughling had taken up residence exactly where the Custodian had fallen — smaller, greyer, wearing an official expression it had no real right to. Ground already fought over once that morning was, at least, ground the party knew how to move through fast.\n\n'Not again,' said Owlgorithm, without much surprise.",
        enemy: { name: "A Roughling Under-Clerk", emoji: "🔒", hits: 3, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and a stamp-shaped chunk of the Under-Clerk sloughed away into ordinary fog.",
          strikeText: "A grey official hand caught someone across the shoulder, and the whole Vault went briefly, unhelpfully approximate." },
        victory: "act4_vault_win", retreat: "act4_vault_retreat" },
      act4_vault_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🔒", bonusStars: 3, text: "The junior clerk re-checked the Vault ledger against the now-settled shelves and found it held, clean, for the first time in weeks. 'Whatever you did the first time through here,' they said, 'it's clearly still working.' (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_vault_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Under-Clerk held the Vault a second time, and the ledger stayed unsettled a while longer. The junior clerk sighed the sigh of someone who has started to suspect this is simply how the week is going to go.", next: "act5_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FIVE: reconverge, forge, finale ========================= */
      act5_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "Whatever the second visit had turned up, the trail now led, unmistakably, to the Archive's own Reckoning Hall — the room where every riddle's true answer had always been struck, and where, judging by the grey ribbons pouring gently from beneath its door, something had gone very wrong indeed.\n\nOwlgorithm checked the loupe, the note-slate, the lantern. All present. This was, by now, an entirely automatic reflex.", next: "captain_fight" },
      captain_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Inside, something large loomed over the Hall's own master ledger, smearing every settled answer into 'roughly, near enough' with a wet grey thumb. The Roughling Head Archivist was bigger than its Smudges and greyer than its Clerks, and it looked up with eyes like un-inked full stops.\n\n'FILED,' it said. 'UNDER APPROXIMATE.'\n\n'Nothing in this building is filed under approximate,' said Owlgorithm, setting down the lantern for good this time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Head Archivist", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer landed like a returned proof, and the Archivist lost an edge it had clearly been fond of.",
          strikeText: "The Archivist's fist came down and the whole Hall went approximate; the shelving was suddenly only mostly where it should be." },
        victory: "captain_win", retreat: "captain_retreat" },
      captain_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Entry by entry, the master ledger's true answers crept back into their proper places, and the grey ribbons stopped pouring from beneath the door. Somewhere in the stacks, a riddle that had been giving two different answers for a fortnight finally, quietly, settled on one.", next: "forge" },
      captain_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Archivist's fist came down again and the Hall went so approximate that the doors were the only certain thing left, so the party used them, regrouped outside, and came back in by the gallery stair while the Archivist glowered over the main floor below.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Reckoning Hall worked a binder who could true a warped spine from twenty paces and had once, reportedly, argued a forged signature back into an honest one. 'A Reckoning Quill, is it,' she said, peering over half-moon spectacles. 'Brass Bookend, Silver Ribbon, Iron Key — show me all three, and I'll bind you a quill worth writing with.'",
        forged: "The binder's own small forge roared like an approving crowd. She set the bookend, ribbon and key together into a quill's spine, and trimmed the nib true against a whetstone older than the Archive itself.\n\nHeld over a riddle, the Reckoning Quill simply wrote the true answer on its own, no cross-checking required, ever.\n\nThe Reckoning Quill is yours! (+1 Geometry, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "The binder counted the components on her bench, then counted them again, which was polite of her, since it didn't take long.\n\n'Short,' she said, tapping each gap. 'A quill wants all three or it's just a stick with ink on it. Come back with the full set.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "owlgorith_quill", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the master ledger itself.\n\nOwlgorithm found the trouble on a page near the back: a single riddle that had been answered, once, long ago, by someone in a hurry who wrote 'roughly this' instead of committing to a true answer — and the whole ledger had been quietly arguing with itself ever since, the way any reference book does if you let one entry stay uncertain too long.\n\nOwlgorithm crossed out the roughly. Owlgorithm wrote in the exactly, in a hand steady enough to settle four hundred years of disagreement, and underlined it twice.\n\nSomewhere in the stacks, every argued-over answer finally, audibly, agreed with itself.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "The Archive held a hush-quiet celebration in its own reading room, which for an Archive counts as throwing every door open at once. The head clerk gave a speech of scrupulously verified length, the binder was seen to almost smile, and the desk-keeper waived the toll a second, historic time.\n\nAt the end of it, the Archive pressed the Index of True Shelves into Owlgorithm's keeping: the founding record of where everything truly belongs, cross-checked against itself and never again likely to argue. Owlgorithm flew home counting the stars, and found, for once, that the count agreed with itself from every angle.",
        midText: "The Archive was grateful, in a slightly muddled way — the celebration ran a scroll short of what was planned and the head clerk's speech lost its place twice. Still, the answers held steady, the shelving agreed with itself, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate, the head clerk pressed the Index of True Shelves into Owlgorithm's keeping anyway. 'You got here,' they said. 'The route was scenic. Routes often are.' Owlgorithm flew home already planning a tidier attempt, because an answer worth getting right is worth getting right again.",
        grantItem: "owlgorith_index" },
    },
  },

  flaskfox: {
    id: "flaskfox",
    title: "The Cure That Cured Nothing",
    tagline: "Every remedy brewed in the valley is supposed to actually work. Lately, though, the tinctures come out flat, the salves do nothing, and the cures cure nothing at all — and only a fox who carries a whole portable laboratory in its own tail is going to work out why.",
    items: [
      { id: "flaskfox_vial",     name: "The Alembic Vial",       stat: 3, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Science. Won by measuring a spilled tincture correctly, right at the start of the journey." },
      { id: "flaskfox_ledger",   name: "The Ledger of Remedies", stat: 0, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "flaskfox_antidote", name: "The Swift Antidote",     stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components gathered along three different corners of the apothecary." },
    ],
    components: { copper_still: "The Copper Still", glass_stopper: "The Glass Stopper", waxed_seal: "The Waxed Seal" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      /* ========================= ACT ONE ========================= */
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🦊", text: "Flaskfox had never once brewed a remedy that didn't work. Not the cough tonic, not the fever salve, not even the truly ambitious potion for curing hiccups by way of curing the patient's opinion of hiccups. Measurements mattered. Ratios mattered. A tincture was a promise written in liquid, and Flaskfox did not break promises.\n\nSo when the village healer sent word that every cure brewed in the last fortnight had come out flat — tonics that tasted right and did nothing, salves that smelled correct and healed nothing — Flaskfox did not wait to be asked twice.\n\n'Fetch the travelling case,' said Flaskfox, already counting vials. 'Something's wrong with the measuring, somewhere down the whole chain, and I intend to find exactly where.'", next: "vial_check" },
      vial_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧪", stat: 3, dc: 10,
        text: "The lane to the apothecary ran past a cart that had shed its cargo — bottles everywhere, most cracked, a few still sealed and glinting in the grass. Flaskfox had seen this before: roughly one bottle in six still held its tincture intact, the rest just broken glass catching the light the same way.\n\nThe trick was never the spotting. The trick was working out which sixth, before the damp got to what was left.\n\nSomebody sharp with Science had better do the checking.", success: "vial_found", fail: "vial_missed" },
      vial_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🧪", grantItem: "flaskfox_vial", text: "The check came out clean, and the sixth bottle checked turned out whole: an alembic vial, its measuring marks still crisp, clearly the work of someone who had never once eyeballed a dosage in their life.\n\nFlaskfox turned it over. Whoever had owned this had been exact right up until the cart tipped over, which was a small unsettling thought to carry into a valley full of remedies gone wrong.\n\nThe Alembic Vial is yours. (+1 Science, kept forever.)", next: "spill_dice" },
      vial_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🧪", text: "The check wobbled — five whole bottles, or six? — and the one that mattered rolled under the cart before the sixth could be confirmed. Flaskfox noted the failure and pressed on toward the apothecary regardless.", next: "spill_dice" },
      spill_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌊", die: 20, target: 15, text: "A drainage channel had overflowed across the lane, slick with spilled tincture and considerably more slippery than plain water has any right to be. Roll 15 or more on a twenty-sided die to cross without a slip.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 15 or more on a fair twenty-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "3/10", "2/5", "1/4"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 15 through 20 succeed — 6 outcomes out of 20, so 6/20 = 3/10. A properly slick channel. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your paws: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "spill_good", fail: "spill_miss" },
      spill_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🦊", text: "Flaskfox crossed the overflow in four neat, unbothered steps, tail held high out of the worst of it. The party followed with considerably less grace.", next: "gate_fight" },
      spill_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "💦", text: "The channel had opinions about being crossed gracefully by four passengers at once, and voiced them by depositing everyone in the shallows at the least dignified possible moment.", next: "gate_fight" },
      gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It sat on the apothecary's own threshold, small and grey and thoroughly pleased with itself: a Smudge, gently blurring the dosage chart from THREE drops into 'three-ish, give or take, who's measuring.'\n\n'I am measuring,' said Flaskfox, setting down the case. 'That is, rather precisely, the whole point of me.'",
        enemy: { name: "The Dosage Smudge", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed clean, and a corner of the Smudge sloughed away in a puff of corrected grey.",
          strikeText: "The Smudge billowed forward and, for one worrying moment, every measurement in the room went approximate at once.",
          defeatText: "The Smudge came apart with an embarrassed little pop, and the dosage chart said THREE again — clear, unblurred, exact." },
        victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
      gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "Flaskfox dusted off and stepped over the (now correctly measured) threshold. Small grey scouts, sitting on exact numbers to see what gave — and somewhere deeper in, whatever was sending them.", next: "crossroads" },
      gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "There is no shame in an orderly withdrawal, provided it is properly logged afterwards. It was logged. Flaskfox went in by the side door instead, trailing a faint smear of grey that took the rest of the morning to shake off.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🏥", text: "Just past the threshold, the apothecary split three ways under a hand-painted sign.\n\nThe left door led to the HERB GARDEN, where every ingredient was grown and weighed. The centre door led to the DISTILLERY, where raw tinctures were reduced and timed. The right door led to the SICK WARD, where the worst-affected patients were being kept comfortable.\n\nSomewhere behind each door — the healer swore to this — a Copper Still was waiting to be earned, the very still that used to reduce every remedy to its true strength.", options: [
        { label: "Search the Herb Garden", next: "herb_1", preview: "A dosage-ratio puzzle guards the Still. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Search the Distillery", next: "dist_1", preview: "A dice challenge guards the Still. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Search the Sick Ward", next: "ward_1", preview: "A fight guards the Still. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
      ] },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: HERB GARDEN ========================= */
      herb_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🌿", setRoute: "herb", text: "The Herb Garden smelled of crushed leaves and old rain. A gardener knelt over a scale, visibly frustrated. 'Every remedy needs willow-bark and honeywort mixed in a fixed ratio — three parts bark to five parts wort. I've forty-five grams of honeywort measured out and haven't the faintest how much bark to add.'", next: "herb_puzzle" },
      herb_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "The gardener tapped the scale. 'Three parts willow-bark to five parts honeywort. Forty-five grams of honeywort, exactly. How much bark, to use it all up with none left over?'", question: "A remedy mixes willow-bark and honeywort in a ratio of 3:5. If there are 45g of honeywort, how much willow-bark is needed to use it all up exactly?", options: ["21g", "24g", "27g", "30g", "33g"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Work out what one 'part' weighs from the honeywort amount, then use that to find the willow-bark amount.",
        solution: "45g of honeywort is 5 parts, so one part = 45 ÷ 5 = 9g. Willow-bark is 3 parts: 3 × 9 = 27g.",
        good: "herb_good", mid: "herb_mid" },
      herb_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", grantComponent: "copper_still", text: "The gardener checked the working twice, weighed out exactly 27g of bark, and the mixture settled into the correct ratio at once. Buried under the scale, wrapped in oilcloth, sat the Copper Still.\n\nComponent found: the Copper Still.", next: "herb_subchoice" },
      herb_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The ratio came out wrong twice before the gardener worked it through by hand, step by careful step. The Still stayed wrapped under the scale, unclaimed, for this visit at least.", next: "herb_subchoice" },
      herb_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🌱", text: "'Two more things, while you're here,' said the gardener. 'A blight's got into the north beds, or I could use a second opinion on a batch that smells wrong. Time for one.'", options: [
        { label: "Deal with the blight (a fight)", next: "herb_blight_fight", preview: "Something has taken root that shouldn't have." },
        { label: "Check the wrong-smelling batch (a check)", next: "herb_smell_check", preview: "A Science check on a suspicious tincture." },
      ] },
      herb_blight_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🍄", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The blight turned out to be a Roughling Mildew, fog packed loosely into something damp and spreading, currently working its way through an entire bed of honeywort with visible relish.\n\n'That's my ratio you're eating,' said Flaskfox, which is a genuinely alarming sentence to have to say out loud.",
        enemy: { name: "A Roughling Mildew", emoji: "🍄", hits: 2, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a patch of the Mildew unravelled into ordinary damp mist.",
          strikeText: "Something cold and spreading brushed past, and somebody in the party came away smelling faintly of old cellar." },
        victory: "herb_blight_win", retreat: "herb_blight_retreat" },
      herb_blight_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌿", text: "The Mildew dissolved into scattered damp, and the surviving honeywort bed was, thankfully, mostly untouched. The gardener counted the remaining plants with real relief.", next: "converge1" },
      herb_blight_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Mildew held the bed for the day, and finished off rather more honeywort than the gardener could comfortably spare. They took the loss the way gardeners take losses: hard, briefly, then back to work.", next: "converge1" },
      herb_smell_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔬", stat: 3, dc: 11,
        text: "The batch in question smelled almost right, which in an apothecary is its own particular kind of alarming. Working out whether it was safe meant checking the underlying chemistry properly, not just trusting the nose.\n\nSomebody sharp with Science had better verify it.", success: "herb_smell_good", fail: "herb_smell_bad" },
      herb_smell_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "The chemistry checked out clean, once properly tested rather than just sniffed. The gardener breathed out with real relief and pressed a purse of stars into Flaskfox's paw. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      herb_smell_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The chemistry very nearly checked out but not quite, and the batch had to be quietly poured away. 'Close doesn't count in medicine,' the gardener said, without much bitterness.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: DISTILLERY ========================= */
      dist_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🏭", setRoute: "dist", text: "The Distillery hissed and dripped in the low light, copper pipes coiling overhead. A distiller stood beside a still, stopwatch in paw. 'Timing's everything in here. Get the drip-rate wrong and the whole batch comes out thin. Reckon you can time this one properly?'", next: "dist_dice" },
      dist_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💧", die: 10, target: 9, text: "Roll 9 or more on a ten-sided die to catch the still's drip at exactly the right moment.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 9 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "3/10", "1/5", "2/5"], answer: 2, why: "The rolls 9 and 10 succeed — 2 outcomes out of 10, so 2/10 = 1/5. A narrow window indeed. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your paw: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "dist_good", fail: "dist_miss" },
      dist_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", grantComponent: "copper_still", text: "The drip caught exactly on time, and the still hissed its approval. Wedged in the condenser coil, clearly left there by someone in a hurry long ago, sat the Copper Still itself — a spare, or perhaps the original.\n\nComponent found: the Copper Still.", next: "dist_subchoice" },
      dist_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "💧", setMid: true, text: "The drip came a half-second late, and the whole batch turned thin and useless. Whatever had been wedged in the condenser coil stayed wedged, unclaimed, for this visit.", next: "dist_subchoice" },
      dist_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "⚗️", text: "The distiller wiped their paws on an apron. 'Two more jobs, if you've a moment. The pressure gauge needs resetting true, or there's a batch-timing puzzle I can't work out. Pick one.'", options: [
        { label: "Reset the pressure gauge (a check)", next: "dist_gauge_check", preview: "A Speed check — the gauge won't wait." },
        { label: "Work out the batch timing (a puzzle)", next: "dist_timing_puzzle", preview: "An Arithmetic puzzle about the still's drip rate." },
      ] },
      dist_gauge_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⚡", stat: 4, dc: 11,
        text: "The pressure gauge had a habit of drifting if you didn't reset it fast, faster than careful thought really allowed for. This one called for quick paws over quick reasoning.\n\nSomebody fast had better take it on.", success: "dist_gauge_good", fail: "dist_gauge_bad" },
      dist_gauge_good: { type: "story", emoji: "⚡", bonusStars: 2, text: "The gauge snapped back to true in one clean motion. The distiller looked almost disappointed not to get to watch a longer struggle. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      dist_gauge_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The gauge drifted a hair further before settling, stubborn as only old brass ever manages. It would hold, roughly, for now.", next: "converge1" },
      dist_timing_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⏱️", text: "'This still drips at 3 drops every 4 seconds,' said the distiller. 'Batch needs exactly two minutes of steady dripping. How many drops is that, all told?'", question: "A still drips at a rate of 3 drops every 4 seconds. How many drops does it produce in exactly 2 minutes?", options: ["80", "85", "90", "95", "100"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Convert 2 minutes into seconds, then work out how many 4-second intervals fit into that.",
        solution: "2 minutes = 120 seconds. 120 ÷ 4 = 30 intervals of dripping. Each interval gives 3 drops: 30 × 3 = 90 drops.",
        good: "dist_timing_good", mid: "dist_timing_mid" },
      dist_timing_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'Ninety, exactly,' said the distiller, checking the collection flask. 'Bang on the mark.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      dist_timing_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The intervals miscounted twice before the distiller walked it through by hand, tapping out each four-second beat aloud.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: SICK WARD ========================= */
      ward_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🛏️", setRoute: "ward", text: "The Sick Ward held the worst-affected patients, kept comfortable but not yet cured. A healer's assistant stood guard over the medicine cabinet, and guarding THEM was something that had clearly moved in uninvited.", next: "ward_fight" },
      ward_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🤒", qLevel: 2,
        text: "A Roughling Malaise had settled over the cabinet, fog packed hard into something that made the whole room feel faintly, unfairly unwell just standing near it.\n\n'Nobody asked you,' said Flaskfox, setting down the case.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Malaise", emoji: "🤒", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a corner of the Malaise's grey bulk sloughed away into ordinary stale air.",
          strikeText: "A grey wave of fatigue rolled over the party, and somebody sat down rather harder than intended." },
        victory: "ward_win", retreat: "ward_retreat" },
      ward_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", grantComponent: "copper_still", text: "The Malaise dissolved into ordinary stale air, and the whole ward seemed to breathe easier at once. Tucked behind the cabinet, exactly where the assistant swore it had vanished from weeks ago, sat the Copper Still.\n\nComponent found: the Copper Still.", next: "ward_subchoice" },
      ward_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", setMid: true, text: "The Malaise held the cabinet, and the whole ward felt worse for its presence a while longer. The assistant took the setback with the tired patience of someone used to setbacks.", next: "ward_subchoice" },
      ward_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "💊", text: "The assistant straightened up. 'Two things, if you've the time. A patient's dosage needs working out properly, or the ward's restless and could use calming. Pick one.'", options: [
        { label: "Calm the restless ward (a check)", next: "ward_calm_check", preview: "A Logic check — patience and reasoning, not force." },
        { label: "Work out the dosage split (a puzzle)", next: "ward_dosage_puzzle", preview: "A ratio puzzle about three patients' doses." },
      ] },
      ward_calm_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧠", stat: 2, dc: 11,
        text: "The ward had grown restless in the aftermath, and settling it meant reasoning through which patient needed reassuring first, in what order, without missing anyone.\n\nSomebody sharp with Logic had better work through it.", success: "ward_calm_good", fail: "ward_calm_bad" },
      ward_calm_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧠", bonusStars: 2, text: "The order fell into place cleanly, and the whole ward settled within minutes. The assistant pressed a purse of stars into Flaskfox's paw with real gratitude. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      ward_calm_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The order tangled somewhere in the middle, and the ward took the better part of an hour to properly settle regardless.", next: "converge1" },
      ward_dosage_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "💉", text: "'Three patients,' said the assistant, 'doses in the ratio 2 to 3 to 4. Five hundred and forty milligrams, total, across all three. I need the LARGEST dose worked out properly before I give anyone anything.'", question: "Three patients are treated with doses in the ratio 2:3:4. The total dose given is 540mg. How much did the patient with the LARGEST dose receive?", options: ["180mg", "200mg", "220mg", "240mg", "260mg"], answer: 3,
        hint: "Add up the ratio parts to find what one part is worth, then find the largest patient's share.",
        solution: "The ratio parts add to 2+3+4 = 9. One part = 540 ÷ 9 = 60mg. The largest dose (4 parts) = 4 × 60 = 240mg.",
        good: "ward_dosage_good", mid: "ward_dosage_mid" },
      ward_dosage_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'Two hundred and forty,' the assistant repeated, measuring it out exactly. 'That's the fastest that split's ever been worked out in this ward.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      ward_dosage_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The ratio came out wrong twice before the assistant worked it through by hand, checking each patient's share against the total as they went.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT THREE: shared ========================= */
      converge1: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", text: "Whichever room had given up its Copper Still, Flaskfox returned to the apothecary's main hall with a growing certainty that this was not the only piece of the true measuring chain gone missing.\n\nTwo more landmarks awaited: a Glass Stopper that used to seal every tincture at exactly the right pressure, and a Waxed Seal that marked a remedy as properly tested.", next: "stopper_dice" },
      stopper_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🫙", die: 8, target: 4, text: "The stopper cabinet had jammed, its release catch stiff with old wax. Roll 4 or more on an eight-sided die to free it without cracking the glass.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 4 or more on a fair eight-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "3/4", "5/8", "1/4"], answer: 2, why: "The rolls 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8 succeed — 5 outcomes out of 8, so 5/8. A little better than even. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your grip: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "stopper_good", fail: "stopper_miss" },
      stopper_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🫙", grantComponent: "glass_stopper", text: "The catch gave way clean, no cracks at all, and there inside the cabinet sat the Glass Stopper — ground to a precision that no ordinary glassblower could manage twice.\n\nComponent found: the Glass Stopper.", next: "seal_check" },
      stopper_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", setMid: true, text: "The catch held fast and a hairline crack ran up the cabinet door for the trouble. The Stopper stayed exactly where it was, unclaimed, for this visit at least.", next: "seal_check" },
      seal_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🕯️", stat: 0, dc: 12,
        text: "The Waxed Seal had been misfiled decades ago among a shelf of ordinary receipts, and finding it meant reasoning through the apothecary's own accounts to work out which entry didn't actually belong.\n\nSomebody sharp with Arithmetic had better trace it.", success: "seal_good", fail: "seal_bad" },
      seal_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", grantComponent: "waxed_seal", text: "The accounts untangled cleanly, one column at a time, and led straight to a drawer nobody had opened in years: the Waxed Seal, exactly where the sums said it had to be.\n\nComponent found: the Waxed Seal.", next: "apoth_scene" },
      seal_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The accounts tangled somewhere around the third column, and the trail went cold. The Waxed Seal stayed misfiled, unrecovered, for this visit.", next: "apoth_scene" },
      apoth_scene: { type: "story", emoji: "📋", text: "Deep in the apothecary's back room, an old dispensary clerk sat hunched over a ledger of remedy schedules. 'Trying to work out when two remedies' doses next line up,' the clerk said, not looking up. 'Not a simple sum. A THINKING job.'", next: "apoth_puzzle" },
      apoth_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔢", text: "'I need the smallest dose-count,' said the clerk, 'that leaves remainder 2 when divided by 3, AND remainder 4 when divided by 5. Both remedies need to line up on that count.'", question: "What is the smallest positive number that leaves a remainder of 2 when divided by 3, and a remainder of 4 when divided by 5?", options: ["8", "11", "14", "17", "19"], answer: 2,
        hint: "List numbers that leave remainder 2 when divided by 3 (2, 5, 8, 11, 14, ...), then check each against the second condition.",
        solution: "Numbers leaving remainder 2 when divided by 3: 2, 5, 8, 11, 14... Checking each against 'remainder 4 when divided by 5': 2÷5 rem 2, 5÷5 rem 0, 8÷5 rem 3, 11÷5 rem 1, 14÷5 rem 4 ✓. The smallest such number is 14.",
        good: "apoth_good", mid: "apoth_mid" },
      apoth_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'Fourteen,' said the clerk, checking it against the schedule. 'Fastest that's ever been worked out in this dispensary.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "night_choice" },
      apoth_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🕰️", text: "The list of candidates ran rather longer than strictly necessary before '14' turned up, and the clerk waited it out with visible patience.", next: "night_choice" },
      night_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🕯️", text: "'The healer's proper surgery is an hour on from here,' said the clerk, banking the desk-candle. 'Carry this dispensary's post through the night and there's a favour in it. Or wait for a safe dawn crossing.'", options: [
        { label: "Travel through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A d6 roll with ADVANTAGE. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up a heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward, but the morning surgery is genuinely pleasant." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 6, target: 4, advantage: true, text: "The night road wound between hedgerows like a sum done in a hurry. Roll with ADVANTAGE: roll the six-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 4 or more to hold a steady pace in the dark.", predict: { question: "One roll of this die succeeds (4 or more) exactly half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher — what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "2/3", "5/8"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fail. Each fails half the time, so both fail 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4 — meaning you succeed the other 3/4 of the time. Advantage turns a coin toss into a strong majority. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Flaskfox held a steady pace through the dark and delivered the dispensary's post before the moon had properly set. The promised favour — three bright stars, and a hot meal that put a whole heart's courage back into the party. Bonus: +3 ⭐ and one heart restored.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", setMid: true, text: "The night road won this time. Flaskfox came down in an unfamiliar hedge, fur thoroughly ruffled, the post rather worse for the detour, and the favour quietly withdrawn.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "The party waited out the dark hours by the dispensary desk, and the surgery opened at a properly civilised hour, morning light falling clean across every shelf. Sometimes the boring choice is simply the right one.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      ambush_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💊", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The surgery's own dispensing counter, which normally charged one correctly-measured dose per visitor, was being run by the wrong management this morning: two Roughling Quacks had set up behind it with a hand-lettered sign reading CURE: EVRYTHING, while the real dispensary clerk glared from behind a bolted hatch.\n\n'That's not even spelled right,' said Flaskfox, which as declarations of war go was entirely accurate.",
        enemy: { name: "Roughling Quacks", emoji: "💊", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and one of the Quacks thinned noticeably, the way fog does when corrected firmly.",
          strikeText: "A grey paw swept a tray of vials aside, and somebody in the party got dosed with something they hadn't agreed to." },
        victory: "ambush_win", retreat: "ambush_retreat" },
      ambush_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏥", text: "The real clerk unbolted the hatch, then their expression, in that order. 'Waived, for pest control,' they said, sweeping the false sign into the bin. 'But nobody's dispensed to without a properly measured dose. Rules are rules.'", next: "dispensary_puzzle" },
      ambush_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The party fell back down the corridor with the Quacks' false cheer still ringing behind them, and came at the counter eventually by the side stair, scratched and simmering. 'Bad morning,' said the clerk. 'But nobody's dispensed to without measuring properly.'", next: "dispensary_puzzle" },
      dispensary_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧾", text: "The clerk slid a ledger across. 'Take your time,' they said, in the voice of someone counting it.", question: "Tinctures cost 6 sickles each and salves cost 11 sickles each. Flaskfox buys 12 items in total for exactly 97 sickles. How many salves were bought?", options: ["3", "4", "5", "6", "7"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Let t be tinctures and s be salves. Then t + s = 12 and 6t + 11s = 97. Substitute t = 12 − s into the second equation.",
        solution: "t + s = 12, so t = 12 − s. Then 6(12 − s) + 11s = 97, giving 72 − 6s + 11s = 97, so 5s = 25, meaning s = 5. Check: 7 tinctures and 5 salves: 6×7 + 11×5 = 42 + 55 = 97. ✓",
        good: "dispensary_good", mid: "dispensary_mid" },
      dispensary_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The clerk checked the ledger twice, made a small approving noise, and waved the party straight through. 'Go on,' they said. 'The valley could use someone who measures like that.'", next: "act4_intro" },
      dispensary_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The clerk shook their head, not unkindly, and worked the true sum out step by step, making the party repeat it back before waving them through the side gate.", next: "act4_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FOUR: second branch ========================= */
      act4_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🗺️", text: "Word among the staff was that the apothecary's trouble hadn't started all at once — it had come apart at three specific corners: the Herb Garden, the Distillery, the Sick Ward. The very three already searched once, in a hurry. A second, calmer look at whichever was already familiar might turn up what a first pass had missed.", next: "act4_choice" },
      act4_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🔁", text: "'Only time for one more proper look before the trail goes cold,' said the head healer. 'Herb Garden, Distillery, or Sick Ward — wherever you already know your way around ought to go easiest.'", options: [
        { label: "Return to the Herb Garden", next: "act4_herb", preview: "A Science check — much easier if you already know the Herb Garden." },
        { label: "Return to the Distillery", next: "act4_dist", preview: "A dice roll — much easier if you already know the Distillery." },
        { label: "Return to the Sick Ward", next: "act4_ward", preview: "A fight — much easier if you already know the Sick Ward." },
      ] },
      act4_herb: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🌿", stat: 3, dc: 13, routeBonus: { route: "herb", amount: 3 },
        text: "A second, calmer pass through the Herb Garden turned up a mislabelled bed that had been throwing every dosage off by a fraction — exactly the kind of check that goes twice as fast for someone who already knows every plant's usual place.\n\nSomebody sharp with Science had better verify it properly this time.", success: "act4_herb_good", fail: "act4_herb_bad" },
      act4_herb_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🌿", bonusStars: 3, text: "The mislabelling resolved cleanly under a second, familiar look — ground already walked once that morning made the check faster the second time round. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_herb_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The mislabelling refused to resolve however carefully it was checked, and the bed was logged as provisional, pending fresher eyes.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_dist: { type: "dice", emoji: "🏭", die: 12, target: 8, routeBonus: { route: "dist", amount: 3 },
        text: "The Distillery's main still needed timing once more, against a much fussier full-batch run — though considerably less fussy for paws that already knew its particular rhythm. Roll 8 or more on a twelve-sided die.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 8 or more on a fair twelve-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "5/12", "1/3", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 8 through 12 succeed — 5 outcomes out of 12, so 5/12. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your hand: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "act4_dist_good", fail: "act4_dist_bad" },
      act4_dist_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🏭", bonusStars: 3, text: "The full-batch timing landed clean on the first proper attempt, and the distiller nearly wept with relief, pressing a full purse of stars into Flaskfox's paw. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_dist_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The full-batch timing proved rougher than the morning's version, and the run came out thin regardless. The distiller took it in good humour, mostly.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_ward: { type: "combat", emoji: "🛏️", qLevel: 3, routeBonus: { route: "ward", amount: 3 },
        text: "A second Roughling had settled over the ward exactly where the Malaise had fallen — smaller, greyer, wearing an unwell air it had no real right to. Ground already fought over once that morning was, at least, ground the party knew how to move through fast.\n\n'Not again,' said Flaskfox, without much surprise.",
        enemy: { name: "A Roughling Relapse", emoji: "🛏️", hits: 3, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and a grey wave of it sloughed away into ordinary stale air.",
          strikeText: "A tired grey fog rolled over someone in the party, who sat down rather harder than intended." },
        victory: "act4_ward_win", retreat: "act4_ward_retreat" },
      act4_ward_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🛏️", bonusStars: 3, text: "The assistant re-checked the ward against the now-settled patients and found it held, clean, for the first time in weeks. 'Whatever you did the first time through here,' they said, 'it's clearly still working.' (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_ward_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Relapse held the ward a second time, and the patients stayed unsettled a while longer. The assistant sighed the sigh of someone who has started to suspect this is simply how the week is going to go.", next: "act5_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FIVE: reconverge, forge, finale ========================= */
      act5_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🏥", text: "Whatever the second visit had turned up, the trail now led, unmistakably, to the apothecary's own Compounding Hall — the room where every remedy's true recipe had always been struck, and where, judging by the grey vapour pouring gently from beneath its door, something had gone very wrong indeed.\n\nFlaskfox checked the case. Vials, scales, notebook, all present. This was, by now, an entirely automatic reflex.", next: "captain_fight" },
      captain_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Inside, something large loomed over the Hall's master recipe-book, smearing every measurement into 'roughly enough' with a wet grey thumb. The Roughling Head Compounder was bigger than its Smudges and greyer than its Quacks, and it looked up with eyes like un-inked full stops.\n\n'DILUTED,' it said. 'ADEQUATELY.'\n\n'Nothing adequate has ever cured anyone,' said Flaskfox, setting down the case for good this time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Head Compounder", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer landed like a returned prescription, and the Compounder lost a measure it had clearly been fond of.",
          strikeText: "The Compounder's fist came down and the whole Hall went approximate; every vial on the shelf read a slightly different amount." },
        victory: "captain_win", retreat: "captain_retreat" },
      captain_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Measure by measure, the recipe-book's true amounts crept back into their proper places, and the grey vapour stopped pouring from beneath the door. Somewhere in the ward, a tonic that had been coming out flat for a fortnight finally, quietly, took proper effect.", next: "forge" },
      captain_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Compounder's fist came down again and the Hall went so approximate that the doors were the only certain thing left, so the party used them, regrouped outside, and came back in by the side stair while the Compounder glowered over the main floor below.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Compounding Hall worked an old glassblower who could true a warped flask from twenty paces and had once, reportedly, distilled an argument down to its one honest drop. 'A Swift Antidote, is it,' she said, peering over half-moon spectacles. 'Copper Still, Glass Stopper, Waxed Seal — show me all three, and I'll blow you a flask worth trusting.'",
        forged: "The glassblower's own forge roared like an approving crowd. She set the still, stopper and seal together into a single travelling flask, and sealed it with wax steady enough to trust with a life.\n\nA dose from the Swift Antidote worked before the patient had finished flinching.\n\nThe Swift Antidote is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "The glassblower counted the components on her bench, then counted them again, which was polite of her, since it didn't take long.\n\n'Short,' she said, tapping each gap. 'An antidote wants all three or it's just a flask with good intentions. Come back with the full set.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "flaskfox_antidote", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the master recipe-book itself.\n\nFlaskfox found the trouble on a page near the back: a single measurement that had been recorded, once, long ago, as 'roughly this much' by someone in a hurry — and every remedy brewed from that page since had been quietly a little bit wrong, the way any recipe drifts if you let one measurement stay approximate too long.\n\nFlaskfox crossed out the roughly. Flaskfox wrote in the exactly, in a steady paw, and underlined it twice.\n\nSomewhere in the valley, a tonic that had been curing nothing for a fortnight finally, audibly, began to work.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "The valley held a quiet celebration at the apothecary's own door, which for a healer's house counts as throwing every window open at once. The head healer gave a speech of properly measured length, the glassblower was seen to almost smile, and the dispensary clerk waived the toll a second, historic time.\n\nAt the end of it, the valley pressed the Ledger of Remedies into Flaskfox's keeping: the founding record of every true measurement, cross-checked and never again likely to drift. Flaskfox walked home counting the stars, and found, for once, that the count came out exactly right.",
        midText: "The valley was grateful, in a slightly muddled way — the celebration ran a tincture short of what was planned and the head healer's speech lost its place twice. Still, the remedies held steady, the measurements agreed with themselves, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate, the head healer pressed the Ledger of Remedies into Flaskfox's keeping anyway. 'You got here,' they said. 'The route was scenic. Routes often are.' Flaskfox walked home already planning a tidier attempt, because a measurement worth getting right is worth getting right again.",
        grantItem: "flaskfox_ledger" },
    },
  },

  fractail: {
    id: "fractail",
    title: "The Harvest That Wouldn't Share Fair",
    tagline: "Fractail had one rule, and it was older than any law in the forest: split it fair, split it exact, and never once let anyone go home with less than their proper share. Lately, though, shares have started coming out wrong — a bit here, a smudge there, always in somebody's favour — and a raccoon who splits snacks into perfect fractions for a living takes that personally.",
    items: [
      { id: "fractail_claw",    name: "The Measuring Claw",    stat: 1, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Geometry. Won by working out exactly which sixth of a scattered haul was still whole." },
      { id: "fractail_journal", name: "The Fair-Share Journal", stat: 0, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "fractail_shears",  name: "The Exact Shears",      stat: 2, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Logic. Forged from three components gathered across the forest's harvest grounds." },
    ],
    components: { acorn_press: "The Acorn Press", woven_basket: "The Woven Basket", carved_ladle: "The Carved Ladle" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      /* ========================= ACT ONE ========================= */
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🦝", text: "Fractail had never once split a share unfairly. Not the acorns, not the blackberries, not even the single last honeycake that six hungry cousins wanted at once — that one had gone out in sixths, precisely, with a spare crumb set aside for whoever asked politely.\n\nSo when word reached the forest that the harvest shares had started coming out wrong — a squirrel getting a 'bit less, roughly,' a family of voles told their portion was 'about right, give or take' — Fractail did not wait to hear the whole story twice.\n\n'Fetch my satchel,' said Fractail, already counting on all five claws of one paw. 'Something's wrong with the splitting, somewhere down the whole line, and I intend to find exactly where.'", next: "claw_check" },
      claw_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🌰", stat: 1, dc: 10,
        text: "The path to the harvest grounds ran past a spilled hoard — a whole winter's acorns scattered across the path after some cart or creature had knocked them flying. Most had cracked open uselessly. Roughly one in six sat whole, glinting, indistinguishable from the cracked ones at a glance.\n\nWorking out which sixth, before the squirrels descended, was a job for someone who trusted geometry over guessing.", success: "claw_found", fail: "claw_missed" },
      claw_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🌰", grantItem: "fractail_claw", text: "The check came out clean, and the whole acorns turned out to sit in a perfect ring — the shape gave them away before the counting even finished. Buried at the ring's exact centre sat a small curved claw, honed to a precise edge.\n\nThe Measuring Claw is yours. (+1 Geometry, kept forever.)", next: "stream_dice" },
      claw_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🌰", text: "The ring's shape wobbled under a hasty glance, and whatever sat at its centre stayed buried under a fresh scatter of squirrels before it could be confirmed. Fractail noted the failure and pressed on toward the harvest grounds regardless.", next: "stream_dice" },
      stream_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "💧", die: 10, target: 6, text: "A stream had swollen with the last of the autumn rain, and crossing it clean meant timing a jump between two rocks. Roll 6 or more on a ten-sided die to land it.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 6 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "3/10", "2/5", "1/4"], answer: 0, why: "The rolls 6 through 10 succeed — 5 outcomes out of 10, so 5/10 = 1/2. An even bet. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your paws: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "stream_good", fail: "stream_miss" },
      stream_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🦝", text: "Fractail landed the jump in one clean bound, tail forking out behind for balance. The party followed with rather more splashing.", next: "gate_fight" },
      stream_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "💦", text: "The stream had opinions about being crossed gracefully by four passengers at once, and voiced them by depositing everyone in the shallows at the least dignified possible moment.", next: "gate_fight" },
      gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It sat right across the harvest gate, small and grey and thoroughly pleased with itself: a Roughling Halfshare, gently blurring every posted portion from ONE THIRD into 'roughly a third, more or less, don't fuss.'\n\n'I fuss,' said Fractail, setting down the satchel. 'Fussing over exactly this much is rather the whole point of me.'",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Halfshare", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed clean, and a corner of the Halfshare sloughed away in a puff of corrected grey.",
          strikeText: "The Halfshare billowed forward and, for one worrying moment, every portion in sight looked a little bit smaller than it had a right to." },
        victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
      gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Halfshare came apart with an embarrassed little pop, and the posted portion said ONE THIRD again — clear, exact, undisputed. Fractail stepped through the gate. Small grey scouts, sitting on exact fractions to see what gave — and somewhere deeper in, whatever was sending them.", next: "crossroads" },
      gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "There is no shame in an orderly withdrawal, provided the portion gets logged properly afterwards. It was logged. Fractail went in by the hedge-gap instead, trailing a faint smear of grey that took the rest of the morning to shake off.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🌳", text: "Just past the gate, the harvest grounds split three ways under a carved wooden sign.\n\nThe left path led to the ORCHARD, where the apple crop was weighed and shared. The centre path led to the KITCHEN CLEARING, where the harvest was cooked down and portioned out hot. The right path led to the BRAMBLE PATCH, where the last of the blackberries were being fought over by something that wasn't a bird.\n\nSomewhere behind each path — the forest elders swore to this — an Acorn Press was waiting to be earned, the very press that used to grind every share down to its true, exact weight.", options: [
        { label: "Search the Orchard", next: "orchard_1", preview: "A fraction puzzle guards the Press. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Search the Kitchen Clearing", next: "kitchen_1", preview: "A dice challenge guards the Press. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Search the Bramble Patch", next: "bramble_1", preview: "A fight guards the Press. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
      ] },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: ORCHARD ========================= */
      orchard_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🍎", setRoute: "orchard", text: "The Orchard smelled of windfall apples and old ladders. An orchard-keeper stood over a laden cart, tail — no, apron — knotted in frustration. 'This whole harvest gets split three ways every year, same as always: some to market, some to cider, the rest kept back for seed. Only nobody's told me what's LEFT for seed this year, and I daren't guess.'", next: "orchard_puzzle" },
      orchard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🍏", text: "The keeper tapped the ledger. 'Three-eighths of the harvest goes to market. One-quarter is pressed for cider. Whatever's left over is kept back for seed. What fraction of the whole harvest is that?'", question: "A harvest is shared out: 3/8 goes to market and 1/4 is pressed for cider. What fraction of the whole harvest is kept for seed?", options: ["1/8", "1/4", "3/8", "1/2", "5/8"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Give both fractions the same denominator, add them together, then subtract the total from a whole (1).",
        solution: "3/8 and 1/4 = 2/8. Added together: 3/8 + 2/8 = 5/8 spent on market and cider. What's left for seed: 1 − 5/8 = 3/8.",
        good: "orchard_good", mid: "orchard_mid" },
      orchard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", grantComponent: "acorn_press", text: "The keeper checked the working twice, weighed out exactly three-eighths of the crop for seed, and the whole harvest balanced out at last. Buried under the cart, wrapped in sacking, sat the Acorn Press.\n\nComponent found: the Acorn Press.", next: "orchard_subchoice" },
      orchard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The fraction came out wrong twice before the keeper worked it through by hand, step by careful step. The Press stayed wrapped under the cart, unclaimed, for this visit at least.", next: "orchard_subchoice" },
      orchard_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🌱", text: "'Two more things, while you're here,' said the keeper. 'Something's been at the fallen apples in the north row, or I could use a second opinion on a tricky bit of reasoning. Time for one.'", options: [
        { label: "Deal with the windfall pest (a fight)", next: "orchard_pest_fight", preview: "Something has been eating more than its fair share." },
        { label: "Help with the tricky reasoning (a check)", next: "orchard_logic_check", preview: "A Logic check on a puzzling bit of orchard bookkeeping." },
      ] },
      orchard_pest_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🐛", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The pest turned out to be a Roughling Windfall, fog packed loosely into something that had clearly decided its own share of the fallen apples was 'basically all of them, near enough.'\n\n'That's not near enough anything,' said Fractail, which is a very raccoon thing to say with total conviction.",
        enemy: { name: "A Roughling Windfall", emoji: "🐛", hits: 2, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a patch of the Windfall unravelled into ordinary damp leaf-mould.",
          strikeText: "Something soft and grey rolled through the row, and somebody in the party came away smelling faintly of bruised apple." },
        victory: "orchard_pest_win", retreat: "orchard_pest_retreat" },
      orchard_pest_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🍎", text: "The Windfall dissolved into scattered leaf-mould, and the surviving apples in the north row were, thankfully, mostly untouched. The keeper counted what remained with real relief.", next: "converge1" },
      orchard_pest_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Windfall held the row for the day, and finished off rather more apples than the keeper could comfortably spare. They took the loss the way orchard-keepers take losses: hard, briefly, then back to the ladder.", next: "converge1" },
      orchard_logic_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "📖", stat: 2, dc: 11,
        text: "The bookkeeping in question nearly balanced, which in a harvest ledger is its own particular kind of alarming. Working out whether the sums genuinely held meant reasoning it through properly, not just trusting how tidy the columns looked.\n\nSomebody sharp with Logic had better verify it.", success: "orchard_logic_good", fail: "orchard_logic_bad" },
      orchard_logic_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "The reasoning checked out clean, once properly tested rather than just eyeballed. The keeper breathed out with real relief and pressed a purse of stars into Fractail's paw. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      orchard_logic_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The reasoning very nearly held but not quite, and a whole column of the ledger had to be quietly redone. 'Close doesn't count in a harvest count,' the keeper said, without much bitterness.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: KITCHEN CLEARING ========================= */
      kitchen_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🍲", setRoute: "kitchen", text: "The Kitchen Clearing steamed and clattered over three cookfires at once. A cook stood beside the biggest pot, ladle in paw. 'Everything in here gets scaled up or down depending who's eating. Get the scaling wrong and somebody goes hungry, or somebody else gets twice their share. Reckon you can scale one properly?'", next: "kitchen_dice" },
      kitchen_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🔥", die: 12, target: 5, text: "Roll 5 or more on a twelve-sided die to pull the stew off the fire at exactly the right moment, before it reduces past its proper thickness.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair twelve-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/3", "1/3", "3/4"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 5 through 12 succeed — 8 outcomes out of 12, so 8/12 = 2/3. A generous window. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your paw: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "kitchen_good", fail: "kitchen_miss" },
      kitchen_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", grantComponent: "acorn_press", text: "The stew came off exactly on time, thick and proper. Wedged in the ash beneath the cookfire, clearly left there by someone in a hurry long ago, sat the Acorn Press — a spare, or perhaps the original.\n\nComponent found: the Acorn Press.", next: "kitchen_subchoice" },
      kitchen_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", setMid: true, text: "The stew came off a moment late, and reduced past all good sense into something closer to jam. Whatever had been wedged in the ash stayed wedged, unclaimed, for this visit.", next: "kitchen_subchoice" },
      kitchen_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🥣", text: "The cook wiped their paws on an apron. 'Two more jobs, if you've a moment. The serving bowls need portioning out fast, or there's a recipe I can't scale up properly. Pick one.'", options: [
        { label: "Portion the serving bowls (a check)", next: "kitchen_speed_check", preview: "A Speed check — the bowls won't wait." },
        { label: "Scale up the recipe (a puzzle)", next: "kitchen_puzzle", preview: "An Arithmetic puzzle about scaling a fraction of flour." },
      ] },
      kitchen_speed_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⚡", stat: 4, dc: 11,
        text: "The serving bowls had to be filled and out the door before the whole clearing queued up hungry, faster than careful measuring really allowed for. This one called for quick paws over quick reasoning.\n\nSomebody fast had better take it on.", success: "kitchen_speed_good", fail: "kitchen_speed_bad" },
      kitchen_speed_good: { type: "story", emoji: "⚡", bonusStars: 2, text: "The bowls went out fast and full, not a short measure among them. The cook looked almost disappointed not to get to watch a longer struggle. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      kitchen_speed_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The queue grew a little restless before the last bowl went out, stubborn as only a hungry clearing ever manages. It would hold, roughly, for now.", next: "converge1" },
      kitchen_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🥧", text: "'This recipe,' said the cook, 'serves 4 and uses three-quarters of a cup of hazelnut flour. I've got 10 mouths to feed tonight. How much flour, keeping the same ratio?'", question: "A recipe serving 4 uses 3/4 cup of hazelnut flour. How much flour is needed to serve 10, using the same ratio?", options: ["1 1/2 cups", "1 5/8 cups", "1 3/4 cups", "1 7/8 cups", "2 cups"], answer: 3,
        hint: "Work out how much flour is needed per person first, then multiply by the new number of people.",
        solution: "Per person: 3/4 ÷ 4 = 3/16 cup. For 10 people: 3/16 × 10 = 30/16 = 15/8 = 1 7/8 cups.",
        good: "kitchen_puzzle_good", mid: "kitchen_puzzle_mid" },
      kitchen_puzzle_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'One and seven-eighths, exactly,' said the cook, levelling off the measuring cup. 'Bang on the mark.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      kitchen_puzzle_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The scaling miscounted twice before the cook walked it through by hand, working out one portion first and building up from there.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: BRAMBLE PATCH ========================= */
      bramble_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🫐", setRoute: "bramble", text: "The Bramble Patch tangled thick and dark at the forest's edge, thorns catching on everything. A berry-picker's basket lay overturned, and guarding it was something that had clearly moved in uninvited.", next: "bramble_fight" },
      bramble_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "A Roughling Overgrowth had settled across the patch, fog packed hard into brambles that grew thicker every time anyone tried to work out how much was thorn and how much was path.\n\n'Nobody asked you to grow,' said Fractail, setting down the satchel.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Overgrowth", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a tangle of the Overgrowth sloughed away into ordinary bramble.",
          strikeText: "A grey thorn caught someone in the party, who came away rather more scratched than expected." },
        victory: "bramble_win", retreat: "bramble_retreat" },
      bramble_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", grantComponent: "acorn_press", text: "The Overgrowth dissolved into ordinary bramble, and the path through the patch opened up clean at last. Tucked behind the overturned basket, exactly where the picker swore it had vanished from weeks ago, sat the Acorn Press.\n\nComponent found: the Acorn Press.", next: "bramble_subchoice" },
      bramble_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", setMid: true, text: "The Overgrowth held the patch, and the thorns grew thicker across the path a while longer. The picker took the setback with the tired patience of someone used to setbacks.", next: "bramble_subchoice" },
      bramble_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🫐", text: "The picker straightened up, plucking thorns from one sleeve. 'Two things, if you've the time. The bramble roots need a quick check for spreading, or there's a sharing-out puzzle I can't work through. Pick one.'", options: [
        { label: "Check the spreading roots (a check)", next: "bramble_arith_check", preview: "An Arithmetic check — quick figuring, not deep reasoning." },
        { label: "Work through the sharing-out puzzle (a puzzle)", next: "bramble_puzzle", preview: "A puzzle about repeatedly halving a shared pie." },
      ] },
      bramble_arith_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧮", stat: 0, dc: 11,
        text: "The roots had spread further than looked likely at a glance, and working out by how much meant a quick, careful bit of figuring rather than a guess.\n\nSomebody sharp with Arithmetic had better work it out.", success: "bramble_arith_good", fail: "bramble_arith_bad" },
      bramble_arith_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧮", bonusStars: 2, text: "The figures came out clean, and the picker marked the true spread on a stake before it could get away from them. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      bramble_arith_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The figures tangled somewhere in the middle, same as the roots themselves, and the true spread stayed a mystery for another season.", next: "converge1" },
      bramble_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🥧", text: "'Every year,' said the picker, 'Fractail's own rule: give away exactly half of whatever pie's left, to a different friend, each time. Four friends came by this morning. What fraction of the original pie is left for Fractail, after all four?'", question: "Fractail gives away exactly half of whatever pie is left, to a different friend, each time. After giving to 4 friends this way, what fraction of the original pie is left?", options: ["1/4", "1/8", "1/16", "1/32", "1/2"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Each friend takes half of what's left. Multiply 1/2 by itself once for each friend.",
        solution: "Half remains after each friend: (1/2) × (1/2) × (1/2) × (1/2) = 1/16.",
        good: "bramble_puzzle_good", mid: "bramble_puzzle_mid" },
      bramble_puzzle_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'One sixteenth, exactly,' said the picker, checking it against the crumbs left in the tin. 'That's the fastest that's ever been worked out in this patch.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      bramble_puzzle_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The halving miscounted at the third friend before the picker walked it through slice by slice, out loud, to be sure.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT THREE: shared ========================= */
      converge1: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", text: "Whichever ground had given up its Acorn Press, Fractail returned to the harvest grounds' central clearing with a growing certainty that this was not the only piece of the true sharing chain gone missing.\n\nTwo more landmarks awaited: a Woven Basket that used to hold every share at exactly the right weight, and a Carved Ladle that measured a portion as properly fair.", next: "basket_dice" },
      basket_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🧺", die: 8, target: 6, text: "The basket store had jammed, its lid stuck fast with old sap. Roll 6 or more on an eight-sided die to prise it open without splitting the weave.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 6 or more on a fair eight-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "3/8", "5/8", "1/4"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 6, 7 and 8 succeed — 3 outcomes out of 8, so 3/8. A narrow window. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your grip: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "basket_good", fail: "basket_miss" },
      basket_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧺", grantComponent: "woven_basket", text: "The lid gave way clean, no splits at all, and there inside the store sat the Woven Basket — woven so tight and even that no ordinary basket-maker could match it twice.\n\nComponent found: the Woven Basket.", next: "ladle_check" },
      basket_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", setMid: true, text: "The lid held fast and a thin crack ran along the weave for the trouble. The Basket stayed exactly where it was, unclaimed, for this visit at least.", next: "ladle_check" },
      ladle_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🥄", stat: 1, dc: 12,
        text: "The Carved Ladle had been misfiled decades ago among a shelf of ordinary cooking spoons, and finding it meant reasoning through the shapes on the shelf to work out which one didn't actually belong.\n\nSomebody sharp with Geometry had better trace it.", success: "ladle_good", fail: "ladle_bad" },
      ladle_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🥄", grantComponent: "carved_ladle", text: "The shapes sorted themselves out cleanly, one curve at a time, and led straight to a drawer nobody had opened in years: the Carved Ladle, exactly where the reasoning said it had to be.\n\nComponent found: the Carved Ladle.", next: "grove_scene" },
      ladle_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The shapes tangled somewhere around the third shelf, and the trail went cold. The Carved Ladle stayed misfiled, unrecovered, for this visit.", next: "grove_scene" },
      grove_scene: { type: "story", emoji: "🌰", text: "Deep in the harvest grounds' oldest grove, an elder squirrel sat hunched over a ledger of the season's stores. 'Trying to work out what's left after the morning's gathering,' the elder said, not looking up. 'Not a simple sum. A THINKING job.'", next: "grove_puzzle" },
      grove_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🌰", text: "'This morning,' said the elder, 'I gathered four and three-fifths baskets of chestnuts. This evening, I used two and seven-tenths of a basket making stew. How many baskets are left, exactly?'", question: "Fractail's elder gathers 4 3/5 baskets of chestnuts in the morning and uses 2 7/10 baskets making stew that evening. How many baskets remain?", options: ["1 7/10", "1 4/5", "1 9/10", "2 1/10", "2 3/10"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Convert both mixed numbers to the same denominator (tenths), then subtract.",
        solution: "4 3/5 = 4 6/10 = 46/10. 2 7/10 = 27/10. 46/10 − 27/10 = 19/10 = 1 9/10 baskets.",
        good: "grove_good", mid: "grove_mid" },
      grove_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'One and nine-tenths,' said the elder, checking it against the store count. 'Fastest that's ever been worked out in this grove.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "night_choice" },
      grove_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🕰️", text: "The tenths tangled twice before '1 9/10' turned up properly, and the elder waited it out with visible patience.", next: "night_choice" },
      night_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🌙", text: "'The proper harvest hall is an hour on from here,' said the elder, banking the grove-fire. 'Carry this grove's tally through the night and there's a favour in it. Or wait for a safe dawn crossing.'", options: [
        { label: "Travel through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A d6 roll with ADVANTAGE. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up a heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward, but the morning crossing is genuinely pleasant." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 6, target: 5, advantage: true, text: "The night path wound between brambles like a sum done in a hurry. Roll with ADVANTAGE: roll the six-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 5 or more to hold a steady pace in the dark.", predict: { question: "One roll of this die succeeds (5 or more) exactly one-third of the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher — what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/3", "5/9", "2/3", "4/9"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fail. Each fails 2/3 of the time, so both fail 2/3 × 2/3 = 4/9 — meaning you succeed the other 5/9 of the time. Advantage turns a long-shot into a better-than-even bet. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Fractail held a steady pace through the dark and delivered the grove's tally before the moon had properly set. The promised favour — three bright stars, and a hot meal that put a whole heart's courage back into the party. Bonus: +3 ⭐ and one heart restored.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", setMid: true, text: "The night path won this time. Fractail came down in an unfamiliar thicket, fur thoroughly ruffled, the tally rather worse for the detour, and the favour quietly withdrawn.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "The party waited out the dark hours by the grove-fire, and the crossing came at a properly civilised hour, morning light falling clean across every branch. Sometimes the boring choice is simply the right one.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      ambush_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🍯", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The harvest hall's own sharing-counter, which normally doled out one correctly-measured portion per visitor, was being run by the wrong management this morning: two Roughling Skimmers had set up behind it with a hand-lettered sign reading SHARE: EVRYONE GETS SUM, while the real hall-keeper glared from behind a bolted hatch.\n\n'That's not even spelled right,' said Fractail, which as declarations of war go was entirely accurate.",
        enemy: { name: "Roughling Skimmers", emoji: "🍯", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and one of the Skimmers thinned noticeably, the way fog does when corrected firmly.",
          strikeText: "A grey paw swept a tray of shares aside, and somebody in the party got served a portion they hadn't agreed to." },
        victory: "ambush_win", retreat: "ambush_retreat" },
      ambush_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "The real hall-keeper unbolted the hatch, then their expression, in that order. 'Waived, for pest control,' they said, sweeping the false sign into the bin. 'But nobody's served without a properly measured share. Rules are rules.'", next: "storeroom_puzzle" },
      ambush_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The party fell back down the corridor with the Skimmers' false cheer still ringing behind them, and came at the counter eventually by the side stair, scratched and simmering. 'Bad morning,' said the keeper. 'But nobody's served without measuring properly.'", next: "storeroom_puzzle" },
      storeroom_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧾", text: "The keeper slid a tally-book across. 'Take your time,' they said, in the voice of someone counting it.", question: "A storeroom basket holds apples and pears. The number of apples is 2/3 of the number of pears. Altogether there are 35 fruits. How many pears are there?", options: ["14", "18", "21", "24", "28"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Let the number of pears be p. Apples are 2/3 of p. Add apples and pears together and set the total equal to 35.",
        solution: "Let pears = p, so apples = (2/3)p. Then p + (2/3)p = 35, which is (5/3)p = 35, so p = 21. Check: 21 pears + 14 apples = 35. ✓",
        good: "storeroom_good", mid: "storeroom_mid" },
      storeroom_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The keeper checked the tally-book twice, made a small approving noise, and waved the party straight through. 'Go on,' they said. 'The forest could use someone who shares like that.'", next: "act4_intro" },
      storeroom_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The keeper shook their head, not unkindly, and worked the true sum out step by step, making the party repeat it back before waving them through the side gate.", next: "act4_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FOUR: second branch ========================= */
      act4_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🗺️", text: "Word among the foresters was that the harvest's trouble hadn't started all at once — it had come apart at three specific corners: the Orchard, the Kitchen Clearing, the Bramble Patch. The very three already searched once, in a hurry. A second, calmer look at whichever was already familiar might turn up what a first pass had missed.", next: "act4_choice" },
      act4_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🔁", text: "'Only time for one more proper look before the trail goes cold,' said the eldest forester. 'Orchard, Kitchen, or Bramble Patch — wherever you already know your way around ought to go easiest.'", options: [
        { label: "Return to the Orchard", next: "act4_orchard", preview: "A Geometry check — much easier if you already know the Orchard." },
        { label: "Return to the Kitchen Clearing", next: "act4_kitchen", preview: "A dice roll — much easier if you already know the Kitchen." },
        { label: "Return to the Bramble Patch", next: "act4_bramble", preview: "A fight — much easier if you already know the Bramble Patch." },
      ] },
      act4_orchard: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🍎", stat: 1, dc: 13, routeBonus: { route: "orchard", amount: 3 },
        text: "A second, calmer pass through the Orchard turned up a row of trees planted at the wrong spacing, throwing every yield-per-row estimate off by a fraction — exactly the kind of check that goes twice as fast for someone who already knows every row's usual shape.\n\nSomebody sharp with Geometry had better verify it properly this time.", success: "act4_orchard_good", fail: "act4_orchard_bad" },
      act4_orchard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🍎", bonusStars: 3, text: "The spacing resolved cleanly under a second, familiar look — ground already walked once that morning made the check faster the second time round. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_orchard_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The spacing refused to resolve however carefully it was checked, and the row was logged as provisional, pending fresher eyes.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_kitchen: { type: "dice", emoji: "🍲", die: 20, target: 14, routeBonus: { route: "kitchen", amount: 3 },
        text: "The Kitchen Clearing's main cookfire needed timing once more, against a much fussier full-batch stew — though considerably less fussy for paws that already knew its particular rhythm. Roll 14 or more on a twenty-sided die.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 14 or more on a fair twenty-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "7/20", "1/4", "2/5"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 14 through 20 succeed — 7 outcomes out of 20, so 7/20. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your hand: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "act4_kitchen_good", fail: "act4_kitchen_bad" },
      act4_kitchen_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🍲", bonusStars: 3, text: "The full-batch timing landed clean on the first proper attempt, and the cook nearly wept with relief, pressing a full purse of stars into Fractail's paw. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_kitchen_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The full-batch timing proved rougher than the morning's version, and the stew came out thin regardless. The cook took it in good humour, mostly.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_bramble: { type: "combat", emoji: "🫐", qLevel: 3, routeBonus: { route: "bramble", amount: 3 },
        text: "A second Roughling had settled over the patch exactly where the Overgrowth had fallen — smaller, greyer, wearing a tangled air it had no real right to. Ground already fought over once that morning was, at least, ground the party knew how to move through fast.\n\n'Not again,' said Fractail, without much surprise.",
        enemy: { name: "A Roughling Regrowth", emoji: "🫐", hits: 3, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and a tangle of it sloughed away into ordinary bramble.",
          strikeText: "A grey thorn caught someone in the party, who came away rather more scratched than expected." },
        victory: "act4_bramble_win", retreat: "act4_bramble_retreat" },
      act4_bramble_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🫐", bonusStars: 3, text: "The picker re-checked the patch against the now-cleared path and found it held, clean, for the first time in weeks. 'Whatever you did the first time through here,' they said, 'it's clearly still working.' (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_bramble_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Regrowth held the patch a second time, and the thorns tangled the path a while longer. The picker sighed the sigh of someone who has started to suspect this is simply how the week is going to go.", next: "act5_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FIVE: reconverge, forge, finale ========================= */
      act5_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "Whatever the second visit had turned up, the trail now led, unmistakably, to the harvest hall's own Great Sharing Table — the surface where every season's true portion had always been struck, and where, judging by the grey vapour pouring gently from beneath its legs, something had gone very wrong indeed.\n\nFractail checked the satchel. Claw, shears-in-waiting, journal, all present. This was, by now, an entirely automatic reflex.", next: "captain_fight" },
      captain_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Inside, something large loomed over the Table's master tally-book, smearing every portion into 'plenty for me, enough for you' with a wet grey claw. The Roughling Highshare was bigger than its Halfshares and greyer than its Skimmers, and it looked up with eyes like un-inked full stops.\n\n'MINE,' it said. 'MOSTLY.'\n\n'Nothing that's mostly mine has ever been fair to anyone,' said Fractail, setting down the satchel for good this time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Highshare", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer landed like a returned portion, and the Highshare lost a share it had clearly been fond of.",
          strikeText: "The Highshare's claw came down and the whole Table went approximate; every tally on the book read a slightly different amount." },
        victory: "captain_win", retreat: "captain_retreat" },
      captain_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Share by share, the tally-book's true amounts crept back into their proper places, and the grey vapour stopped pouring from beneath the Table's legs. Somewhere in the grove, a portion that had been coming out short for a fortnight finally, quietly, came out whole.", next: "forge" },
      captain_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Highshare's claw came down again and the Table went so approximate that the doors were the only certain thing left, so the party used them, regrouped outside, and came back in by the side stair while the Highshare glowered over the Table below.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Great Sharing Table worked an old tool-carver who could true a warped blade from twenty paces and had once, reportedly, split an argument down to its one honest half. 'Exact Shears, is it,' she said, peering over half-moon spectacles. 'Acorn Press, Woven Basket, Carved Ladle — show me all three, and I'll carve you shears worth trusting.'",
        forged: "The carver's own forge roared like an approving crowd. She set the press, basket and ladle together into a single pair of shears, and honed the edge steady enough to trust with a whole season's harvest.\n\nA cut from the Exact Shears split a share fair before anyone had finished asking.\n\nThe Exact Shears are yours! (+1 Logic, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "The carver counted the components on her bench, then counted them again, which was polite of her, since it didn't take long.\n\n'Short,' she said, tapping each gap. 'A pair of shears wants all three or it's just a blade with good intentions. Come back with the full set.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "fractail_shears", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the master tally-book itself.\n\nFractail found the trouble on a page near the back: a single share that had been recorded, once, long ago, as 'roughly this much' by someone in a hurry — and every harvest split from that page since had been quietly a little bit unfair, the way any share drifts if you let one portion stay approximate too long.\n\nFractail crossed out the roughly. Fractail wrote in the exactly, in a steady paw, and underlined it twice.\n\nSomewhere in the forest, a portion that had been coming out short for a fortnight finally, audibly, came out whole.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "The forest held a quiet celebration at the harvest hall's own door, which for a hall built on fair shares counts as throwing every basket open at once. The eldest forester gave a speech of properly measured length, the tool-carver was seen to almost smile, and the hall-keeper waived the toll a second, historic time.\n\nAt the end of it, the forest pressed the Fair-Share Journal into Fractail's keeping: the founding record of every true portion, cross-checked and never again likely to drift. Fractail walked home counting the stars, and found, for once, that the count came out exactly right.",
        midText: "The forest was grateful, in a slightly muddled way — the celebration ran a basket short of what was planned and the eldest forester's speech lost its place twice. Still, the shares held steady, the portions agreed with themselves, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate, the eldest forester pressed the Fair-Share Journal into Fractail's keeping anyway. 'You got here,' they said. 'The route was scenic. Routes often are.' Fractail walked home already planning a tidier attempt, because a share worth getting right is worth getting right again.",
        grantItem: "fractail_journal" },
    },
  },

  voltbird: {
    id: "voltbird",
    title: "The Storm That Wouldn't Keep Time",
    tagline: "Voltbird had never once misjudged a wind, a distance, or an arrival. That was the whole point of an eagle who crackled faintly with her own private weather: every flight was a sum, and every sum came out exact, down to the last minute. Lately, though, storms have started arriving early, late, or not quite at all — and a bird who charges through the sky at full speed does not appreciate being kept waiting on an answer.",
    items: [
      { id: "voltbird_feather",     name: "The Timing Feather",   stat: 0, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won by working out exactly which sixth of a scattered flock was still in true formation." },
      { id: "voltbird_compass",     name: "The True Compass",     stat: 1, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Geometry. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
      { id: "voltbird_prism",       name: "The Storm Prism",      stat: 3, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Science. Forged from three components gathered across three different weather stations." },
    ],
    components: { copper_coil: "The Copper Coil", glass_lens: "The Glass Lens", brass_weathervane: "The Brass Weathervane" },
    start: "intro",
    scenes: {
      /* ========================= ACT ONE ========================= */
      intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🦅", text: "Voltbird had never once misjudged an arrival. Not the dawn thermals, not the coastal squalls, not even the one legendary crossing where she'd told the whole flock 'four hours, seventeen minutes' and landed with the sun exactly where she'd promised it would be. Timing mattered. Speed mattered. A flight plan was a promise written in wind, and Voltbird did not break promises.\n\nSo when the weather stations up and down the range reported storms arriving 'sometime probably,' crossing valleys in 'roughly however long it takes,' Voltbird did not wait to hear the forecast twice.\n\n'Fetch the flight log,' said Voltbird, already crackling with impatience. 'Something's wrong with the timing, somewhere down the whole current, and I intend to find exactly where.'", next: "feather_check" },
      feather_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🪶", stat: 0, dc: 10,
        text: "The ridge below the Eyrie was scattered with downed feathers — a whole flock had been startled off course in the night, and most of the feathers lying about were ordinary moult, worthless for tracking. Roughly one in six still carried a true flight-charge, glinting faintly if you knew to look.\n\nWorking out which sixth, before the wind scattered them further, was a job for someone who trusted arithmetic over guessing.", success: "feather_found", fail: "feather_missed" },
      feather_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🪶", grantItem: "voltbird_feather", text: "The check came out clean, and the true feathers turned out to sit in an exact one-in-six scatter, easy to pick out once counted properly. Among them lay a single feather still crackling with old charge, its quill sharp as a stylus.\n\nThe Timing Feather is yours. (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)", next: "wind_dice" },
      feather_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🪶", text: "The scatter wobbled under a hasty glance, and the one true feather blew off the ridge before it could be confirmed. Voltbird noted the failure and pressed on toward the weather stations regardless.", next: "wind_dice" },
      wind_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌬️", die: 20, target: 11, text: "A crosswind had picked up along the ridge path, gusting hard enough to knock a flight off true heading. Roll 11 or more on a twenty-sided die to hold your line through it.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 11 or more on a fair twenty-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "9/20", "11/20", "2/5"], answer: 0, why: "The rolls 11 through 20 succeed — 10 outcomes out of 20, so 10/20 = 1/2. An even bet. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your wings: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "wind_good", fail: "wind_miss" },
      wind_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🦅", text: "Voltbird held the line clean through the gust, feathers barely ruffled. The party followed with considerably less composure.", next: "gate_fight" },
      wind_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "💨", text: "The crosswind had opinions about being crossed gracefully by four passengers at once, and voiced them by scattering everyone sideways at the least dignified possible moment.", next: "gate_fight" },
      gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "It hovered right over the Eyrie's own gate, small and grey and thoroughly pleased with itself: a Roughling Driftwind, gently blurring the posted flight time from TEN MINUTES into 'ten-ish, could be more, could be less.'\n\n'I don't do could-be,' said Voltbird, wings crackling. 'Exactly ten is rather the whole point of me.'",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Driftwind", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
          hitText: "The answer landed clean, and a corner of the Driftwind sloughed away in a puff of corrected grey.",
          strikeText: "The Driftwind billowed forward and, for one worrying moment, every heading in sight looked a little bit less certain than it had a right to." },
        victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
      gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Driftwind came apart with an embarrassed little pop, and the posted flight time said TEN MINUTES again — clear, exact, undisputed. Voltbird passed the gate. Small grey scouts, sitting on exact timings to see what gave — and somewhere higher up, whatever was sending them.", next: "crossroads" },
      gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "There is no shame in an orderly withdrawal, provided the timing gets logged properly afterwards. It was logged. Voltbird went in by the updraft instead, trailing a faint smear of grey that took the rest of the morning to shake off.", next: "crossroads" },
      crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "⛰️", text: "Just past the gate, the range split three ways under a weathered signpost.\n\nThe left path climbed to the EYRIE, where every flight was planned and timed. The centre path led to the WEATHER STATION, where storm fronts were tracked and clocked. The right path led to STORM RIDGE, where the worst of the wind was currently being contested by something that wasn't weather.\n\nSomewhere behind each path — the range's old flyers swore to this — a Copper Coil was waiting to be earned, the very coil that used to time every crossing down to its true minute.", options: [
        { label: "Search the Eyrie", next: "eyrie_1", preview: "A speed-and-distance puzzle guards the Coil. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Search the Weather Station", next: "station_1", preview: "A dice challenge guards the Coil. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
        { label: "Search Storm Ridge", next: "ridge_1", preview: "A fight guards the Coil. Sets the route for the rest of the journey." },
      ] },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: EYRIE ========================= */
      eyrie_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🏔️", setRoute: "eyrie", text: "The Eyrie perched high over the range, wind-charts pinned to every ledge. An old flight-planner squinted at one, visibly frustrated. 'A storm front's sitting a hundred and fifty miles out, drifting toward us at twenty miles an hour. I'm flying out to meet it at forty. I need to know exactly when we'll meet, and I daren't guess.'", next: "eyrie_puzzle" },
      eyrie_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🌪️", text: "The planner tapped the chart. 'A hundred and fifty miles apart. The storm's closing at twenty miles an hour. I fly to meet it at forty miles an hour. How many hours until we meet?'", question: "A storm front is 150 miles away, closing in at 20mph. Voltbird flies to meet it at 40mph. How many hours until they meet?", options: ["2 hours", "2.5 hours", "3 hours", "3.5 hours", "5 hours"], answer: 1,
        hint: "When two things move toward each other, add their speeds together to get the closing speed, then divide the distance by that.",
        solution: "Combined closing speed = 40 + 20 = 60mph. Time to meet = 150 ÷ 60 = 2.5 hours.",
        good: "eyrie_good", mid: "eyrie_mid" },
      eyrie_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", grantComponent: "copper_coil", text: "The planner checked the working twice, timed the flight to the exact half-hour, and the whole crossing went off without a hitch. Buried under the chart table, wrapped in oilcloth, sat the Copper Coil.\n\nComponent found: the Copper Coil.", next: "eyrie_subchoice" },
      eyrie_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The timing came out wrong twice before the planner worked it through by hand, step by careful step. The Coil stayed wrapped under the table, unclaimed, for this visit at least.", next: "eyrie_subchoice" },
      eyrie_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🗻", text: "'Two more things, while you're here,' said the planner. 'A rockslide's blocked the east ledge, or I could use a second opinion on a tangled bit of reasoning. Time for one.'", options: [
        { label: "Clear the rockslide (a fight)", next: "eyrie_slide_fight", preview: "Something has taken up residence in the rubble." },
        { label: "Untangle the reasoning (a check)", next: "eyrie_logic_check", preview: "A Logic check on a puzzling bit of chart-work." },
      ] },
      eyrie_slide_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪨", qLevel: 2,
        text: "The rockslide turned out to hide a Roughling Slack, fog packed loosely into loose stone, currently making every ledge measurement wobble between 'about here' and 'somewhere near there.'\n\n'That's my ledge you're wobbling,' said Voltbird, which is an alarming sentence to need to say to a rockslide.",
        enemy: { name: "A Roughling Slack", emoji: "🪨", hits: 2, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a patch of the Slack unravelled into ordinary loose scree.",
          strikeText: "Something cold and shifting rolled underfoot, and somebody in the party came away with a scraped wing-tip." },
        victory: "eyrie_slide_win", retreat: "eyrie_slide_retreat" },
      eyrie_slide_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏔️", text: "The Slack dissolved into scattered scree, and the east ledge held firm underfoot at last. The planner tested the footing with real relief.", next: "converge1" },
      eyrie_slide_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Slack held the ledge for the day, and the east route stayed unsteady a while longer. The planner took the setback the way old flyers take setbacks: hard, briefly, then back to the charts.", next: "converge1" },
      eyrie_logic_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "📐", stat: 2, dc: 11,
        text: "The chart-work in question nearly balanced, which for a flight plan is its own particular kind of alarming. Working out whether the reasoning genuinely held meant checking it properly, not just trusting how tidy the lines looked.\n\nSomebody sharp with Logic had better verify it.", success: "eyrie_logic_good", fail: "eyrie_logic_bad" },
      eyrie_logic_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "The reasoning checked out clean, once properly tested rather than just eyeballed. The planner breathed out with real relief and pressed a purse of stars into Voltbird's talon. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      eyrie_logic_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The reasoning very nearly held but not quite, and a whole chart had to be quietly redrawn. 'Close doesn't count in a flight plan,' the planner said, without much bitterness.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: WEATHER STATION ========================= */
      station_1: { type: "story", emoji: "🌡️", setRoute: "station", text: "The Weather Station clicked and whirred with instruments, dials everywhere. A tracker stood beside the biggest gauge, stopwatch in claw. 'Every storm we track needs its speed clocked exact. Get the reading wrong and the whole forecast drifts. Reckon you can clock this one properly?'", next: "station_dice" },
      station_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "⏱️", die: 10, target: 7, text: "Roll 7 or more on a ten-sided die to catch the gauge needle at exactly the right moment, before the reading drifts.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 7 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "3/10", "2/5", "1/4"], answer: 2, why: "The rolls 7 through 10 succeed — 4 outcomes out of 10, so 4/10 = 2/5. A middling window. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your claw: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "station_good", fail: "station_miss" },
      station_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", grantComponent: "copper_coil", text: "The needle caught exactly on time, and the gauge hummed its approval. Wedged behind the instrument panel, clearly left there by someone in a hurry long ago, sat the Copper Coil — a spare, or perhaps the original.\n\nComponent found: the Copper Coil.", next: "station_subchoice" },
      station_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "⏱️", setMid: true, text: "The needle drifted a half-second late, and the whole reading came out useless. Whatever had been wedged behind the panel stayed wedged, unclaimed, for this visit.", next: "station_subchoice" },
      station_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🔬", text: "The tracker wiped instrument-grease off their claws. 'Two more jobs, if you've a moment. The wind-vane needs resetting fast, or there's a round-trip speed puzzle I can't work out. Pick one.'", options: [
        { label: "Reset the wind-vane (a check)", next: "station_science_check", preview: "A Science check — the vane won't wait." },
        { label: "Work out the round-trip speed (a puzzle)", next: "station_puzzle", preview: "An Arithmetic puzzle about average speed on a there-and-back flight." },
      ] },
      station_science_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🌡️", stat: 3, dc: 11,
        text: "The wind-vane had a habit of drifting off true if you didn't reset it fast, faster than careful measurement really allowed for. This one called for quick claws over quick reasoning.\n\nSomebody sharp with Science had better take it on.", success: "station_science_good", fail: "station_science_bad" },
      station_science_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🌡️", bonusStars: 2, text: "The vane snapped back to true in one clean motion. The tracker looked almost disappointed not to get to watch a longer struggle. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      station_science_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The vane drifted a hair further before settling, stubborn as only old brass ever manages. It would hold, roughly, for now.", next: "converge1" },
      station_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔁", text: "'Sixty miles from here to the Lighthouse,' said the tracker. 'Voltbird flies out at thirty miles an hour, and back at sixty. What's the average speed for the whole round trip? And don't just average thirty and sixty — that's the trap.'", question: "Voltbird flies 60 miles out at 30mph, then flies the same 60 miles back at 60mph. What is the average speed for the whole round trip?", options: ["30 mph", "35 mph", "40 mph", "45 mph", "50 mph"], answer: 2,
        hint: "Work out the total distance and total time for the whole trip separately, then divide distance by time — don't just average the two speeds.",
        solution: "Total distance = 60 + 60 = 120 miles. Time out = 60 ÷ 30 = 2 hours. Time back = 60 ÷ 60 = 1 hour. Total time = 3 hours. Average speed = 120 ÷ 3 = 40mph.",
        good: "station_puzzle_good", mid: "station_puzzle_mid" },
      station_puzzle_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'Forty, exactly,' said the tracker, checking the log twice. 'Bang on the mark — and no, it's never just the average of thirty and sixty.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      station_puzzle_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The average came out as forty-five twice before the tracker walked it through by hand, showing why the simple average of the two speeds doesn't work.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT TWO, LANE: STORM RIDGE ========================= */
      ridge_1: { type: "story", emoji: "⛈️", setRoute: "ridge", text: "Storm Ridge caught every gale the range produced, wind-worn and treacherous. A weather-flag lay torn from its post, and guarding the wreckage was something that had clearly moved in uninvited.", next: "ridge_fight" },
      ridge_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌫️", qLevel: 2,
        text: "A Roughling Squall had settled over the ridge, fog packed hard into wind that made every gust feel unfairly, unpredictably strong just standing near it.\n\n'Nobody asked you,' said Voltbird, wings crackling.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Squall", emoji: "🌫️", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true, and a corner of the Squall's grey bulk sloughed away into ordinary blustering air.",
          strikeText: "A grey gust rolled over the party, and somebody got knocked rather harder off their feet than expected." },
        victory: "ridge_win", retreat: "ridge_retreat" },
      ridge_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", grantComponent: "copper_coil", text: "The Squall dissolved into ordinary blustering air, and the whole ridge seemed to calm at once. Tucked behind the torn flagpost, exactly where the old flyers swore it had vanished from weeks ago, sat the Copper Coil.\n\nComponent found: the Copper Coil.", next: "ridge_subchoice" },
      ridge_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", setMid: true, text: "The Squall held the ridge, and the wind stayed unpredictable a while longer. The old flyers took the setback with the tired patience of creatures used to setbacks.", next: "ridge_subchoice" },
      ridge_subchoice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🚩", text: "The old flyers straightened their feathers. 'Two things, if you've the time. The flag needs re-hoisting fast, or there's a rate puzzle about wingbeats I can't work through. Pick one.'", options: [
        { label: "Re-hoist the flag (a check)", next: "ridge_arith_check", preview: "An Arithmetic check — quick figuring, not deep reasoning." },
        { label: "Work through the wingbeat puzzle (a puzzle)", next: "ridge_puzzle", preview: "A rate puzzle about scaling distance covered per second." },
      ] },
      ridge_arith_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧮", stat: 0, dc: 11,
        text: "The flag's true height had to be worked out from the length of rope left on the post, which meant a quick, careful bit of figuring rather than a guess.\n\nSomebody sharp with Arithmetic had better work it out.", success: "ridge_arith_good", fail: "ridge_arith_bad" },
      ridge_arith_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧮", bonusStars: 2, text: "The figures came out clean, and the flag went back up at exactly the right height, snapping smartly in the wind. (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      ridge_arith_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The figures tangled somewhere in the middle, and the flag went up a little askew, same as the wind itself.", next: "converge1" },
      ridge_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🌀", text: "'Voltbird's wingbeat,' said an old flyer, 'covers fifteen metres every four seconds in a steady glide. At that rate, how many metres does she cover in one full minute?'", question: "Voltbird's wingbeat covers 15 metres every 4 seconds. At that rate, how many metres does she cover in one minute (60 seconds)?", options: ["180m", "200m", "210m", "225m", "240m"], answer: 3,
        hint: "Work out how many 4-second intervals fit into a minute, then multiply by the distance covered in each interval.",
        solution: "60 ÷ 4 = 15 intervals in a minute. 15 × 15m = 225m.",
        good: "ridge_puzzle_good", mid: "ridge_puzzle_mid" },
      ridge_puzzle_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'Two hundred and twenty-five, exactly,' said the flyer, checking it against the wind-gauge. 'That's the fastest that's ever been worked out on this ridge.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "converge1" },
      ridge_puzzle_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "The intervals miscounted twice before the flyer walked it through by hand, tapping out each four-second beat aloud.", next: "converge1" },

      /* ========================= ACT THREE: shared ========================= */
      converge1: { type: "story", emoji: "🔥", text: "Whichever path had given up its Copper Coil, Voltbird returned to the range's central lookout with a growing certainty that this was not the only piece of the true timing chain gone missing.\n\nTwo more landmarks awaited: a Glass Lens that used to focus every storm-sighting at exactly the right distance, and a Brass Weathervane that once pointed true no matter how the wind lied.", next: "lens_dice" },
      lens_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🔭", die: 12, target: 9, text: "The lens cabinet had jammed, its catch stiff with old frost. Roll 9 or more on a twelve-sided die to free it without cracking the glass.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 9 or more on a fair twelve-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "1/3", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "The rolls 9 through 12 succeed — 4 outcomes out of 12, so 4/12 = 1/3. A narrow window. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your grip: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "lens_good", fail: "lens_miss" },
      lens_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔭", grantComponent: "glass_lens", text: "The catch gave way clean, no cracks at all, and there inside the cabinet sat the Glass Lens — ground to a precision that no ordinary lens-maker could manage twice.\n\nComponent found: the Glass Lens.", next: "weathervane_check" },
      lens_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", setMid: true, text: "The catch held fast and a hairline crack ran up the cabinet door for the trouble. The Lens stayed exactly where it was, unclaimed, for this visit at least.", next: "weathervane_check" },
      weathervane_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧭", stat: 2, dc: 12,
        text: "The Brass Weathervane had been misfiled decades ago among a shelf of ordinary instruments, and finding it meant reasoning through the lookout's own records to work out which entry didn't actually belong.\n\nSomebody sharp with Logic had better trace it.", success: "weathervane_good", fail: "weathervane_bad" },
      weathervane_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧭", grantComponent: "brass_weathervane", text: "The records untangled cleanly, one column at a time, and led straight to a drawer nobody had opened in years: the Brass Weathervane, exactly where the reasoning said it had to be.\n\nComponent found: the Brass Weathervane.", next: "tower_scene" },
      weathervane_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The records tangled somewhere around the third column, and the trail went cold. The Weathervane stayed misfiled, unrecovered, for this visit.", next: "tower_scene" },
      tower_scene: { type: "story", emoji: "📋", text: "Deep in the lookout's clock tower, an old timekeeper sat hunched over a ledger of storm crossings. 'Trying to work out how fast two winds cross a valley together,' the timekeeper said, not looking up. 'Not a simple sum. A THINKING job.'", next: "tower_puzzle" },
      tower_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🌪️", text: "'A storm front alone,' said the timekeeper, 'would cross this valley in six hours. A tailwind alone would push weather through in four hours. Working together, how long would they take to cross it?'", question: "A storm front alone crosses a valley in 6 hours. A tailwind alone would push weather across it in 4 hours. Working together, how long do they take to cross the valley?", options: ["2 hours", "2 hours 24 minutes", "2 hours 30 minutes", "2 hours 40 minutes", "3 hours"], answer: 1,
        hint: "Work out what fraction of the valley each one crosses in one hour, add those fractions together, then find the reciprocal of the total.",
        solution: "Storm alone: 1/6 of the valley per hour. Tailwind alone: 1/4 per hour. Together: 1/6 + 1/4 = 2/12 + 3/12 = 5/12 of the valley per hour. Time = 12/5 hours = 2.4 hours = 2 hours 24 minutes.",
        good: "tower_good", mid: "tower_mid" },
      tower_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", bonusStars: 2, text: "'Two hours twenty-four,' said the timekeeper, checking it against the log. 'Fastest that's ever been worked out in this tower.' (+2 ⭐)", next: "night_choice" },
      tower_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🕰️", text: "The fractions tangled twice before '2 hours 24 minutes' turned up properly, and the timekeeper waited it out with visible patience.", next: "night_choice" },
      night_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🌙", text: "'The proper storm observatory is an hour on from here,' said the timekeeper, banking the tower-lamp. 'Carry this tower's readings through the night and there's a favour in it. Or wait for a safe dawn crossing.'", options: [
        { label: "Fly through the night (risky, with a reward)", next: "night_dice", preview: "A d6 roll with ADVANTAGE. Success earns 3 bonus stars and patches up a heart." },
        { label: "Wait for dawn (safe and steady)", next: "dawn", preview: "No risk, no reward, but the morning crossing is genuinely pleasant." },
      ] },
      night_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 6, target: 4, advantage: true, text: "The night sky churned like a sum done in a hurry. Roll with ADVANTAGE: roll the six-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 4 or more to hold a steady heading in the dark.", predict: { question: "One roll of this die succeeds (4 or more) exactly half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher — what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "2/3", "5/8"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fail. Each fails half the time, so both fail 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4 — meaning you succeed the other 3/4 of the time. Advantage turns a coin toss into a strong majority. +1 to your roll." },
        success: "night_good", fail: "night_bad" },
      night_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 3, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Voltbird held a steady heading through the dark and delivered the tower's readings before the moon had properly set. The promised favour — three bright stars, and a warm thermal that put a whole heart's courage back into the party. Bonus: +3 ⭐ and one heart restored.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      night_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", setMid: true, text: "The night sky won this time. Voltbird came down in an unfamiliar gully, feathers thoroughly ruffled, the readings rather worse for the detour, and the favour quietly withdrawn.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      dawn: { type: "story", emoji: "🌅", text: "The party waited out the dark hours by the tower-lamp, and the observatory opened at a properly civilised hour, morning light falling clean across every dial. Sometimes the boring choice is simply the right one.", next: "ambush_fight" },
      ambush_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🌩️", qLevel: 3,
        text: "The observatory's own timing-counter, which normally clocked one correctly-measured crossing per storm, was being run by the wrong management this morning: two Roughling Lags had set up behind it with a hand-lettered sign reading TIMED: APROXIMATLY, while the real timekeeper glared from behind a bolted hatch.\n\n'That's not even spelled right,' said Voltbird, which as declarations of war go was entirely accurate.",
        enemy: { name: "Roughling Lags", emoji: "🌩️", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and one of the Lags thinned noticeably, the way fog does when corrected firmly.",
          strikeText: "A grey gust swept the clocks off true, and somebody in the party got timed at something they hadn't agreed to." },
        victory: "ambush_win", retreat: "ambush_retreat" },
      ambush_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏢", text: "The real timekeeper unbolted the hatch, then their expression, in that order. 'Waived, for pest control,' they said, sweeping the false sign into the bin. 'But nobody's clocked without a properly measured reading. Rules are rules.'", next: "observatory_puzzle" },
      ambush_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The party fell back down the tower stair with the Lags' false cheer still ringing behind them, and came at the counter eventually by the side door, scratched and simmering. 'Bad morning,' said the timekeeper. 'But nobody's clocked without measuring properly.'", next: "observatory_puzzle" },
      observatory_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧾", text: "The timekeeper slid a flight-log across. 'Take your time,' they said, in the voice of someone counting it.", question: "Voltbird flies 16 loops total between the Short Loop (10 minutes each) and the Long Loop (25 minutes each), totalling 310 minutes of flight. How many Long Loops did she fly?", options: ["6", "8", "9", "10", "12"], answer: 3,
        hint: "Let s be Short Loops and l be Long Loops. Then s + l = 16 and 10s + 25l = 310. Substitute s = 16 − l into the second equation.",
        solution: "s + l = 16, so s = 16 − l. Then 10(16 − l) + 25l = 310, giving 160 − 10l + 25l = 310, so 15l = 150, meaning l = 10. Check: 6 Short Loops and 10 Long Loops: 10×6 + 25×10 = 60 + 250 = 310. ✓",
        good: "observatory_good", mid: "observatory_mid" },
      observatory_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", text: "The timekeeper checked the flight-log twice, made a small approving noise, and waved the party straight through. 'Go on,' they said. 'The range could use someone who times like that.'", next: "act4_intro" },
      observatory_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "The timekeeper shook their head, not unkindly, and worked the true sum out step by step, making the party repeat it back before waving them through the side gate.", next: "act4_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FOUR: second branch ========================= */
      act4_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🗺️", text: "Word among the flyers was that the range's trouble hadn't started all at once — it had come apart at three specific corners: the Eyrie, the Weather Station, Storm Ridge. The very three already searched once, in a hurry. A second, calmer look at whichever was already familiar might turn up what a first pass had missed.", next: "act4_choice" },
      act4_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🔁", text: "'Only time for one more proper look before the trail goes cold,' said the head flyer. 'Eyrie, Weather Station, or Storm Ridge — wherever you already know your way around ought to go easiest.'", options: [
        { label: "Return to the Eyrie", next: "act4_eyrie", preview: "A Geometry check — much easier if you already know the Eyrie." },
        { label: "Return to the Weather Station", next: "act4_station", preview: "A dice roll — much easier if you already know the Weather Station." },
        { label: "Return to Storm Ridge", next: "act4_ridge", preview: "A fight — much easier if you already know Storm Ridge." },
      ] },
      act4_eyrie: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🏔️", stat: 1, dc: 13, routeBonus: { route: "eyrie", amount: 3 },
        text: "A second, calmer pass through the Eyrie turned up a chart drawn to the wrong scale, throwing every bearing off by a fraction — exactly the kind of check that goes twice as fast for someone who already knows every ledge's usual shape.\n\nSomebody sharp with Geometry had better verify it properly this time.", success: "act4_eyrie_good", fail: "act4_eyrie_bad" },
      act4_eyrie_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🏔️", bonusStars: 3, text: "The scale error resolved cleanly under a second, familiar look — ground already walked once that morning made the check faster the second time round. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_eyrie_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The scale error refused to resolve however carefully it was checked, and the chart was logged as provisional, pending fresher eyes.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_station: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌡️", die: 10, target: 4, routeBonus: { route: "station", amount: 3 },
        text: "The Weather Station's main gauge needed clocking once more, against a much fussier full-storm reading — though considerably less fussy for claws that already knew its particular rhythm. Roll 4 or more on a ten-sided die.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 4 or more on a fair ten-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "3/10", "7/10", "2/5"], answer: 2, why: "The rolls 4 through 10 succeed — 7 outcomes out of 10, so 7/10. Predicting the odds correctly steadies your claw: +1 to your roll." },
        success: "act4_station_good", fail: "act4_station_bad" },
      act4_station_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🌡️", bonusStars: 3, text: "The full-storm reading landed clean on the first proper attempt, and the tracker nearly wept with relief, pressing a full purse of stars into Voltbird's talon. (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_station_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😬", text: "The full-storm reading proved rougher than the morning's version, and the forecast came out thin regardless. The tracker took it in good humour, mostly.", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_ridge: { type: "combat", emoji: "⛈️", qLevel: 3, routeBonus: { route: "ridge", amount: 3 },
        text: "A second Roughling had settled over the ridge exactly where the Squall had fallen — smaller, greyer, wearing an unsteady air it had no real right to. Ground already fought over once that morning was, at least, ground the party knew how to move through fast.\n\n'Not again,' said Voltbird, without much surprise.",
        enemy: { name: "A Roughling Gust", emoji: "⛈️", hits: 3, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer struck true and a grey gust of it sloughed away into ordinary blustering air.",
          strikeText: "A tired grey wind rolled over someone in the party, who got knocked rather harder off their feet than expected." },
        victory: "act4_ridge_win", retreat: "act4_ridge_retreat" },
      act4_ridge_win: { type: "story", emoji: "⛈️", bonusStars: 3, text: "The old flyers re-checked the ridge against the now-settled wind and found it held, clean, for the first time in weeks. 'Whatever you did the first time through here,' they said, 'it's clearly still working.' (+3 ⭐)", next: "act5_intro" },
      act4_ridge_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "The Gust held the ridge a second time, and the wind stayed unpredictable a while longer. The old flyers sighed the sigh of creatures who have started to suspect this is simply how the week is going to go.", next: "act5_intro" },

      /* ========================= ACT FIVE: reconverge, forge, finale ========================= */
      act5_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🏢", text: "Whatever the second visit had turned up, the trail now led, unmistakably, to the observatory's own Great Clock Hall — the room where every crossing's true time had always been struck, and where, judging by the grey vapour pouring gently from beneath its door, something had gone very wrong indeed.\n\nVoltbird checked the flight log. Feather, coil, compass, all present. This was, by now, an entirely automatic reflex.", next: "captain_fight" },
      captain_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💀", qLevel: 3,
        text: "Inside, something large loomed over the Hall's master clock, smearing every reading into 'roughly on time' with a wet grey talon. The Roughling Stillwind was bigger than its Driftwinds and greyer than its Lags, and it looked up with eyes like un-inked full stops.\n\n'STOPPED,' it said. 'MOSTLY.'\n\n'Nothing that's mostly stopped has ever kept a promise,' said Voltbird, wings crackling for good this time.",
        enemy: { name: "The Roughling Stillwind", emoji: "💀", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
          hitText: "The answer landed like a returned reading, and the Stillwind lost a minute it had clearly been fond of.",
          strikeText: "The Stillwind's talon came down and the whole Hall went approximate; every clock on the wall read a slightly different time." },
        victory: "captain_win", retreat: "captain_retreat" },
      captain_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌤️", text: "Minute by minute, the master clock's true time crept back into place, and the grey vapour stopped pouring from beneath the door. Somewhere over the range, a storm that had been arriving wrong for a fortnight finally, quietly, kept its appointment.", next: "forge" },
      captain_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Stillwind's talon came down again and the Hall went so approximate that the doors were the only certain thing left, so the party used them, regrouped outside, and came back in by the side stair while the Stillwind glowered over the clock below.", next: "forge" },
      forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Behind the Great Clock Hall worked an old instrument-maker who could true a warped lens from twenty paces and had once, reportedly, focused a whole argument down to its one honest point. 'A Storm Prism, is it,' she said, peering over half-moon spectacles. 'Copper Coil, Glass Lens, Brass Weathervane — show me all three, and I'll grind you a prism worth trusting.'",
        forged: "The instrument-maker's own forge roared like an approving crowd. She set the coil, lens and weathervane together into a single travelling prism, and ground its edge steady enough to trust with the whole sky.\n\nA glance through the Storm Prism named a storm's speed before it had finished forming.\n\nThe Storm Prism is yours! (+1 Science, kept forever.)",
        notforged: "The instrument-maker counted the components on her bench, then counted them again, which was polite of her, since it didn't take long.\n\n'Short,' she said, tapping each gap. 'A prism wants all three or it's just glass with good intentions. Come back with the full set.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
        grantItem: "voltbird_prism", next: "finale" },
      finale: { type: "story", emoji: "👑", text: "And so, at last, to the master clock itself.\n\nVoltbird found the trouble on a page near the back of its workings: a single timing that had been recorded, once, long ago, as 'roughly this much' by someone in a hurry — and every storm timed from that page since had been quietly a little bit wrong, the way any clock drifts if you let one minute stay approximate too long.\n\nVoltbird crossed out the roughly. Voltbird wrote in the exactly, in a steady talon, and underlined it twice.\n\nSomewhere over the range, a storm that had been arriving wrong for a fortnight finally, audibly, kept perfect time.", next: "end" },
      end: { type: "end",
        goodText: "The range held a quiet celebration at the observatory's own door, which for a clock tower counts as chiming every bell at once. The head flyer gave a speech of properly measured length, the instrument-maker was seen to almost smile, and the timekeeper waived the toll a second, historic time.\n\nAt the end of it, the range pressed the True Compass into Voltbird's keeping: the founding record of every true bearing, cross-checked and never again likely to drift. Voltbird flew home counting the stars, and found, for once, that the count came out exactly right.",
        midText: "The range was grateful, in a slightly muddled way — the celebration ran a storm short of what was planned and the head flyer's speech lost its place twice. Still, the readings held steady, the timings agreed with themselves, and that was the job.\n\nAt the gate, the head flyer pressed the True Compass into Voltbird's keeping anyway. 'You got here,' they said. 'The route was scenic. Routes often are.' Voltbird flew home already planning a tidier attempt, because a timing worth getting right is worth getting right again.",
        grantItem: "voltbird_compass" },
    },
  },
};